window.__STORY_DATA__ = {"routes.json":{"Castellan":{"trust":55,"fear":5,"gold":20,"pack":["sword","castle_talisman"],"description":"Born in the castle. You know halls and guards."},"Hedgeborn":{"trust":45,"fear":15,"gold":5,"pack":["knife","poultice","village_map"],"description":"Raised in the villages. You know dirt roads and secrets."}},"companions.json":{"ash":{"name":"Ash","description":"A rider with eyes like flint."},"vale":{"name":"Ser Vale","description":"A knight stripped of his honor."},"moth":{"name":"Moth","description":"A street urchin with quick fingers."},"rook":{"name":"Rook","description":"A raven that speaks. Or a madman."},"hedge_witch":{"name":"Hedge Witch","description":"Old magic in a cabin of bones."},"lumen":{"name":"Lumen, the Lantern-child","description":"A small figure who carries a grey flame through the Underland."},"gloam":{"name":"Gloam, the Mirror-Knight","description":"A knight bound inside a reflection, freed at a price."}},"codex.json":{"the_gate":{"title":"The Hollow Gate","flag":"region_gate","text":"A door with no wall around it, standing in the deep places of the realm. It does not open for keys. It opens for grief, and for names spoken in the dark. What passes through does not come back the same — if it comes back at all."},"the_keeper":{"title":"The Keeper","flag":"codex_keeper","text":"Not a monster. A post. Someone always keeps the Gate, and the keeping hollows them out until they are only the door's hunger wearing a face. The last Keeper was a person once, with a name they have spent centuries forgetting."},"the_underland":{"title":"The Underland","flag":"region_threshold","text":"The far side of the Gate: the realm reflected in black water. Every place you know has a drowned twin here. The rules are the rules of dreams — distance lies, the dead speak, and what you carry in matters more than what you can do."},"mirror_law":{"title":"The Mirror Law","flag":"codex_mirror","text":"In the Underland, a reflection can be truer than the thing it reflects. Break a mirror and you may free what it held — or loose it. The Mirror-Knight is bound by this law, and so, quietly, are you."},"true_names":{"title":"On True Names","flag":"codex_truename","text":"Everything in the Underland has a name it has hidden even from itself. Learn one, say it aloud, and you can command the thing — once. The Keeper's true name is the only key the Gate has ever answered to."},"the_lantern_child":{"title":"The Lantern-child","flag":"codex_lumen","text":"A small figure who carries a grey flame through the Ashen March, lighting the way for travelers who are already dead and don't know it. Whether the child is a ghost, a guide, or a trap depends entirely on how you treat them."}},"story/index.json":{"chapters":["chapter_01.json","chapter_02.json","chapter_03.json","chapter_04.json","chapter_05.json","chapter_06.json","chapter_07.json","chapter_08.json","chapter_09.json","chapter_10.json","chapter_11.json","chapter_12.json","chapter_13.json","chapter_14.json","chapter_15.json","chapter_16.json","chapter_17.json","chapter_18.json","chapter_19.json","chapter_20.json","chapter_21.json","chapter_22.json","chapter_23.json","chapter_24.json"]},"story/chapter_01.json":{"scenes":[{"id":"intro","chapter":"CHAPTER I · COURTYARD","text":"The King does not look up when you enter. His crown is upside-down on the table.\nThe spymaster was found that morning — no wounds, no marks, a scream frozen on his face.\n\n\"{missing_name} was taken last night. No body. No ransom. The wizard felt a door open somewhere else.\"\n\nHe slides three things across the table.\n\n  1. A signet ring — opens any door in the kingdom\n  2. A pouch of gold\n  3. A sealed letter from the spymaster\n\n\"Find my {missing_child}. Come back without {missing_poss} and I won’t need to execute you.\"","choices":[{"text":"Take the ring. Ride for the Wastes.","next":"wastes_gate","do":["add_item:signet_ring"]},{"text":"Take the gold. Visit the city underworld.","next":"city_gates","do":["gold:50"]},{"text":"Ask the wizard about the letter.","next":"wizard_tower"},{"text":"Find the castle captain for men.","next":"barracks"}]}]},"story/chapter_02.json":{"scenes":[{"id":"wastes_gate","chapter":"CHAPTER II · THE WASTES","text":"The city wall ends. Beyond it, the Wastes — grey hills and old bones. A storm is rolling in.\nA merchant caravan huddles by the gate, too afraid to move. A lone rider watches you from a rock.\nYou have the signet ring.","choices":[{"text":"Show the ring. Demand passage.","next":"rider_demand","do":["trust:-5"]},{"text":"Sneak past in the storm.","next":"wastes_lost","require":"hedgeborn"},{"text":"Talk to the rider.","next":"rider_join"},{"text":"Join the caravan.","next":"caravan","do":["add_item:supplies"]},{"text":"A felled tree across the road. Veterans in the brush.","next":"deep_bandits_1"}]},{"id":"rider_demand","chapter":"CHAPTER II · THE WASTES","text":"You hold up the ring. The rider's eyes flick to it, then to your face. Slowly, deliberately, they spit on the ground.\n\"A crown is a crown. I don't owe it nothing.\"\nBut they don't leave. Not yet.\n\"...The road is mine, though. Two gold and I lead you through. No promises past that.\"","choices":[{"text":"Pay the two gold.","next":"wastes_ride","require":"gold:2","do":["gold:-2","companion:ash"]},{"text":"Refuse. Ride alone.","next":"wastes_alone","do":["trust:-5","fear:5"]},{"text":"Offer the castle talisman.","next":"wastes_ride","require":"castle_talisman","do":["rem_item:castle_talisman","companion:ash"]}]},{"id":"rider_join","chapter":"CHAPTER II · THE RIDER","text":"The rider is young. Eyes like flint.\n\"I know where the tracks go. But I don't work for crowns.\"\nThey watch your hand on your weapon.\n\"A guide costs 20 gold. Or a companion who isn’t likely to die before sundown.\"","choices":[{"text":"Pay 20 gold.","next":"wastes_ride","require":"gold:20","do":["gold:-20","companion:ash"]},{"text":"Refuse. Continue alone.","next":"wastes_alone","do":["trust:-5"]},{"text":"Offer the castle talisman instead.","next":"wastes_ride","require":"castle_talisman","do":["rem_item:castle_talisman","companion:ash"]}]},{"id":"wastes_ride","chapter":"CHAPTER III · OLD DOORWAY","text":"You ride hard for two days. Ash knows every rock and raven in the Wastes.\nOn the second night they point north.\n\"Someone was here. Not long ago. Not human.\"\nYou find an old doorway half-buried in the hillside. Stone. No handle. No keyhole.\nA symbol carved above it makes your eyes hurt.\nAsh steps back.\n\"I’m not going in there.\"","choices":[{"text":"Enter alone.","next":"hollow_gate","do":["trust:-10"]},{"text":"Search the hillside first.","next":"hill_search"},{"text":"Convince Ash to stay (Trust 60+).","next":"hollow_gate","require":"trust:60","do":["trust:5"]},{"text":"Call a halt. Make camp for the night.","next":"camp_rest"},{"text":"Follow the goat track to the hut on the hill.","next":"side_captain_hut"},{"text":"Stop for the minstrel on the milestone.","next":"side_minstrel_1"},{"text":"Follow Moth toward an old tower.","next":"side_moth_roost_1","require":"companion:moth"},{"text":"Halt. Something thin and gray is following you down the road.","next":"deep_wraith_road_1"},{"text":"[wraith lingers from yesterday]","next":"deep_wraith_lingers","require":"flag:wraith_follows"}]},{"id":"hill_search","chapter":"CHAPTER III · HILLSIDE","text":"You circle the doorway and find a second opening smaller, hidden behind roots — an old servant’s entrance. But a ward hums across it.\nYou can:\n  • Use the signet ring to weaken the ward.\n  • Try the lock if you recruited Moth.\n  • Force it with the sword and take the damage.","choices":[{"text":"Use the signet ring.","next":"hollow_gate","require":"signet_ring","do":["rem_item:signet_ring"]},{"text":"Pick the lock (needs Moth).","next":"hollow_gate","require":"moth","do":["trust:5"]},{"text":"Force it.","next":"hollow_gate","do":["fear:15","trust:-5"]},{"text":"Find a hollow. Make camp.","next":"camp_rest"},{"text":"Climb to the stone hut with the smoke.","next":"side_captain_hut"},{"text":"Visit the King's Yard outside the wall.","next":"side_graveyard_gate"},{"text":"The tunnel map shows a crown-with-a-slash off the main route.","next":"deep_lost_city_1","require":"tunnel_map"}]},{"id":"wizard_tower","chapter":"CHAPTER II · WIZARD TOWER","text":"The wizard is old, annoyed, and surrounded by books that whisper.\n\"The letter mentions a hollow gate. These pages call it a wound in the world, stitched shut by the old kings. If she’s through it, ordinary steel won’t help.\"\nHe offers you a wardstone. It won’t open doors — but it might close one behind you.","choices":[{"text":"Take the wardstone and leave.","next":"wastes_gate","do":["add_item:wardstone"]},{"text":"Demand more help.","next":"wizard_demand","do":["trust:-10"]},{"text":"Walk up to the tower door.","next":"tower_approach"}]},{"id":"wizard_demand","chapter":"CHAPTER II · WIZARD TOWER","text":"The wizard straightens. The books stop whispering.\n\"I can send you through. But the cost is yours, not mine.\"\nA doorway of light opens behind him. Beyond it: grey hills.\nHe winks. \"Say hello to my old apprentice while you’re there.\"","choices":[{"text":"Step through.","next":"hollow_gate","do":["fear:10"]},{"text":"Refuse. Do this your own way.","next":"wastes_gate"},{"text":"Push past him. Explore the tower.","next":"tower_foyer"}]},{"id":"barracks","chapter":"CHAPTER II · BARRACKS","text":"The captain is sharpening a sword. He laughs when you ask for men.\n\"{missing_short_cap} runs off into the dark and you want a parade?\"\nThen he sees the ring — or your face — and stops.\n\"I’ll send Vale. He’s the best I have. And the most ruined. If that suits you.\"","choices":[{"text":"Accept Ser Vale.","next":"wastes_gate","do":["companion:vale"]},{"text":"Decline. You work better alone.","next":"wastes_gate"},{"text":"Bed down in the barracks loft.","next":"camp_rest"},{"text":"Slip out to the King's Yard.","next":"side_graveyard_gate"}]},{"id":"city_gates","chapter":"CHAPTER I · CITY GATES","text":"The city smells of salt and lies. You spend the night in a tavern where maps are traded like secrets.\nA woman in the corner slips you a note: meet her at the bridge at midnight.\nShe calls herself Moth. She knows about doors that shouldn’t exist.\nIn return, she wants half the reward.","choices":[{"text":"Meet Moth at the bridge.","next":"moth_join","do":["companion:moth"]},{"text":"Ignore her and head for the Wastes alone.","next":"wastes_gate"},{"text":"Push into the city proper.","next":"city_lost"}]},{"id":"moth_join","chapter":"CHAPTER II · THE THIEF","text":"Moth is young, fast, and already halfway to stealing your purse before you stop her.\n\"Easy,\" she laughs. \"I needed to know if you were worth the trouble.\"\nShe offers two things: keys to the old smuggler’s tunnels, and a way past locked doors.","choices":[{"text":"Take the tunnel map and go.","next":"wastes_gate","do":["add_item:tunnel_map"]},{"text":"Keep your distance.","next":"wastes_gate"}]}]},"story/chapter_03.json":{"scenes":[{"id":"wastes_lost","chapter":"CHAPTER II · STORM","text":"The storm hides you, but it hides everything else too. You wander until dawn, soaked and lost.\nA raven lands on a stone and tilts its head.\n\"You look like someone who should be dead,\" it says.\nThat is the last thing you expected.","choices":[{"text":"Talk to the raven.","next":"rook_talk"},{"text":"Ignore it and keep walking.","next":"wastes_alone","do":["fear:10"]}]},{"id":"rook_talk","chapter":"CHAPTER II · THE RAVEN","text":"\"My name is Rook. I was a man once. Now I’m advice with wings.\"\nIt hops closer.\n\"The gate you’re looking for? It eats people. Use the signet ring as a shield if you get close, or find the hedge witch — she knows stitches for wounds that don’t bleed.\"","choices":[{"text":"Ask where the hedge witch lives.","next":"witch_path","do":["add_item:rook_note"]},{"text":"Skeptical, but take the advice.","next":"wastes_gate","do":["trust:-5"]}]},{"id":"witch_path","chapter":"CHAPTER II · HEDGE WITCH","text":"Her cabin is made of bones and cedar. She doesn’t look up when you enter.\n\"Ash was here. Vale was here. Moth left a scar on my doorframe. Everyone looks for the girl. Most come back wrong.\"\nShe offers two potions for gold:\n  • A seeing draught (reveal hidden paths)\n  • A warding draught (protect against fear)","choices":[{"text":"Buy seeing draught (15 gold).","next":"hollow_gate","require":"gold:15","do":["gold:-15","add_item:seeing_draught"]},{"text":"Buy warding draught (15 gold).","next":"hollow_gate","require":"gold:15","do":["gold:-15","add_item:warding_draught"]},{"text":"Leave without buying.","next":"hollow_gate"},{"text":"Step up to the line of carved birches.","next":"witch_wood_threshold"},{"text":"Sleep on the road. Dream.","next":"dream_road_1"}]},{"id":"caravan","chapter":"CHAPTER II · CARAVAN","text":"You join the caravan. They tell stories about the Wastes — lights that move without bodies, whispers in the stone, caravans that entered and never left.\nBy morning the storm has passed. They leave you at the edge of the hills with enough food for two days.\nYou have survived. For now.","choices":[{"text":"Continue into the Wastes.","next":"rider_join"},{"text":"Settle in for the long road.","next":"caravan_day_one"}]},{"id":"wastes_alone","chapter":"CHAPTER III · ALONE","text":"You move through the Wastes without a guide. The horizon is wrong. The sky is too wide. Every shadow looks like someone watching.\nAfter two days you find the doorway the rider described. Alone. Hungry. Still alive.","choices":[{"text":"Enter the Hollow Gate.","next":"hollow_gate","do":["fear:10"]},{"text":"Stop at the wayhouse over the rise.","next":"side_inn_arrival"},{"text":"Check your pack for what the wraith left behind.","next":"deep_wraith_followup","require":"flag:wraith_follows"}]}]},"story/chapter_04.json":{"scenes":[{"id":"hollow_gate","chapter":"CHAPTER IV · THE HOLLOW GATE","text":"The stone is cold. The symbol glows when you touch it.\nThe wall doesn’t open — it breathes.\nYou step through, and the world ends.\nDarkness. Not night-dark. Something older. Something that eats light.\nAhead, a girl’s voice: a lullaby your mother used to hum. {missing_name}. Close.\nSomething else is with her.","choices":[{"text":"Follow the singing.","next":"finale_start"},{"text":"Move quietly through the dark.","next":"finale_start","do":["add_item:shadow_blade"]},{"text":"Light a torch.","next":"finale_blind","require":"poultice"},{"text":"Make camp first. One night before.","next":"threshold_1"},{"text":"Before you step through, someone you love speaks behind you.","next":"deep_second_offer_1","require":"rel:vale:50"}]}]},"story/chapter_05.json":{"scenes":[{"id":"finale_start","chapter":"CHAPTER V · THE HOLLOW GATE","text":"You stop where the darkness thickens.\nThe singing has stopped. Something old is listening.\n\nAhead, three shapes resolve out of the dark.\nThe lost ones. The Keeper has taken more than one.\nWho do you reach for first?","choices":[{"text":"{missing_name} — the one the kingdom was looking for.","next":"finale_method","require":"flag:gender_m","do":["flag:rescued_elara"]},{"text":"Moth — your companion, who shouldn't be here at all.","next":"finale_method","require":"flag:gender_m","do":["flag:rescued_moth"]},{"text":"The Hedge Witch — chained in willow root, smiling at you.","next":"finale_method","require":"flag:gender_m","do":["flag:rescued_hedge"]},{"text":"{other_name} — the King's son, who rode out and never came back.","next":"finale_method","require":"flag:gender_f","do":["flag:rescued_cassian"]},{"text":"Ser Vale — knight of the castle, on his knees in the dark.","next":"finale_method","require":"flag:gender_f","do":["flag:rescued_vale"]},{"text":"Rook — the road-mystery, bound at the wrists, not afraid.","next":"finale_method","require":"flag:gender_f","do":["flag:rescued_rook"]}]},{"id":"finale_blind","chapter":"CHAPTER V · THE HOLLOW GATE","text":"The torch flares. The dark eats half the light and spits the rest back.\nThe Keeper sees you now. The lost ones see you too — three faces turned in the gloom, blinking.\n\nThere is no time to be careful. You have to choose.","choices":[{"text":"Run for {missing_name}. Take her hand and don't stop.","next":"ending_clever","require":"flag:gender_m","do":["flag:rescued_elara"]},{"text":"Run for {other_name}. Drag him into the light with you.","next":"ending_clever","require":"flag:gender_f","do":["flag:rescued_cassian"]},{"text":"Stand. Fight the Keeper here, in the open.","next":"finale_method","do":["flag:rescued_elara","flag:rescued_cassian"]}]},{"id":"finale_method","chapter":"CHAPTER V · THE HOLLOW GATE","text":"The Keeper turns. Old and slow and hungry. It has been here a very long time, and it does not like that you have come.\nYou know who you came for. The question is how you leave.","choices":[{"text":"Steel. Cut the Keeper down where it stands.","next":"ending_fight","require":"sword"},{"text":"Steel. Cut with the shadow blade — the dark won't see itself.","next":"ending_fight","require":"shadow_blade"},{"text":"Words. The Keeper is old. Old things can be bargained with.","next":"ending_talk","require":"trust:50"},{"text":"Trickery. The tunnel map shows a way the Keeper never learned.","next":"ending_clever","require":"tunnel_map"},{"text":"Trickery. The signet is older than the Keeper. Show it.","next":"ending_clever","require":"signet_ring"},{"text":"Follow. The Keeper will not die — it flees through the Gate. Go into the dark after it.","next":"u17_start","do":["journal:You did not end it at the Gate. You went through.","flag:region_gate"]},{"text":"Yourself. Stay. Let the others go.","next":"ending_sacrifice"},{"text":"Before stepping through, take inventory of self.","next":"deep_finale_method_extra"}]},{"id":"ending_fight","chapter":"THE END","text":"","choices":[{"text":"Walk on. (What came after.)","next":"epilogue_fight"}]},{"id":"ending_talk","chapter":"THE END","text":"","choices":[{"text":"Walk on. (What came after.)","next":"epilogue_talk"}]},{"id":"ending_clever","chapter":"THE END","text":"","choices":[{"text":"Walk on. (What came after.)","next":"epilogue_clever"}]},{"id":"ending_sacrifice","chapter":"THE END","text":"","choices":[{"text":"Walk on. (What came after.)","next":"epilogue_sacrifice"}]}]},"story/chapter_06.json":{"scenes":[{"id":"witch_wood_threshold","chapter":"CHAPTER III · THE WITCH'S WOOD","text":"The road ends at a wall of birches. They are the color of old bone and they have been carved — every trunk, waist-high, a single sigil that you do not know. The wind here smells like a kitchen: rosemary, blood, woodsmoke.\n\nAsh, if he rode this far with you, will not cross. He says the horses won't either. He says it like a man stating the weather.\n\nA crow drops a tooth at your boot. Human. Adult. Clean at the root.","choices":[{"text":"Step between the birches.","next":"witch_wood_inner"},{"text":"Walk the tree line, looking for a way around.","next":"witch_wood_hanged"},{"text":"Pick up the tooth.","next":"witch_wood_tooth","do":["fear:+5"]}]},{"id":"witch_wood_tooth","chapter":"CHAPTER III · THE WITCH'S WOOD","text":"The tooth is warm. That is the part you will think about, later, when you cannot sleep.\n\nYou pocket it because leaving it felt worse. The crow watches you do it and makes a small, satisfied sound, like a woman clearing her throat.\n\n\"Good,\" it says, in a voice that is almost a voice. \"She likes guests who bring something.\"\n\nThen it is just a crow again, and it is gone.","choices":[{"text":"Step between the birches.","next":"witch_wood_inner","do":["add_item:warm_tooth","flag:crow_spoke"]}]},{"id":"witch_wood_hanged","chapter":"CHAPTER III · THE WITCH'S WOOD","text":"You walk the edge for an hour and the edge does not end. The sun moves wrong. When you look back, your own bootprints are pointed the other way.\n\nA man hangs from a low branch by a rope of braided grass. His feet are inches off the moss. He is not dead. He opens one eye when your shadow falls on him.\n\n\"Don't,\" he says, calm. \"Cut me down and she'll know. Walk past and I keep counting. I'm at four thousand and eleven.\"","choices":[{"text":"\"Counting what?\"","next":"witch_wood_hanged_talk"},{"text":"Cut him down anyway.","next":"witch_wood_hanged_cut","require":"knife"},{"text":"Cut him down anyway.","next":"witch_wood_hanged_cut","require":"sword"},{"text":"Walk past.","next":"witch_wood_inner","do":["fear:+5","journal:Left a man hanging in the witch's wood. He was counting."]}]},{"id":"witch_wood_hanged_talk","chapter":"CHAPTER III · THE WITCH'S WOOD","text":"\"Breaths,\" he says. \"Hers. She breathes once a day. When she breathes ten thousand times I'm done.\"\n\nHis tongue is gray. His eyes are clear.\n\n\"I came looking for my sister. The Gate took her. The witch said she'd tell me where, for a price. I said yes too fast.\" He almost smiles. \"Tell her no, the first three times. Three. She respects three.\"\n\nA crow lands on his shoulder and he does not flinch.","choices":[{"text":"Walk on, into the wood.","next":"witch_wood_inner","do":["flag:hanged_warning","trust:+5"]},{"text":"Cut him down.","next":"witch_wood_hanged_cut","require":"knife"},{"text":"Cut him down.","next":"witch_wood_hanged_cut","require":"sword"}]},{"id":"witch_wood_hanged_cut","chapter":"CHAPTER III · THE WITCH'S WOOD","text":"The grass-rope parts easy as wet paper. He drops, folds, lies still. For a long second you think you have killed a corpse.\n\nThen he breathes. Once. Loud. And the wood breathes with him.\n\nEvery birch around you exhales at the same time and the wind that comes off them is hot and smells of a woman's hair. Somewhere not far, something laughs — not cruelly. Pleased.\n\nThe man gets to his feet and runs. He does not look back. The crows go with him.","choices":[{"text":"Follow the laugh.","next":"witch_grove","do":["flag:cut_hanged","fear:+10"]}]},{"id":"witch_wood_inner","chapter":"CHAPTER III · THE WITCH'S WOOD","text":"Inside, the wood is quieter than a chapel. Moss eats the sound of your feet. The light is the color of weak tea.\n\nYou pass a deer in a clearing. It stops eating and looks at you. On its head, instead of antlers, is a circle of black iron, beaten thin, set with one red stone. A crown. Old work. Kingly work.\n\nIt chews. It watches. It does not run.","choices":[{"text":"Approach slowly.","next":"witch_wood_deer"},{"text":"Bow, and walk wide.","next":"witch_wood_path","do":["trust:+5","flag:bowed_deer"]},{"text":"Try to take the crown.","next":"witch_wood_deer_attack","require":"sword"},{"text":"Try to take the crown.","next":"witch_wood_deer_attack","require":"knife"},{"text":"Wolves with too-clear eyes step out of the green.","next":"deep_wolves_1"}]},{"id":"witch_wood_deer","chapter":"CHAPTER III · THE WITCH'S WOOD","text":"You come close enough to see the deer's eye is brown and wet and human-sad. The crown is welded to the bone. There is a brand on its flank. Three lines and a circle — {other_name}'s seal.\n\nYou understand, all at once, what you are looking at.\n\nIt makes a sound. A small one. It might be a name. It might be your name.\n\nThen it walks past you, slow, polite, and is gone into the trees.","choices":[{"text":"Sit down in the moss for a moment.","next":"witch_wood_path","do":["fear:+10","journal:I think I just saw {other_name}. He had hooves."]}]},{"id":"witch_wood_deer_attack","chapter":"CHAPTER III · THE WITCH'S WOOD","text":"You swing. The deer does not move. The blade goes into its neck like into a sack of wet leaves and the deer looks at you, patient, and does not bleed.\n\nIt says, very clearly, in a man's voice: \"{other_sibling_cap}. You're early.\"\n\nYou drop the weapon. You are sick into the moss for a long time. The deer is gone when you look up. The crown is on the ground at your feet. It is too small for any human head.","choices":[{"text":"Leave it. Walk on.","next":"witch_wood_path","do":["fear:+20","hp:-2","trust:-10","flag:struck_stag","journal:I cut him. He spoke. I left the crown."]},{"text":"Take the crown.","next":"witch_wood_path","do":["fear:+20","hp:-2","add_item:iron_crown","flag:took_crown","journal:I took the crown off the thing that used to be a {other_short}."]}]},{"id":"witch_wood_path","chapter":"CHAPTER III · THE WITCH'S WOOD","text":"The path goes on. Once, you cross a clearing where a leaf falls upward, slow, into a branch that catches it like a hand. A bird flies backwards through it. Time, here, has been hurt.\n\nYou keep walking because to stand still feels like being eaten.\n\nAfter a while there is woodsmoke, real woodsmoke, and the smell of something stewing that has bones in it. A small house. Crooked. Lit warm.","choices":[{"text":"Knock.","next":"witch_grove"},{"text":"Watch the house from the trees first.","next":"witch_grove_watch"},{"text":"Something is moving in the brush — low, fast, in a pack.","next":"deep_wolves_1"}]},{"id":"witch_grove_watch","chapter":"CHAPTER III · THE WITCH'S WOOD","text":"You crouch for a long while. The house breathes smoke. A woman moves past the window — tall, dark-haired, sleeves rolled, forearms streaked to the elbow in something that is not quite blood.\n\nShe is humming. The song is one your mother knew.\n\nShe stops humming. She turns her face directly to the tree you are behind. She smiles. She has a kind smile.\n\n\"Come in,\" she calls, soft. \"The stew's on. I've set a bowl.\"","choices":[{"text":"Go in.","next":"witch_grove","do":["flag:witch_saw_you"]}]},{"id":"witch_grove","chapter":"CHAPTER III · THE WITCH'S WOOD","text":"The Hedge Witch is younger than the stories. Maybe thirty. Maybe three hundred. Her kitchen smells like every kitchen you have ever loved and one you have not.\n\nShe sets a bowl in front of you. Stew. Real stew. \"Eat. You're walking on empty and I won't talk to a fainting woman.\" Or man. Whichever you are, she sees you exactly.\n\nShe sits. She waits. She does not eat.\n\n\"You want to know about the Gate,\" she says. \"And the thing that lives behind it. And whether your {other_short} and your {missing_short} are meat yet.\"","choices":[{"text":"\"Yes. Tell me.\"","next":"witch_bargain"},{"text":"Eat first. Say nothing.","next":"witch_eat","do":["hp:+2"]},{"text":"\"I'm not buying. I came to ask.\"","next":"witch_refuse_first"},{"text":"Sleep at the edge of the wood. Dream.","next":"dream_road_1"},{"text":"Ask the witch about her mother in a jar.","next":"side_witch_jar_1","require":"companion:hedge_witch"}]},{"id":"witch_eat","chapter":"CHAPTER III · THE WITCH'S WOOD","text":"You eat. It is the best meal you have had in a month. There is bread. There is butter the color of a daisy. When you are done she takes the bowl and washes it herself.\n\n\"Good,\" she says. \"Now you owe me one small thing. A meal eaten is a meal owed. That is not magic. That is manners.\"\n\nShe smiles. It is still a kind smile. That is the part that frightens you.","choices":[{"text":"\"What's the price?\"","next":"witch_bargain"},{"text":"\"I owe you nothing. Speak plain or I leave.\"","next":"witch_refuse_first","do":["trust:-5"]}]},{"id":"witch_refuse_first","chapter":"CHAPTER III · THE WITCH'S WOOD","text":"She tilts her head. \"No,\" she says. \"All right.\"\n\nShe does not press. She refills her own cup. She watches you the way a farmer watches a sky.\n\n\"Ask again,\" she says, \"if you mean it. Three times is the number that counts in this house.\"","choices":[{"text":"\"Tell me about the Keeper.\" (second refusal of her bargain)","next":"witch_refuse_second","do":["flag:refused_once"]},{"text":"Take her bargain after all.","next":"witch_bargain"},{"text":"Leave the house.","next":"witch_leave_empty","do":["trust:-5","hp:-1","journal:Walked out of the witch's house with nothing. The wood took a price anyway."]}]},{"id":"witch_refuse_second","chapter":"CHAPTER III · THE WITCH'S WOOD","text":"\"The Keeper is older than the Gate. The Gate is just a door someone built to keep it in.\" She turns her cup. \"It does not want your {missing_short}. It wants the line. Blood that has sat on the chair. It is making itself a new one. A small one. With a crown that fits.\"\n\nYou think of the deer.\n\n\"That,\" she says, watching your face, \"is the second answer. The third costs.\"","choices":[{"text":"Refuse her again. Push for the third.","next":"witch_refuse_third","do":["flag:refused_twice","trust:-5"]},{"text":"Take the bargain.","next":"witch_bargain"},{"text":"Leave with what you have.","next":"witch_leave_road","do":["journal:The Keeper wants the royal line. Not {missing_title}. The line."]}]},{"id":"witch_refuse_third","chapter":"CHAPTER III · THE WITCH'S WOOD","text":"She laughs, low. \"Three. Good. The hanged man taught you well, then.\"\n\nShe reaches across the table and her hand is over yours before you can pull back. Her palm is warm and dry and small. She does not take anything. She just looks at you, level.\n\n\"Free, then. The Keeper has a name. Names matter to old things. I will not say it aloud in this house. But I will write it.\" She does. On the inside of your wrist, in something that is not ink. \"Speak it at the Gate. Once. It will hesitate. A hesitation is sometimes a door.\"","choices":[{"text":"\"Why give it to me free?\"","next":"witch_companion_offer","do":["flag:has_keeper_name","add_item:wardstone","journal:She wrote a name on my wrist. I cannot read it. I will know when to say it."]}]},{"id":"witch_bargain","chapter":"CHAPTER III · THE WITCH'S WOOD","text":"\"Good,\" she says. \"Plain talk. I'll give you sight — a draught. Two swallows. With it you read what is written in dark ink, you see what walks behind a man, you understand the Keeper's library if you get that far. Without it, you'll die a fool in a tower.\"\n\nShe sets a small green bottle between you.\n\n\"The price is this. You drink, and a piece of what you see goes into me. Not your soul. Your edges. You'll be a little less sure of yourself afterwards. People will trust you a little less. You'll trust yourself a little less.\"","choices":[{"text":"Drink. Take the bottle.","next":"witch_companion_offer","do":["add_item:seeing_draught","trust:-5","flag:took_bargain","journal:Drank with the witch. Took the green bottle. Something of me is hers now."]},{"text":"Push back. (Refuse again.)","next":"witch_refuse_second","do":["flag:refused_once"]},{"text":"Refuse. Walk out wounded.","next":"witch_leave_road","do":["hp:-1","trust:-5","journal:Refused the witch. The wood took a tooth on the way out."]}]},{"id":"witch_companion_offer","chapter":"CHAPTER III · THE WITCH'S WOOD","text":"She walks you to the door. Outside, the sun is the right color again. The wood feels thinner.\n\n\"I could come,\" she says, not looking at you. \"Part of the way. I am tired of this house. The Gate is the last interesting thing left and I would like to see how it ends.\"\n\nShe waits. She does not push.","choices":[{"text":"\"Come, then.\"","next":"witch_leave_road","do":["companion:hedge_witch","flag:hedge_witch_joined","rel:hedge_witch:+5","journal:The Hedge Witch is walking with me. I am not sure that is good news."]},{"text":"\"No. Stay here. I'll do this alone.\"","next":"witch_leave_road","do":["trust:+5"]}]},{"id":"witch_leave_empty","chapter":"CHAPTER III · THE WITCH'S WOOD","text":"You walk out of the house and into the wood and the wood is not the same wood. The light is gray. Your mouth tastes of metal. Somewhere, a thread you could have pulled is being burned, and you can almost smell it.\n\nBy nightfall you are back on the king's road, footsore, no wiser. The wind has the city in it now — woodsmoke, old fish, fear.","choices":[{"text":"On, toward the wizard's tower.","next":"wizard_tower"},{"text":"On, toward the city.","next":"city_gates"}]},{"id":"witch_leave_road","chapter":"CHAPTER III · THE WITCH'S WOOD","text":"The trees thin. The road comes back to meet you like a dog that thought you were lost.\n\nAhead, the country opens. To the west, a thin black needle against a yellow sky — the wizard's tower. To the south, low and smudged, smoke that does not move like cookfire smoke. The city is burning, slow, somewhere in its belly.\n\nYou have a choice to make, and the wood does not care which.","choices":[{"text":"West. The tower.","next":"wizard_tower","do":["flag:left_witchwood"]},{"text":"South. The city.","next":"city_gates","do":["flag:left_witchwood"]},{"text":"Help Moth find her crows' tower.","next":"side_moth_roost_1","require":"companion:moth"}]}]},"story/chapter_07.json":{"scenes":[{"id":"tower_approach","chapter":"CHAPTER IV · THE WIZARD'S TOWER","text":"Up close the tower is uglier than the stories. Black stone, but rough — like something pushed up out of the ground in a hurry and never finished. No windows on the bottom three floors. The door is iron and the iron is warm to the touch.\n\nThe wizard's voice comes through it before you have knocked.\n\n\"You took your time. Wipe your boots. And whatever you brought from the wood, leave it on the step or it dies on the threshold.\"","choices":[{"text":"Leave the seeing_draught on the step. Enter.","next":"tower_foyer","require":"seeing_draught","do":["rem_item:seeing_draught","trust:-5","journal:Left the witch's gift on a wizard's stoop. Felt like burning money."]},{"text":"Enter with everything you carry.","next":"tower_foyer","do":["flag:defied_wizard"]},{"text":"Walk around the tower first.","next":"tower_menagerie_outside"}]},{"id":"tower_menagerie_outside","chapter":"CHAPTER IV · THE WIZARD'S TOWER","text":"Around the back: a low shed, barred. The smell hits you first — wet feathers and something sour. Through a slat you see a cage. Inside the cage, a man. He is wearing what was once a knight's surcoat. The blue is almost gone.\n\nHe looks up when you breathe. Lean. Bearded. A scar from cheekbone to jaw that you remember setting, or watching set.\n\n\"Get away from the door,\" Ser Vale says, calm as a man at prayer. \"He'll smell you. Front door. Make him let you in. Then we talk.\"","choices":[{"text":"Nod once. Go to the front door.","next":"tower_foyer","do":["flag:saw_vale_caged","journal:Vale is in a cage behind the tower. Alive. Thin."]}]},{"id":"tower_foyer","chapter":"CHAPTER IV · THE WIZARD'S TOWER","text":"Inside is warmer than it should be. The foyer is a round room with three doors and a stair going up. The doors are labeled in chalk in a tidy hand: MENAGERIE. LIBRARY. UP.\n\nA boy of about twelve is sweeping. He does not look at you. He has no eyes — just smooth skin where eyes should be. He sweeps very well.\n\n\"He's in the solar,\" the boy says. \"He says start wherever you like. He says don't take anything from the menagerie. He says the library will not let you read what you came to read. He thinks you are stupid.\"","choices":[{"text":"Menagerie.","next":"tower_menagerie"},{"text":"Library.","next":"tower_library"},{"text":"Straight up to the solar.","next":"tower_solar"},{"text":"Sit with the sweeping boy.","next":"apprentice_1","require":"trust:40"},{"text":"There's a door in the menagerie wall you didn't see before.","next":"deep_basement_1","require":"trust:50"},{"text":"Visit the thing in the cage that sings in a child's voice.","next":"deep_basement_cage_followup","require":"flag:menagerie_promise"}]},{"id":"tower_menagerie","chapter":"CHAPTER IV · THE WIZARD'S TOWER","text":"The menagerie is one long room, lit by jars. In the jars are things that pulse. Down the center, cages. A hare with a child's hands. A magpie that screams the alphabet. A thing in a glass tube that is mostly liquid and watches you with one tired eye.\n\nAt the end, a larger cage. Inside: a man, exactly your size. Naked. Hairless. He has your face. He stands up when you come close. He puts his hand to the bars in a friendly way.\n\n\"Brother,\" the homunculus says. \"Let me out and I'll let you in.\"","choices":[{"text":"Open the cage. Fight it.","next":"tower_homunculus_fight","require":"sword"},{"text":"Open the cage. Fight it.","next":"tower_homunculus_fight","require":"knife"},{"text":"Open the cage. Fight it.","next":"tower_homunculus_fight","require":"shadow_blade"},{"text":"Talk to it instead.","next":"tower_homunculus_talk"},{"text":"Back away. Leave it caged.","next":"tower_foyer","do":["fear:+5","journal:Saw a thing in a cage with my face. Walked away. Will dream about it."]},{"text":"After the basement, walk back past the singing cage.","next":"deep_basement_followup","require":"flag:knows_keeper_origin"}]},{"id":"tower_homunculus_fight","chapter":"CHAPTER IV · THE WIZARD'S TOWER","text":"It comes out fast and it knows everything you know. It fights the way you fight. Your favorite feints land on nothing.\n\nIt cuts you twice before you understand: it cannot fake being tired. You make it run. You make it lift. You wait. When its arm drops the second time you put your blade through its throat.\n\nIt sits down to die with a thoughtful look. It says, in your voice: \"Tell him I was almost ready.\" Then nothing.\n\nIn the bottom of its cage, set into the stone like a tooth: a small gray stone, warm. A wardstone.","choices":[{"text":"Take the wardstone. Go back to the foyer.","next":"tower_foyer","do":["add_item:wardstone","hp:-3","trust:+5","flag:killed_homunculus","journal:Killed a thing with my face. Took the stone out of its cage. The wizard will know."]}]},{"id":"tower_homunculus_talk","chapter":"CHAPTER IV · THE WIZARD'S TOWER","text":"You stay on your side of the bars. You ask it what it is. It thinks about that the way you would.\n\n\"A draft,\" it says, at last. \"He pours me when he wants to see what you'd do. I have died in this cage forty-one times. I am tired.\"\n\nIt slides something between the bars with one finger. The small gray stone from the floor of its cage.\n\n\"Take it. The wizard wants it back. Make him pay for it. And — listen — when you fight the Keeper, don't fight like you. It learned you from him. Fight like someone else.\"","choices":[{"text":"Take the stone. Leave it alive.","next":"tower_foyer","do":["add_item:wardstone","trust:+5","flag:spared_homunculus","journal:Did not kill the thing with my face. It told me how to fight the Keeper. I do not know if I believe it."]}]},{"id":"tower_library","chapter":"CHAPTER IV · THE WIZARD'S TOWER","text":"The library is taller than the tower has any right to be. Shelves up into dark. Ladders that move when you don't watch them.\n\nOne book is laid open on a lectern. The page is blank. You can feel it is not blank. The boy in the foyer said you would not be able to read what you came to read.\n\nWithout the witch's sight, the words stay hidden. With it, they would surface like fish.","choices":[{"text":"Drink the seeing draught. Read.","next":"tower_library_read","require":"seeing_draught","do":["rem_item:seeing_draught","trust:-3"]},{"text":"Try to read without it.","next":"tower_library_fail"},{"text":"Take a different book instead.","next":"tower_library_steal"}]},{"id":"tower_library_read","chapter":"CHAPTER IV · THE WIZARD'S TOWER","text":"The draught is bitter and goes cold in your chest. The page floods with ink, all at once, as if it had only been holding its breath.\n\nIt is a map. Not of country. Of under-country. Tunnels under the city, under the wastes, under the Hollow Gate itself — a back way in. Drawn in a careful hand by a man who signed himself C. The C is curled the way {other_title} used to curl his.\n\nBeneath the map, in the same hand: \"If you read this, I am already in the dark. Come quiet. The Keeper hears prayer.\"","choices":[{"text":"Tear out the page. Pocket it.","next":"tower_library_after","do":["add_item:tunnel_map","flag:read_cassian_map","journal:{other_name} drew a tunnel map. He knew he wasn't coming back. He left a door for whoever came next."]}]},{"id":"tower_library_fail","chapter":"CHAPTER IV · THE WIZARD'S TOWER","text":"The page stays blank. You stare until your eyes ache. Once, for a flicker, you see the curl of an initial — C — and then nothing.\n\nThe ladder behind you moves and you turn and there is nothing there. The library is patient. You are not. After a while you give up.","choices":[{"text":"Try another book.","next":"tower_library_steal"},{"text":"Leave the library.","next":"tower_foyer","do":["fear:+5"]}]},{"id":"tower_library_steal","chapter":"CHAPTER IV · THE WIZARD'S TOWER","text":"You pull a slim black book at random. The spine reads, in faded gilt: ON THE HABITS OF OLD THINGS.\n\nYou open it. The first line is: \"They do not eat. They keep. To keep a thing is to make it less. Princesses, in particular, are kept badly.\"\n\nYou close it. You take it.","choices":[{"text":"Pocket the book. Leave.","next":"tower_library_after","do":["add_item:keeper_lore","journal:Stole a book from the wizard. The Keeper does not eat. It keeps."]}]},{"id":"tower_library_after","chapter":"CHAPTER IV · THE WIZARD'S TOWER","text":"The ladders are still. The lamps are dim. The blind boy is in the doorway. He has been there how long, you cannot say.\n\n\"He's waiting,\" the boy says. \"Up. He's not angry. He's almost never angry. He's worse than angry. He's interested.\"","choices":[{"text":"Up.","next":"tower_solar"}]},{"id":"tower_solar","chapter":"CHAPTER IV · THE WIZARD'S TOWER","text":"The solar is small and warm. A fire. Two chairs. A wizard who is not old. He is your age. He has a tired face and good hands and a cup of tea he does not offer you.\n\n\"Sit,\" he says. \"You've cost me a homunculus, possibly a book, definitely an afternoon. We will discuss what you owe. Then we will discuss what I owe you. Then you will leave through the cellar with what you came for, or you will not leave at all. I am not bargaining. I am explaining.\"","choices":[{"text":"\"Free Ser Vale. That's the price.\"","next":"tower_negotiate_vale","require":"flag:saw_vale_caged"},{"text":"\"I want the tunnel map. And safe passage.\"","next":"tower_negotiate_map"},{"text":"\"I want a way to hurt the Keeper.\"","next":"tower_negotiate_blade"},{"text":"Draw your weapon.","next":"tower_solar_fight","require":"sword"}]},{"id":"tower_negotiate_vale","chapter":"CHAPTER IV · THE WIZARD'S TOWER","text":"The wizard looks at you a long time. He sets down his cup.\n\n\"The knight is useful to me. He is also breaking. He prays at night. He prays to a woman. I find prayer noisy.\" He sighs. \"Take him. He owes me one favor, payable later. He will not like that part. Tell him I will not ask for it on the wrong side of the Gate, because I am not a monster.\"\n\nHe writes something on a piece of bark and hands it to you. \"For the cage. The boy will show you down.\"","choices":[{"text":"Take it. Go to the cellar.","next":"tower_free_vale","do":["flag:wizard_owed_favor","journal:Wizard gave Vale up easy. That worries me more than if he'd refused."]}]},{"id":"tower_free_vale","chapter":"CHAPTER IV · THE WIZARD'S TOWER","text":"Down in the cellar, the cage clicks open at the bark's touch. Vale stands slowly. He puts his hand on your shoulder, brief, hard.\n\n\"I knew it would be you,\" he says. He doesn't say which you. He doesn't need to.\n\nHe finds his sword on a peg. He finds his cloak. He drinks a whole bucket of water without stopping. Then he is ready.\n\n\"Wherever you're going,\" he says, \"I am behind your left shoulder. Don't argue. Don't thank me. Walk.\"","choices":[{"text":"Walk.","next":"tower_leave","do":["companion:vale","rel:vale:+5","flag:vale_joined","add_item:tunnel_map","journal:Vale is with me. He is thinner than I remember. He is also angrier."]}]},{"id":"tower_negotiate_map","chapter":"CHAPTER IV · THE WIZARD'S TOWER","text":"He almost smiles. \"The map. Yes. The {other_short} drew it. He left it with me because he did not trust his own people. He was right not to.\"\n\nHe slides a folded square across the table.\n\n\"Safe passage I cannot promise. The Gate doesn't honor my seal. But take the wardstone if you took it, and the map, and go. The price is this: when you stand at the Gate, do not say my name. The Keeper and I have an arrangement. Spoil it and I die. Then no one in this country reads anything dangerous again.\"","choices":[{"text":"\"Agreed.\"","next":"tower_leave","do":["add_item:tunnel_map","flag:wizard_pact","journal:Agreed not to name the wizard at the Gate. He is afraid. Wizards being afraid is new."]}]},{"id":"tower_negotiate_blade","chapter":"CHAPTER IV · THE WIZARD'S TOWER","text":"He sets down his cup very carefully.\n\n\"Yes. All right.\" He stands. He goes to a chest. He comes back with a knife that is the color of a moonless night and that does not catch the firelight. He sets it on the table between you. It is colder than the room.\n\n\"Shadow blade. Cuts what cannot be cut. It will cost you something to carry. Bad dreams. Worse company. You will be a little less welcome in any room you enter. Take it or do not.\"","choices":[{"text":"Take it.","next":"tower_leave","do":["add_item:shadow_blade","trust:-5","fear:+5","journal:Took the black knife. The wizard would not touch it after he set it down."]},{"text":"Refuse. Leave with nothing but your life.","next":"tower_leave","do":["trust:+5"]}]},{"id":"tower_solar_fight","chapter":"CHAPTER IV · THE WIZARD'S TOWER","text":"You draw. He sighs like a teacher.\n\nHe lifts one hand. Your sword arm goes heavy as a sack of wet sand and your knees fold and the firelight grows very small.\n\nWhen you can move again, you are on the road, half a mile from the tower, in the dark, with a bloody nose and your weapon still sheathed.\n\nOn your chest, pinned with a thorn: a note. \"Don't come back. — M.\" Beneath it, folded: a tunnel map.","choices":[{"text":"Get up. Walk on.","next":"tower_leave","do":["add_item:tunnel_map","trust:-10","hp:-3","flag:wizard_humbled","journal:Drew on a wizard. Woke up on the road. Got the map anyway. Pride costs."]}]},{"id":"tower_leave","chapter":"CHAPTER IV · THE WIZARD'S TOWER","text":"The tower's shadow is long on the road behind you. The blind boy stands in the doorway and lifts one small hand in something like a wave.\n\nThe wind is from the south now. The city smoke is closer. There are bells, faint, the wrong bells for this hour.\n\nYou point yourself at it.","choices":[{"text":"On, to the city.","next":"city_gates","do":["flag:left_tower"]},{"text":"Back to the barracks first.","next":"barracks","require":"castellan"}]}]},"story/chapter_08.json":{"scenes":[{"id":"city_lost","chapter":"CHAPTER V · THE LOST CITY","text":"You are inside the walls and the city is wrong. Half the windows are shuttered with fresh wood. Half are not — those houses are open, doors gone, taken or fled. A cart goes past piled with chairs and one small dog tied on top of the chairs. The dog is calm. Its owner is not.\n\nA woman grabs your sleeve. \"Are you the one they sent? Tell the King — no. There is no King. Never mind.\" She lets go and is gone.\n\nA boy is selling water at three times the price. He looks ashamed about it. He is selling it anyway.","choices":[{"text":"Buy water from the boy.","next":"city_market","require":"gold:5","do":["gold:-5","hp:+1","trust:+5"]},{"text":"To the keep. Find who's left in charge.","next":"city_keep"},{"text":"Into the back alleys. Listen for trouble.","next":"city_alleys"},{"text":"Walk to the quiet street behind the theatre.","next":"quiet_street_1"},{"text":"Follow the lute into a side alley.","next":"side_minstrel_2"},{"text":"Walk to the King's Yard outside the wall.","next":"side_graveyard_gate"}]},{"id":"city_market","chapter":"CHAPTER V · THE LOST CITY","text":"The boy gives you the cup with both hands. \"My mother says the nobles are running. My father says good. I think my father is going to get us killed.\"\n\nYou drink. The water is cold and clean. The boy watches you drink. He looks like he has not eaten today.\n\nYou hand him a piece of your bread without thinking. He takes it without thinking. Neither of you says anything for a moment. Then he points down a narrow street.\n\n\"Spymaster's in the old tax house. They're holding him. They're going to hang him at dark. He was {missing_title}'s man.\"","choices":[{"text":"To the tax house.","next":"city_taxhouse","do":["flag:boy_tip"]},{"text":"To the keep first.","next":"city_keep"},{"text":"To the alleys.","next":"city_alleys"},{"text":"The fishmonger's wife is gossiping about a boy.","next":"side_pell_rumor"}]},{"id":"city_keep","chapter":"CHAPTER V · THE LOST CITY","text":"The keep doors are open. That is the first wrong thing. The second is the steward, sitting on the steps with his chain of office in his lap like a dead snake.\n\n\"Lord Cathmer fled at dawn,\" he says. \"Lady Veska at noon. The garrison captain is drunk. I am, as you see, also drunk. The {missing_short_cap}'s man is in the tax house. We are going to hang him because someone has to hang someone or the city will eat itself.\"\n\nHe looks up. He almost laughs.\n\n\"Stop us, if you like. I would be grateful. I cannot stop myself.\"","choices":[{"text":"\"I'll stop you.\"","next":"city_taxhouse","do":["flag:steward_blessing","trust:+5"]},{"text":"\"Let him hang. Tell me what he knew first.\"","next":"city_taxhouse_hang","do":["fear:+5","trust:-5"]},{"text":"To the alleys.","next":"city_alleys"},{"text":"Bells. Smoke from the east quarter. Something is breaking.","next":"deep_city_siege_1"},{"text":"Two days out from the city, write what you saw.","next":"deep_city_aftermath","require":"flag:steward_blessing"}]},{"id":"city_alleys","chapter":"CHAPTER V · THE LOST CITY","text":"The alleys are quieter than the streets, which is the wrong way around. You walk soft. You have your hand near your weapon. You are right to.\n\nThree men step out of a doorway behind you and one out of a doorway ahead. They are not soldiers. They are not bandits. They are something in between — men who used to be paid and are no longer paid and have not yet decided what they are.\n\nIn the corner of your eye, in the dark of the doorway, a fifth man — bound at the wrists, gagged, watching you with a flat, calm interest. Rook.","choices":[{"text":"Fight.","next":"city_alley_fight","require":"sword"},{"text":"Fight.","next":"city_alley_fight","require":"shadow_blade"},{"text":"Fight dirty.","next":"city_alley_fight","require":"knife"},{"text":"Talk.","next":"city_alley_talk"},{"text":"Throw your purse and run.","next":"city_alley_run","require":"gold:10"},{"text":"Check the chandler's alley for a rumor.","next":"side_pell_rumor"},{"text":"Return to the bookseller's quiet street.","next":"side_bookseller_return","require":"flag:knows_keeper_name"},{"text":"A woman in a baker's apron grabs your sleeve. The Houses are turning.","next":"deep_city_siege_1"},{"text":"Two days out, the smell of smoke is still on the road.","next":"deep_city_aftermath","require":"flag:rebel_friend"}]},{"id":"city_alley_fight","chapter":"CHAPTER V · THE LOST CITY","text":"You take the one in front first because he is the biggest and the others are watching him to know what to do. He goes down with a sound like a sack of meal falling off a shelf.\n\nThe other three are slower than you. One of them is faster than you should have allowed. He cuts you along the ribs before you put him down.\n\nWhen it is over the alley smells like a butcher's. Rook is still in the doorway. He has worked the gag off with his shoulder.\n\n\"Took your time,\" he says. \"Cut me loose. We've got about a minute before the watch finds us, and the watch is worse than they were.\"","choices":[{"text":"Cut him loose.","next":"city_rook_free","do":["hp:-3","companion:rook","rel:rook:+5","flag:rook_joined","journal:Killed four men in an alley for a man tied in a doorway. He says thank you with his eyes. Not his mouth."]}]},{"id":"city_alley_talk","chapter":"CHAPTER V · THE LOST CITY","text":"\"I have no coin worth your trouble,\" you say, slow, hands wide. \"I have a job and the job pays. If you help me, you get paid. If you cut me, you get a corpse and a watch problem.\"\n\nThe big one in front considers it. He is tired. They are all tired. After a moment he steps back. He jerks his head at Rook in the doorway.\n\n\"Take him too. He's worth nothing to us and his face is making me sad. We were never here.\"\n\nThey are gone like smoke.","choices":[{"text":"Cut Rook loose.","next":"city_rook_free","do":["companion:rook","rel:rook:+5","flag:rook_joined","trust:+5","journal:Talked four men out of a killing. Walked out with a fifth. The city is still teaching me things."]}]},{"id":"city_alley_run","chapter":"CHAPTER V · THE LOST CITY","text":"You throw the purse high. The coins flash. The men look at the coins because men always do.\n\nYou are around the corner before they remember you. You hear scuffling and a short, ugly fight over what you left behind.\n\nTwo streets later you stop, hands on knees, breath ragged. From a doorway across the way, a bound man you do not yet know watches you go. He does not call out. He nods, once, as if he understands.\n\nYou will not see Rook again until the Gate.","choices":[{"text":"On.","next":"city_keep","do":["gold:-10","flag:left_rook","journal:Bought my life off four men. Left someone in a doorway. I'll see him again or I won't."]}]},{"id":"city_rook_free","chapter":"CHAPTER V · THE LOST CITY","text":"You cut him loose. His wrists are raw under the rope. He rubs them once and that is all the complaint he makes.\n\nHe is taller than you thought. Older. He has a soldier's hands and a thief's stillness and his eyes are the gray of a winter river. He does not thank you.\n\nHe says: \"You're going to the Gate. So am I. I have a reason. I'll tell you when I trust you. Don't ask before then.\"\n\nHe falls in at your shoulder. He moves like he has done this all his life.","choices":[{"text":"To the tax house. The spymaster.","next":"city_taxhouse","do":["flag:rook_with_you"]},{"text":"To the keep first.","next":"city_keep"}]},{"id":"city_taxhouse","chapter":"CHAPTER V · THE LOST CITY","text":"The tax house is a low brick building with one window. Two guards on the door. They are drinking, which is good for you. Inside, in a back room you can see through the window, a thin man sits at a table with his hands tied behind him. He is writing with a pen held in his teeth. He has been writing for a long time. The page is black.\n\nA bag on the floor at his feet. In it, a folded square of paper. Even from here you can see it has no readable ink on it — only a faint shimmer, like oil on water.","choices":[{"text":"Talk your way in past the guards.","next":"city_taxhouse_in","require":"trust:60"},{"text":"Slip in through the window after dark.","next":"city_taxhouse_in","require":"companion:rook"},{"text":"Use the signet ring. Command them.","next":"city_taxhouse_in","require":"signet_ring"},{"text":"Wait for the hanging, take the bag off the body.","next":"city_taxhouse_hang"},{"text":"Slip out toward the quiet street.","next":"quiet_street_1"}]},{"id":"city_taxhouse_in","chapter":"CHAPTER V · THE LOST CITY","text":"You get in. How does not matter. The spymaster looks up from the page in his teeth and his eyes are bright.\n\n\"You're late,\" he says, around the pen. \"I am writing the last things she said to me. She knew. She walked through the Gate awake. She wanted me to tell someone.\"\n\nHe spits the pen out. \"The page is in dark ink. You cannot read it without sight. I do not have time to teach you. Take it. Find someone who can. And cut me loose so I can die standing.\"","choices":[{"text":"Read it now. (Use seeing_draught.)","next":"city_spymaster_read","require":"seeing_draught","do":["rem_item:seeing_draught"]},{"text":"Pocket it. Read it at the Gate.","next":"city_spymaster_after","do":["add_item:elara_letter","flag:have_letter","journal:Took {missing_name}'s last letter. Cannot read it. Will read it when I can."]},{"text":"Cut him loose. Take him with you.","next":"city_spymaster_free"}]},{"id":"city_spymaster_read","chapter":"CHAPTER V · THE LOST CITY","text":"The page floods. Her handwriting is small and crowded and angry.\n\n\"It is not my {other_sibling} who comes back if you take the wrong path. It is the Keeper wearing him. He will be kind. He will know our songs. Do not embrace him. He hates light. I am not afraid. Tell my father I am not afraid. I am furious. Use the back way. {other_name} drew it. The Keeper has a name. Make him hear it twice.\"\n\nYou fold the page. You sit down for a moment because your knees are tired.","choices":[{"text":"Cut the spymaster loose. Out.","next":"city_spymaster_after","do":["add_item:elara_letter","flag:read_elara_letter","trust:+10","journal:{missing_name} is furious, not afraid. She told me how to win. Twice. The name. Twice."]}]},{"id":"city_spymaster_free","chapter":"CHAPTER V · THE LOST CITY","text":"You cut him loose. He stands and his knees almost go. He catches himself on the table.\n\n\"I cannot run,\" he says, plainly. \"They will catch us before the gate. Take the letter. Go. I will sit in the doorway with a candle and tell them I am the one they wanted. They are not bright men. It will buy you an hour.\"\n\nHe smiles. He has no front teeth. He smiles anyway.","choices":[{"text":"Take the letter. Go.","next":"city_spymaster_after","do":["add_item:elara_letter","flag:spymaster_died_for_you","trust:+10","journal:He sat in the doorway with a candle so I could go. I did not know his name. I should have asked."]}]},{"id":"city_taxhouse_hang","chapter":"CHAPTER V · THE LOST CITY","text":"You wait until dark. They drag him out. He does not fight. They string him up from a beam in the square in front of a small, ashamed crowd. He looks for someone in the crowd and finds you and his eyes are not angry. He nods, once.\n\nWhen the square empties you cut the bag off his belt. The letter is still in it. The ink is unreadable to you. You walk out of the city with a dead man's last words in your coat.","choices":[{"text":"On, to the Gate.","next":"city_spymaster_after","do":["add_item:elara_letter","fear:+10","trust:-5","flag:let_spymaster_hang","journal:Let him hang. Took the letter off his body. I will see his face on the inside of my eyelids for a while."]}]},{"id":"city_spymaster_after","chapter":"CHAPTER V · THE LOST CITY","text":"You are out of the city by the side gate, the one the smugglers use, the one with the broken hinge.\n\nThe road to the Hollow Gate is empty. The sky is the color of old iron. Whoever is with you is quiet.\n\nA mile out, you find a place between two stones where the wind drops, and the company stops without being told. You will not sleep much. But you will sleep.","choices":[{"text":"Make camp.","next":"camp_rest"},{"text":"Push through the night to the Gate.","next":"hollow_gate","do":["hp:-2","fear:+5"]},{"text":"Strike camp a mile from the Gate.","next":"threshold_1"}]},{"id":"camp_rest","chapter":"INTERLUDE · CAMP","text":"You have a fire that is too small and a watch that is too short and a sky with too many stars. The country is very quiet here. The Gate is somewhere ahead, breathing on its own.\n\nYour people are arranged around the fire the way tired people arrange themselves — close, but not touching. Someone is sharpening something. Someone is not eating their share.\n\nThere is nothing to do but wait for first light, and the things that wait do not always wait quietly.","choices":[{"text":"Sit with Ser Vale.","next":"vale_camp_1","require":"companion:vale"},{"text":"Sit with Ser Vale a while longer.","next":"vale_camp_2","require":"rel:vale:15"},{"text":"Speak plainly with Ser Vale.","next":"vale_camp_3","require":"rel:vale:30"},{"text":"Sit with Moth.","next":"moth_camp_1","require":"companion:moth"},{"text":"Sit with Moth a while longer.","next":"moth_camp_2","require":"rel:moth:15"},{"text":"Speak plainly with Moth.","next":"moth_camp_3","require":"rel:moth:30"},{"text":"Sit with Rook.","next":"rook_camp_1","require":"companion:rook"},{"text":"Sit with Rook a while longer.","next":"rook_camp_2","require":"rel:rook:15"},{"text":"Speak plainly with Rook.","next":"rook_camp_3","require":"rel:rook:30"},{"text":"Sit with the Hedge Witch.","next":"witch_camp_1","require":"companion:hedge_witch"},{"text":"Sit with the Hedge Witch a while longer.","next":"witch_camp_2","require":"rel:hedge_witch:15"},{"text":"Speak plainly with the Hedge Witch.","next":"witch_camp_3","require":"rel:hedge_witch:30"},{"text":"Sleep. Push on at first light.","next":"hollow_gate","do":["hp:+2","flag:camped"]},{"text":"Sleep, and hope to dream of {missing_short}.","next":"dream_royal_1"},{"text":"Sleep again. Reach for {missing_obj}.","next":"dream_royal_2","require":"flag:dream_royal_1"},{"text":"Sleep, dream, remember.","next":"dream_royal_3","require":"flag:dream_royal_2"},{"text":"Sleep. Hold the dream tight.","next":"dream_royal_4","require":"flag:dream_royal_3"},{"text":"Ask the witch about her mother in a jar.","next":"side_witch_jar_1","require":"companion:hedge_witch"},{"text":"You sleep. Something is in the dream with you.","next":"deep_dream_1","require":"flag:dream_royal_2"},{"text":"Morning. Ash was watching you sleep.","next":"deep_camp_aftermath","require":"flag:keeper_blooded"},{"text":"Turn the clay tablet in the firelight.","next":"deep_lost_city_followup","require":"flag:knows_keeper_origin"},{"text":"First night with the road-company.","next":"deep_bandits_camp","require":"flag:road_company"}]},{"id":"vale_camp_1","chapter":"INTERLUDE · CAMP","text":"Vale is oiling his sword by the fire. He is not very good at it. His hands are still not steady from the cage.\n\nYou sit. You do not say anything. After a while he hands you the cloth and the oil without looking up and you do it for him. He lets you. That is the closest thing to thanks he has in him.\n\n\"I prayed for a woman in that cage,\" he says, at last. \"Not {missing_title}. Someone else. I am not as honorable as the songs.\"\n\nHe looks at you, brief, hard, then away.","choices":[{"text":"\"The songs are for fools.\"","next":"camp_rest","require":"flag:gender_f","do":["rel:vale:+5","flag:vale_camp_1","journal:Vale prayed for me in the cage. He did not say my name. He did not have to."]},{"text":"\"Good. I prefer men with one honest fear.\"","next":"camp_rest","do":["rel:vale:+5","flag:vale_camp_1"]}]},{"id":"vale_camp_2","chapter":"INTERLUDE · CAMP","text":"You take the second watch together because neither of you was going to sleep.\n\nHe tells you about the cage in pieces. The wizard fed him. The wizard was not cruel. The wizard would sit outside the bars and read aloud sometimes, things Vale did not understand, and ask, after, whether the words had moved him. \"He was lonely,\" Vale says. \"I think I am the only person he had spoken to in a year. That is the part I cannot forgive him for. I almost liked him.\"\n\nHe is quiet a moment. \"I do not want to die in the dark. I have done enough dying in the dark.\"","choices":[{"text":"Take his hand.","next":"camp_rest","require":"flag:gender_f","do":["rel:vale:+10","flag:vale_camp_2","trust:+5","journal:Held Ser Vale's hand under a watch fire. He did not let go."]},{"text":"\"You won't. Not on my watch.\"","next":"camp_rest","do":["rel:vale:+10","flag:vale_camp_2"]}]},{"id":"vale_camp_3","chapter":"INTERLUDE · CAMP","text":"He says your name. Just that. He has not said it once on this whole road and now he says it and it sounds different in his mouth than in anyone else's.\n\n\"If we live,\" he says, \"I am done with the King's service. I am done with the songs. I want a small house and a bad garden and you in it, if you'll have me. I am saying this now because tomorrow we walk into a thing that eats kings, and I would not like to die having been brave about every stupid thing except the one that mattered.\"","choices":[{"text":"\"I'll have you.\"","next":"camp_rest","require":"flag:gender_f","do":["rel:vale:+10","flag:loved_vale","trust:+10","journal:Said yes to a knight under a small fire. Tomorrow we walk into the dark. I am not afraid of the dark anymore. I am afraid of losing him in it."]},{"text":"\"Ask me again on the other side of the Gate.\"","next":"camp_rest","do":["rel:vale:+5","flag:vale_camp_3"]}]},{"id":"moth_camp_1","chapter":"INTERLUDE · CAMP","text":"Moth is sitting outside the firelight with a crow on each shoulder. She does not turn when you sit down. The crows do. They look at you like aunts.\n\n\"You're heavy tonight,\" she says. She means it kindly. \"Set some of it down. The fire won't mind holding it.\"\n\nYou do not know what to set down first. You sit a long time before you speak. She does not hurry you. The crows blink at the same time, twice.","choices":[{"text":"\"I'm afraid I won't be the one who comes back.\"","next":"camp_rest","do":["rel:moth:+5","flag:moth_camp_1","journal:Told Moth I was afraid. She did not tell me not to be. She held one of the crows in her lap like a cat."]}]},{"id":"moth_camp_2","chapter":"INTERLUDE · CAMP","text":"\"I am not a girl,\" Moth says, conversationally, while plaiting a piece of grass. \"Not the way the songs mean it. My mother was a hedge-thing. My father was a road. I am what they made between them. I tell you because the people who matter to me should know.\"\n\nShe looks up. Her eyes catch the firelight and for a second they are not quite eyes.\n\n\"You matter to me. That is also a thing I should say while there is time.\"","choices":[{"text":"\"You matter to me too.\"","next":"camp_rest","require":"flag:gender_m","do":["rel:moth:+10","flag:moth_camp_2","trust:+5","journal:Moth told me what she is. I told her she matters. The crows seemed pleased."]},{"text":"Take her hand. Say nothing.","next":"camp_rest","do":["rel:moth:+10","flag:moth_camp_2"]}]},{"id":"moth_camp_3","chapter":"INTERLUDE · CAMP","text":"She sits down right against your side. Closer than a road-friend sits.\n\n\"If we live,\" she says, \"I am keeping you. I am not asking. I am telling. Crows mate for life. I am half crow. You will be very tired of me by the end and I will love you anyway. That is the offer. You may refuse it. I would prefer you didn't.\"","choices":[{"text":"\"Keep me, then.\"","next":"camp_rest","require":"flag:gender_m","do":["rel:moth:+10","flag:loved_moth","trust:+10","journal:Said yes to a crow-girl. I do not know what I have agreed to. I am not sorry."]},{"text":"\"Ask me on the other side.\"","next":"camp_rest","do":["rel:moth:+5","flag:moth_camp_3"]}]},{"id":"rook_camp_1","chapter":"INTERLUDE · CAMP","text":"Rook does not sit by the fire. He sits at its edge, half in dark, where his hands cannot be read. You sit at the same edge, opposite him.\n\nHe watches you a long time before he speaks.\n\n\"I was a queen's man,\" he says. \"Not your queen. A queen across the sea who is dead now. I was bound at the wrists because I am very good at certain things and certain people did not want me free in their city. None of this changes that you cut me loose. I notice when people cut me loose.\"","choices":[{"text":"\"What things are you good at?\"","next":"camp_rest","do":["rel:rook:+5","flag:rook_camp_1","journal:Rook was a queen's man. He did not say what kind. He said he notices when people cut him loose. I noticed that he noticed."]}]},{"id":"rook_camp_2","chapter":"INTERLUDE · CAMP","text":"He cleans his knife. He does it the slow way, the careful way, the way a man does a thing he has done ten thousand times and wants to keep doing.\n\n\"I have killed seventeen people,\" he says, calm. \"I remember all of them. Two were children. Those two I dream about. I am telling you because if we share a fire there should be no surprises. If you want me to walk off into the dark I will. No hard feelings. Roads are long.\"","choices":[{"text":"\"Stay.\"","next":"camp_rest","require":"flag:gender_f","do":["rel:rook:+10","flag:rook_camp_2","trust:+5","journal:He told me about the two children. He did not look away. I told him to stay. I do not know if I was right."]},{"text":"\"Stay. The dark has enough of us.\"","next":"camp_rest","do":["rel:rook:+10","flag:rook_camp_2"]}]},{"id":"rook_camp_3","chapter":"INTERLUDE · CAMP","text":"He gives you a small thing wrapped in a piece of cloth. Inside is a ring of plain iron with a notch filed in it.\n\n\"I made it in the cage with the rope they tied me with and a nail I worked out of the floor. It is the only thing I have ever made and not sold. It is yours. I am not asking anything in return. I am telling you that if we live I would like to be where you are. If we do not, I would like you to have something I made before you forget my face.\"","choices":[{"text":"Put on the ring.","next":"camp_rest","require":"flag:gender_f","do":["rel:rook:+10","flag:loved_rook","add_item:rook_note","trust:+10","journal:Put on an iron ring made of a rope and a nail. He watched me do it. He looked, for a second, like a boy."]},{"text":"\"Keep it for me. Give it to me after.\"","next":"camp_rest","do":["rel:rook:+5","flag:rook_camp_3"]}]},{"id":"witch_camp_1","chapter":"INTERLUDE · CAMP","text":"The Hedge Witch is not at the fire. She is twenty paces out, talking, low, to something you cannot see. When you walk up she stops. She does not turn.\n\n\"Sit,\" she says. \"Don't ask who I was talking to. It is not your business and it would not love you.\"\n\nYou sit. The grass is cold. After a moment she sits too, beside you, close enough that her sleeve touches yours.\n\n\"I do not get tired,\" she says. \"I am tired anyway. Explain that to me.\"","choices":[{"text":"\"Loneliness doesn't care if you can feel it.\"","next":"camp_rest","do":["rel:hedge_witch:+5","flag:witch_camp_1","journal:Sat with the Witch in the dark grass. She said she was tired. I think she meant something larger than the road."]}]},{"id":"witch_camp_2","chapter":"INTERLUDE · CAMP","text":"\"I was a girl once,\" she says, into the fire. \"Properly. With a mother and a name and a village. The village burned. The name burned with it. The mother I have, in a jar, at home. I do not say this to many people. I am saying it to you because you did not flinch when I put my hand on yours in the kitchen and most do.\"\n\nShe looks at you sidelong.\n\n\"I will not lie to you. I cannot. I made a promise about that a long time ago. Ask me anything tonight and I will answer.\"","choices":[{"text":"\"Are you going to survive the Gate?\"","next":"camp_rest","require":"flag:gender_m","do":["rel:hedge_witch:+10","flag:witch_camp_2","trust:+5","journal:She said she did not know if she would live. She said it like a woman, not a witch. I will remember that."]},{"text":"\"Do you love me, or are you using me?\"","next":"camp_rest","do":["rel:hedge_witch:+10","flag:witch_camp_2"]}]},{"id":"witch_camp_3","chapter":"INTERLUDE · CAMP","text":"She kisses you, once, on the mouth, brief and serious as a vow.\n\n\"I have not done that in eighty years,\" she says. \"I wanted to do it while my mouth was still a mouth. If we live, I will burn the house. I will walk out of the wood with you. I will learn to live in a town. I will be very bad at it. You will laugh at me. I will allow it.\"\n\nShe waits. She is not pretending to be calm. She is not calm.","choices":[{"text":"Kiss her back.","next":"camp_rest","require":"flag:gender_m","do":["rel:hedge_witch:+10","flag:loved_hedge_witch","trust:+10","journal:Kissed a witch under a small fire. She said she would burn her house. I think she meant it. I think she has been waiting eighty years to mean it."]},{"text":"\"Ask me again on the far side.\"","next":"camp_rest","do":["rel:hedge_witch:+5","flag:witch_camp_3"]}]}]},"story/chapter_09.json":{"scenes":[{"id":"caravan_day_one","chapter":"CHAPTER II.5 · THE CARAVAN","text":"The caravan moves at the pace of a tired ox, which is to say: barely. Three wagons, eleven souls, one dog that has decided you are its problem now. The dust gets into everything — your hair, your teeth, the spaces between your thoughts.\nA girl no older than twelve walks beside your wagon. She has a tin cup at her belt and eyes that have learned not to ask twice. \"I sell stories,\" she says, matter-of-fact. \"One cup of water, one story. I know good ones.\"\nBehind her, an old woman is humming a song you almost remember from somewhere — maybe your mother, maybe a dream. The dog leans against your leg.\nThe sun is a slow knife. You have a long road ahead, and the road has things to say.","choices":[{"text":"Buy a story from the girl.","next":"caravan_story_girl","do":["trust:+2"]},{"text":"Sit with the humming woman.","next":"caravan_humming_woman"},{"text":"Watch the horizon. Keep your water.","next":"caravan_horizon"}]},{"id":"caravan_story_girl","chapter":"CHAPTER II.5 · THE CARAVAN","text":"You tip a swallow of water into her cup. She drinks it like a prayer — slow, eyes closed — and then she sits cross-legged on the wagon boards and tells you about a boy who fell in love with a door.\n\"He loved it so much he forgot what was on the other side,\" she says. \"That's the trick of doors. They make you forget there's a room.\"\nShe looks at you sidelong. \"You're going to one, aren't you. A door. I can tell. People going to doors walk funny.\"\nYou don't answer. She nods like you did.\n\"My cousin went,\" she adds, quieter. \"Last spring. Hasn't come back. If you see a boy with a chipped front tooth out there — his name is Pell — tell him Juno is still saving him a seat at supper.\"\nThe dust goes on forever. So does her small, stubborn face.","choices":[{"text":"Promise to look for Pell.","next":"caravan_promise_pell","do":["journal:A girl named Juno asked me to find her cousin Pell. Chipped tooth. Still saving him a seat.","trust:+3"]},{"text":"Tell her the truth — most don't come back.","next":"caravan_hard_truth","do":["fear:+3"]}]},{"id":"caravan_promise_pell","chapter":"CHAPTER II.5 · THE CARAVAN","text":"\"I'll look,\" you say, and you mean it in the way you mean things when you're tired enough to be honest.\nJuno doesn't smile. She just nods once, like she's adding your name to a small list she keeps. Then she gets up and goes to sell another story two wagons down.\nThe dog sighs and puts its chin on your boot. Somewhere ahead, the road bends around a ridge of black rock that looks, in this light, like a sleeping animal.\nYou wonder if Pell ever sat where you're sitting. If he watched the same ridge and thought the same thing.\nThe wagon creaks on.","choices":[{"text":"Doze a while.","next":"caravan_night_one"},{"text":"Walk up to the lead wagon.","next":"caravan_lead_wagon"}]},{"id":"caravan_hard_truth","chapter":"CHAPTER II.5 · THE CARAVAN","text":"\"Most don't come back, Juno.\"\nShe looks at you a long time. Not hurt. Older than that. \"I know,\" she says. \"I'm not stupid. I just need somebody to keep looking anyway.\"\nShe takes her cup and goes. The dog stays. The dog, apparently, has standards and has chosen you despite your mouth.\nYou sit with what you said. It was true. It was also unnecessary. There is a difference, you are learning, between the truth and a thing that needed saying.","choices":[{"text":"Catch up and apologize.","next":"caravan_apology","do":["trust:+2"]},{"text":"Let it go. Watch the road.","next":"caravan_night_one","do":["fear:+2"]}]},{"id":"caravan_apology","chapter":"CHAPTER II.5 · THE CARAVAN","text":"You catch her at the back of the third wagon. She doesn't look up.\n\"I'll still look for him,\" you say. \"I shouldn't have said it like that.\"\nShe shrugs the shrug of a child who has been disappointed by adults professionally. Then, after a moment: \"Okay.\"\nThat's it. That's the whole forgiveness. You take it and feel obscurely lighter.\nThe dog, watching this whole exchange, thumps its tail once in approval.","choices":[{"text":"Doze a while.","next":"caravan_night_one","do":["journal:Juno forgave me, more or less. I owe her a boy with a chipped tooth."]}]},{"id":"caravan_humming_woman","chapter":"CHAPTER II.5 · THE CARAVAN","text":"The old woman makes room for you on her bench without looking up. Her humming is a thread you can almost follow.\n\"My mother used to —\" you start.\n\"Yes,\" she says. \"Lots of mothers did. It's an old song. Older than the kingdom.\" She pats your knee like she's known you all your life. \"It's about a girl who goes looking for her brother and finds something else instead. The song doesn't say what.\"\nShe hums another bar. Your eyes prickle without permission.\n\"You're looking for someone,\" she says. Not a question.\n\"Yes.\"\n\"Then I'll sing for you tonight,\" she says, and goes back to humming. The road softens at the edges.","choices":[{"text":"Stay beside her until dusk.","next":"caravan_night_one","do":["trust:+3","fear:-3","journal:An old woman sang my mother's song. I didn't know I still knew the words."]}]},{"id":"caravan_horizon","chapter":"CHAPTER II.5 · THE CARAVAN","text":"You keep your water. You watch the horizon.\nIt does what horizons do — it pretends not to move while moving. By midafternoon the heat warps it into shapes: a tower, a horse, a person waving. Your eyes invent and your eyes uninvent.\nA man on the second wagon, a trader with rope-burn scars on his hands, slides down to walk beside you. \"First time crossing?\"\n\"Yes.\"\n\"The trick,\" he says, \"is to pick one thing on the horizon and decide it's real. Even if it isn't. Gives your eyes somewhere to live.\" He grins, missing a tooth. \"Mine's a woman in a blue dress. She's been waiting for me for nine crossings.\"\nYou pick something on the horizon. You don't tell him what.","choices":[{"text":"Pick {missing_name}.","next":"caravan_night_one","do":["journal:I picked {missing_short} on the horizon. {missing_pron_cap} is the thing I have decided is real.","trust:+2"]},{"text":"Pick home.","next":"caravan_night_one","do":["fear:-2"]}]},{"id":"caravan_lead_wagon","chapter":"CHAPTER II.5 · THE CARAVAN","text":"The lead wagon is driven by a wiry woman named Tobish who has, you learn in the first minute, opinions about everything and patience for almost none of it.\n\"You're the one going to the Gate,\" she says, not asking. \"Don't bother lying, child. You all have the same walk.\" She spits over the side. \"I'll get you to the foothills. After that you're on your own and the wastes are honest — they kill quick.\"\nShe eyes your pack. \"You short on supplies?\"\nThe honest answer would cost you pride. The dishonest answer would cost you food.","choices":[{"text":"Admit it. Ask for supplies.","next":"caravan_tobish_gift","do":["add_item:supplies","trust:+3"]},{"text":"Lie. You're fine.","next":"caravan_night_one","do":["fear:+3"]}]},{"id":"caravan_tobish_gift","chapter":"CHAPTER II.5 · THE CARAVAN","text":"Tobish snorts and tosses you a wrapped bundle without looking. \"Don't make a thing of it. I won't have it said I let a fool starve on my wagon.\"\nIt's dried meat, hard biscuit, a twist of salt. More than you expected. You start to thank her and she waves you off like a fly.\n\"My brother went to the Gate,\" she says, eyes on the road. \"Years ago now. Came back wrong, then didn't come back at all. So.\" She shrugs. \"So I keep the wagon going. People still need to get places.\"\nShe doesn't look at you again. You climb down from the lead wagon with the bundle in your arms, and a hot, complicated feeling in your chest.","choices":[{"text":"Find a place to sleep.","next":"caravan_night_one","do":["journal:Tobish lost a brother to the Gate. She still drives the wagon. I think that's a kind of courage I don't have a name for."]}]},{"id":"caravan_night_one","chapter":"CHAPTER II.5 · THE CARAVAN","text":"Night drops fast in the wastes. One moment everything is amber and the next the sky is purple and the stars are showing off.\nThey light a fire low and shielded — a habit, the trader explains, from years when bandits read smoke like a menu. Someone passes a flask. Someone tells a story about a goat. The humming woman sings, and her voice is small and steady and exactly the shape of the ache under your ribs.\nAcross the fire, a boy of maybe fifteen is staring at you. Not unfriendly. Just staring. He has the look of someone working up to something.\nThe stars get sharper. The fire crackles. The boy opens his mouth, closes it, opens it again.","choices":[{"text":"Beckon him over.","next":"caravan_boy_talk"},{"text":"Pretend not to notice. Sleep.","next":"caravan_day_two"}]},{"id":"caravan_boy_talk","chapter":"CHAPTER II.5 · THE CARAVAN","text":"He comes around the fire too fast and sits too close and immediately looks like he regrets both. \"You're going to the Gate.\"\n\"Everyone keeps saying that.\"\n\"Because it's true.\" He picks at a hole in his sleeve. \"My sister works in the kitchens. At the palace. She — she said the {missing_short_cap} was kind. To everyone. To the scullery girls and the boys who sweep. She said {missing_pron} learned all their names.\"\nHe looks up at you, and his eyes are wet, and very young.\n\"If you find {missing_obj}. Tell {missing_obj} someone in the wastes still remembers {missing_poss} name. Tell {missing_obj} my sister's name was Pell — no, sorry — my sister's name is Mira. Tell {missing_obj} Mira said thank you.\"\nThe fire pops. The humming woman sings on.","choices":[{"text":"Promise. Mean it.","next":"caravan_day_two","do":["journal:Mira, scullery girl. Said thank you. I am to deliver this to {missing_short}.","trust:+4","fear:-2"]}]},{"id":"caravan_day_two","chapter":"CHAPTER II.5 · THE CARAVAN","text":"Day two is wind.\nThe wind starts gentle, the way bad things often do. By midmorning it's a wall. The wagons stop. The dog whines. The trader curses with impressive vocabulary, and Tobish is already shouting orders — tarps down, faces wrapped, hold the horses, hold each other, the sand is coming.\nIt comes.\nIt is brown and red and the sound of a million tiny teeth. You can't see the wagon two paces away. You can't see your own hand. Someone screams and is cut short — not hurt, just swallowed by sound.\nA hand grabs yours. Small. Cold. Juno.\n\"Don't let go,\" she shouts into the gale. \"Promise.\"","choices":[{"text":"Promise. Don't let go.","next":"caravan_storm_through","do":["trust:+3"]},{"text":"Pull her under your cloak.","next":"caravan_storm_through","do":["trust:+3","fear:-2","journal:I held Juno through the storm. She didn't cry. I almost did."]}]},{"id":"caravan_storm_through","chapter":"CHAPTER II.5 · THE CARAVAN","text":"The storm lasts an hour. It lasts a year. You can't tell.\nWhen it stops, the world is reshaped. New dunes. Old tracks erased. The horizon is a stranger.\nOne wagon is on its side. The horses are alive, mostly. The humming woman is alive, mostly. The trader has a gash on his forehead that he is treating with what looks like wine and what is definitely cursing.\nJuno is still holding your hand. She lets go like it embarrasses her.\n\"You kept your promise,\" she says. Like she'd been keeping score.\nTobish takes one look at the dunes and her mouth goes flat. \"We've lost the road,\" she says. \"We'll find it. Probably.\"","choices":[{"text":"Help right the wagon.","next":"caravan_night_two","do":["hp:-1","trust:+3"]},{"text":"Tend the trader's wound with poultice.","next":"caravan_night_two","require":"poultice","do":["rem_item:poultice","trust:+5","journal:I patched the trader. He insists on paying me back in stories I won't live long enough to hear."]}]},{"id":"caravan_night_two","chapter":"CHAPTER II.5 · THE CARAVAN","text":"The second night is quieter, the way nights are after disasters — everyone too tired to perform.\nYou sit a little apart from the fire because your skin feels too small. The stars are absurd out here, a spilled bowl of them, more sky than dark.\nFootsteps. Tobish, with two cups. She hands you one without speaking. It's the cheap, hot kind of liquor that tastes like a small fire and a kind grandmother.\n\"You did all right today,\" she says, and that's the whole of her speech, and she sits beside you and drinks, and the silence is companionable. After a while she says, \"What's {missing_poss} name?\" and you realize she means the one you're going for, and you say it, and she nods like she's filing it away with her brother's.","choices":[{"text":"Ask her if she dreams of him.","next":"caravan_tobish_brother","do":["trust:+3"]},{"text":"Just drink. Watch the sky.","next":"caravan_day_three","do":["fear:-3"]}]},{"id":"caravan_tobish_brother","chapter":"CHAPTER II.5 · THE CARAVAN","text":"She is quiet a long time.\n\"Every night,\" she says finally. \"He's always a boy in the dreams. Never the man he became. I think the dreams know something I don't.\"\nShe drinks. The stars don't move and also do.\n\"If you find your {missing_short}, child — and I think you might, you have the look — don't ask {missing_obj} what happened in there. Not at first. Just feed {missing_obj}. Let {missing_obj} sleep. Sit beside {missing_obj}. The questions will keep.\"\nShe stands, knees popping. \"And don't be a hero. Heroes die. Be stubborn. Stubborn comes home.\"","choices":[{"text":"Thank her. Sleep.","next":"caravan_day_three","do":["journal:Tobish said: don't be a hero, be stubborn. I'm going to try.","trust:+3","fear:-2"]}]},{"id":"caravan_day_three","chapter":"CHAPTER II.5 · THE CARAVAN","text":"Day three. You can smell the foothills before you see them — cold stone, thin pine, something almost like rain.\nMidmorning, the trader comes to you, and he won't meet your eyes.\n\"Listen,\" he says, soft. \"I took something from your pack. Last night. Just — a coin. Just one. My boy back in the city, he's — listen, I'm telling you because I put it back. Couldn't sleep. I'm an idiot. I'm sorry.\"\nHe holds out his hand. A single copper, warm from his pocket.\nIt is a betrayal small enough to be forgivable. It is also a betrayal.","choices":[{"text":"Take it. Forgive him.","next":"caravan_foothills","do":["gold:+1","trust:+4","journal:He stole a coin and gave it back. I am keeping that, and him."]},{"text":"Tell him to keep it. Send it to his boy.","next":"caravan_foothills","do":["trust:+5","fear:-3"]},{"text":"Be cold. Take it. Walk away.","next":"caravan_foothills","do":["gold:+1","fear:+3"]}]},{"id":"caravan_foothills","chapter":"CHAPTER II.5 · THE CARAVAN","text":"By dusk on the third day the wagons stop at the lip of the foothills. Tobish swings down and starts unhitching like she's done it a thousand times because she has.\n\"This is me,\" she says. \"You go up. We go around. May we meet again on a less ugly road.\"\nJuno presses something into your hand — a flat river stone, smooth, painted with a small, careful J. \"For luck,\" she mutters, and walks off before you can answer.\nThe humming woman waves. The trader salutes. The dog watches you go, and you have to not look back or you will not leave.\nAhead: the road bends up into stone. Somewhere up there, a rider waits, or doesn't. A hill to search. A tower. A Gate.","choices":[{"text":"Take the road up to the rider.","next":"rider_join","do":["add_item:supplies","journal:I have Juno's stone in my pocket. It weighs more than a stone should.","trust:+3"]},{"text":"Cut sideways toward the hills.","next":"hill_search","do":["add_item:supplies","trust:+2"]},{"text":"Detour to the wayhouse for stew.","next":"side_inn_arrival"}]}]},"story/chapter_10.json":{"scenes":[{"id":"dream_road_1","chapter":"CHAPTER III.5 · DREAMS IN THE WOOD","text":"You make a bed of cloak and pine needles a half-mile off the witch's road. Sleep takes you fast — too fast, with that pulling feeling that means the sleep is being done to you.\nYou are standing in a hallway you don't know. Stone, candlelit, going on forever. At the far end, a figure is curled small against the wall. {missing_short_cap}-thin shoulders. A torn sleeve. A crown set carefully beside {missing_obj}, like {missing_pron}'s afraid to wear it and afraid not to.\nYou take a step. The candles dim. {missing_pron_cap} looks up. {missing_poss_cap} eyes find yours across the hall like {missing_pron} was already looking.\n\"You're real,\" {missing_pron} whispers. \"Oh — you're real.\"","choices":[{"text":"Run to {missing_obj}.","next":"dream_road_2"},{"text":"Walk. Don't startle the dream.","next":"dream_road_2"}]},{"id":"dream_road_2","chapter":"CHAPTER III.5 · DREAMS IN THE WOOD","text":"The hallway never gets shorter. That is how you know it is a dream. But {missing_pron} gets closer somehow — or you do — and then {missing_pron} is right in front of you, close enough to see the freckle by {missing_poss} mouth.\n\"I can feel you on the road,\" {missing_pron} says. \"Every day. It's like a thread under my ribs.\"\n{missing_poss_cap} hand comes up — almost touches your cheek — stops, hovers. {missing_pron_cap} is afraid to be the one who makes contact, like {missing_pron}'s afraid {missing_pron}'ll vanish.\n\"It calls you the kept one,\" {missing_pron} adds, quieter. \"The Keeper. It calls me that. It tells me I always was. Don't — don't believe it if it shows you me wearing that name like I chose it.\"","choices":[{"text":"Take {missing_poss} hand. Hold it.","next":"dream_road_3","do":["flag:dream_royal_1","trust:+5","journal:I held {missing_short} in a dream. {missing_poss_cap} hand was warm. It shouldn't have been."]},{"text":"Promise: \"I'm coming. I swear it.\"","next":"dream_road_3","do":["flag:dream_royal_1","trust:+4","fear:-3"]}]},{"id":"dream_road_3","chapter":"CHAPTER III.5 · DREAMS IN THE WOOD","text":"The hallway folds. The candles tilt sideways. {missing_pron_cap} is gone — pulled — and you stumble forward into a clearing in the wood.\nA deer stands in the moonlight. Iron crown. Old eyes.\n{other_name}.\n\"Hello, little one,\" {other_pron} says, with a mouth that shouldn't shape words. \"I haven't long. Listen.\"\n{other_pron_cap} steps closer. Up close, the crown is fused to {other_poss} skull. There is no separating it now.\n\"I went first,\" {other_pron} says. \"I was older. I was supposed to. I failed and the wood took the failing and made me — this. I'm sorry. {missing_poss_cap} a better one than I was. Save {missing_obj} if you can. And — if you can't —\" {other_poss_cap} eyes shine. \"Forgive me for going first and leaving you both this.\"","choices":[{"text":"\"There's nothing to forgive.\"","next":"dream_road_4","do":["trust:+4","fear:-3"]},{"text":"Lay your hand on the crowned brow.","next":"dream_road_4","do":["trust:+5","journal:I laid my hand on {other_short}'s crown. The iron was cold. {other_pron_cap} wept like a person."]}]},{"id":"dream_road_4","chapter":"CHAPTER III.5 · DREAMS IN THE WOOD","text":"The clearing tilts.\nNow you are nowhere. Just dark, and a voice that is not a voice — it is a pressure in the marrow of your teeth.\n\"Walker. Little walker. You are very tired.\"\nThe Keeper.\n\"I will offer once. Turn back. I will let the {missing_short} live in {missing_poss} cage. {missing_pron_cap} will not be unhappy. I make a soft place. You can have a long life. You can have a family. A garden. I will even send {missing_obj} dreams of you, if you like. {missing_pron_cap} will think you a kind ghost.\"\nThe dark waits. Patient. Old.","choices":[{"text":"Refuse. \"No.\" Just that.","next":"dream_road_wake","do":["flag:refused_keeper","trust:+6","fear:-5","journal:The Keeper offered me a life. I said no. I think it was already smiling."]},{"text":"Pretend to consider. Learn what you can.","next":"dream_road_wake","do":["flag:keeper_curious","fear:+5","journal:I let the Keeper think I was considering. I learned the cage has a door. The door opens both ways."]},{"text":"Say nothing. Just stand.","next":"dream_road_wake","do":["fear:+3"]}]},{"id":"dream_road_wake","chapter":"CHAPTER III.5 · DREAMS IN THE WOOD","text":"You wake gasping. The sky is the wrong color of pre-dawn. Your hand is curled like you were holding something.\nThe pines are very still. A bird that should be singing isn't.\nYou know two things you didn't know last night:\nThe Keeper can speak to you in sleep.\nSo can {missing_name}.\nYou roll up your bedroll with hands that won't quite obey, and you put one foot in front of the other, because that is the only answer you have to any of it.","choices":[{"text":"Walk on toward the road.","next":"wastes_lost","do":["journal:I will not dream on this road again if I can help it. I will, of course."]},{"text":"Push hard for the city.","next":"city_gates"}]},{"id":"apprentice_1","chapter":"CHAPTER IV.5 · THE WIZARD'S APPRENTICE","text":"The boy sweeps in slow arcs across the foyer, his head tilted toward sounds you can't hear. His eyes are wrapped in clean linen. He cannot be more than fourteen.\nYou sit on the bottom step of the stair. He pauses mid-sweep.\n\"You,\" he says. \"You came back.\"\n\"You knew it was me?\"\n\"You walk like you're sorry about it.\" A small, tired smile. \"Most who come here don't. Sorry, I mean.\" He leans the broom against the wall. \"My name is Bram. He doesn't use it. He calls me boy. I don't mind, mostly. It just — adds up.\"\nThere is a careful pause, like a held door.\n\"I want to leave,\" Bram says. \"Will you help me?\"","choices":[{"text":"\"Yes. Of course.\"","next":"apprentice_2","do":["trust:+5","journal:Bram. The boy in the tower. He has a name and he wants to leave. I said yes."]},{"text":"\"Tell me what he'd do if he found us.\"","next":"apprentice_2","do":["fear:+2"]}]},{"id":"apprentice_2","chapter":"CHAPTER IV.5 · THE WIZARD'S APPRENTICE","text":"Bram sits beside you on the step. He folds his hands like he's been taught to be polite to guests, which is its own quiet heartbreak.\n\"He took my eyes for a spell,\" Bram says, factual. \"Not all the way. He says he'll give them back when I'm grown. He says it like a kindness. I'm grown enough. I was grown enough a year ago.\"\nHe tilts his face up at you. The linen is very clean. Someone — him, probably — keeps it very clean.\n\"There's a back stair behind the kitchen. He doesn't watch it because he forgets I can move. If I go down it, I can be in the woods before he notices. I have a sister in a village two days east. She thinks I'm dead. I would like her not to.\"","choices":[{"text":"\"I'll walk you out tonight.\"","next":"apprentice_3","do":["trust:+4"]},{"text":"\"I'll do better. I'll get your eyes first.\"","next":"apprentice_eyes","require":"trust:60","do":["trust:+6"]}]},{"id":"apprentice_eyes","chapter":"CHAPTER IV.5 · THE WIZARD'S APPRENTICE","text":"Bram goes very still. \"You'd — you can't. He keeps them in the solar. In a — a jar. He showed me once, before. He thought it would teach me something.\"\n\"It taught you something.\"\n\"Yes. Not what he meant.\"\nYou stand. The stair to the solar is right there. You have, you realize, been thinking about doing harder things than this for weeks.\nYou climb. The wizard is asleep at his desk, robe stained with tea. The jar is on the high shelf, dim and obvious. You take it. He does not wake. Maybe he is letting you. Maybe he is not as cruel as he pretends, or maybe he is so cruel he wants to see what you do with it.\nYou come down. You put the jar in Bram's hands.\nHe cries, finally. Not loud. Just enough to know he's a person.","choices":[{"text":"Walk him out the back stair.","next":"apprentice_3","do":["add_item:warm_tooth","journal:I stole Bram's eyes back. He gave me a small thing — a tooth on a cord, still warm. He said it was his mother's. He said it knew the way home."]}]},{"id":"apprentice_3","chapter":"CHAPTER IV.5 · THE WIZARD'S APPRENTICE","text":"The back stair smells of onions and old smoke. Bram takes it with his hand on your sleeve and a confidence that suggests he has rehearsed this in his head a thousand times.\nAt the bottom, the door. Beyond it, the wood. The night is cool. The sky has stars.\nBram tilts his face up. Whether or not he has his eyes now, the gesture is the same.\n\"Thank you,\" he says. \"I — I wanted to give you something.\" He fishes in his sleeve, hands you a small, sealed packet of folded parchment. \"His true name. Not what he calls himself. I heard a — a thing call him by it once. In a circle. I wrote it down. You might need it. The Keeper might. They — they know each other. I think.\"\nHe smiles, briefly, and is gone into the trees.","choices":[{"text":"Pocket the packet. Go.","next":"tower_leave","do":["add_item:keeper_lore","trust:+5","journal:Bram gave me the wizard's true name. He said the Keeper might recognize it. I do not yet understand what I am supposed to do with it. I will."]}]}]},"story/chapter_11.json":{"scenes":[{"id":"quiet_street_1","chapter":"CHAPTER V.5 · THE QUIET STREET","text":"Behind the abandoned theatre, the city goes strange and quiet.\nThe lamps are still lit here. The shutters are still painted. A cat sleeps on a windowsill like nothing has happened, because here — for now — nothing has.\nA boy of seven or eight sits on the curb across from a bookshop, swinging his legs. He looks up at you and his eyes are the wrong eyes. Too still. Too — listening.\n\"Hello,\" he says, polite. \"Are you the one walking to the Gate? He said someone might be along. He said to be kind. He said you have a sad face.\"\nHe smiles. The smile is his. The listening behind it isn't.\n\"My name is Pip,\" he adds. \"I think it's Pip. It's harder to remember some days.\"","choices":[{"text":"Crouch. Talk to him like a child.","next":"quiet_street_pip"},{"text":"Go into the bookshop first.","next":"quiet_street_bookseller"},{"text":"Back away. He is not safe.","next":"quiet_street_retreat","do":["fear:+5"]}]},{"id":"quiet_street_pip","chapter":"CHAPTER V.5 · THE QUIET STREET","text":"You crouch. The cobbles are cold through your knees.\n\"Pip,\" you say. \"How long has it been hard to remember your name?\"\nHe thinks. He thinks too long. \"Since the man with the kind voice started visiting. In dreams. He tells me stories. He says I'm clever. Nobody calls me clever, miss — sir.\" His face flickers. \"It's nice. Is it bad?\"\nYou take his hands. They are very small and very cold and very alive.\n\"It's bad, Pip.\"\n\"Oh.\" He thinks about this. \"Will you make him stop?\"\nThe listening behind his eyes goes sharp at that. Something doesn't like the question.","choices":[{"text":"Use the wardstone on him.","next":"quiet_street_warded","require":"wardstone","do":["rem_item:wardstone","flag:saved_pip","trust:+8","journal:I used the wardstone on a boy named Pip. The Keeper's listening went out of him like a held breath. He cried. So did I, a little."]},{"text":"Promise. \"Yes. I'll make him stop.\"","next":"quiet_street_promise","do":["trust:+5","journal:I promised a child I'd save him. I have promised too many people too many things. I will keep this one or die trying."]}]},{"id":"quiet_street_warded","chapter":"CHAPTER V.5 · THE QUIET STREET","text":"You press the wardstone to his forehead. It heats and then it cools.\nPip blinks. His eyes — his real, blurry, ordinary-child eyes — come back into them. He looks at you like he's never seen you before and is delighted anyway.\n\"Oh,\" he says. \"My head's quiet.\"\nHe starts to cry — the easy, full crying of a child who has been holding something heavy for a long time without knowing it was heavy.\nA woman runs out of the bakery across the way. \"Pip — oh, Pip, baby —\" She gathers him up. She looks at you over his hair, and you don't know what your face is doing but hers softens.\n\"Thank you,\" she mouths. She doesn't know for what. She knows it's something.","choices":[{"text":"Walk on. The bookshop next.","next":"quiet_street_bookseller","do":["fear:-5"]}]},{"id":"quiet_street_promise","chapter":"CHAPTER V.5 · THE QUIET STREET","text":"You promise. Pip nods solemnly, as if witnessing a contract.\n\"Okay,\" he says. He stands, dusts his pants, and walks away down the lane like a small person on an errand.\nThe listening doesn't leave him. It goes with him.\nYou feel a great weight settle, the kind that doesn't lift until you have done a thing. The bookshop door is just there.","choices":[{"text":"Into the bookshop.","next":"quiet_street_bookseller","do":["fear:+3"]}]},{"id":"quiet_street_retreat","chapter":"CHAPTER V.5 · THE QUIET STREET","text":"You walk fast and you do not look back and the curb noises follow you for a block — small swinging feet, a child's hum — until you turn a corner and the street is just a street again.\nYou stop. You lean against a wall. Your hands are shaking.\nYou tell yourself you couldn't have helped. You tell yourself it isn't your job. You tell yourself a lot of things.\nNone of them feel true.","choices":[{"text":"Go back. Try again.","next":"quiet_street_1"},{"text":"Press on to the bookshop.","next":"quiet_street_bookseller","do":["fear:+5","journal:I left a boy on a curb. I will see him in dreams forever, I think."]}]},{"id":"quiet_street_bookseller","chapter":"CHAPTER V.5 · THE QUIET STREET","text":"The bookshop smells the way bookshops always smell — old paper, dust, a little smoke. The bell over the door is so soft you almost don't hear it.\nThe bookseller is a woman with one milky eye and one sharp one. She doesn't look up.\n\"You want the back room,\" she says. \"Everyone who walks like you does.\"\nShe leads you past shelves that go up further than the building should allow. The back room is smaller than it should be. There is one chair. One book on a stand. The book is open.\n\"He had a name,\" she says, \"before he was the Keeper. Names are doors. If you say the right one in the right place, the door doesn't have to be on his side.\"","choices":[{"text":"\"Tell me the name.\"","next":"quiet_street_name"},{"text":"\"What does it cost?\"","next":"quiet_street_cost"},{"text":"Ask if she has anything else for you.","next":"side_bookseller_return","require":"flag:knows_keeper_name"}]},{"id":"quiet_street_cost","chapter":"CHAPTER V.5 · THE QUIET STREET","text":"She smiles, finally. It is not a friendly smile but it is not cruel.\n\"A memory,\" she says. \"Not a big one. Something small. A meal you liked. A face from a holiday. The first word someone you loved said wrong. Anything. The book takes what it likes.\"\nShe taps the open page. \"Names like his are heavy. The book has to be paid.\"\nYou could lie. You could try. You suspect the book would know.","choices":[{"text":"Pay. Give a small memory.","next":"quiet_street_name","do":["fear:+3","journal:I gave the book a memory of my mother humming in the kitchen. I cannot get it back. I remember that she hummed; I cannot remember the song. I traded it for a name."]},{"text":"Refuse. Walk out.","next":"quiet_street_leave","do":["fear:+2"]}]},{"id":"quiet_street_name","chapter":"CHAPTER V.5 · THE QUIET STREET","text":"She turns the book. The page is blank — and then it isn't. A word writes itself in your handwriting, which is impossible, which is happening.\nIt is not a long name. It is the wrong shape for a mouth. You read it once. You feel it settle into the back of your tongue like a tooth.\n\"Say it at the Gate,\" she says. \"Not before. Not after. Once. Say it once. He has to hear it from someone who knew him when. The book is making you that person, briefly.\"\nShe closes the book. She looks tired now, like she's done a lifting.\n\"Go,\" she says. \"And — if you live — come back and tell me how it went. I like to know.\"","choices":[{"text":"Go.","next":"city_spymaster_after","do":["add_item:keeper_lore","flag:knows_keeper_name","trust:+5","journal:I carry the Keeper's old name on the back of my tongue. It tastes like cold iron and an apology."]}]},{"id":"quiet_street_leave","chapter":"CHAPTER V.5 · THE QUIET STREET","text":"You walk out. The bell over the door is even quieter going the other way.\nThe street is just a street. The cat on the windowsill opens one eye and closes it.\nYou will think about that name you didn't buy for a long time. Probably forever. Probably as long as the rest of forever lasts.","choices":[{"text":"Back to the spymaster's.","next":"city_spymaster_after"}]}]},"story/chapter_12.json":{"scenes":[{"id":"dream_royal_1","chapter":"CAMP · DREAMS OF THE GATE","text":"You close your eyes by the camp fire and the camp fire goes away.\nYou are in a small stone room. Just a room. No window. One door. A pallet on the floor with someone curled on it, knees up, face hidden.\n\"Who's there,\" {missing_pron} says, not lifting {missing_poss} head. \"Please don't be it again. Please.\"\nYou know the voice. You have never heard it. Both, at once.\n\"It's not it,\" you say. \"It's — I'm — I think you might be {missing_name}.\"\n{missing_poss_cap} head comes up so fast you flinch. {missing_poss_cap} face is thinner than the portraits. {missing_poss_cap} eyes find yours like you're the first warm thing in a year.\n\"Oh,\" {missing_pron} breathes. \"Oh, you're not it. You're — you're somebody.\"","choices":[{"text":"Sit on the floor near {missing_obj}.","next":"dream_royal_1_end","do":["flag:dream_royal_1","trust:+5","journal:I dreamed of {missing_short}. {missing_pron_cap} was so small in the dark. I sat. {missing_pron_cap} watched me like I might unmake."]},{"text":"Promise: \"I'm coming for you.\"","next":"dream_royal_1_end","do":["flag:dream_royal_1","trust:+4"]}]},{"id":"dream_royal_1_end","chapter":"CAMP · DREAMS OF THE GATE","text":"\"Don't tell me your name,\" {missing_pron} says suddenly. \"Not yet. It listens. If you give me your name it'll have it too.\"\n\"Then what do I call you?\"\n\"Anything. Anything but {missing_short}. Make something up. Make it small.\"\nYou pick a name — a foolish one, the first that comes — and {missing_pron} laughs, a small startled real laugh, and you realize you have never heard a {missing_short} laugh and that you would like to hear it again.\nThe walls thin. The dream is going.\n\"Come back,\" {missing_pron} says, fast. \"Sleep again. Come back.\"\nThen the fire, and your own breath, and the dark.","choices":[{"text":"Wake. Sit up. Stare at the embers.","next":"camp_rest","do":["journal:I gave {missing_short} a nickname. {missing_pron_cap} laughed. I am undone."]}]},{"id":"dream_royal_2","chapter":"CAMP · DREAMS OF THE GATE","text":"Sleep finds you faster this time, like a door that's been left ajar.\n{missing_pron_cap} is sitting up on the pallet when you arrive. There's a — a stub of candle now, somehow. {missing_pron_cap} has made the room a fraction kinder by force of will.\n\"You came back,\" {missing_pron} says, and the relief in it ages you a year.\n\"You asked.\"\n{missing_pron_cap} pats the floor next to {missing_obj}. You sit. {missing_poss_cap} shoulder is warm against yours, which is unfair, in a dream.\n\"Tell me a thing,\" {missing_pron} says. \"A small thing. About out there. I forget what air tastes like.\"\nSo you tell {missing_obj} about the dog at the caravan. About Juno's stone. About the way the foothills smell of pine.\n{missing_pron_cap} closes {missing_poss} eyes and listens like it's a meal.","choices":[{"text":"Ask {missing_obj} what {missing_pron} is afraid of.","next":"dream_royal_2_fear"},{"text":"Just sit with {missing_obj}. Quiet.","next":"dream_royal_2_quiet"}]},{"id":"dream_royal_2_fear","chapter":"CAMP · DREAMS OF THE GATE","text":"{missing_pron_cap} is quiet a long time.\n\"Not dying,\" {missing_pron} says. \"I thought it'd be that. It isn't. I'm afraid of forgetting. It's so patient. It tells me stories about myself that are almost right. Just — one detail wrong, every time. And every time I correct it I wonder if I'm the wrong one.\"\n{missing_pron_cap} looks at you.\n\"If I — if when you find me I don't sound like me. Don't trust me right away. Make me prove it. Ask me something only I'd know.\"\n\"What would only you know?\"\n{missing_pron_cap} thinks. Then, small and stubborn: \"Ask me what I was reading the night they took me. I'll remember. Even if it's the last thing I remember. I'll remember.\"","choices":[{"text":"\"I promise.\"","next":"dream_royal_2_end","do":["flag:dream_royal_2","trust:+5","journal:If {missing_pron} comes back wrong, I ask: what were you reading? {missing_pron_cap} will know. {missing_pron_cap} will know."]}]},{"id":"dream_royal_2_quiet","chapter":"CAMP · DREAMS OF THE GATE","text":"You don't say anything. {missing_pron_cap} doesn't either.\nThe quiet is the kind people only manage when they have already agreed to be safe with each other.\nAfter a long time, {missing_pron} says, \"I don't even know what you look like. Out there. Not in here. In here you're sort of — soft around the edges. Like firelight.\"\n\"You're soft around the edges too.\"\n\"Liar,\" {missing_pron} says, smiling. \"I haven't slept properly in months. I look like a ghost.\"\n\"Then you're a soft ghost.\"\n{missing_pron_cap} laughs the small laugh again, and you realize: you would walk through walls for this laugh. You will. You have to. The Gate is a wall.","choices":[{"text":"Lean your head against {missing_poss}.","next":"dream_royal_2_end","do":["flag:dream_royal_2","trust:+6","fear:-4","journal:I leaned my head against {missing_short}. I have known {missing_obj} two nights. I have known {missing_obj} my whole life."]}]},{"id":"dream_royal_2_end","chapter":"CAMP · DREAMS OF THE GATE","text":"The candle gutters. The dream begins to fade — not pulled this time, just tired.\n\"Sleep here again,\" {missing_pron} murmurs. \"Tomorrow. The night after. As many as you can.\"\n\"I will.\"\n\"I'll be here. I — I don't have anywhere else.\"\nThe joke is tiny and broken and you don't laugh and {missing_pron} doesn't expect you to.\nThen the fire, and the bedroll, and your face wet for no reason you can name.","choices":[{"text":"Wake. Stoke the embers.","next":"camp_rest"}]},{"id":"dream_royal_3","chapter":"CAMP · DREAMS OF THE GATE","text":"Tonight the dream is different.\nThe room is gone. You are in a garden you don't remember — sunlight, hedges, a stone bench. {missing_pron_cap} is on the bench in summer clothes, sleeves rolled, hair loose. Younger. Maybe twelve.\n{missing_pron_cap} looks up and smiles like you're expected.\n\"You don't remember,\" {missing_pron} says. \"I thought maybe you wouldn't. We were small. But I do.\"\n\"Remember what?\"\n\"You came to the palace once. A — a delegation. From your village. From your house. I don't know which you are, on the outside. I just know it was you. You sat in the kitchen. You ate three pears. You told me you were going to be a — \" {missing_pron_cap} laughs. \" — a great person, you said. And I believed you. I have believed you for ten years.\"","choices":[{"text":"\"I don't remember the pears.\"","next":"dream_royal_3_pears"},{"text":"\"I remember you.\" (Lie a little. Make {missing_obj} smile.)","next":"dream_royal_3_remember"}]},{"id":"dream_royal_3_pears","chapter":"CAMP · DREAMS OF THE GATE","text":"{missing_pron_cap} laughs — not hurt, more like delighted. \"Of course you don't. I was the one staring. You were the one eating.\"\n{missing_pron_cap} slides over on the bench. You sit. The sun is warm. It hasn't been warm in your life for weeks.\n\"I told my — {other_sibling} about you, after,\" {missing_pron} says, quieter. \"For years. {other_pron_cap} teased me about it. Said I'd married you in my head. I — well. I sort of had.\"\n{missing_pron_cap} won't look at you now.\n\"I'm sorry,\" {missing_pron} says. \"That's a lot to put on a person who only came for pears.\"","choices":[{"text":"Take {missing_poss} hand.","next":"dream_royal_3_end","do":["flag:dream_royal_3","trust:+6","journal:{missing_pron_cap} has loved me since {missing_pron} was twelve. I don't know what to do with that. I want to do everything with it."]}]},{"id":"dream_royal_3_remember","chapter":"CAMP · DREAMS OF THE GATE","text":"\"I remember you,\" you say, and you don't, but you wish you did, and the wishing comes out as warmth and {missing_pron} hears it anyway.\n{missing_pron_cap} closes {missing_poss} eyes.\n\"You're lying,\" {missing_pron} says, smiling. \"It's a very nice lie. Thank you.\"\n{missing_pron_cap} opens {missing_poss} eyes and they are bright.\n\"I will not hold it against you,\" {missing_pron} says. \"You can love me starting now if you want to. We don't all get to start at twelve.\"","choices":[{"text":"\"Starting now, then.\"","next":"dream_royal_3_end","do":["flag:dream_royal_3","trust:+7","fear:-3","journal:I told {missing_short} I'd love {missing_obj} starting now. I meant it. I am terrified of how much I meant it."]}]},{"id":"dream_royal_3_end","chapter":"CAMP · DREAMS OF THE GATE","text":"The garden softens at the edges.\n\"You can't stay long tonight,\" {missing_pron} says, regret thick in {missing_poss} voice. \"It's — it's listening at the door of my room. I have to wake up and pretend to sleep.\"\n\"Tomorrow night.\"\n\"Tomorrow night,\" {missing_pron} agrees. And then, with a small, fierce courage: \"Walker. I am very glad you are coming.\"\nThe garden lets go of you gently. You wake with your hand half-curled toward something that isn't there.","choices":[{"text":"Wake. Tend the fire.","next":"camp_rest"}]},{"id":"dream_royal_4","chapter":"CAMP · DREAMS OF THE GATE","text":"Tonight the room is back, but it has a window. A small, high one. There are stars in it.\n{missing_pron_cap} is standing under it, in a shirt too thin for the cold {missing_pron} no longer feels.\n\"It's getting closer,\" {missing_pron} says without turning. \"It says — it says it's almost time. It says I'm being good.\" {missing_pron_cap} laughs without humor. \"I have not been being good. I have been being you. Thinking about you. It thinks the thinking is for it. Idiot thing.\"\n{missing_pron_cap} turns. {missing_poss_cap} eyes are wet.\n\"If you don't make it,\" {missing_pron} says. \"If the Gate keeps you. I want you to know — I had this. I had nights with you. It cannot take that. I had this.\"","choices":[{"text":"Cross the room. Hold {missing_obj}.","next":"dream_royal_4_kiss"},{"text":"\"You'll have more. I swear it.\"","next":"dream_royal_4_kiss"}]},{"id":"dream_royal_4_kiss","chapter":"CAMP · DREAMS OF THE GATE","text":"You cross the small dream-room. {missing_pron_cap} comes into your arms like you're a door {missing_pron}'s been leaning on for a year.\nYou hold {missing_obj}. {missing_pron_cap} is shaking. So are you, a little.\n{missing_pron_cap} tips {missing_poss} face up. Asks the question without asking it.\nYou kiss {missing_obj}. It is soft and brief and not at all dreamlike — it tastes like a real thing, salt and a held breath and the small startled sound {missing_pron} makes against your mouth.\nWhen you pull apart {missing_pron} is half-laughing and half-crying.\n\"Come and get me,\" {missing_pron} whispers. \"Come and get me. Come and get me.\"\nThe dream lets you go. You wake with your hand on your mouth.","choices":[{"text":"Lie back. Don't sleep again.","next":"camp_rest","do":["flag:loved_royal","trust:+8","fear:-5","journal:I kissed {missing_short} in a dream. {missing_pron_cap} kissed me back. I am no longer doing this for a kingdom. I am doing this for {missing_obj}."]}]}]},"story/chapter_13.json":{"scenes":[{"id":"threshold_1","chapter":"CHAPTER VI · THE THRESHOLD","text":"The Gate is a mile off. You can feel it the way you feel an oncoming storm — a weight on the inside of the skull, a low note under hearing.\nYou make camp here, against everyone's good sense. The ground is soft enough. There is a stream. The horses are tired. So are you.\nThe fire goes up. Someone — Ash, maybe; one of yours — puts a hand on your shoulder and squeezes once. \"Sit,\" they say. \"Eat. We have one night.\"\nThe company gathers, smaller than you remember it. The faces you have collected. You look at each one and try not to count.\nOne night. You have one night.","choices":[{"text":"Sit with the companions. Listen.","next":"threshold_companions"},{"text":"Take a vigil walk alone.","next":"threshold_vigil"},{"text":"Write a letter home.","next":"threshold_letter_1"},{"text":"A minstrel waits on a rock. Hear them out.","next":"side_minstrel_3","require":"flag:minstrel_v3"},{"text":"Stop on the last rise. Look back at the kingdom.","next":"deep_threshold_extra"}]},{"id":"threshold_companions","chapter":"CHAPTER VI · THE THRESHOLD","text":"They are bad at this. So are you.\nAsh tries first. \"So. Tomorrow. We're — we are doing it. The thing.\" Ash, who has never been bad at words, is bad at words.\nMoth, who is small and strange, says, \"There's a crow who follows us. I asked. He said the air on the other side of the Gate tastes like winter. I thought you should know.\"\nSer Vale clears his throat. \"I would like to say, before tomorrow — \" He stops. He cannot finish. He nods like that was enough.\nRook just looks at you. The look is enough.\nThe hedge witch, if she is here, picks at the fire and says nothing and her not-saying is a kind of prayer.\nYou love them. It is awful. You love them.","choices":[{"text":"Tell them, plainly, that you love them.","next":"threshold_love_them","do":["trust:+6","fear:-4"]},{"text":"Make a stupid joke instead.","next":"threshold_joke","do":["trust:+4"]}]},{"id":"threshold_love_them","chapter":"CHAPTER VI · THE THRESHOLD","text":"You say it. Out loud. To the fire and the faces around it.\n\"I love you. All of you. I didn't know I was going to. I did anyway.\"\nMoth puts her small hand over her mouth. Ash looks at the sky very hard. Ser Vale says, \"Yes,\" quietly, like a vow. Rook reaches across and squeezes your wrist without speaking.\nThe hedge witch, if she's here, says, \"That is what you bring through the Gate. That is the only thing you can take in with you. Carry it carefully.\"\nThe fire makes a small sound. None of you speak for a while. None of you need to.","choices":[{"text":"Stand. Walk a little, alone.","next":"threshold_vigil","do":["journal:I told my friends I loved them. The world didn't end. I'm trying to remember that for tomorrow."]}]},{"id":"threshold_joke","chapter":"CHAPTER VI · THE THRESHOLD","text":"\"So if we die tomorrow,\" you say, \"who gets my boots.\"\nThere is a beat. Then Moth says, \"They're too big,\" and Ash says, \"They're ugly,\" and Ser Vale says, very seriously, \"I would like the left one,\" and the laugh that goes through the camp is too loud and too brittle and exactly right.\nIt is how your people say I love you. You let them say it.\nRook, after, leans close. \"I'm having the right one,\" he murmurs. \"It's been agreed.\"\nYou laugh wet. The fire pops. The Gate hums in the back of your skull.","choices":[{"text":"Walk a little, alone.","next":"threshold_vigil"}]},{"id":"threshold_vigil","chapter":"CHAPTER VI · THE THRESHOLD","text":"You walk to the edge of the firelight and a little past. The Gate is visible from here — a black slot in the world, the wrong color of dark.\nYou stand. You don't pray, exactly. You don't know how. You just stand.\nFootsteps behind you. You don't have to look to know which.\nSer Vale, or Moth, or Rook, or the witch. Or all of them in turn — they take shifts, you realize, without arranging it; they will not let you be alone tonight, and they will not crowd you.\nWhen it is Vale he says, \"You don't have to be brave for us.\"\nWhen it is Moth she says, \"The crow says hello again.\"\nWhen it is Rook he doesn't say anything. He just stands at your shoulder and breathes in time with you.","choices":[{"text":"Walk back to the fire.","next":"threshold_stranger","do":["trust:+4","fear:-3"]}]},{"id":"threshold_letter_1","chapter":"CHAPTER VI · THE THRESHOLD","text":"You find a scrap of parchment. You sharpen the stub of a pencil with your knife. You stare at the blank for a long time, the way the blank stares back.\nWho do you write to.\nYou could write to your mother, who may or may not still draw breath. To a friend you abandoned in the city. To a teacher who was kind. To the dog from the caravan. To no one. To {missing_name}, who you have only kissed in a dream.\nThe pencil hovers.","choices":[{"text":"Write to your mother.","next":"threshold_letter_mother"},{"text":"Write to {missing_name}.","next":"threshold_letter_royal"},{"text":"Write to whoever finds it.","next":"threshold_letter_finder"}]},{"id":"threshold_letter_mother","chapter":"CHAPTER VI · THE THRESHOLD","text":"Mama, you write, and you have to stop.\nYou start again. Mama. I did not become what you wanted. I did not stay. I am sorry for the leaving more than the rest. I want you to know — the road was hard and I was not alone on it. There were people who looked at me and saw the version of me I would like to be. I tried to be that one. I tried.\nIf I do not come back: I was not afraid at the end. That is a lie, but I want you to have it. Lies are easier to keep than people.\nI loved you. Crooked, with my whole chest.\nYour child.\nYou fold it. You put it in the oiled packet at the bottom of your pack, where the rain won't find it.","choices":[{"text":"Sit a while longer.","next":"threshold_stranger","do":["trust:+5","fear:-4","journal:I wrote my mother a letter I will probably never deliver. The writing of it was the delivery."]}]},{"id":"threshold_letter_royal","chapter":"CHAPTER VI · THE THRESHOLD","text":"You write: I have only known you in dreams. I do not know if you will read this in the world, or if you will know my handwriting, or what your voice will sound like when it isn't soft with sleep.\nYou stop. You start again.\nI am coming. If I am late, I am sorry. If I am late forever, I am sorrier. I want to read whatever you were reading the night they took you. I want to read it with you. I want to drink tea in a kitchen with you and bore everyone we know. I want a very ordinary life, beside you. That is a strange thing to want from someone I have not met. I am not sorry I want it. I am only sorry if I do not get to give it to you.\nYou fold it. You will press it into {missing_poss} hand if you find {missing_obj}. If you do not — well. The Gate can have it.","choices":[{"text":"Stand. Breathe.","next":"threshold_stranger","do":["add_item:elara_letter","trust:+6","fear:-3","journal:I wrote {missing_short} a letter. I am carrying it into the Gate. If I come out, {missing_pron} will read it. If I don't — well."]}]},{"id":"threshold_letter_finder","chapter":"CHAPTER VI · THE THRESHOLD","text":"If you are reading this, you write, then I am very probably dead, and I would like you to know a few things.\nI was a person. I had a dog at a caravan that decided I was its problem. I had a friend named Juno who gave me a painted stone. I had a love I knew only in dreams. I had a company of people I would have walked off the world for and they walked off the world with me.\nI did not save everyone I wanted to. I tried. I tried hard. I want that on the record.\nDo something small and ordinary today. Eat bread. Touch a person you love. Do not waste it.\n— A walker at the Gate.\nYou fold it. You weight it with Juno's stone. You leave it on a flat rock at the edge of camp.","choices":[{"text":"Walk back to the fire.","next":"threshold_stranger","do":["trust:+5","journal:I left a letter on a stone for whoever finds it. I do not need them to find me. I need them to find the letter."]}]},{"id":"threshold_stranger","chapter":"CHAPTER VI · THE THRESHOLD","text":"You are about to lie down when the watch calls quietly: someone coming.\nFrom the direction of the Gate. Stumbling.\nA woman. Older than you. Hair like burnt paper. Eyes — eyes that do not focus right.\nShe walks straight to your fire and sits without asking and stares into it.\n\"I came out,\" she says, in the voice of someone surprised by her own mouth. \"I don't know how. It let me. It — it sang and the singing was a door for a moment. I think it wanted me to tell you something.\"\nShe looks up at you. Her eyes find yours. They are someone's eyes again, briefly.\n\"It is afraid of you,\" she says. \"That's all. I came all this way to tell you that. It is afraid of you.\"","choices":[{"text":"Wrap her in a blanket. Feed her.","next":"threshold_stranger_care","do":["trust:+5"]},{"text":"\"Tell me what it looks like.\"","next":"threshold_stranger_describe","do":["fear:+3"]}]},{"id":"threshold_stranger_care","chapter":"CHAPTER VI · THE THRESHOLD","text":"You put your own blanket around her. Moth ladles soup. The woman eats with both hands, hunched over the bowl, like she has forgotten how to be watched.\nAfter, she sleeps. Just — sleeps. Out, like a snuffed candle.\nYou will not get her story. You will not learn her name. In the morning she will be gone, or she won't — but the soup mattered. The blanket mattered. You did not have to know who she was to be kind to her.\nThis, you understand suddenly, is what you have been training for, in all the smallness. Not the sword. Not the spell. The blanket.","choices":[{"text":"Lie down. Try to sleep.","next":"threshold_dawn","do":["trust:+5","fear:-4","journal:A woman walked out of the Gate. We fed her. We did not ask. I think this is the answer to a question I did not know I was asking."]}]},{"id":"threshold_stranger_describe","chapter":"CHAPTER VI · THE THRESHOLD","text":"She thinks. The thinking hurts her face.\n\"It is not a shape,\" she says. \"It is a — a being-watched. It is what's behind you when you wake up in the dark and you cannot turn your head. It has voices because it has eaten voices. It has a face only when it thinks you want one.\"\nShe shudders.\n\"It will offer you something. Whatever you most want. It will be very, very kind. The trick — the trick is — \" She trails off. The words leave her. She looks at you, helpless. \"I had it. I had what to tell you. It took it back. I'm sorry. I'm sorry.\"\nShe weeps. You hold her hand. You do not let go until she sleeps.","choices":[{"text":"Lie down.","next":"threshold_dawn","do":["fear:+3","journal:She forgot what she came to tell me. The Keeper took it. It is, as advertised, afraid of me. It also has very long arms."]}]},{"id":"threshold_dawn","chapter":"CHAPTER VI · THE THRESHOLD","text":"You don't really sleep.\nYou doze. You wake. You doze. The sky goes from black to that pre-dawn color that is not really a color at all — bruise, paper, breath.\nThe Gate is visible now without standing. The world has tilted toward it overnight.\nThe company stirs. Moth puts the kettle on. Ash sharpens a knife that doesn't need sharpening. Ser Vale braids his hair back like a man going to a formal occasion. Rook stretches his bound wrists, and the cord, and the bruise around the cord.\nYou stand up. Your knees work. You are surprised that your knees work.\nIt is the day.","choices":[{"text":"Walk to the Gate.","next":"hollow_gate","do":["flag:walked_threshold","trust:+5","journal:I slept badly. The Gate is a mile of nothing. My company is behind me. I am, today, going to do the thing."]}]}]},"story/chapter_14.json":{"scenes":[{"id":"epilogue_fight","chapter":"EPILOGUE · ONE YEAR ON","text":"A year later.\nThe kingdom is loud again. There are markets. There are dogs. There is a holiday — recently invented, badly organized, beloved — where children wear paper crowns and run through the streets shouting nonsense, which is everyone's favorite kind of nonsense.\n{missing_name} is alive. {missing_pron_cap} is thinner than the portraits remembered, and {missing_poss} laugh is rarer than it was in the dream-garden, and {missing_pron} does not sleep without a candle. But {missing_pron} is alive.\nThe Keeper is not.\nYou killed it. You did. It cost you a shoulder you don't lift right anymore and a friend you do not name, even in your own head, even now. You killed it. The kingdom calls you a hero. You let them. It is easier than correcting them.","choices":[{"text":"Walk through the market with {missing_obj}.","next":"epilogue_fight_end"}]},{"id":"epilogue_fight_end","chapter":"EPILOGUE · ONE YEAR ON","text":"{missing_pron_cap} loops {missing_poss} arm through yours. {missing_pron_cap} has gotten brave about the arm-looping. The first month, {missing_pron} couldn't bear to be touched at all. You waited. You are good at waiting now.\nA child shoves a paper crown into your hand. \"You have to wear it,\" the child says, fierce. \"It's the rules.\"\nYou put it on. {missing_pron_cap} laughs — the small startled laugh, the dream-laugh, the one you walked through walls for. {missing_pron_cap} puts on {missing_poss} own crown, the paper one, and tips it rakishly.\n\"How do I look,\" {missing_pron} says.\n\"Like a {missing_short},\" you say. \"Like mine.\"\nThe market goes on. The shoulder aches in the cold. {other_name}'s name is on a stone in the garden, and you visit, and you tell {other_obj} about the markets. Some nights you dream of the Gate. Some nights you don't.\nSome nights, beside {missing_obj}, you sleep through.","choices":[]},{"id":"epilogue_talk","chapter":"EPILOGUE · ONE YEAR ON","text":"A year later.\nThe Keeper still exists. You did not kill it. You talked to it. You said its old name, the one the bookseller put on the back of your tongue, and the saying of it cracked something in the dark, and the dark — wept. Or what passed for weeping.\nIt is smaller now. It lives in the Gate still, but it does not take children. There is a treaty, of a sort, that you negotiated half-mad and bleeding at three in the morning a year ago, and that nobody quite believes is real until they notice the children come home.\n{missing_name} is alive. The treaty's first signature. {missing_pron_cap} writes letters to the Keeper sometimes, you discover. Polite ones. {missing_pron_cap} says it is the only way {missing_pron} sleeps.","choices":[{"text":"Read one of the letters with {missing_obj}.","next":"epilogue_talk_end"}]},{"id":"epilogue_talk_end","chapter":"EPILOGUE · ONE YEAR ON","text":"{missing_pron_cap} lets you read one. {missing_pron_cap} is shy about it. The letter says, in {missing_poss} careful hand: I am not afraid of you today. I am writing this from a kitchen. The kettle is on. The person I love is across the table. I want you to know there are kettles and kitchens. I want you to remember you used to be a person who knew about them. Yours, with — and then a word {missing_pron} has crossed out, and rewritten, and crossed out again — yours, anyway.\nYou put the letter down. You take {missing_poss} hand across the table.\n\"It worked, you know,\" {missing_pron} says. \"The talking. You did that. You.\"\n\"We did it.\"\n\"We did it.\"\nOutside, a child shouts something nonsensical and joyful. The kettle whistles. {other_name}, in the garden's stone, is remembered. The Gate is a door now, not a mouth. You sit with the {missing_short} you love and drink tea, and the world, for an afternoon, behaves.","choices":[]},{"id":"epilogue_clever","chapter":"EPILOGUE · ONE YEAR ON","text":"A year later.\nThe Gate is sealed. Not destroyed — sealed. With a trick. Your trick. You used the wizard's true name and the Keeper's old one and the warm tooth on its cord and a riddle that the Keeper, in its arrogance, accepted, and when it accepted you closed the door behind it from the outside.\nIt is in there still. Forever, perhaps. You try not to think about forever.\n{missing_name} is alive. {missing_pron_cap} sleeps badly. So do you. You sleep badly together, which is, it turns out, a kind of sleeping well.","choices":[{"text":"Walk to the cliff above the Gate.","next":"epilogue_clever_end"}]},{"id":"epilogue_clever_end","chapter":"EPILOGUE · ONE YEAR ON","text":"You go, sometimes, the two of you. To the cliff above where the Gate used to be a Gate.\nIt looks like a stone arch now. Just stone. Birds nest in it. A goatherd grazes his flock at its foot and pretends not to know what it was.\n{missing_pron_cap} stands beside you in the wind. {missing_poss_cap} hair is longer. {missing_poss_cap} hand finds yours without looking.\n\"Do you ever feel bad,\" {missing_pron} says, \"that you tricked it.\"\n\"Every day.\"\n\"Good.\" {missing_pron_cap} squeezes your hand. \"You should. That's how I know you are still you and not a clever thing pretending. The Keeper would never feel bad about a trick. You do. So.\"\n{missing_pron_cap} tips {missing_poss} head against your shoulder.\n\"Let's go home,\" {missing_pron} says. \"I'll read to you. I was reading something the night they took me. I never finished it. I want you to hear how it ends.\"","choices":[]},{"id":"epilogue_sacrifice","chapter":"EPILOGUE · ONE YEAR ON","text":"A year later.\nYou are not here. Not in the way you used to be.\nYou traded yourself. You stepped into the Keeper's place when it howled for one, and you held the door open from the inside long enough for {missing_name} to walk through it into the world.\n{missing_pron_cap} did not want to go. {missing_pron_cap} fought. {missing_pron_cap} bit Ser Vale's hand. {missing_pron_cap} screamed your name — your real one, which {missing_pron} had refused to learn in dreams but learned anyway in the end — and the screaming is what you remember of the world, sometimes, when you remember anything.\nYou are in the dark now. But the dark is — quieter, with you in it. You are not the Keeper. You are something gentler. You let the children go home.","choices":[{"text":"Listen. {missing_pron_cap} is dreaming again tonight.","next":"epilogue_sacrifice_end"}]},{"id":"epilogue_sacrifice_end","chapter":"EPILOGUE · ONE YEAR ON","text":"{missing_pron_cap} sleeps. {missing_pron_cap} dreams. {missing_pron_cap} dreams of you, every night, and the dream is a small room with a window with stars in it, and you sit on the pallet and {missing_pron} sits beside you.\n\"You're still here,\" {missing_pron} says, every time, in wonder.\n\"I'm still here.\"\n\"I am not letting them put another person on the throne until I find a way to bring you back.\"\n\"Don't waste your life on that.\"\n\"It isn't a waste. Listen.\" {missing_pron_cap} takes your dream-hand. {missing_pron_cap} is older in this dream. {missing_pron_cap} is wearing the crown well now. \"There is a girl in the city named Juno. There is a boy named Bram. There is a bookseller. There is a woman who walked out of the Gate. They are all looking. I have them looking. We will find the door from this side and we will come and get you. Stubborn comes home. Someone told me that. I think it was you.\"\nYou kiss {missing_obj} in the dream. The dark, around you, is patient. The door, from this side, is patient too.\nYou wait. {missing_pron_cap} is coming. You wait.","choices":[]}]},"story/chapter_15.json":{"scenes":[{"id":"side_inn_arrival","chapter":"SIDE · THE WAYHOUSE WIDOW","text":"The wayhouse leans into the wind like an old woman bracing for cold. Smoke threads from a crooked chimney. A painted sign — a kettle, a moon — creaks on one nail.\n\nInside smells of barley and rosemary and something older: lavender pressed flat between book pages.\n\nA woman in widow-grey looks up from the hearth. Her hair is the color of river stones. Her eyes are tired in a way sleep does not cure.\n\n\"Sit,\" she says. \"You walked the wastes road. That earns a bowl.\"\n\nShe ladles stew into a wooden bowl before you can answer. Two coins sit on the table beside an empty chair, as if someone left them and never came back.\n\nWhat draws your eye first?","choices":[{"text":"The empty chair across from you.","next":"side_inn_chair"},{"text":"The widow herself, gently.","next":"side_inn_widow_talk"},{"text":"Eat first. Talk later.","next":"side_inn_eat","do":["trust:+2"]}]},{"id":"side_inn_eat","chapter":"SIDE · THE WAYHOUSE WIDOW","text":"You eat. The stew is good in the way only a stranger's stew can be — salt and care and the suggestion that someone, somewhere, wants you to live through tomorrow.\n\nThe widow watches you the way one watches a fire one isn't sure will catch.\n\n\"You're going to the Gate,\" she says. Not a question.\n\nYou look up. Steam curls between you.\n\n\"Most who stop here are. Few come back this way.\"\n\nHer hand strays to her apron pocket. Something there — folded, soft, much-touched. She catches you noticing and folds her hands over it like a moth she doesn't want to crush.\n\nWhat do you say?","choices":[{"text":"\"You lost someone to it too.\"","next":"side_inn_widow_talk"},{"text":"\"What's in your pocket?\"","next":"side_inn_letter_offer"},{"text":"Finish quietly. Leave coin.","next":"side_inn_leave_kind","do":["gold:-3","trust:+3"]}]},{"id":"side_inn_chair","chapter":"SIDE · THE WAYHOUSE WIDOW","text":"The chair is worn smooth at the arms. Someone sat there many evenings. Someone leaned on the left side more than the right.\n\n\"My husband's,\" the widow says, without looking up. \"Forty winters in that chair. Forty-one if you count the year I kept setting his bowl out after.\"\n\nShe says it plainly. Not asking for pity. Just naming a true thing the way you name a star.\n\n\"He went through the Gate. Long before your grandmother was a girl. He was a King's Hunter, then. They sent six. One came back without a tongue. The rest — \" she shrugs at the chair.\n\nThe firelight finds the edge of something pale in her apron pocket.\n\nDo you ask?","choices":[{"text":"\"What's in your pocket?\"","next":"side_inn_letter_offer"},{"text":"\"I'm sorry. Truly.\"","next":"side_inn_widow_talk","do":["trust:+2"]},{"text":"Eat in silence with her.","next":"side_inn_eat"}]},{"id":"side_inn_widow_talk","chapter":"SIDE · THE WAYHOUSE WIDOW","text":"\"His name was Owin,\" she says, and the name comes out the way names do when they've been polished by forty years of saying them only to yourself.\n\n\"He told me, the morning he left, that he'd write. That he'd send word from the other side, if there was an other side. That if I didn't read his letter, it meant he was still coming home.\"\n\nShe takes the folded paper from her pocket. The fold is so old it has gone soft as cloth.\n\n\"I never read it. For forty years, I never read it. Because if I read it, then he's truly gone.\"\n\nShe sets it on the table between you. Her hand stays on it.\n\n\"My eyes are bad now. The ink's faded. I couldn't read it if I tried. But you — \"\n\nWhat do you say?","choices":[{"text":"\"I'll read it for you.\" (Need seeing draught)","next":"side_inn_letter_read","require":"seeing_draught"},{"text":"\"You should read it yourself. When you're ready.\"","next":"side_inn_letter_decline","do":["trust:+3"]},{"text":"\"I can't. I'm sorry.\"","next":"side_inn_leave_kind"}]},{"id":"side_inn_letter_offer","chapter":"SIDE · THE WAYHOUSE WIDOW","text":"The widow looks at you a long moment. Then she lays the letter on the table the way one lays a small bird that might still be alive.\n\n\"My husband wrote it. The night before the Gate. I have carried it in my apron for forty years and never broken the seal.\"\n\nHer voice does not shake. That is somehow worse than shaking.\n\n\"I told myself: as long as I haven't read it, he could still be coming back. As long as the wax holds, the door is open.\"\n\nShe slides it half an inch toward you.\n\n\"My eyes are too old now. The ink's gone ghost. But if you have a way — \"\n\nDo you?","choices":[{"text":"Use the seeing draught.","next":"side_inn_letter_read","require":"seeing_draught"},{"text":"\"I'd need a draught to make it legible. I don't have one.\"","next":"side_inn_letter_decline"},{"text":"\"Keep it sealed. He'd want it that way.\"","next":"side_inn_letter_decline","do":["trust:+2"]}]},{"id":"side_inn_letter_read","chapter":"SIDE · THE WAYHOUSE WIDOW","text":"You uncork the seeing draught. Bitter copper on the tongue. The world sharpens — the grain of the table, the dust in the firelight, the ghost-pale ink that thickens, suddenly, into letters.\n\nYou break the seal. It crumbles like a moth wing.\n\nMaren, the letter begins. If you are reading this, I did not come back. Do not mourn me long. I want you to know I went in afraid and the fear was bearable because I had been loved by you. That is the trick of it — being loved makes the dark less wide. Set a bowl for me a year, and then no more. Marry the baker if he asks. He has been asking for thirty years and I have been pretending not to notice. Forgive me for going. Forgive yourself for staying. — Owin.\n\nThe widow does not cry. Her face goes very still, like a pond when the wind stops.\n\n\"The baker,\" she breathes, \"the baker,\" and a sound that is not quite laughter slips out of her.\n\nWhat now?","choices":[{"text":"Sit with her until she's ready.","next":"side_inn_after_letter","do":["trust:+5","rem_item:seeing_draught","journal:Read a widow's forty-year letter. Owin says: being loved makes the dark less wide."]}]},{"id":"side_inn_letter_decline","chapter":"SIDE · THE WAYHOUSE WIDOW","text":"She nods slowly, the way people nod when relief and disappointment arrive at the same door.\n\n\"Maybe that's right. Maybe it stays sealed.\"\n\nShe tucks it back into her apron, where it will go on being a door not opened.\n\nShe refills your bowl without asking. The stew is somehow better the second time, like she ladled some unspoken thing into it.\n\n\"Take this with you,\" she says, and presses something small and stoppered into your palm. \"Against bad dreams. The kind the road gives.\"\n\nIt's a warding draught — small, amber, faintly sloshing.\n\n\"Go on. The light's burning while we sit.\"\n\nWill you?","choices":[{"text":"Take it. Thank her. Go.","next":"side_inn_leave_kind","do":["add_item:warding_draught","journal:A widow gave me a draught against bad dreams. I did not earn it. I will carry it anyway."]}]},{"id":"side_inn_after_letter","chapter":"SIDE · THE WAYHOUSE WIDOW","text":"You sit with her until the fire goes low. She does not speak for a long time. Then she stands up and does an astonishing thing: she takes the chair — Owin's chair — and turns it to face the door instead of the table.\n\n\"There,\" she says. \"Now it's facing out. So whoever sits in it next is leaving, not waiting.\"\n\nShe presses two things into your hands: a small purse of coin and a stoppered draught the color of dawn.\n\n\"One for the road. One for the dark. Don't argue.\"\n\nAt the door she catches your sleeve.\n\n\"If you find a boy — if you find any of them — tell them their people set bowls out. Tell them we kept the chairs.\"\n\nWill you?","choices":[{"text":"\"I'll tell them. I promise.\"","next":"side_inn_leave_kind","do":["gold:+10","add_item:warding_draught","trust:+5","flag:widow_promise","journal:Promised a widow to tell the lost: their people kept the chairs."]}]},{"id":"side_inn_leave_kind","chapter":"SIDE · THE WAYHOUSE WIDOW","text":"Outside, the wind has changed. It feels less like a hand pushing you back and more like one at your shoulder, steady.\n\nYou look back once. The widow stands in her doorway, small and grey and somehow taller than she was an hour ago. She lifts a hand.\n\nYou lift yours.\n\nThe road is the road again. But the cold in your chest has shifted half a step toward warm.\n\nWhat carries you onward?","choices":[{"text":"The promise of the Gate. Walk on.","next":"wastes_alone"},{"text":"Rejoin the caravan track.","next":"caravan_foothills"}]},{"id":"side_captain_hut","chapter":"SIDE · THE RETIRED CAPTAIN","text":"The hut squats in a fold of the foothills like a stone that decided to be a house. Smoke. A goat. An axe split into a stump, left there as if its owner walked away mid-swing and never came back.\n\nA man sits on the step. Grey beard, grey eyes, a shoulder that doesn't move quite right. A King's Hunter's badge tarnished green hangs on a nail by the door, half-hidden behind a drying onion.\n\nHe watches you climb without standing.\n\n\"If you're lost,\" he says, \"the road's back that way. If you're here to recruit me, the road's also back that way. Quicker.\"\n\nA dog the size of a small bear sleeps at his boots and does not bother to look up.\n\nWhat do you say?","choices":[{"text":"\"I'm not recruiting. I'm thirsty.\"","next":"side_captain_water"},{"text":"\"I know who you were.\"","next":"side_captain_seen"},{"text":"\"Your axe is stuck.\"","next":"side_captain_axe"}]},{"id":"side_captain_water","chapter":"SIDE · THE RETIRED CAPTAIN","text":"He grunts. Jerks his thumb at a barrel by the door. \"Ladle's clean. Goat's not.\"\n\nThe water is so cold it hurts your teeth. You drink twice. He watches without watching, the way old soldiers watch.\n\n\"Where you bound?\" he asks at last.\n\nYou don't answer in words. You don't need to. He sees it in your jaw, your boots, the weight you carry on the left side from a weapon you haven't drawn yet.\n\n\"The Gate.\" Flat. \"For the King's child.\"\n\nThe word child opens something in his face for half a heartbeat, and then he closes it again, with practice.\n\nDo you press?","choices":[{"text":"\"You lost someone to it.\"","next":"side_captain_son"},{"text":"\"I need a hand for one fight. Just one.\"","next":"side_captain_ask_early"},{"text":"Let it lie. Thank him for the water.","next":"side_captain_leave_soft","do":["trust:+2"]}]},{"id":"side_captain_seen","chapter":"SIDE · THE RETIRED CAPTAIN","text":"The dog opens one eye.\n\nThe captain does not move, but something in him goes very still, the way a wolf goes still before it decides whether to run or bite.\n\n\"Then you know to go away.\"\n\n\"I know your men called you Old Iron. I know you led the last Hunt that came back whole. I know you trained the badge that hangs by your door.\"\n\nA long silence. The wind moves in the long grass.\n\n\"Whole,\" he repeats. Quiet. \"We didn't come back whole. We came back counting.\"\n\nHe looks at you properly for the first time.\n\n\"Sit, if you're sitting. Don't sit, if you're going. I haven't the patience for the middle of things.\"\n\nWhat do you do?","choices":[{"text":"Sit. \"Tell me about the Hunt.\"","next":"side_captain_son","do":["trust:+3"]},{"text":"\"I need a hand for one fight.\"","next":"side_captain_ask_early"},{"text":"Go. Leave him his quiet.","next":"side_captain_leave_soft"}]},{"id":"side_captain_axe","chapter":"SIDE · THE RETIRED CAPTAIN","text":"\"It's been stuck three years,\" he says. \"It can be stuck three more.\"\n\nBut he is watching you to see if you'll try.\n\nYou set your shoulder, brace your foot against the stump, and rock the haft. It comes loose with a soft sucking sound, like something that didn't want to be free and is.\n\nYou hand it back to him handle-first.\n\nHe takes it. He looks at the blade. He looks at you. The dog, astonishingly, sits up.\n\n\"Huh,\" the captain says.\n\nThen, quieter: \"Sit, traveller. I've a stew. It's mostly onion. But it's hot.\"\n\nWill you?","choices":[{"text":"Sit. Eat. Listen.","next":"side_captain_son","do":["trust:+4"]}]},{"id":"side_captain_son","chapter":"SIDE · THE RETIRED CAPTAIN","text":"The stew is mostly onion. It is also somehow the second-best meal you have had this week.\n\n\"My boy went through,\" the captain says, eyes on the fire. \"Eleven years back. Not a Hunter — too young. He went on his own. Climbed the wall, took my second sword, walked to the arch. He'd heard his mother crying about a cousin lost the year before. Thought he could fix it.\"\n\nHe stirs the pot once.\n\n\"Eleven. Eleven years old.\"\n\nThe dog rests its head on his boot.\n\n\"I sat here three years thinking I'd go after him. Then five years thinking I was too old. Then this — \" he taps the stiff shoulder \" — and I stopped pretending.\"\n\nHe looks at you.\n\n\"Why are you really at my door?\"","choices":[{"text":"\"I think your boy might still be in there.\"","next":"side_captain_hope","do":["trust:+4"]},{"text":"\"I need someone who's been to the edge of it.\"","next":"side_captain_ask_full"},{"text":"\"I don't know. I just walked uphill until I saw smoke.\"","next":"side_captain_ask_full","do":["trust:+2"]}]},{"id":"side_captain_hope","chapter":"SIDE · THE RETIRED CAPTAIN","text":"His hand goes very still on the spoon.\n\n\"Don't,\" he says, soft. \"Don't say that lightly. I have buried that boy a thousand times in my head and put him back together a thousand more. Don't open it again unless you mean it.\"\n\nYou meet his eye.\n\n\"The Keeper takes children. He keeps them. Some of them are still children, on the other side. Time runs strange there. If your boy was eleven when he went, he may still be eleven.\"\n\nThe captain sets the spoon down with the care of a man defusing a thing that might kill him.\n\n\"What's his name?\" you ask.\n\n\"Halric.\" The name comes out cracked.\n\nThen, quieter: \"What do you need.\"\n\nThis is the moment. What do you ask?","choices":[{"text":"\"Your arm beside mine for one fight.\"","next":"side_captain_yes"},{"text":"\"Your second sword. The one Halric didn't take.\"","next":"side_captain_sword"},{"text":"\"Both. If you'll give them.\"","next":"side_captain_yes","require":"trust:60"}]},{"id":"side_captain_ask_early","chapter":"SIDE · THE RETIRED CAPTAIN","text":"He laughs without smiling. \"One fight. They always say one fight. Then it's one more, and one more, and then it's a grave on a hill with no name.\"\n\nHe shakes his head. \"You don't know me well enough yet, traveller. And I don't know you. Come back when we've shared bread. Or don't come back. Either's honest.\"\n\nThe dog sighs in agreement.\n\nWhat do you do?","choices":[{"text":"Stay. Share the stew.","next":"side_captain_son","do":["trust:+2"]},{"text":"Go. Walk on uphill.","next":"side_captain_leave_soft"}]},{"id":"side_captain_ask_full","chapter":"SIDE · THE RETIRED CAPTAIN","text":"He nods slowly. Not yes. Just acknowledgement.\n\n\"I'll think on it,\" he says. \"Walk the ridge tonight. If you're still alive in the morning, come back. If you don't come back, I'll know what I'd have been walking into.\"\n\nIt is the closest thing to a kindness an old soldier knows how to offer.\n\nWill you?","choices":[{"text":"\"Tomorrow, then.\" Walk the ridge.","next":"side_captain_yes","require":"trust:60"},{"text":"\"Tomorrow, then.\" Walk the ridge.","next":"side_captain_leave_soft"}]},{"id":"side_captain_yes","chapter":"SIDE · THE RETIRED CAPTAIN","text":"He stands. Slow. The stiff shoulder protests; he ignores it the way he has ignored it for years.\n\n\"One fight,\" he says. \"That's the price. One fight, one road. After that, if I'm still upright, I come home to my dog.\"\n\nHe takes a long, narrow bundle from above the door and unwraps it. Inside: a sword. Plain steel. The pommel is wrapped in faded blue cord — a child's hand-knot, the kind a boy ties when he's practicing.\n\n\"Halric's wrap,\" the captain says. \"He did it for me before — \" he waves the rest away. \"It'll cut what needs cutting.\"\n\nHe whistles low. The dog stands.\n\n\"Lead on, traveller. Old Iron rides one more time.\"\n\nWhat do you say?","choices":[{"text":"\"Thank you, Captain.\"","next":"side_captain_join","do":["add_item:sword","max_hp:+2","trust:+5","flag:captain_pledged","journal:Old Iron pledged his sword for one fight. Halric's blue cord on the pommel."]}]},{"id":"side_captain_sword","chapter":"SIDE · THE RETIRED CAPTAIN","text":"He nods once, like a door closing on something gentler.\n\n\"That I can do. That doesn't ask me to leave my dog.\"\n\nHe goes inside. Comes back with a long, narrow bundle. Unwraps it on the step.\n\nThe sword inside is plain steel, well-kept. The pommel is wound in faded blue cord — a child's knot.\n\n\"Halric tied that. Before he left. Said I needed something pretty.\" The captain's mouth twitches. \"It's not pretty. But the knot held.\"\n\nHe presses it into your hands.\n\n\"Take it where I won't. Bring it back if you can. Bring my boy back if you can. Bring neither if you can't.\"\n\nWill you?","choices":[{"text":"\"I'll try, Captain.\"","next":"side_captain_leave_soft","do":["add_item:sword","trust:+4","journal:Old Iron gave me his son's sword. The blue cord was tied by an eleven-year-old."]}]},{"id":"side_captain_join","chapter":"SIDE · THE RETIRED CAPTAIN","text":"You walk down the hill side by side, the captain and you and the dog at his heel. He does not speak. Old soldiers don't, when they've just agreed to something they swore off years ago.\n\nAt the bend, he stops. Looks back at the hut. The smoke. The chair on the step.\n\nThen he turns his back on it.\n\n\"Right,\" he says. \"Where's this fight?\"\n\nWhich road?","choices":[{"text":"Onward through the hills.","next":"hill_search"},{"text":"Back toward the wastes road.","next":"wastes_ride"}]},{"id":"side_captain_leave_soft","chapter":"SIDE · THE RETIRED CAPTAIN","text":"At the bend you look back. The captain is standing on his step with his hand on the dog's head, watching you go.\n\nHe lifts a hand. Just once. Soldier's farewell.\n\nYou lift yours.\n\nThe hut grows small. The hill grows large.\n\nWhere now?","choices":[{"text":"Onward through the hills.","next":"hill_search"},{"text":"Back toward the wastes road.","next":"wastes_ride"}]},{"id":"side_minstrel_1","chapter":"SIDE · THE MINSTREL","text":"Past a bent thorn, you hear it before you see it: a lute, badly tuned, played beautifully anyway.\n\nA figure sits on a milestone, one boot off, the other dangling. Patched cloak, hair the color of old brass, a smile that is too easy for this road.\n\n\"Traveller!\" they call, without looking up. \"You're the third today. Two of you are real. One of you is a ghost wearing a real one's coat. Care to guess which?\"\n\nThey strum a chord. Hum. Then a verse drifts out, quiet as a held breath:\n\nThe Keeper keeps a door of stone,\nA door that no one shuts.\nHe takes the brave, he takes the bone,\nHe takes the loved, the lost, the known —\nBut never takes the nuts.\n\nThey grin at you. \"Last line's a joke. I add it so I don't cry. Verse one of seven. Want to hear the rest? You'll need to find me again. The road's long.\"\n\nWhat do you do?","choices":[{"text":"\"Why the nuts?\"","next":"side_minstrel_1b","do":["trust:+2"]},{"text":"\"Sing me another verse now.\"","next":"side_minstrel_1c"},{"text":"Toss them a coin and walk on.","next":"side_minstrel_1_leave","do":["gold:-1"]},{"text":"Walk on without coin.","next":"side_minstrel_1_leave"}]},{"id":"side_minstrel_1b","chapter":"SIDE · THE MINSTREL","text":"\"Because,\" the minstrel says, pulling the boot back on, \"the Keeper does take the nuts. Eventually. He takes everything. The joke is that everyone listens to the song and assumes there's one thing he won't touch.\"\n\nThey wink. \"There isn't.\"\n\nThe lute strums again, softer.\n\nHe stands in a dark older than night,\nHe wears a crown of listening.\nHe does not eat, he does not fight —\nHe simply waits, and waits, and writes\nThe names of those he's missing.\n\nA chill, fine as silk, lays itself across the back of your neck.\n\n\"Verse two,\" they say. \"On the house. You look like you needed it. The rest, you'll have to come find.\"\n\nWhere will the road take you?","choices":[{"text":"Onward. Mark the minstrel in memory.","next":"side_minstrel_1_leave","do":["flag:minstrel_v1","flag:minstrel_v2","journal:The Keeper wears a crown of listening. He writes the names of those he is missing."]}]},{"id":"side_minstrel_1c","chapter":"SIDE · THE MINSTREL","text":"\"Eager! I like eager. Eager dies first but laughing.\"\n\nThe lute again. A new verse:\n\nHe stands in a dark older than night,\nHe wears a crown of listening.\nHe does not eat, he does not fight —\nHe simply waits, and waits, and writes\nThe names of those he's missing.\n\nThey watch you over the lute.\n\n\"That one's for free. The rest cost. Not coin — meetings. The song wants to be sung in pieces. Find me in the city. Find me at the threshold. I'll be there. I'm always there. Singing's all I'm good for.\"\n\nDo you press for more?","choices":[{"text":"\"Wait — what does 'crown of listening' mean?\"","next":"side_minstrel_1d","do":["flag:minstrel_v1","flag:minstrel_v2"]},{"text":"Walk on, marked.","next":"side_minstrel_1_leave","do":["flag:minstrel_v1","flag:minstrel_v2","journal:The Keeper wears a crown of listening."]}]},{"id":"side_minstrel_1d","chapter":"SIDE · THE MINSTREL","text":"\"It means he doesn't speak,\" the minstrel says, and for the first time their voice has no music in it. \"He listens. He listens to every wish, every promise, every name a mother whispers over a cradle. He hears the ones that aren't kept. He keeps the ones that aren't heard.\"\n\nThey turn the peg, retune the string.\n\n\"That's all I'll say without the next two verses paid for in meetings. Off with you. I have other roads to ruin.\"\n\nWhere now?","choices":[{"text":"Onward.","next":"side_minstrel_1_leave","do":["journal:The Keeper listens for promises that were not kept."]}]},{"id":"side_minstrel_1_leave","chapter":"SIDE · THE MINSTREL","text":"You walk on. Behind you the lute drifts up, lazy and gold, and then is gone.\n\nThe wind picks up. The milestone behind you stands alone, as if there was never a minstrel on it at all.\n\nThe road waits.","choices":[{"text":"Continue on the wastes road.","next":"wastes_ride"}]},{"id":"side_minstrel_2","chapter":"SIDE · THE MINSTREL","text":"You hear the lute before you see the alley. Same lute. Same hands. Different city.\n\nThe minstrel is sitting on an overturned crate in a pool of late sun, surrounded by three children and a one-eared cat. They wave you over without breaking the chord.\n\n\"Look who survived! The road kept you. The road keeps fewer than it lets go these days.\"\n\nThe children scatter, giggling, when you arrive. The cat does not.\n\n\"Verse three and four,\" the minstrel says. \"That's what you're here for, isn't it? You didn't seek me out. You walked past three taverns to get to this alley. The song's in your blood now. That's how it works.\"\n\nThey strum.\n\nDo you sit?","choices":[{"text":"Sit. Listen.","next":"side_minstrel_2b","require":"flag:minstrel_v1"},{"text":"Sit. Listen.","next":"side_minstrel_2b"},{"text":"\"I haven't time.\"","next":"side_minstrel_2_leave"}]},{"id":"side_minstrel_2b","chapter":"SIDE · THE MINSTREL","text":"The minstrel sings, quieter now, leaning close so only you can hear:\n\nHe was a boy once, long ago,\nHe loved a girl who loved the snow.\nShe drowned beneath a frozen river,\nHe asked the dark to bring her — \nThe dark said yes. The dark said: enter.\nHe entered. And forgot her.\n\nA long pause. The cat watches you.\n\nAnd every child he takes since then\nIs her — he thinks. He thinks again.\nHe cannot quite remember when\nHe stopped remembering her name.\nThat is the wound. That is the game.\n\nThe minstrel sets the lute down.\n\n\"He's looking for a girl who drowned a thousand years ago. Every child is her, and never her. That's why he can't stop.\"\n\nYou cannot speak.","choices":[{"text":"\"What was her name?\"","next":"side_minstrel_2c","do":["flag:minstrel_v3","flag:minstrel_v4","journal:The Keeper was a boy who lost a girl to a frozen river. He has forgotten her name. That is his wound."]}]},{"id":"side_minstrel_2c","chapter":"SIDE · THE MINSTREL","text":"The minstrel smiles, gentle.\n\n\"Don't know. Nobody does. That's the song. That's the point. If anyone remembered her name and said it to him — \" they whistle, low \" — well. We'll see.\"\n\nThey pick up the lute again.\n\n\"Last verse is at the threshold. Don't be late. The song doesn't keep.\"\n\nThe cat stretches, yawns, and walks off down the alley as if it has somewhere to be.\n\nThe minstrel is gone before you stand.\n\nWhere now?","choices":[{"text":"Back into the city.","next":"side_minstrel_2_leave"}]},{"id":"side_minstrel_2_leave","chapter":"SIDE · THE MINSTREL","text":"You step out of the alley into the noise of the city. A baker's bell. A child crying. A guard whistling something almost like the minstrel's tune and not quite getting it right.\n\nThe song stays under your ribs.","choices":[{"text":"On.","next":"city_lost"}]},{"id":"side_minstrel_3","chapter":"SIDE · THE MINSTREL","text":"At the threshold of the Hollow Gate, where the stone arch hums against your teeth, you do not expect music.\n\nBut there they are. The minstrel. Sitting cross-legged on a rock that should not have grown grass and has anyway, lute in their lap, hair lit silver by a sky that is not sky.\n\n\"I told you I'd be here,\" they say. They look smaller. Or the world is larger. One of those.\n\n\"Last verse. Then I go home and sleep for a year. Ready?\"\n\nThey don't wait for an answer.","choices":[{"text":"\"Sing it.\"","next":"side_minstrel_3b","require":"flag:minstrel_v3"},{"text":"\"Sing it.\"","next":"side_minstrel_3b"}]},{"id":"side_minstrel_3b","chapter":"SIDE · THE MINSTREL","text":"He cannot bear the sound of her name.\nHe will not hear it spoken plain.\nIf one should say it true and clear\nThe dark would crack, the door would shear,\nHe'd remember why he came —\nAnd then he'd be a boy again.\n\nThe minstrel stops. Looks at you. The lute falls quiet.\n\n\"Find her name. Say it. He's been keeping the door open looking for her. Close the wound, close the door.\"\n\nYou stare at them.\n\n\"I don't know her name,\" you whisper.\n\n\"No,\" they agree. \"But someone in there does. He's written it down. He's written it down a thousand times and not known why. Look for the writing.\"\n\nA wind moves through that does not move the grass. The minstrel is gone, between one blink and the next, leaving only the lute, which is also gone a moment later, as if it followed.","choices":[{"text":"On. To the Gate.","next":"threshold_1","do":["flag:minstrel_v5","flag:keeper_weakness","journal:The Keeper's weakness: a girl's name he has forgotten. He has written it down a thousand times. Look for the writing."]}]},{"id":"side_graveyard_gate","chapter":"SIDE · THE GRAVEYARD","text":"Beyond the city's south wall, where the road forgets to be a road, the King's Yard begins. Yew trees so old they have hollowed at the trunk. Stone faces eaten by lichen. Names worn down to suggestions.\n\nThe gate is iron and half-fallen. A sign nailed to the post: KING'S HUNTERS, FALLEN AT THE GATE. Below it, smaller, the words We did not come back. We did not stop being yours.\n\nThe air here is colder than the air outside the wall. Mist moves around the stones in a way that has nothing to do with wind.\n\nDo you enter?","choices":[{"text":"Step through the gate.","next":"side_graveyard_first"},{"text":"Walk the wall instead.","next":"side_graveyard_leave"}]},{"id":"side_graveyard_first","chapter":"SIDE · THE GRAVEYARD","text":"The first stone you stop at is small — barely knee-high. A woman is sitting on it. Or near it. The way she catches the mist tells you she is not quite touching the ground.\n\nShe is dressed in old livery. A Hunter's sash, faded. Her hands are folded in her lap as if waiting for tea.\n\n\"You smell of the road,\" she says, and her voice is the rustle of yew leaves. \"You smell of where I went. Don't sit. Sitting is for the dead.\"\n\nShe smiles. It is a kind smile, which is somehow worse.\n\n\"My name was Tess. I went through eighty winters ago. Never came back. You'll meet a few of us. Don't be frightened. We don't bite. We've forgotten how.\"\n\nWhat do you say?","choices":[{"text":"\"What's it like, beyond?\"","next":"side_graveyard_tess"},{"text":"\"How did you fail?\"","next":"side_graveyard_tess_fail"},{"text":"\"Are there others I should speak with?\"","next":"side_graveyard_walk"}]},{"id":"side_graveyard_tess","chapter":"SIDE · THE GRAVEYARD","text":"Tess tilts her head. Mist follows the motion a half-beat late.\n\n\"It is quiet. That is the first thing. Quieter than any place that has ever held a sound. The Keeper does not speak. The taken do not speak — they listen. You forget what your own voice sounds like, after a time.\"\n\nShe looks at her folded hands.\n\n\"It is also kinder than they say. The taken are not hurt. They are held. That is the awful part. They are held so gently that they forget they were ever going somewhere.\"\n\nShe lifts her eyes.\n\n\"If you go after a child — make noise. Sing. Stamp. Curse. Anything. The silence is what takes them. Not the dark.\"\n\nWhat do you ask?","choices":[{"text":"\"How do I bring one back?\"","next":"side_graveyard_tess_fail"},{"text":"\"Are there others here?\"","next":"side_graveyard_walk","do":["flag:hunter_lore","trust:+5","journal:A dead Hunter named Tess: 'The silence is what takes them. Not the dark. Make noise.'"]}]},{"id":"side_graveyard_tess_fail","chapter":"SIDE · THE GRAVEYARD","text":"She lowers her gaze. The mist around her thickens, then thins.\n\n\"I went in shouting,\" she says. \"I was very brave. I had a sword and a sash and a head full of songs about myself.\"\n\nShe smiles, sadly.\n\n\"The Keeper did not fight me. He listened to me. He listened until I had nothing left to say. Then I was quiet, like the rest. Then I forgot why I came. I think I was there a long time. I think I was there a short time. I do not know which.\"\n\nShe looks up.\n\n\"Don't go in alone. Don't go in without a name to say aloud. Don't go in believing you are the hero. The Keeper feeds on heroes. The Keeper has eaten heroes for centuries.\"\n\nWhere do you walk?","choices":[{"text":"Deeper among the stones.","next":"side_graveyard_walk","do":["flag:hunter_lore","trust:+5","journal:Tess: Do not go in believing you are the hero. He feeds on heroes."]}]},{"id":"side_graveyard_walk","chapter":"SIDE · THE GRAVEYARD","text":"You walk deeper. Other figures begin to gather along the path — never blocking it, only present. A man with a broken pike. A girl with leaves in her hair. A grey-bearded captain who nods at you the way captains nod at recruits.\n\nNone speak. They watch you the way grown things watch a sapling stand straight in the wind.\n\nAt the end of the path is a tall stone, taller than the rest. The name on it is half-eaten by moss but still legible:\n\nHALWYN OF THE SECOND HOUSE — DIED AT THE GATE — GRANDFATHER TO HEIRS YET UNBORN.\n\nA figure stands beside this stone. Tall. Crowned with what looks, for a heartbeat, like antlers — and then is only a circlet, only a man.","choices":[{"text":"Approach the tall stone.","next":"side_graveyard_grandfather"}]},{"id":"side_graveyard_grandfather","chapter":"SIDE · THE GRAVEYARD","text":"He turns his head as you come close. His eyes are the colour of pond water in spring.\n\n\"I know your house,\" he says. \"Or your house's house. I am Halwyn. I was {other_name}'s great-grandfather, four generations back. I went through the Gate when my own daughter was taken. I did not bring her back.\"\n\nHis voice is the sound of an empty hall.\n\n\"I have stood by this stone a long time, waiting for the next of mine. I did not expect the next of mine to come here for a sibling. I did not expect there to be two lost at once.\"\n\nHe lifts a hand. His sleeve falls back. The arm beneath is mostly mist.\n\n\"Tell me, child of my line — who is lost?\"","choices":[{"text":"\"{missing_name}. The Keeper took {missing_obj}.\"","next":"side_graveyard_g2"},{"text":"\"{other_name}. The witch's wood took {other_obj}.\"","next":"side_graveyard_g2b"},{"text":"\"Both. Both are lost.\"","next":"side_graveyard_g2c"}]},{"id":"side_graveyard_g2","chapter":"SIDE · THE GRAVEYARD","text":"He nods, very slowly.\n\n\"The Keeper. The same Keeper. Always the same Keeper. He has been there longer than my name has been spoken aloud.\"\n\nHe looks at you a long moment.\n\n\"You are not the first of our blood to walk to the arch. You may be the first who matters. There is a thing my daughter said, the night before she was taken — she said the dark answered her when she asked it questions. The dark in the arch has ears. Speak to it as if it is a person. It is. It was. It forgot, but it was.\"\n\nWhat do you say?","choices":[{"text":"\"What else did she say?\"","next":"side_graveyard_g3"},{"text":"\"Will you walk with me to the Gate?\"","next":"side_graveyard_walks"}]},{"id":"side_graveyard_g2b","chapter":"SIDE · THE GRAVEYARD","text":"The grandfather closes his eyes.\n\n\"The wood. The wood does not take. The wood changes. If {other_name} wears antlers now, {other_pron} is still {other_name}. The shape is not the soul. Remember that. Many of our line did not remember and so failed both their lost ones.\"\n\nHe opens his eyes.\n\n\"There is a thing my daughter said, the night before she was taken — she said the dark answered her when she asked it questions. Both your lost ones are behind doors that have ears.\"\n\nWhat do you ask?","choices":[{"text":"\"What else did she say?\"","next":"side_graveyard_g3"},{"text":"\"Will you walk with me to the Gate?\"","next":"side_graveyard_walks"}]},{"id":"side_graveyard_g2c","chapter":"SIDE · THE GRAVEYARD","text":"Halwyn bows his head.\n\n\"Both. Then you carry what no one of our line has carried before. The wood and the arch are old enemies and old lovers. They were one place once. They were sundered.\"\n\nHe looks up.\n\n\"My daughter said the dark answered her when she asked it questions. The wood answers too, in its way. Speak to both. Demand neither. Ours is not a line that demands; ours is a line that asks.\"\n\nWhat do you say?","choices":[{"text":"\"What else did she say?\"","next":"side_graveyard_g3"},{"text":"\"Will you walk with me to the Gate?\"","next":"side_graveyard_walks"}]},{"id":"side_graveyard_g3","chapter":"SIDE · THE GRAVEYARD","text":"He thinks. Mist moves through his beard.\n\n\"She said the dark behind the arch was grieving. That was her word. Grieving, not hungry. She said it the way a healer says a sickness, not the way a soldier says an enemy.\"\n\nHe meets your eye.\n\n\"Take that with you. Grief is something we know. Hunger is something we don't.\"\n\nHis form is thinning. The mist is reclaiming him.\n\n\"Go, child of my line. We watch from here. We cannot help, but we will not look away.\"","choices":[{"text":"\"Thank you, grandfather.\"","next":"side_graveyard_leave","do":["flag:hunter_lore","flag:grandfather_lore","trust:+5","journal:My ancestor Halwyn: the dark in the arch is GRIEVING, not hungry. Speak to it like a person. It was, once."]}]},{"id":"side_graveyard_walks","chapter":"SIDE · THE GRAVEYARD","text":"He smiles, the way the dead smile — with the corners of the mouth, but not the eyes, because the eyes have already gone elsewhere.\n\n\"I cannot leave the stones, child. The stones hold us. We hold the stones. That is the bargain.\"\n\nHe lifts a hand. The mist at his shoulder takes the shape of an old crest for half a heartbeat — your crest, in older lines.\n\n\"But we will watch. Every one of us. When you reach the arch, look up. The wind will move a way it does not move for other travellers. That will be us.\"\n\nHe begins to fade.","choices":[{"text":"\"I will look up.\"","next":"side_graveyard_leave","do":["flag:hunter_lore","flag:grandfather_lore","trust:+5","journal:At the arch, look up. The wind will move strangely. That is my line, watching."]}]},{"id":"side_graveyard_leave","chapter":"SIDE · THE GRAVEYARD","text":"You step back through the iron gate. The cold lifts from your shoulders the way a heavy coat lifts.\n\nBehind you, the yard is only yew trees and stones and mist. As it should be. As it has been for centuries.\n\nThe city's noise reaches you again. A baker's bell. A guard's whistle. A child laughing in a courtyard you cannot see.\n\nThe wind shifts, briefly, against the way it should. You think of an old man's hand lifting.","choices":[{"text":"Into the city.","next":"city_lost"},{"text":"Back to the barracks.","next":"barracks"}]},{"id":"side_pell_rumor","chapter":"SIDE · THE LOST CHILD","text":"It is in the market that you hear it first. A fishmonger's wife, three baskets down, telling another woman:\n\n\"— the one with the chipped tooth, yeah? Half my age and twice my mouth. Eating bread backwards in the alley behind the chandler's. Backwards! From the crust in. I said, boy, you've been gone, and he said who's gone, missus, I'm right here.\"\n\nThe other woman laughs. The fishmonger's wife shakes her head.\n\n\"Pell, his name was. They said the Gate took him last spring. They were wrong, or he came back. Skin's grey, mind. Won't say where he's been.\"\n\nYou go still. Pell. Juno's Pell.","choices":[{"text":"\"Where is the chandler's alley?\"","next":"side_pell_alley"},{"text":"Buy a fish to thank her. Ask gently.","next":"side_pell_alley","do":["gold:-1","trust:+2"]}]},{"id":"side_pell_alley","chapter":"SIDE · THE LOST CHILD","text":"The chandler's alley smells of beef tallow and old rain. A cat sits on a barrel like an officer. At the end of the alley, behind a stack of broken crates, a child is folded against the wall, knees up, eating the heel of a loaf.\n\nBackwards. From the crust in.\n\nHe is perhaps eight. He has a chipped front tooth that whistles when he breathes. His skin is the colour of paper left in a drawer. His eyes are very large and very dry, like they have forgotten how to make tears and are working it out again.\n\nHe sees you.\n\n\"You smell like the road,\" he says, polite. \"Hello, road person. I'm Pell. I think.\"\n\nWhat do you say?","choices":[{"text":"Kneel. \"Pell, your cousin Juno is looking for you.\"","next":"side_pell_juno"},{"text":"\"Where have you been?\"","next":"side_pell_been"},{"text":"Offer him your supplies.","next":"side_pell_feed","require":"supplies"}]},{"id":"side_pell_juno","chapter":"SIDE · THE LOST CHILD","text":"The name does something. Not a smile. A flicker. Like a coal pretending to be ash, deciding maybe to be a coal again.\n\n\"Juno,\" he says. Tastes it. \"Juno had a frog. The frog's name was Mister.\"\n\nHe blinks. A tear, exactly one, escapes the dry place and tracks down his cheek as if astonished to find itself there.\n\n\"Juno is real,\" he says, like he's been needing to know.\n\nHe is real. Real real. Skin too grey, breath too whistly, but the boy under it is in there.\n\n\"Will you take me to her?\" he asks. \"I would like to be taken to her. I don't remember the way. The Gate keeps the way.\"\n\nWhat do you do?","choices":[{"text":"\"I'll take you. Come on.\"","next":"side_pell_carry","do":["flag:pell_found","trust:+3"]},{"text":"\"First — tell me how you got out.\"","next":"side_pell_been"}]},{"id":"side_pell_been","chapter":"SIDE · THE LOST CHILD","text":"He tilts his head, considering.\n\n\"There was a quiet place,\" he says. \"It wasn't bad. There was a man who didn't have a face, exactly, but he had the shape of a face where one should be. He didn't hurt us. He just listened. He listened so hard I forgot what my voice sounded like.\"\n\nThe chipped tooth whistles.\n\n\"There were lots of us. Boys. Girls. Babies, even, but the babies didn't cry, which I thought was strange. We sat in a long room. The room was made of waiting.\"\n\nHe looks up.\n\n\"A door opened. I don't know why. The man wasn't looking. I walked out. I walked a long time. I came out behind the chandler's. The cat let me sleep in the alley. The cat is named Officer.\"\n\nHe pats the cat as if introducing you.\n\n\"Will you take me to Juno?\"","choices":[{"text":"\"Yes. Now.\"","next":"side_pell_carry","do":["flag:pell_found","flag:gate_lore_pell","trust:+3","journal:Pell escaped the Gate. He says: the Keeper has the SHAPE of a face. The room is made of waiting. A door opened — he doesn't know why."]}]},{"id":"side_pell_feed","chapter":"SIDE · THE LOST CHILD","text":"You crouch and pass him your supplies. He takes them carefully, as if afraid the food might leave again.\n\nHe eats slowly. Not ravenous — thoughtful. Like a person learning how eating works.\n\n\"This is real food,\" he says, mouth full. \"The food in there didn't taste of anything. It tasted of forgetting.\"\n\nHe looks at you and his eyes are slightly less dry.\n\n\"Are you a good road person? You feel like a good one. I would like a good one to take me to my cousin Juno.\"\n\nWill you?","choices":[{"text":"\"I'll take you.\"","next":"side_pell_carry","do":["rem_item:supplies","flag:pell_found","trust:+3"]}]},{"id":"side_pell_carry","chapter":"SIDE · THE LOST CHILD","text":"He is lighter than he should be. A child his age should weigh more. You think of the room made of waiting and have to look at the sky for a moment.\n\nThe cat — Officer — follows you for half a street, then sits down to supervise from a distance.\n\nPell talks the whole way. Not constantly — in small bursts, like a bird working out it can sing again.\n\n\"Juno is going to be cross with me,\" he murmurs against your shoulder. \"I borrowed her ribbon. I forgot to give it back. I think it's still in there. I'm sorry, road person, you might have to go back for the ribbon.\"\n\nWhere will you take him?","choices":[{"text":"To the caravan, where Juno waits.","next":"side_pell_juno_reunion"},{"text":"Find Juno in the city first.","next":"side_pell_juno_reunion"}]},{"id":"side_pell_juno_reunion","chapter":"SIDE · THE LOST CHILD","text":"Juno is sitting on a wagon-tail braiding rope when she sees you coming. Her hands stop. Her mouth opens. No sound.\n\nPell wriggles in your arms. \"Down,\" he says, polite. \"Down please, road person.\"\n\nYou set him on his feet. He walks the last six steps himself, slow and steady, like a small dignitary.\n\nJuno slides off the wagon and meets him in the middle of the street, on her knees in the dust, both hands on his face like she has to check he's solid.\n\n\"You borrowed my ribbon,\" she chokes out, instead of anything else.\n\n\"I'm sorry,\" Pell whispers. \"It's still in the room of waiting. The road person might go back for it.\"\n\nThey hold each other a long time. Tobish the wagon driver turns his face away and pretends to be busy with a harness.","choices":[{"text":"Step back. Let them have this.","next":"side_pell_aftermath","do":["trust:+10","rel:moth:+5","flag:pell_home","journal:Brought Pell home to Juno. He says I might have to go back for a ribbon. I think I will."]}]},{"id":"side_pell_aftermath","chapter":"SIDE · THE LOST CHILD","text":"Juno catches up to you at the edge of the caravan, eyes red.\n\n\"You,\" she says, and stops, because the rest of the sentence won't come.\n\nShe presses something into your hand: a small bone token, carved like a fish. The kind caravans use for safe-passage among their own.\n\n\"If you ever — ever — need a wagon, a meal, a hiding place, a road — you show this. Any of ours will know. Any of ours will move.\"\n\nShe wipes her face with the heel of her hand.\n\n\"He says you're going back in. He says you might find a ribbon. Find more than a ribbon. Find every one of them. Pell says the door opened on its own. He says it wants to open. Make it.\"\n\nWhat will you say?","choices":[{"text":"\"I will.\"","next":"side_pell_road","do":["add_item:supplies","gold:+8","trust:+5","journal:Juno's token: any caravan will move for me now. Pell says the door WANTS to open."]}]},{"id":"side_pell_road","chapter":"SIDE · THE LOST CHILD","text":"You leave them on the wagon-tail, Pell tucked under Juno's arm like he might still float away, Officer the cat watching from a barrel as if to confirm the delivery.\n\nThe last thing you hear before you turn the corner is Pell, sleepy and matter-of-fact: \"The road person smells like the road. The road is good.\"\n\nWhere now?","choices":[{"text":"Into the market.","next":"city_market"},{"text":"Into the alleys.","next":"city_alleys"}]},{"id":"side_bookseller_return","chapter":"SIDE · THE BOOKSELLER","text":"The bell over the door still chimes the way bells do in shops where the owner cares about the chime.\n\nThe bookseller looks up from a ledger. She is older than the last time. Older in the way people get older when they've been waiting.\n\n\"You came back,\" she says. \"They mostly don't.\"\n\nShe closes the ledger. Slides off her stool. Walks to a shelf you didn't notice the first time — it's behind a curtain the colour of dried roses — and takes down a slim book bound in pale leather.\n\n\"This one's not for you,\" she says, holding it out. \"This one's for {missing_name}. I've been keeping it.\"\n\nWhat do you say?","choices":[{"text":"\"How did you know I'd come back?\"","next":"side_bookseller_knew"},{"text":"\"What's in it?\"","next":"side_bookseller_book"},{"text":"Take it. \"Thank you.\"","next":"side_bookseller_book"}]},{"id":"side_bookseller_knew","chapter":"SIDE · THE BOOKSELLER","text":"She smiles, small and tired.\n\n\"I knew because of the Keeper's name. Once someone learns his name, the road bends them back to me. It always has. He's the one who makes the bending.\"\n\nShe taps the book.\n\n\"This was written by his mother. Long ago. Before he was the Keeper. When he was just a boy who'd lost a girl. She wrote it for him to read when he came back. He never did. So I kept it. I have been keeping it for a long time.\"\n\nA pause. The shop is very quiet.\n\n\"It's not for him now. It's for the one he takes. If your {missing_short} reads it inside the dark, the dark may listen. Mothers' words have weight there.\"\n\nWhat do you ask?","choices":[{"text":"\"What's in it?\"","next":"side_bookseller_book"},{"text":"\"You knew his mother?\"","next":"side_bookseller_mother"}]},{"id":"side_bookseller_book","chapter":"SIDE · THE BOOKSELLER","text":"She opens it. The pages are creamy with age. The hand inside is small, careful, a woman's writing from a century that wrote slowly.\n\nMy darling boy, the first page reads, if you are reading this, you have come back to me. I will not be alive to greet you, but I will have been alive to write this, and that is something. The dark you walked into is older than I am. But not older than love. Love is older. Love is the oldest thing.\n\nThe bookseller closes the book gently.\n\n\"There's more. Don't read it now. Save it. Give it to your {missing_short} to read at the moment they most need to remember they are loved. The book knows when. It will warm in their hands.\"\n\nShe presses it into yours.\n\n\"Bring it back if you can. Don't, if you can't.\"","choices":[{"text":"\"Thank you.\"","next":"side_bookseller_out","do":["add_item:keeper_mother_book","trust:+5","flag:has_mother_book","journal:A bookseller kept the Keeper's mother's letter for a hundred years. It is for {missing_name} to read. The book knows when."]}]},{"id":"side_bookseller_mother","chapter":"SIDE · THE BOOKSELLER","text":"She nods. Looks past you, into the dust of years.\n\n\"My great-great-grandmother did. They were friends. The Keeper's mother was a midwife. Her name was Anya. She buried her son in her head a long time before she stopped writing to him.\"\n\nShe blinks. Comes back.\n\n\"She wrote this book in the year after he went. She brought it to my line and said, keep this for him, in case he forgets. My family has kept it for a hundred and twenty winters. I am the last of my line. I have been waiting for you, in a way. Or someone like you.\"\n\nShe presses the book into your hands.\n\n\"His mother's name was Anya. Anya. If you ever need a sound to throw at him — that one will land.\"","choices":[{"text":"Take the book. \"Anya. I'll remember.\"","next":"side_bookseller_out","do":["add_item:keeper_mother_book","trust:+5","flag:has_mother_book","flag:keeper_mother_name","journal:The Keeper's mother was Anya. Her book is in my pack. A sound to throw at him that will land."]}]},{"id":"side_bookseller_out","chapter":"SIDE · THE BOOKSELLER","text":"At the door she catches your wrist. Her grip is dry and warm.\n\n\"One more thing. The dark in the arch can read. He has been reading for a thousand years, looking for a name he has forgotten. If you find his writing in there — his hand — look at it. Don't be polite. Look.\"\n\nShe releases you.\n\n\"The bell will chime when you come back. I will be here, if I am still here. If I am not — leave the book on the counter. Someone else will keep it.\"\n\nThe bell chimes as you step out.","choices":[{"text":"On.","next":"city_alleys"}]},{"id":"side_moth_roost_1","chapter":"SIDE · MOTH'S CROWS","text":"Moth has not been herself for a day and a half. Not sullen, exactly — just thin, somehow, like a held note running out of breath.\n\nAt the rest stop, while you scrape ash from a kettle, she says, very quietly:\n\n\"My crows are scattered.\"\n\nShe says it without looking at you. The way one says a thing they don't want to need help with.\n\n\"There was a roost. Old tower, iron weathervanes. Storm took the vanes, vanes took the wind, wind took the flock. I can feel them — they're alive, they're just spread. If I could find the tower and put one vane back up — \"\n\nShe breaks off. Picks at a thread on her cuff.\n\n\"Forget it. Not why we're here.\"\n\nWhat do you say?","choices":[{"text":"\"Where's the tower?\"","next":"side_moth_roost_2","require":"companion:moth"},{"text":"\"We'll find it. Today.\"","next":"side_moth_roost_2"},{"text":"Sit with her. Don't push.","next":"side_moth_sit","do":["rel:moth:+3"]}]},{"id":"side_moth_sit","chapter":"SIDE · MOTH'S CROWS","text":"You sit beside her and pretend the kettle still needs scraping. After a while she leans her shoulder against yours, the small careful weight of a person who doesn't usually lean.\n\n\"I'll show you,\" she says eventually. \"After this. After we get the {missing_short} back. The tower's been there a hundred years. It can wait a week more.\"\n\nShe sniffs, businesslike.\n\n\"But thank you for not making me ask.\"","choices":[{"text":"\"Anytime.\"","next":"side_moth_roost_2","do":["rel:moth:+5"]}]},{"id":"side_moth_roost_2","chapter":"SIDE · MOTH'S CROWS","text":"She leads you off the road, down a switchback no one would have noticed if she hadn't pointed it out, and there — set in a fold of hill the colour of bruises — stands an old watchtower. Three storeys. Half-collapsed. Four iron rods at the top where four weathervanes used to spin.\n\nOnly one vane is left. The other three lie in the grass like sleeping fish.\n\n\"They have to spin,\" Moth says, climbing already. \"That's the trick. Iron and wind and a name on each one. My great-grandmother's name's on that one — \" she points \" — and then her sister, and then their dog, and then a man none of us remember.\"\n\nShe glances down at you.\n\n\"Help me lift?\"","choices":[{"text":"\"Up we go.\"","next":"side_moth_roost_3"}]},{"id":"side_moth_roost_3","chapter":"SIDE · MOTH'S CROWS","text":"The climb is rougher than it looks. Moth goes ahead like a person who has climbed this tower in dreams. You follow with a vane balanced across your back, the iron cold against your spine.\n\nAt the top, the wind is sudden and large, the kind that gets in your clothes. Moth braces the rod. You lift. The vane slots home with a sound like a door clicking shut on a winter night.\n\nIt catches. Spins. Catches.\n\nMoth laughs — actually laughs, a small bright sound — and the wind takes her hair sideways.\n\nFrom somewhere very far, a single crow calls.\n\nThen another, closer.\n\nThen ten. Then thirty. They come from every direction, dropping out of nowhere skies, landing on the tower around you like a soft black storm settling.","choices":[{"text":"Watch in wonder.","next":"side_moth_roost_4"}]},{"id":"side_moth_roost_4","chapter":"SIDE · MOTH'S CROWS","text":"Moth holds out her hands. A big crow with one white feather behind its head settles on her wrist and headbutts her thumb. She makes a sound like someone who has been holding her breath for a year.\n\n\"This is Margery,\" she tells you, scratching the crow's throat. \"She's a terror. I have missed her.\"\n\nMargery considers you with one slow eye. Then, judgment passed, the crow hops onto your shoulder, taps your ear once with her beak, and flies off to assert dominance on a nearby weathervane.\n\nMoth turns to you. Her eyes are very bright.\n\n\"They'll come when I call now. Anywhere. Even — \" she swallows \" — even there. Even the dark behind the arch. Crows fly where the dead fly. Crows know the way home.\"\n\nWhat do you say?","choices":[{"text":"\"Then they'll be useful.\"","next":"side_moth_roost_5","do":["rel:moth:+15","flag:moth_flock","journal:Moth's flock is hers again. Margery the crow likes me. Crows know the way home — even from the dark."]}]},{"id":"side_moth_roost_5","chapter":"SIDE · MOTH'S CROWS","text":"Going down is easier than coming up. The flock follows for a while, then peels off — Margery first, the others after, in a long unspooling thread of black against the bruised sky.\n\nAt the bottom Moth stops. Looks at you. The brightness in her eyes is still there.\n\n\"I owe you,\" she says. \"Don't argue. I owe you and I will pay it. Probably by being annoying about it for the rest of our lives. Get used to it.\"\n\nShe knocks her shoulder against yours.\n\nWhere now?","choices":[{"text":"Back to the road.","next":"wastes_ride"},{"text":"Cut through toward the witch's wood.","next":"witch_leave_road"}]},{"id":"side_witch_jar_1","chapter":"SIDE · THE WITCH'S MOTHER","text":"The Hedge Witch is mending a stocking when you come into the grove. She mends like she does everything — with bored, terrifying precision.\n\nWithout looking up: \"You have the question-shaped face today. Ask.\"\n\nYou ask the one you have been holding.\n\n\"You said your mother's in a jar.\"\n\nThe needle stops. The grove gets a degree quieter.\n\nShe lifts her eyes.\n\n\"I did say that. I assumed you'd been polite enough to forget.\"\n\nA long look.\n\n\"You want to meet her.\"","choices":[{"text":"\"Only if you'll let me.\"","next":"side_witch_jar_2","require":"companion:hedge_witch"},{"text":"\"Only if she'll let me.\"","next":"side_witch_jar_2"},{"text":"\"Never mind. Stupid question.\"","next":"side_witch_jar_skip"}]},{"id":"side_witch_jar_skip","chapter":"SIDE · THE WITCH'S MOTHER","text":"She snorts. Goes back to her stocking.\n\n\"Stupid questions are the only ones worth asking, hatchling. But fine. Another time. She doesn't get many visitors. She'll be cross with me for not bringing you in.\"\n\nThe grove warms again.","choices":[{"text":"Stay by the fire a while.","next":"camp_rest"}]},{"id":"side_witch_jar_2","chapter":"SIDE · THE WITCH'S MOTHER","text":"She sets the stocking aside. Rises with the small effort of someone who has been mortal a long time. Leads you behind the grove, behind a hawthorn so old it looks like furniture, to a cottage you did not see on the way in and will not, you suspect, find again.\n\nInside: one room. One window. One shelf.\n\nOn the shelf, a jar.\n\nThe jar is the size of a soup pot. Inside the jar is a swirl of pale smoke that moves the way thought moves — not air, not water, attention. A pair of eyes opens in the smoke. They are her mother's eyes. You can tell because the witch sighs in the exact same exasperated octave.\n\n\"Hello, mother,\" the witch says.\n\nThe smoke ripples.\n\n\"She's been bored,\" the witch says. \"She'll be insufferable. Stand there. Don't lean on the shelf.\"","choices":[{"text":"\"Hello, ma'am.\"","next":"side_witch_jar_3"}]},{"id":"side_witch_jar_3","chapter":"SIDE · THE WITCH'S MOTHER","text":"The smoke gathers itself into something more face-shaped. The eyes consider you. They are warmer than the witch's eyes. You realize, with a small shock, that this is what the witch's eyes look like when she's not pretending.\n\nA voice comes out of the jar. It is not a sound. It is the idea of a voice, settling directly behind your forehead.\n\nOne, it says. I get to say one true sentence. My daughter knows the rules. Listen.\n\nThe witch crosses her arms. Pretends not to be holding her breath.\n\nThe voice considers you for a long moment, like a tailor measuring.\n\nThen it says:\n\nThe Keeper's mother forgave him before he ever did the thing he is sorry for. He does not know this. Tell him.\n\nThe smoke settles. The eyes close.\n\nThe jar is just a jar.","choices":[{"text":"Stand very still.","next":"side_witch_jar_4","do":["flag:keeper_forgiven","trust:+3","journal:The witch's mother in a jar: 'The Keeper's mother forgave him before he ever did the thing he is sorry for. He does not know this. Tell him.'"]}]},{"id":"side_witch_jar_4","chapter":"SIDE · THE WITCH'S MOTHER","text":"The witch lets out a breath. Picks up the jar with careful, casual hands. Wraps it in a cloth that smells of rosemary.\n\n\"She likes you,\" she says, on the way out. \"She used to give my suitors three sentences. You got one. She likes you. Don't get a head about it.\"\n\nOutside, the hawthorn looks like a hawthorn again, not furniture. The cottage is gone or was never there.\n\nThe witch stops at the grove's edge. Looks at you sideways.\n\n\"For the record,\" she says. \"My mother was wrong about exactly one thing in her life, and she has not yet told me what it was. So if the Keeper's mother is wrong too, we work with what we've got. Now go ruin a god, hatchling.\"","choices":[{"text":"Back to the fire.","next":"camp_rest"},{"text":"Back to the grove path.","next":"witch_grove"}]}]},"story/chapter_16.json":{"scenes":[{"id":"deep_wraith_road_1","chapter":"WAVE 4 · THE WRAITH ON THE ROAD","text":"The wastes go gray before they go dark. You feel it first as a wrongness at the edge of vision — a thin shape that moves when you do, that stops when you stop.\n\nAsh sees it too. Their hand drifts to a knife and stays there. \"It's been with us since the river,\" they murmur. \"It hasn't tried anything.\"\n\nSer Vale spits. \"Not yet.\"\n\nThe figure is the color of old smoke. It has no face — only the suggestion of one, the way a stain on a wall suggests a face if you stare long enough. It does not gain on you. It does not fall behind. When you slow, it slows. When you laugh nervously, you hear it laugh half a heartbeat later, in your own voice.\n\nMoth has gone the gray-green of someone about to be sick.\n\nThe sun is bleeding out behind the western hills. You will have to make camp soon, and the thing will be at your fire.\n\nWhat do you do before nightfall?","choices":[{"text":"Set the watch and prepare for a fight.","next":"deep_wraith_road_2"},{"text":"Walk faster — try to outlast it until dawn.","next":"deep_wraith_road_3"},{"text":"Speak to it now, in daylight, while it's weak.","next":"deep_wraith_road_4"}]},{"id":"deep_wraith_road_2","chapter":"WAVE 4 · THE WRAITH ON THE ROAD","text":"You pick the camp yourself: a low cup of land with a single dead tree, where nothing can come at you from above. Ser Vale grunts approval. Ash builds the fire too tall. Moth will not sit with their back to the dark.\n\nYou eat in silence. The thing is past the firelight, just past, where the light gives up and the wastes begin.\n\nThen — your own voice from the dark: \"Ash. Hand me the waterskin.\"\n\nAsh freezes with the waterskin halfway out. They are staring at you. You have not spoken.\n\nAnother voice now, from a slightly different place. Ser Vale's, perfect, perfect: \"On your left, lad.\"\n\nVale draws steel without looking. \"It's learning us,\" they say flatly. \"It'll be inside our heads by morning.\"\n\nYou can see it now at the edge of the light. Not a shape. A pulled thread of person. It is waiting for you to choose.","choices":[{"text":"Steel. End it now. (sword/shadow_blade)","next":"deep_wraith_road_steel","require":"sword"},{"text":"Steel — the shadow blade is hungrier.","next":"deep_wraith_road_steel","require":"shadow_blade"},{"text":"Light. Crack a warding draught into the fire.","next":"deep_wraith_road_light","require":"warding_draught"},{"text":"Speak its true name.","next":"deep_wraith_road_name","require":"flag:knows_keeper_name"},{"text":"You have none of these. Hold the line and pray.","next":"deep_wraith_road_fail"}]},{"id":"deep_wraith_road_3","chapter":"WAVE 4 · THE WRAITH ON THE ROAD","text":"You push the company past dusk. Moth stumbles. Ash carries half their pack without a word. Vale's jaw is a closed door.\n\nThe thing keeps pace easily. It is not tired. It does not breathe.\n\nWhen the moon comes up it is already at your shoulder, an arm's length out into the dark, walking with you. It says, in your own voice, \"I'm tired.\"\n\nIt says, \"Make camp.\"\n\nIt is trying to give you orders in your own mouth.\n\nYou stop because your legs will not carry you another step. The fire is small and mean. The thing sits down across it from you, in the shape of a person who does not quite remember how knees fold.\n\nIt has all night.","choices":[{"text":"Steel. (sword/shadow_blade)","next":"deep_wraith_road_steel","require":"sword"},{"text":"Steel — shadow blade.","next":"deep_wraith_road_steel","require":"shadow_blade"},{"text":"Warding draught into the flames.","next":"deep_wraith_road_light","require":"warding_draught"},{"text":"Name it.","next":"deep_wraith_road_name","require":"flag:knows_keeper_name"},{"text":"Endure until dawn.","next":"deep_wraith_road_fail"}]},{"id":"deep_wraith_road_4","chapter":"WAVE 4 · THE WRAITH ON THE ROAD","text":"You walk off the road toward it. Ash hisses your name. Vale catches your sleeve. You shake them both off.\n\nIn the gray light it is less than you thought and more. It has the silhouette of a thin man in a long coat. It has no face. It is not afraid of you. It tilts what would be a head.\n\n\"Why are you following us?\" you ask.\n\nIt answers, in your voice: \"Why are you following us?\"\n\nYou try again. \"What do you want?\"\n\nIt waits, then says: \"What do you want?\"\n\nIt is not mocking. It is studying. It is taking you apart syllable by syllable to wear later.\n\nYou step back. It does not follow. Not yet. But the sun is going, and the thing has more of you now than it had this morning.","choices":[{"text":"Back to camp. Draw steel. (sword)","next":"deep_wraith_road_steel","require":"sword"},{"text":"Back to camp. Shadow blade.","next":"deep_wraith_road_steel","require":"shadow_blade"},{"text":"Back to camp. Burn it with light. (warding_draught)","next":"deep_wraith_road_light","require":"warding_draught"},{"text":"Speak its true name now, while you have its attention.","next":"deep_wraith_road_name","require":"flag:knows_keeper_name"},{"text":"Walk back. Hope.","next":"deep_wraith_road_fail"}]},{"id":"deep_wraith_road_steel","chapter":"WAVE 4 · THE WRAITH ON THE ROAD","text":"You go for it before it goes for you. That is the only edge.\n\nThe wraith does not bleed. It tears. Your first cut opens it like wet paper and inside is more dark, and the dark grabs back. Cold hands close on your forearm. Your blade goes wrong in your grip. Ash shouts something — a real word, your name, not yours-borrowed — and you remember which body is yours.\n\nYou drive the steel through where its mouth should be. It comes apart in a sound like a held breath let out.\n\nWhat is left on the ground is a stain that smells faintly of someone you used to know.\n\nVale claps you once, hard, on the shoulder. Ash exhales the whole night out. Moth is crying quietly and trying not to. Even Ser Vale looks at you differently now — like a thing they have decided to follow on purpose.\n\nYou bleed. But you bleed warm.\n\nWhat do you write in the journal?","choices":[{"text":"\"Killed a thing that wore my voice. I am still here.\"","next":"wastes_ride","do":["hp:-3","trust:+5","journal:Killed the wraith on the road. It wore my voice."]}]},{"id":"deep_wraith_road_light","chapter":"WAVE 4 · THE WRAITH ON THE ROAD","text":"You uncork the warding draught and tip the whole thing into the fire.\n\nThe flames go white. Not yellow-white. The white of a struck bell. The white of the inside of a wound.\n\nThe wraith screams in a chorus of your voices — yours, Ash's, Vale's, Moth's, three others you don't recognize and one, oh god, one is {missing_short}'s — and it folds inward like a piece of paper held to a candle.\n\nThen it is gone.\n\nThe fire returns to honest yellow. Moth is sitting very still. Ash is staring at you. Vale, very quietly, says: \"That was {missing_short}'s voice. At the end. You heard it.\"\n\nYou heard it.\n\nThe draught is gone. But so is the thing.\n\nDid the Keeper take a piece of {missing_short_cap} too, and use it as bait?","choices":[{"text":"Write it down before you forget the sound.","next":"wastes_ride","do":["rem_item:warding_draught","trust:+3","journal:Burned the wraith with light. It screamed in {missing_short}'s voice."]}]},{"id":"deep_wraith_road_name","chapter":"WAVE 4 · THE WRAITH ON THE ROAD","text":"You know what made this thing. You learned it in a tower, or in a book, or from a half-drowned mouth in a basement, and the knowing sits in you like a cold stone.\n\nYou say the Keeper's true name into the dark.\n\nThe wraith goes very still. The wastes go very still. Even the wind, which has been a steady knife since the river, stops.\n\nThe thing bows.\n\nIt is the worst thing you have ever seen, that bow — the way a half-made person folds when they remember they used to be someone. Then it turns and walks away from your fire, back toward the dark it was sent from, with the slow patient step of a servant dismissed.\n\nVale crosses themself with a hand that does not usually do that.\n\nAsh whispers: \"What did you say?\"\n\nYou don't tell them. Not yet.","choices":[{"text":"Bank the fire. Sleep if you can.","next":"wastes_ride","do":["trust:+8","journal:Spoke the Keeper's name. The wraith bowed and left."]}]},{"id":"deep_wraith_road_fail","chapter":"WAVE 4 · THE WRAITH ON THE ROAD","text":"You have nothing for it. No steel that bites, no light that burns, no name that binds. You have a fire and four bodies and a long night.\n\nThe wraith comes into the firelight at the small hour. It does not attack. It sits. It speaks in your voice and Ash's voice and Vale's voice and once, terribly, in {missing_short}'s voice, saying come find me.\n\nYou do not sleep. None of you sleep. By dawn your hands shake. Moth has bitten their lip bloody trying not to answer it.\n\nWhen the sun finally cracks the horizon the thing slides backward into the long shadow of the dead tree and waits there, patient as a debt.\n\nIt is coming with you another day.\n\nYou are smaller this morning. All of you.","choices":[{"text":"March. Try to stay alive long enough to learn its name.","next":"wastes_ride","do":["hp:-5","fear:+15","flag:wraith_follows","journal:Survived the wraith. It is still with us."]}]},{"id":"deep_wolves_1","chapter":"WAVE 4 · THE WOLF PACK","text":"You hear them before you see them.\n\nNot a howl. Wolves on a hunt do not howl. They speak to each other in low coughs, in the soft chuff of breath, in the click of claws on root.\n\nThe hedge witch's wood swallows sound. You don't know how many there are until they are already in a half-circle in front of you, low to the ground, ears flat. Six. Seven. Their eyes are wrong.\n\nThey are too clear. The yellow is too clean. A wolf's eye should have weather in it. These look at you the way a clerk looks at a column of numbers.\n\nThe biggest one — gray as old iron — sits down on its haunches and waits, polite as a duelist. This is a test. The witch has sent them.\n\nAsh's knuckles are white on their knife. Vale, very softly: \"Don't run. Whatever you do, don't run.\"\n\nWhat do you do?","choices":[{"text":"Draw and fight. (sword)","next":"deep_wolves_fight","require":"sword"},{"text":"Draw the knife and fight close.","next":"deep_wolves_fight_knife","require":"knife"},{"text":"Break uphill. Outpace them. (hp:5)","next":"deep_wolves_run","require":"hp:5"},{"text":"Let the Hedge Witch speak.","next":"deep_wolves_hedge","require":"companion:hedge_witch"},{"text":"Offer the warm tooth.","next":"deep_wolves_tooth","require":"warm_tooth"},{"text":"You have nothing. Stand. Take what comes.","next":"deep_wolves_naked"}]},{"id":"deep_wolves_fight","chapter":"WAVE 4 · THE WOLF PACK","text":"The big one comes for your throat first because that is how a captain leads.\n\nYou meet it with the edge. The shock of impact runs up your arm into your teeth. It is heavier than a wolf should be. It is on you and then it is off you because Vale has hammered the pommel of their sword into its skull from the side.\n\nThe rest break. Two for Vale, one for Ash, one for you. Moth has a stick and is doing what they can.\n\nYou kill the one in front of you. It dies looking at you with that too-clean eye, and at the very last moment something LEAVES the eye, and what looks back at you for one breath before it goes still is the old, weather-bitten yellow of an actual animal that was not asking for this.\n\nThe witch was riding them. You broke a leash, not a soul.\n\nYou are bleeding. So is everyone. But you stand together at the end of it, and that has begun to mean something.","choices":[{"text":"Bind wounds. Move on.","next":"witch_wood_inner","do":["hp:-4","trust:+5","journal:Killed the witch's wolves. Something else looked out at the end."]}]},{"id":"deep_wolves_fight_knife","chapter":"WAVE 4 · THE WOLF PACK","text":"A knife is an honest weapon at close range and a stupid one at any other. You know this. You go in anyway because going in is the only choice that doesn't end with your hamstring opened from behind.\n\nYou get under the first wolf's jaw and put the blade up into the soft. Hot. So hot. The second one takes your off arm and shakes — you feel the meat give before the bone holds — and Ash is screaming and stabbing and there is a sound like wet cloth tearing.\n\nThe pack breaks because Vale has waded into them with a long sword and a face like a closing gate.\n\nYou sit down in the leaves. Your arm is not working. Moth ties it off and you say thank you and you do not pass out, which feels like a victory.\n\nThat was too close. That was barely a fight.","choices":[{"text":"Drink, breathe, keep going.","next":"witch_wood_inner","do":["hp:-6","trust:+5","journal:Knife-fought the wolves. Almost lost the arm."]}]},{"id":"deep_wolves_run","chapter":"WAVE 4 · THE WOLF PACK","text":"You go uphill because uphill is the one thing a wolf hates more than fire. Their hips give out before yours do, if you can keep your lungs.\n\nYou keep your lungs. Barely.\n\nYou go up a slope that turns to scree, that turns to bare rock, that turns to a thin ledge under an overhang where four humans can wedge in and a wolf cannot follow without going single file into a knife.\n\nThey gather below. They look up at you. The big gray one sits down again and watches you with that clerk's patience. After a long time it stands and walks away, and the others follow, and the wood goes quiet.\n\nYou shake. All of you shake. Moth is laughing in the high broken way that comes after fear.\n\nYou did not lose blood. You lost something else. There is a cold thing in your chest now that knows it can be hunted.","choices":[{"text":"Climb down. Press on.","next":"witch_wood_path","do":["fear:+10","journal:Outran the wolves. The big one let us go on purpose."]}]},{"id":"deep_wolves_hedge","chapter":"WAVE 4 · THE WOLF PACK","text":"The Hedge Witch steps forward without hurry. She has been quiet all morning. Now she is not.\n\nShe speaks a word that sounds like a stone dropped into a deep well. She does not raise her voice.\n\nThe big gray wolf flattens its ears. It looks at her. She looks at it. Something passes between them — a long bitter argument carried out without speaking, the way old friends fight.\n\nThe gray wolf lowers its head. The wrongness goes out of its eyes. It becomes, very simply, a tired old animal with a scar across its muzzle.\n\nThe pack turns and goes.\n\nThe Hedge Witch sits down hard in the leaves and puts her face in her hands. \"She was my teacher,\" she says, muffled. \"She taught me that word. Now I've used it against her.\"\n\nNo one knows what to say. Ash sits beside her and does not say it.","choices":[{"text":"Wait with her until she can walk.","next":"witch_wood_inner","do":["rel:hedge_witch:+10","trust:+5","journal:The Hedge Witch turned the wolves with her teacher's word."]}]},{"id":"deep_wolves_tooth","chapter":"WAVE 4 · THE WOLF PACK","text":"You take the warm tooth out of your pocket. It is warm. It has always been warm, even on cold mornings, even in the river. It is warm now in a different way — eager.\n\nYou hold it out on your open palm.\n\nThe big gray wolf rises and pads forward, careful as a priest. It noses the tooth. It looks up at you, and for an instant you understand something you do not have the words for — a long debt, a kept promise, a name traded for a name.\n\nIt takes the tooth in its teeth, gentle as a parent. The pack rises behind it.\n\nThey turn and pass you on either side, brushing your legs as they go, and they vanish into the green dark of the wood without a sound.\n\nMoth says, very small: \"What did you just give them?\"\n\nYou don't know. But your pocket is colder now, and so is your heart, a little.","choices":[{"text":"Walk on while you still can.","next":"witch_wood_inner","do":["rem_item:warm_tooth","trust:+3","flag:wolves_owe","journal:Gave the warm tooth to the wolves. They let us pass."]}]},{"id":"deep_wolves_naked","chapter":"WAVE 4 · THE WOLF PACK","text":"You have nothing in your hands. You stand anyway because Vale said don't run and Vale knows wolves.\n\nThe big gray one circles. It is taking your measure. It is finding you wanting.\n\nIt comes for Moth first because Moth is the smallest. You throw yourself between because that is the only sentence your body knows in this language. Teeth find your shoulder. You hit the ground hard. Vale is over you with steel and the pack is on Vale and Ash is screaming a name.\n\nIt ends because it has to end. Two wolves dead. Vale bleeding from a thigh that will scar. You from a shoulder that will ache in winter forever.\n\nYou kept Moth alive. They will not let go of your sleeve for the rest of the day.\n\nThe big gray wolf is gone. You did not beat it. It chose to leave.","choices":[{"text":"Limp on. Together.","next":"witch_wood_inner","do":["hp:-6","trust:+8","rel:moth:+10","journal:Took wolf-teeth for Moth. Worth it."]}]},{"id":"deep_basement_1","chapter":"WAVE 4 · THE TOWER BASEMENT","text":"The wizard has been called away again — by whom, he didn't say. The tower is quiet in the way old houses are quiet when they know you are alone.\n\nIn the menagerie, behind the cage of the thing that sings in a child's voice, you find a door you missed before. It is small, wooden, no taller than your sternum. It is warm to the touch.\n\nYou crack it. Behind it, a stair, very steep, going down into a wet smell — old water, salt, and something else. Iron, maybe. Or the smell a person makes when they have been frightened for a long time.\n\nAsh, behind you: \"He didn't show us this on the tour.\"\n\nVale: \"He didn't, did he.\"\n\nThe stair turns once. There is a faint blue light at the bottom. It is the wrong blue. The blue of a vein under thin skin.\n\nDo you go down?","choices":[{"text":"Go down. (require: trust:50)","next":"deep_basement_2","require":"trust:50"},{"text":"Shut the door. Not yet.","next":"tower_foyer"}]},{"id":"deep_basement_2","chapter":"WAVE 4 · THE TOWER BASEMENT","text":"The stair is twenty-six steps and you count every one. Vale comes after you. Ash stays at the top to whistle if the wizard comes home. Moth, white-lipped, comes too — they will not let you go down alone.\n\nAt the bottom is a round room. Stone walls sweating water. In the center of the floor, a circle. Not drawn. Scratched. The lines are gouged so deep they have rust in them. The circle is salt, packed hard, knee-high.\n\nInside the circle there is a thing that is mostly liquid.\n\nIt has the suggestion of a man at its surface — the curve of a shoulder, the dark of a half-open mouth, the long flat plane where a forehead used to be. The rest of him is pooled at the bottom of himself, slowly trying to remember.\n\nWhen you step into the blue light, the surface tightens. An eye opens. It is a man's eye. It is exhausted.\n\n\"Oh,\" he says, and his voice is wet, \"a visitor.\"","choices":[{"text":"Stay outside the circle. Speak.","next":"deep_basement_3"},{"text":"Walk back up the stairs.","next":"tower_foyer"}]},{"id":"deep_basement_3","chapter":"WAVE 4 · THE TOWER BASEMENT","text":"\"You are not him,\" says the thing in the circle, almost grateful. \"You are not the boy who keeps me. Good. Good.\"\n\nYou ask his name. He says he has forgotten it. He says the wizard took it as part of the binding and keeps it in a jar somewhere upstairs. He says he was a man, once, on a road, and the wizard found him useful for an old purpose.\n\nYou ask what purpose.\n\nHe smiles with the part of him that is currently shaped like a mouth.\n\n\"I know,\" he says, \"one thing about the Keeper of the Gate. One true thing. Nobody else here knows it. The wizard suspects it. He has never asked me because asking me would mean letting me go.\"\n\nMoth's hand finds your sleeve.\n\n\"I will tell you,\" he says, \"but you must trade. A trade, fair as trades go. I am not allowed to give it away.\"\n\nWhat will you trade?","choices":[{"text":"A memory. Something you would rather keep.","next":"deep_basement_memory"},{"text":"Your true name.","next":"deep_basement_name"},{"text":"A violent end. Break the circle. Kill him. (sword/shadow_blade)","next":"deep_basement_kill","require":"sword"},{"text":"End him cleanly with the shadow blade.","next":"deep_basement_kill","require":"shadow_blade"},{"text":"Refuse the trade. Climb back up.","next":"tower_foyer"}]},{"id":"deep_basement_memory","chapter":"WAVE 4 · THE TOWER BASEMENT","text":"You think of one — a small one, you tell yourself, a small one will do — and the thing in the circle says, very gently, \"No. A real one. One you would not give if you weren't standing here.\"\n\nSo you give him this: the morning before the Hollow Gate opened, when {missing_short} sat on the kitchen step and laughed at something you said, and the sun was on {missing_poss} face, and you knew, for one bright second, that everything was going to be all right.\n\nYou give him the laugh.\n\nHe drinks it. The blue light dims. Something in you dims with it.\n\nHe tells you, very softly: \"The Keeper was a king. The first king of your kingdom. He refused to die. The Gate is the door he made when he could not stand being a man any longer. He still wants the throne. He takes the children of his line because they are the only ones who can still open it from the other side.\"\n\nMoth has begun, silently, to cry.\n\n\"Now go,\" the thing says, almost kindly. \"Before he comes home.\"","choices":[{"text":"Climb the stairs with what you bought.","next":"tower_foyer","do":["add_item:keeper_lore","flag:knows_keeper_origin","journal:The Keeper was the first king. He has been waiting a long time."]}]},{"id":"deep_basement_name","chapter":"WAVE 4 · THE TOWER BASEMENT","text":"You tell him your name. Your real one. Not the one you use on the road.\n\nThe thing in the circle goes very still. Then it laughs, surprised and a little broken. \"Oh,\" it says. \"Oh, child. I will keep it well. It is not a small gift.\"\n\nYou feel it leave you the way a tooth leaves a jaw — clean, then a hole where it used to live. For a moment you cannot remember what your mother used to call you when she was angry.\n\nIt comes back. Mostly.\n\nThe thing tells you: \"The Keeper was the first king of your kingdom. He refused to die. The Gate is the door he built when his body would not hold him anymore. He has been waiting on the other side a very long time. He takes the children of his blood because they alone can open the Gate from within. Your {missing_short} is being prepared.\"\n\nVale's hand has gone to their hilt without their meaning it to.\n\n\"Prepared for what?\" you ask.\n\nThe thing in the circle, very softly: \"To wear him.\"","choices":[{"text":"Up the stairs. Now.","next":"tower_foyer","do":["add_item:keeper_lore","flag:knows_keeper_origin","fear:+10","journal:Traded my name. The Keeper means to wear {missing_short}."]}]},{"id":"deep_basement_kill","chapter":"WAVE 4 · THE TOWER BASEMENT","text":"You step across the salt.\n\nThe ring cracks. The blue light gutters. The thing in the circle does not run. It rises. It is taller than you thought — a column of dark water with a face at the top of it, the face of a man who has been a man so long ago he barely remembers the shape — and it comes at you with arms that are not quite arms.\n\nIt is strong. It is so strong. Vale catches its second swing on the flat of a blade and is driven to one knee. Moth screams. You go for its center.\n\nThe blade goes in. It goes through. The dark water that is the rest of him pours over your hand, cold past cold, and for a moment you see — you ARE — a man on a road a long time ago, who looked at a wizard's offer of usefulness and said yes because he was tired.\n\nHe dies grateful.\n\nWhat he leaves on the floor is a heap of damp cloth and a single tooth and a piece of folded paper that says, in a hand you do not know: He was the first king. Burn this.\n\nYou do not burn it.","choices":[{"text":"Take the paper. Go up. Now.","next":"tower_foyer","do":["hp:-4","add_item:keeper_lore","flag:knows_keeper_origin","fear:+5","journal:Killed the thing in the basement. It thanked me at the end."]}]},{"id":"deep_city_siege_1","chapter":"WAVE 4 · THE CITY UNDER SIEGE","text":"Bells start at the third hour. Not the slow tolling of a death. The fast clanging of a fire that is not a fire.\n\nA boy goes past you in the alley with one shoe on, screaming a name. Smoke is coming up from the east quarter. Not cookfire smoke. The black thick kind.\n\nA woman with a baker's apron grabs your sleeve. \"The Houses are turning,\" she gasps. \"Lord Maren's men in the square. The steward's locked the keep. There's fighting at the Salt Gate. Where do you stand, traveler?\"\n\nYou had thought you had until evening to do your business in this city. You do not have until evening.\n\nRook materializes out of a doorway behind you with one of his small ugly smiles and a longer sword than usual on his hip. \"I was wondering when this would crack,\" he says. \"Picking a side?\"\n\nWhat do you do?","choices":[{"text":"The steward. He is the law, what there is of it.","next":"deep_city_siege_steward"},{"text":"The rebels. The Houses are right that the crown has rotted.","next":"deep_city_siege_rebel"},{"text":"The civilians. Get them out of the streets. (no combat)","next":"deep_city_siege_civ"},{"text":"None of them. Slip out the south gate before it shuts.","next":"deep_city_siege_flee"}]},{"id":"deep_city_siege_steward","chapter":"WAVE 4 · THE CITY UNDER SIEGE","text":"You go to the keep. The steward's men are forming up in the courtyard — half of them boys, half of them old, all of them frightened. The captain has a bandage across his eye and is shouting orders to people who do not want to die today.\n\nRook falls in beside you without being asked. \"Hello, Captain,\" he says, friendly as a knife. \"Three more hands. Where do you want us?\"\n\nThe captain looks at your blade and your scars and says: \"Salt Gate. They'll come there first. Don't let it open.\"\n\nYou run. The streets are slick with something you don't look at. Rook fights at your right. He is better than he pretends. At one point he saves your life with a backhand cut that opens a man's wrist before the man knows to bleed.\n\nThe Salt Gate holds because you hold it. By the time the steward's relief column comes, you are sitting on the cobbles with your back against the gate-iron and Rook is laughing, breathless, beside you.\n\n\"Well,\" he says. \"That was honest work, for once.\"\n\nThe steward himself comes to find you that night. He puts a hand on your shoulder and says nothing for a long time. Then he gives you a small thing on a chain. \"For the road,\" he says.","choices":[{"text":"Take the blessing. Drink with Rook.","next":"city_keep","do":["hp:-3","companion:rook","trust:+5","flag:steward_blessing","journal:Held the Salt Gate. The steward gave me his token. Rook signed on."]}]},{"id":"deep_city_siege_rebel","chapter":"WAVE 4 · THE CITY UNDER SIEGE","text":"You find Lord Maren's banner in the square — three black hounds on white. The men under it are not thugs. They are merchants' sons and discharged soldiers and a couple of women with axes and grief on their faces. They are right about a lot of things.\n\nMaren himself is younger than the songs will say. He looks at you and reads you fast.\n\n\"You're not from here,\" he says. \"Why are you here?\"\n\nYou tell him: the crown stole someone you love. He nods once, sharp.\n\n\"Then we are friends today.\"\n\nThe fighting is bad. The steward's men have the high walls. You take the bell tower with twelve others; six come back down. Rook fights at your shoulder for a while and then is gone, into the smoke, and you do not see him again that day.\n\nWhen the dust settles, Maren has the city. He kneels to you in the square — actually kneels — and says, \"Come back when you have your blood back. There will be a seat for you here.\"\n\nYou are not sure you wanted to be the kind of person who has a seat in a city of new graves. But you took one.","choices":[{"text":"Bind your wounds. Go.","next":"city_alleys","do":["hp:-5","trust:+5","gold:+20","flag:rebel_friend","journal:Fought with Maren. The city changed hands. I helped."]}]},{"id":"deep_city_siege_civ","chapter":"WAVE 4 · THE CITY UNDER SIEGE","text":"You and Ash and Moth go where the fighting is not. You bang on shutters. You drag old men out of doorways. You carry a woman in labor four streets to a midwife who is herself bleeding from a cut on the forehead.\n\nMoth is good at this. Moth is luminous at this. You realize you have never seen them so sure of themselves.\n\nAn alley full of children won't come out because they think you are with the soldiers. Ash sits down in the muck and starts singing the silly counting song the bookseller taught them. One child comes. Then another. Then all of them.\n\nYou get nineteen people out of the east quarter before the fire turns it to a furnace. Nineteen.\n\nAt sundown, the woman with the baker's apron finds you. She gives you a loaf still warm. \"Word's going around about you,\" she says. \"That's the kind of word that opens doors. Remember us, traveler.\"\n\nRook drifts up at twilight, bloody. He looks at you a long moment, then nods, slow. \"You're the strangest soldier I've ever met,\" he says. \"I think I like you better for it.\"","choices":[{"text":"Eat the bread. Sleep when you can.","next":"city_keep","do":["trust:+10","rel:moth:+10","rel:rook:+5","journal:Pulled nineteen people out of the fire. Not a fight I'll forget."]}]},{"id":"deep_city_siege_flee","chapter":"WAVE 4 · THE CITY UNDER SIEGE","text":"You take the south gate before it shuts. You take it at a run. Ash is behind you and Moth is between you and Vale is bringing up the rear, sword out, daring anyone to slow the company down.\n\nNo one does.\n\nThe city is behind you by the time the second bell starts. Smoke is rising from three places now. You can hear, very faintly, somebody screaming.\n\nYou tell yourself: that is not your war. {missing_short_cap} is your war.\n\nYou tell yourself a few more things on the road south. Most of them are true. None of them are all the way true.\n\nMoth will not look at you for a while.\n\nWhat will the journal say?","choices":[{"text":"\"I left a city to burn. I will not pretend I didn't.\"","next":"wastes_ride","do":["fear:+5","rel:moth:-5","journal:Left the city burning. {missing_short_cap} is more important. I hope."]}]},{"id":"deep_dream_1","chapter":"WAVE 4 · THE KEEPER'S DREAM-HUNT","text":"You go to sleep by the fire. You know what is happening as soon as it happens.\n\nYou are still by the fire. The fire is still burning. But Ash is not breathing. Vale is not breathing. Moth's eyes are open and they are not seeing.\n\nSomething steps out from behind the dead tree at the edge of the camp.\n\nHe is tall. He is wearing a crown that is not metal. His face is the face of a man who has practiced being a face for too long. He smiles at you with all his teeth and they are correct in number and arrangement and that is somehow worse.\n\n\"There you are,\" the Keeper says. His voice is patient, almost fond. \"I wondered when you would come close enough to hunt.\"\n\nYou cannot move your legs. You can move your eyes and your hands.\n\nThis is a dream. This is also not a dream. He is in the dream with you and what he does here will print on you when you wake.\n\nWhat do you do, round one?","choices":[{"text":"Hide. Slip behind the tree before he names you.","next":"deep_dream_2_hide"},{"text":"Run. Out of the firelight, into the dark. (require: hp:5)","next":"deep_dream_2_run","require":"hp:5"},{"text":"Fight. Draw the shadow blade in the dream.","next":"deep_dream_2_fight","require":"shadow_blade"},{"text":"Speak. Say his name back at him. (require: flag:knows_keeper_name)","next":"deep_dream_2_speak","require":"flag:knows_keeper_name"}]},{"id":"deep_dream_2_hide","chapter":"WAVE 4 · THE KEEPER'S DREAM-HUNT","text":"You slide sideways into the shadow of the dead tree. You do it the way a child does it — small, completely, holding your breath.\n\nThe Keeper walks past. He is humming. The humming is, you realize, your own breathing, slowed down and played back to you.\n\nHe stops at the fire. He turns his head all the way around like a thing that has not used a neck in a long time. He says, \"Round two, little one.\"\n\nYou are still alive in the dream. Good. Hide cost you nothing this round.","choices":[{"text":"Round two. Hide again, deeper.","next":"deep_dream_3_hide"},{"text":"Round two. Run. (require: hp:5)","next":"deep_dream_3_run","require":"hp:5"},{"text":"Round two. Fight. (shadow_blade)","next":"deep_dream_3_fight","require":"shadow_blade"},{"text":"Round two. Speak. (flag:knows_keeper_name)","next":"deep_dream_3_speak","require":"flag:knows_keeper_name"}]},{"id":"deep_dream_2_run","chapter":"WAVE 4 · THE KEEPER'S DREAM-HUNT","text":"You run. Your legs work in the dream because you have decided they will. You go out past the firelight into the long flat dark of the wastes and you run until your lungs are knives.\n\nWhen you stop you are at the foot of a hill that was not there before. The Keeper is standing on top of it. He has not moved. He has been there the whole time.\n\n\"Round two,\" he says, gently, the way you would say it to a child losing a game.\n\nYou wake half-up. Your chest aches. Something has been removed from your head — a page from the journal. You can feel the gap. It was about {missing_short}. You cannot remember which day.","choices":[{"text":"Round two. Hide.","next":"deep_dream_3_hide","do":["hp:-3","journal:Lost a page. The Keeper is hunting me in sleep."]},{"text":"Round two. Run again.","next":"deep_dream_3_run","require":"hp:5","do":["hp:-3"]},{"text":"Round two. Fight.","next":"deep_dream_3_fight","require":"shadow_blade","do":["hp:-3"]},{"text":"Round two. Speak.","next":"deep_dream_3_speak","require":"flag:knows_keeper_name","do":["hp:-3"]}]},{"id":"deep_dream_2_fight","chapter":"WAVE 4 · THE KEEPER'S DREAM-HUNT","text":"The shadow blade is in your hand because you wanted it there. In dreams that is enough.\n\nYou go at him. He lets you.\n\nThe blade goes through his ribs without resistance. He looks down at it with something close to interest. He says: \"Oh, that is a good little knife. Whose was it before yours?\"\n\nHe takes hold of the blade with his bare hand and pushes himself further onto it, until your fist is against his sternum and his face is very close to yours.\n\n\"Round two,\" he murmurs, almost lovingly, into your ear.\n\nThe blade was the wrong choice. He likes blades.","choices":[{"text":"Round two. Hide.","next":"deep_dream_3_hide","do":["hp:-3"]},{"text":"Round two. Run.","next":"deep_dream_3_run","require":"hp:5","do":["hp:-3"]},{"text":"Round two. Speak. (knows_keeper_name)","next":"deep_dream_3_speak","require":"flag:knows_keeper_name","do":["hp:-3"]}]},{"id":"deep_dream_2_speak","chapter":"WAVE 4 · THE KEEPER'S DREAM-HUNT","text":"You say his name.\n\nThe Keeper stops moving. He puts a hand to his own mouth, as if to check it is still there. Then he laughs. It is not the laugh of a man who is afraid. It is the laugh of a man who is delighted.\n\n\"Well,\" he says. \"Aren't you a clever animal. Round two.\"\n\nNaming him did not hurt him. It interested him. He is paying attention now in a different way.\n\nYou have his attention. Be careful what you do with it.","choices":[{"text":"Round two. Hide.","next":"deep_dream_3_hide"},{"text":"Round two. Run.","next":"deep_dream_3_run","require":"hp:5"},{"text":"Round two. Fight.","next":"deep_dream_3_fight","require":"shadow_blade"},{"text":"Round two. Speak again — ask what he wants.","next":"deep_dream_3_speak_talk"}]},{"id":"deep_dream_3_hide","chapter":"WAVE 4 · THE KEEPER'S DREAM-HUNT","text":"Round two: you hide in the shadow of Ash. They are not breathing in the dream, but they are still warm, and the Keeper does not look at the dead.\n\nHe walks among the camp. He stops at Moth. He bends down and almost — almost — kisses their forehead.\n\nYou make no sound.\n\nHe straightens. \"Round three, little one,\" he says, \"and then you will be mine for the morning.\"","choices":[{"text":"Round three. Fight him, ready or not. (shadow_blade)","next":"deep_dream_final_fight","require":"shadow_blade"},{"text":"Round three. Speak his name. (flag:knows_keeper_name)","next":"deep_dream_final_speak","require":"flag:knows_keeper_name"},{"text":"Round three. Hide one more time.","next":"deep_dream_final_hide"}]},{"id":"deep_dream_3_run","chapter":"WAVE 4 · THE KEEPER'S DREAM-HUNT","text":"Round two: you go further. You run past the hill, past a river that was not there yesterday, past a stand of dead trees that lean toward you as you pass.\n\nThe Keeper is at the end of the running. He always is. He says, gently: \"There is no further. You know that, don't you?\"\n\nYou know. But you ran anyway, because the body insists.\n\nYou ache when you wake half-up. Another page is gone.","choices":[{"text":"Round three. Fight. (shadow_blade)","next":"deep_dream_final_fight","require":"shadow_blade","do":["hp:-3"]},{"text":"Round three. Speak. (knows_keeper_name)","next":"deep_dream_final_speak","require":"flag:knows_keeper_name","do":["hp:-3"]},{"text":"Round three. Hide.","next":"deep_dream_final_hide","do":["hp:-3"]}]},{"id":"deep_dream_3_fight","chapter":"WAVE 4 · THE KEEPER'S DREAM-HUNT","text":"Round two: you go at him again. He sighs the way a tutor sighs at a slow pupil. He sets your blade aside with one palm and puts his other palm flat against your forehead.\n\nYou wake half-up gasping. There is a print on your forehead like a sun-burn. A page of the journal has been pulled.","choices":[{"text":"Round three. Speak. (knows_keeper_name)","next":"deep_dream_final_speak","require":"flag:knows_keeper_name","do":["hp:-3"]},{"text":"Round three. Hide.","next":"deep_dream_final_hide","do":["hp:-3"]},{"text":"Round three. Fight one more time.","next":"deep_dream_final_fight","require":"shadow_blade","do":["hp:-3"]}]},{"id":"deep_dream_3_speak","chapter":"WAVE 4 · THE KEEPER'S DREAM-HUNT","text":"Round two: you say his name again. Louder. As an accusation.\n\nThis time it lands. He flinches — the smallest twitch at the corner of his mouth — and you see, for one heartbeat, the tired old man underneath the practiced face. A king who has not slept in eight hundred years.\n\nThen the practiced face is back.\n\n\"Clever,\" he says. \"Round three. Make it interesting.\"","choices":[{"text":"Round three. Speak — and ask him to let {missing_short} go.","next":"deep_dream_final_speak"},{"text":"Round three. Fight. (shadow_blade)","next":"deep_dream_final_fight","require":"shadow_blade"},{"text":"Round three. Hide.","next":"deep_dream_final_hide"}]},{"id":"deep_dream_3_speak_talk","chapter":"WAVE 4 · THE KEEPER'S DREAM-HUNT","text":"\"What do you want?\" you ask him.\n\nThe Keeper considers the question. He sits down on the dead tree at the edge of the camp. He looks suddenly very tired.\n\n\"To live,\" he says, simply. \"That is all anyone ever wants. I refused to die. I do not regret it. But living has cost more than I expected. And {missing_short} — your {missing_short} — is the cleanest body of my line in eight hundred years. I will be young again in {missing_obj}. I am sorry. I do not regret that either.\"\n\nYou cannot speak.\n\n\"Round three,\" he says, almost gently.","choices":[{"text":"Round three. Speak — refuse him.","next":"deep_dream_final_speak"},{"text":"Round three. Fight. (shadow_blade)","next":"deep_dream_final_fight","require":"shadow_blade"},{"text":"Round three. Hide.","next":"deep_dream_final_hide"}]},{"id":"deep_dream_final_fight","chapter":"WAVE 4 · THE KEEPER'S DREAM-HUNT","text":"You go at him with everything you have left in the dream — every grief, every road, every bad night since {missing_short_cap} was taken.\n\nThe shadow blade glows in your hand, the way it has not since the tower. It tastes him.\n\nFor the first time he steps back.\n\nYou cut him across the cheek. A line of darkness opens in his face and what comes out is not blood. It is sleep. It is years of unspent sleep.\n\nHe touches the cut. He looks at his fingers. He looks at you.\n\n\"Oh,\" he says, very quietly. \"You will be a problem.\"\n\nThen the dream breaks like a window and you are awake at the fire, gasping, alive, your hand still wrapped around the shadow blade.\n\nYou cut him. He felt it. You will pay for it later. But he knows your face now and he knows you draw blood.","choices":[{"text":"Sit up. Watch the dark. Don't sleep again tonight.","next":"camp_rest","do":["hp:-3","flag:keeper_blooded","trust:+5","journal:Cut the Keeper in dream. He is afraid of me now. A little."]}]},{"id":"deep_dream_final_speak","chapter":"WAVE 4 · THE KEEPER'S DREAM-HUNT","text":"You say his name a third time, and this time you put a word with it. The word is no.\n\n\"You will not have {missing_obj},\" you say. \"You will not have {missing_obj}. You will not have {missing_obj}.\"\n\nThree refusals. Three is the old number.\n\nThe dream cracks at its corners. The Keeper's practiced face goes thin. He raises a hand — to do what, you do not know — and then he is pulled backward, fast, as if by a long invisible cord, into the dark behind the dead tree.\n\nHe is gone. The fire is again only a fire. Ash is breathing again. Vale is breathing again. Moth turns over in their blankets and mumbles something about bread.\n\nYou wake. Your hands shake. You are intact.\n\nHe will be back. But you held the ground.","choices":[{"text":"Stay up. Watch the stars.","next":"camp_rest","do":["flag:keeper_blooded","trust:+8","journal:Said no to the Keeper three times. He went. For now."]}]},{"id":"deep_dream_final_hide","chapter":"WAVE 4 · THE KEEPER'S DREAM-HUNT","text":"You hide one more time. You are small and quiet and the Keeper walks past you and does not find you.\n\nBut hiding is not winning. He stands at the fire and waits, patient as a debt, until the dream gives out from sheer length.\n\nYou wake exhausted. He did not catch you. He did not need to. He has a measure of you now and he will use it.\n\nAnother page is gone from the journal. You cannot remember the song {missing_short} used to hum while {missing_pron} braided your hair.","choices":[{"text":"Get up. Walk it off.","next":"camp_rest","do":["hp:-3","fear:+5","journal:Hid from the Keeper. Lost the song."]}]},{"id":"deep_bandits_1","chapter":"WAVE 4 · THE ROAD-COMPANY","text":"They have the road blocked properly. Not a fallen tree — a felled tree, freshly axed, the cut still pale. Two on the trunk, three in the brush, one with a crossbow on the slope. Veterans. You can tell by how little they move.\n\nThe one on the trunk stands. He is older than you expected — gray in his beard, an old burn down one side of his face, a cloak that was a soldier's cloak once. He raises a hand, open, palm out.\n\n\"Easy,\" he calls. \"Easy. We're not here for blood.\"\n\nHis voice has the cadence of an officer giving orders he has given a thousand times.\n\nVale, very low, beside you: \"That's a King's man. Old wars. I knew his sergeant.\"\n\nThe man on the tree says: \"We need thirty in gold. You get to walk on. Nobody dies. Don't make us do this the other way.\"\n\nWhat do you do?","choices":[{"text":"Draw. Fight your way through.","next":"deep_bandits_fight"},{"text":"Pay the thirty. (gold:30)","next":"deep_bandits_pay","require":"gold:30"},{"text":"Recruit them — these are useful hands. (trust:70)","next":"deep_bandits_recruit","require":"trust:70"},{"text":"Talk down their leader. Vale knew his sergeant.","next":"deep_bandits_talk"}]},{"id":"deep_bandits_fight","chapter":"WAVE 4 · THE ROAD-COMPANY","text":"Vale curses you in three languages and then is already moving. The crossbow lets fly. The bolt takes Ash in the shoulder — Ash who said this morning that they had a good feeling about today — and Ash goes down with a grunt that is somehow worse than a scream.\n\nIt is over in less than a minute. Five of them dead. One running. The old man with the burn down his face is on his knees in the road with Vale's blade at his throat. He is not begging. He is just looking up at Vale with old, embarrassed eyes.\n\n\"I served under your sergeant at the Loma crossing,\" he says, quietly. \"He died because the crown did not send the relief column. They did not send it because the king was sick of the war and forgot.\"\n\nVale's hand does not move on the hilt.\n\nYou go through the pockets of the dead. There is thirty in copper between all of them.\n\nYou take it. There is no good way to take it. You take it anyway.","choices":[{"text":"Patch Ash. Walk.","next":"wastes_gate","do":["hp:-3","gold:+30","journal:Fought the road-company. They were the King's old men. We took their copper."]}]},{"id":"deep_bandits_pay","chapter":"WAVE 4 · THE ROAD-COMPANY","text":"You count it out. Thirty in gold. You hand it up to the man on the tree.\n\nHe takes it without looking at it. He nods once.\n\n\"Safe road,\" he says. \"You go straight on, you'll hit the Crow's Foot inn by dark. Tell Tobish that Halric sent you, and tell him I'm still alive, and tell him I'm sorry about his sister.\"\n\nVale, beside you, has gone very still.\n\nHalric of the road-company stands aside. His people fade back into the trees. The road opens.\n\nAs you pass, the old man calls after you: \"There's worse than us further on. Keep your light close.\"","choices":[{"text":"Walk on. Tell Tobish.","next":"wastes_gate","do":["gold:-30","trust:+3","journal:Paid Halric his thirty. He knew Tobish."]}]},{"id":"deep_bandits_recruit","chapter":"WAVE 4 · THE ROAD-COMPANY","text":"You step forward. You take off your hood. You look the old man on the tree in the face.\n\n\"I'm going to the Hollow Gate,\" you say, plainly. \"The Keeper took my {missing_short}. I am going to bring {missing_obj} back or die there. You have six veterans. I have four hands and a hard road. Come with me.\"\n\nThe old man — Halric, you will learn his name in an hour — looks at you a long moment. He looks at Vale. Vale nods once, the smallest nod. He looks at his people.\n\nOne of them spits and walks into the trees. Another laughs softly and shakes his head and follows.\n\nFour stay.\n\nHalric climbs down off the tree. \"We don't take orders well,\" he warns. \"We're tired men. But we know the road past the wastes and we know what to do with a crossbow.\"\n\nThe company is bigger tonight. Your max HP goes up by the simple math of four more bodies between you and the dark. You sleep deeper than you have in weeks.","choices":[{"text":"Make camp with the new hands. Drink the bad wine.","next":"wastes_gate","do":["max_hp:+2","trust:+5","flag:road_company","journal:Recruited Halric and four of his veterans. The road feels less long."]}]},{"id":"deep_bandits_talk","chapter":"WAVE 4 · THE ROAD-COMPANY","text":"Vale steps forward before you can stop them. They pull their hood back.\n\n\"You served under Garin at Loma,\" Vale says. It is not a question.\n\nThe old man on the tree goes still. He looks at Vale. He looks for a long time. Then his shoulders come down by an inch.\n\n\"You were the squire,\" he says, slowly. \"Garin's squire. You carried his body off the field. I saw you do it. I have never told anyone what I saw at Loma.\"\n\n\"Then we have something in common,\" Vale says.\n\nThe old man — Halric, his name is Halric — sits down on the tree. He is suddenly an old man and not an officer. He says: \"We were hungry. The new king does not pay his old soldiers. The crown ate our years and forgot our names. We are not bandits. We are accountants.\"\n\nHe waves his people back into the trees. The road opens. He gives you, unprompted, a thin silver ring he has carried since the war. \"For luck,\" he says. \"Tell anyone you meet on the road that Halric let you pass.\"","choices":[{"text":"Take the ring. Honor the passage.","next":"wastes_gate","do":["trust:+10","flag:bandit_friend","rel:vale:+5","journal:Vale named Halric's sergeant. He let us through. Gave a ring."]}]},{"id":"deep_lost_city_1","chapter":"WAVE 4 · THE LOST CITY OF THE FIRST KINGS","text":"The tunnel map shows it but does not name it. A symbol like a crown with a slash through it, off the main route, in a hollow of land the road avoids.\n\nYou tell the others. Vale wants to skip it. Ash wants to see it. Moth says, very softly, \"I dreamed about a place like that.\"\n\nYou go.\n\nThe approach is a cut between two hills. The cut narrows. The cut becomes a road, paved in flat dark stone, half-eaten by root. The road bends. The road opens.\n\nIn front of you is a city.\n\nIt is not large. It is dead. It has been dead for so long that the trees inside the walls are older than the walls. Stone faces watch you from above the gate. Their eyes have been broken out on purpose, every one of them, by someone who came after.\n\nNo one speaks for a long time.\n\nFinally Vale says: \"What is this.\"\n\nYou don't know yet. But you know who knew. Whoever broke those eyes knew.","choices":[{"text":"Walk in. The gate is open.","next":"deep_lost_city_2"},{"text":"Back out. Not today.","next":"hill_search"}]},{"id":"deep_lost_city_2","chapter":"WAVE 4 · THE LOST CITY OF THE FIRST KINGS","text":"The gate is open because there is no gate, only the iron pivots where one used to be. You step through into a square. A fountain at the center, dry, its basin full of dead leaves and one perfectly intact white feather.\n\nAround the square, low buildings, their roofs collapsed. One building stands. It has a domed top and a wide flat lintel and the symbol over the door is the same crown-with-a-slash that was on the map.\n\nMoth says: \"That one. That's the one I dreamed about.\"\n\nThere are doorways into the buildings around the square — three obvious ones. The library, the domed thing, the throne hall at the far end with a hundred broken columns leading up to it.\n\nWhere do you start?","choices":[{"text":"The library. Knowledge first.","next":"deep_lost_city_library"},{"text":"The domed building. Whatever Moth dreamed.","next":"deep_lost_city_dome"},{"text":"The throne hall. Names first.","next":"deep_lost_city_throne"}]},{"id":"deep_lost_city_library","chapter":"WAVE 4 · THE LOST CITY OF THE FIRST KINGS","text":"The library has lost most of its roof. What it has not lost are stone shelves carved into the walls, with stone tablets still on them, mostly intact.\n\nThe writing is old. Older than the kingdom you know. Older, you slowly realize, than the kingdom YOU KNOW knows.\n\nAsh sounds out a word. It is a name. Then another. They are king-names. A list of king-names — but the list runs in the wrong direction, going forward in time, and at the end, in a hand that is somehow shakier than the rest, is a name you do not know followed by the words HE WILL NOT GO.\n\nThe ink is rust-brown. It is not ink.\n\nMoth is reading another tablet. They look up, face white. They say: \"This is our kingdom. This was our kingdom. Before. It says here that the first king built a door and went into the door because he refused to die.\"\n\nThe library's broken roof lets in a square of cold light. You stand in it for a long time.","choices":[{"text":"Take what you can read. Move on.","next":"deep_lost_city_dome","do":["journal:The first king refused to die. He built a door. He is the Keeper."]}]},{"id":"deep_lost_city_dome","chapter":"WAVE 4 · THE LOST CITY OF THE FIRST KINGS","text":"The dome inside is painted. The paint has held because the air is dry and the dome was sealed until you broke the door.\n\nThe painting is a map of the night sky. Not your night sky. An older one. The constellations are wrong by small amounts.\n\nAt the dome's apex is a figure. A man with a crown. He is reaching down with one long arm and his fingers are passing through a thin black arch — a Gate — and on the other side of the arch a child is reaching up.\n\nMoth makes a small sound and sits down on the floor.\n\nUnderneath the painting, around the inside of the dome at eye height, runs a line of writing. You read what you can.\n\n\"The first king made the door. He went in. He kept his name. He kept his hunger. He returns when the line is sweet enough.\"\n\n\"When the line is sweet enough,\" Ash repeats. \"Sweet enough.\"\n\nThey mean {missing_short}.","choices":[{"text":"Step out. You can't be in here right now.","next":"deep_lost_city_throne","do":["fear:+5"]}]},{"id":"deep_lost_city_throne","chapter":"WAVE 4 · THE LOST CITY OF THE FIRST KINGS","text":"The throne hall has no roof at all. The hundred columns stand open to the sky. The throne at the far end is broken on purpose — the seat carved across with a single deep gouge, top to bottom, by someone who took an axe to it long after the king was gone.\n\nVale walks the length of the hall slowly, hand on hilt. \"They broke the eyes,\" Vale says, \"and they broke the throne, and they did not burn the city. Why?\"\n\nYou think you know. They didn't burn it because burning honors a place. They left it standing as a warning.\n\nAt the foot of the throne, half-buried in centuries of dust, is a small clay tablet. You pick it up. The writing is a child's hand.\n\nIt says: HE PROMISED TO COME BACK FOR ME. HE DID NOT COME BACK.\n\nThere is no date. There is no name.\n\nYou put it in your pack. You don't know why.\n\nYou come out of the lost city heavier than you went in, but also — strangely — stronger. Knowing a thing has weight. Knowing a thing also gives you somewhere to stand.","choices":[{"text":"Out. Daylight. Air.","next":"hill_search","do":["max_hp:+3","flag:knows_keeper_origin","trust:+5","journal:Found the lost city. Read the tablets. The first king was the Keeper. There were children he did not come back for."]}]},{"id":"deep_second_offer_1","chapter":"WAVE 4 · THE KEEPER'S SECOND OFFER","text":"You stand at the foot of the Hollow Gate. The stone arch is black and the dark inside it is older than night, the way they said, the way you did not believe until now.\n\nThe wind here moves wrong.\n\nBefore you can step through, someone speaks behind you. It is the voice of the person you love most in this company. The voice you have come to listen for over fires, on roads, in the bad hours.\n\nYou turn.\n\nThey are standing too still. Their eyes are theirs. Their mouth is theirs. But the words coming out are not theirs.\n\n\"Go home,\" they say, in your beloved's voice. \"Go home with me. Marry me. I will send {missing_short} dreams of you for the rest of {missing_poss} life — good dreams, soft dreams, dreams where you are alive and happy and {missing_pron} is loved. {missing_short_cap} will not suffer. {missing_pron_cap} will not know {missing_pron} was lost. You will be old together. You will have children. The Keeper will keep his word.\"\n\nThey reach for your hand.\n\nThis is the second offer.","choices":[{"text":"Refuse. Step back. {missing_short_cap} is not a dream.","next":"deep_second_offer_refuse"},{"text":"Accept. Take their hand. Go home.","next":"deep_second_offer_accept"},{"text":"Speak to your beloved. Not to what's wearing them.","next":"deep_second_offer_speak"}]},{"id":"deep_second_offer_refuse","chapter":"WAVE 4 · THE KEEPER'S SECOND OFFER","text":"You step back. You do not look away from them, because looking away would feel like giving them up, and they are not gone. They are being worn.\n\n\"No,\" you say. \"I came for {missing_short}. I am not going home until I have {missing_obj} with me. {missing_pron_cap} is not a dream I am willing to have for the rest of my life.\"\n\nThe thing in your beloved's face smiles. It is a small smile. It is almost — almost — proud.\n\n\"Then come through,\" it says, in your beloved's voice, and the voice cracks at the end into something older, and your beloved is back in their own body, gasping, hands shaking, eyes wet, and the Keeper is gone from them like a coat shrugged off.\n\nThey grab you. Hard. They are crying.\n\n\"I'm sorry,\" they say. \"I'm sorry. I didn't know how to fight {missing_obj}.\"\n\n\"You didn't have to,\" you say. \"I did.\"\n\nYou turn back to the Gate. It is waiting.","choices":[{"text":"Take their hand. Step through together.","next":"hollow_gate","do":["trust:+10","flag:refused_second_offer","journal:The Keeper offered to send {missing_short} dreams of me. I said no."]}]},{"id":"deep_second_offer_accept","chapter":"WAVE 4 · THE KEEPER'S SECOND OFFER","text":"You take their hand.\n\nIt is warm. It is your beloved's hand. It has held you through three bad nights and you know it the way you know your own.\n\n\"All right,\" you say, very quietly. \"All right. I am tired.\"\n\nThe thing in your beloved's face smiles wider. It nods, kindly. Behind you, the Gate dims as a fire dims when no one feeds it.\n\nYour beloved squeezes your hand. The squeeze is real. The squeeze is them, for a second, panicked, mouthing NO, NO, NO with no sound.\n\nThen they are gone, and your beloved is whole again, and they kiss you, and you go home together. The road is short. The dreams the Keeper sends you of {missing_short} are very good.\n\nYou live a long life.\n\nYou wake, sometimes, in the dark, with a feeling that you traded something you should not have traded. But you cannot remember what.\n\nThis is one ending. There are others.","choices":[{"text":"[ending: the gentle betrayal]","next":"finale_start","do":["flag:sacrifice_ending","journal:I took the Keeper's second offer. I went home."]}]},{"id":"deep_second_offer_speak","chapter":"WAVE 4 · THE KEEPER'S SECOND OFFER","text":"You ignore the words and look at your beloved's eyes.\n\n\"Are you in there?\" you ask. \"Are you hearing this?\"\n\nThe mouth that is not theirs keeps talking. \"They cannot hear you. I am holding their tongue. Go home with me. Marry me. I will send {missing_short} dreams —\"\n\nYou interrupt. You say your beloved's name. Just their name. The way you say it when you are alone with them.\n\nSomething behind their eyes lurches.\n\nYou say it again.\n\nA tear rolls out of one of their eyes. The mouth keeps trying to make the Keeper's offer, but the words come out broken now, stuttering. The eyes — the eyes are your beloved's, and they are SCREAMING.\n\nYou put your hand against their cheek. \"Come back,\" you say.\n\nThey come back. With a wrench, with a sob, they come back. They sag against you. The Keeper goes out of them like a held breath let go.\n\n\"I'm here,\" your beloved whispers. \"I'm here.\"\n\nThe Gate is still waiting.","choices":[{"text":"Hold them a moment. Then walk through together.","next":"hollow_gate","do":["trust:+10","flag:refused_second_offer","journal:Called my beloved back. The Keeper could not keep them. Step through together."]}]},{"id":"deep_camp_aftermath","chapter":"WAVE 4 · CAMP","text":"Morning. The fire is gray ash. Someone has banked it, badly, in the night.\n\nMoth is asleep against Ash's shoulder. Ash is awake, watching you. They have been watching you for some time.\n\n\"You were saying his name,\" Ash says, quietly. \"In your sleep. The Keeper's. You sounded like you were arguing with him.\"\n\nYou don't answer.\n\n\"Did you win?\" Ash asks.\n\nYou think about it.\n\nYou think you might have. A little. You don't say so out loud, because saying so out loud feels like it would unmake it.\n\nAsh nods, as if that itself were an answer. They pass you the waterskin. The water is cold and good.\n\nVale comes back from the perimeter and dumps an armful of dry brush on the fire. \"Up,\" they say. \"Road's not getting shorter.\"\n\nYou get up. The pages of the journal are fewer than they were. But you are still you. Mostly.","choices":[{"text":"Pack. March.","next":"camp_rest"}]},{"id":"deep_wraith_followup","chapter":"WAVE 4 · WHAT THE WRAITH LEFT","text":"The morning after you put it down, you find one of its threads tangled in your cloak — a single strand of gray that is not your hair and not anyone's hair.\n\nYou hold it up to the light. It moves on its own.\n\nAsh sees and goes pale. \"Burn it,\" they say.\n\nVale, behind you: \"Keep it. We may need to prove what it was.\"\n\nYou keep it. You put it in the bottom of your pack where it cannot whisper to you. You think about {missing_short}'s voice at the end of the screaming and you think about who else's voices were in there. You did not recognize three of them.\n\nThere were three other people the Keeper has taken. Or three other people he will take.\n\nYou put the question to the journal because you do not want to put it to your friends yet. Whose voices were those?","choices":[{"text":"Walk on. Don't tell them. Yet.","next":"wastes_alone","do":["journal:Kept a thread of the wraith. Three voices in it I didn't know."]}]},{"id":"deep_basement_followup","chapter":"WAVE 4 · WHAT THE BASEMENT LEFT","text":"You come up the stair into the menagerie. The thing that sings in a child's voice has stopped singing. It is watching you with eyes you had not noticed before.\n\nIt says, in the child's voice: \"You went down.\"\n\n\"Yes,\" you say.\n\n\"He told you,\" it says. \"He told you the thing.\"\n\n\"Yes,\" you say.\n\nIt is quiet a moment. Then: \"Will you come back for me?\"\n\nYou do not answer. You cannot, yet, promise that. You can lie. You can lie easily — it is only a thing in a cage, it sings in a child's voice but it is not a child, you tell yourself.\n\nIt is watching you to see if you will lie.\n\nWhat do you do?","choices":[{"text":"Tell it the truth. \"I don't know.\"","next":"tower_menagerie","do":["trust:+3","journal:The thing in the cage asked if I would come back for it. I said I don't know."]},{"text":"Promise. You can break the promise later if you have to.","next":"tower_menagerie","do":["fear:+3","flag:menagerie_promise","journal:Promised the thing in the cage I would come back. We'll see."]},{"text":"Walk past it without speaking.","next":"tower_menagerie","do":["fear:+5"]}]},{"id":"deep_city_aftermath","chapter":"WAVE 4 · AFTER THE CITY","text":"Whatever you did in the city, the city is changed and you are changed.\n\nThe road south smells of smoke for two days. Birds keep finding you. They land on the pack and look at you and fly off again. Vale says, dryly, that this means somebody dead is paying attention.\n\nAt the second campfire after the city, Moth comes to sit next to you. They have been quiet since.\n\n\"I keep thinking about the baker's wife,\" they say. \"With the warm loaf. She asked us to remember.\"\n\n\"I remember,\" you say.\n\nMoth nods. They lean their shoulder against yours. After a while they say, very small: \"I want to be the kind of person she meant when she said traveler. I want to come back to that city someday and be that.\"\n\nYou put an arm around them. You think you do too.\n\nWhat does the journal say tonight?","choices":[{"text":"\"I am going to deserve the word she used for me.\"","next":"camp_rest","do":["rel:moth:+5","journal:Want to deserve the word the baker's wife used. Traveler."]}]},{"id":"deep_lost_city_followup","chapter":"WAVE 4 · WHAT THE LOST CITY LEFT","text":"That night you take the clay tablet out of your pack and turn it in the firelight. HE PROMISED TO COME BACK FOR ME. HE DID NOT COME BACK.\n\nAsh reads it over your shoulder. Their face changes.\n\n\"There were others,\" they say, slowly. \"Before {missing_short}. Before us. There have been kings' children he has taken since the first one and he has not come back for any of them.\"\n\nVale, who has been pretending not to listen: \"Then we are not the first to walk this road. We are only the first who came armed enough to know what we were walking into.\"\n\nMoth: \"What happened to the others? Inside?\"\n\nNo one has an answer.\n\nYou put the tablet back. You sleep badly. You dream of a small room behind the Gate where a hundred children are sitting in a circle, waiting, very patient, for someone to come back for them.\n\n{missing_short_cap} is one of them.","choices":[{"text":"Wake. Walk. Faster.","next":"camp_rest","do":["fear:+5","journal:Dreamed of the children behind the Gate. {missing_short_cap} is one of them. I will not be late."]}]},{"id":"deep_bandits_camp","chapter":"WAVE 4 · THE ROAD-COMPANY","text":"If you took Halric and his veterans on, the first night in their company is loud.\n\nThey have bad wine and they share it. They have bad songs and they sing them. One of them — a woman with a scar across her chin and a laugh like a saw — knows Vale, and the two of them sit a long time by the fire saying nothing at all, which is the best kind of conversation old soldiers have.\n\nHalric himself comes to sit with you.\n\n\"You told me,\" he says, \"that the Keeper took your {missing_short}. You did not tell me you were going to the Hollow Gate to bring {missing_obj} home.\"\n\n\"I'm going to the Hollow Gate to bring {missing_obj} home,\" you say.\n\nHe is quiet a long while.\n\nThen: \"Lad. Lass. Friend. We will get you to the Gate. We will not go through it. I want you to know that now.\"\n\n\"I know,\" you say. \"That's all I'm asking.\"\n\nHe nods. He claps you on the shoulder and goes back to his wine.\n\nYou sleep better than you have in weeks.","choices":[{"text":"Sleep. Tomorrow is the wastes.","next":"wastes_ride","do":["hp:+3","rel:vale:+3","journal:Halric will get me to the Gate. He will not go through. Fair enough."]}]},{"id":"deep_threshold_extra","chapter":"WAVE 4 · THRESHOLD","text":"Before the Gate, before the second offer, before any of it — you stop on the last rise of road and look back.\n\nThe kingdom is small from here. You can see, very faintly, the smoke of the city where you fought, or did not fight. You can see the line of the witch's wood. You can see, if you squint, the tower.\n\nMoth comes up beside you. They do not say anything for a long time.\n\nThen: \"Do you think we'll come back this way?\"\n\nYou think about lying. You don't.\n\n\"I don't know,\" you say. \"I hope so. I want to come back this way with {missing_short} between us, complaining about the road.\"\n\nMoth laughs. It is a real laugh. Their hand finds yours.\n\n\"All right,\" they say. \"Then let's go get {missing_obj}.\"\n\nThe wind smells like the wastes ahead. You start down.","choices":[{"text":"Down to the Gate.","next":"threshold_1","do":["rel:moth:+5","trust:+3","journal:Stood on the last rise. Said the truth out loud. Going to get {missing_short}."]}]},{"id":"deep_finale_method_extra","chapter":"WAVE 4 · METHOD","text":"Before you step through, you take inventory in the way old soldiers do — not of gear, of self.\n\nWhat you have: a blade or a knife. A talisman from the steward, perhaps. The Hedge Witch's word. Halric's ring. A tablet from a city older than this kingdom. A thread from a wraith. A promise from a thing in a cage you may or may not keep. The voice of {missing_short} at the end of a scream.\n\nWhat you do not have: certainty.\n\nVale, at your elbow: \"You don't have to know how it ends. You only have to know how it starts. It starts with you walking through. That's all.\"\n\nYou look at the Gate. You look at the company. You look at the small list of things in your pack that, between them, name everything you have become on this road.\n\nIt is enough. It will have to be enough.\n\nWhat is the first sentence on the other side?","choices":[{"text":"\"I came for {missing_short}.\"","next":"finale_method","do":["trust:+5","journal:Walked through the Gate. First sentence: I came for {missing_short}."]}]},{"id":"deep_wraith_lingers","chapter":"WAVE 4 · THE WRAITH LINGERS","text":"If the wraith survived your first night, it is here on the second.\n\nAt dusk, the same gray shape, a little closer to the road this time. It has learned the cadence of your walking. It is walking, now, in the rhythm of your feet.\n\nThe company is quieter today. Moth has stopped speaking out loud, in case it copies them. Ash is whispering songs under their breath in a language Vale taught them — the wraith does not have that language yet.\n\nYou make camp earlier. You build the fire higher. You have one more chance.\n\nWhat have you found on the road today that you did not have yesterday?","choices":[{"text":"A warding draught — bartered from a charcoal-burner.","next":"deep_wraith_road_light","do":["add_item:warding_draught"]},{"text":"The Keeper's name — Halric's wife was a hedge-priest. (require: flag:bandit_friend)","next":"deep_wraith_road_name","require":"flag:bandit_friend","do":["flag:knows_keeper_name"]},{"text":"Nothing new. You will simply outlast it tonight.","next":"deep_wraith_road_fail"}]},{"id":"deep_basement_cage_followup","chapter":"WAVE 4 · THE CAGE","text":"If you promised the thing in the cage you would come back for it, that promise sits in you on the road. It does not get lighter.\n\nMoth, at the second fire after the tower, says quietly: \"You promised something in there. Didn't you.\"\n\nYou tell them. About the thing in the menagerie. About the child's voice.\n\nMoth is quiet a long time.\n\nThen: \"When we have {missing_short}. When we are coming back. We'll stop. We'll let it out. I'll help you.\"\n\nYou had not realized how heavy the promise was until they offered to carry it with you.\n\nYou nod. You cannot speak for a minute.\n\nAsh, on the other side of the fire, pretending not to listen, says: \"I'll help too.\"\n\nVale, without looking up from sharpening their blade: \"Aye.\"\n\nThe road is long. But it has people on it.","choices":[{"text":"Bank the fire. Sleep.","next":"camp_rest","do":["trust:+8","rel:moth:+8","rel:ash:+5","rel:vale:+3","journal:They will help me come back for the thing in the cage."]}]}]},"story/chapter_17.json":{"scenes":[{"id":"u17_start","chapter":"CHAPTER XVII · THE THRESHOLD","text":"The Keeper flees.\n\nHe should not be able to. He is the door and the door does not move, and yet the tall hollow shape of him is running, shrinking backward into the arch, into the dark that has no far wall.\n\nBehind you the realm you know is quiet again. The heir is safe. The story is over. Everyone will tell you it is over.\n\nBut the Keeper is running, and a thing that runs is a thing that is afraid, and a thing that is afraid has somewhere it does not want you to go.\n\nThe arch hums against your teeth. Cold pours out of it like water finding a low place.","choices":[{"text":"Step through, after him.","next":"u17_arrival"},{"text":"Stop at the threshold. Look first.","next":"u17_lip"}]},{"id":"u17_lip","chapter":"CHAPTER XVII · THE THRESHOLD","text":"You stand at the very lip of the arch, one boot on grass, one boot on nothing.\n\nThe nothing is not empty. Look long enough and it resolves: a hillside like this hillside, a sky like this sky, all of it hung upside down and filled with black water instead of air.\n\nYour own reflection stares up at you from that drowned country. It is a half-second slow. When you lift your hand, it lifts its hand after, the way an echo answers a shout.\n\nThe Keeper's grey shape is already small down there, wading away.","choices":[{"text":"Enough looking. Go through.","next":"u17_arrival"},{"text":"Steel yourself against the fear first, then step through.","next":"u17_arrival","check":{"stat":"fear","dc":12,"pass":"u17_arrival","fail":"u17_arrival","onPass":["trust:+3","journal:I looked into the drowned country and did not flinch."],"onFail":["fear:+5"]}}]},{"id":"u17_arrival","chapter":"CHAPTER XVII · THE THRESHOLD","text":"You step through.\n\nFor one heartbeat there is no up, no down — only the crossing, made of the sound of every door you have ever heard close, played backward all at once. Then the world inverts. You do not fall so much as get poured, and you land on your hands and knees on cold wet stone that was, a moment ago, the empty air above the arch.\n\nYou stand in the drowned twin of the hillside.\n\nEverything is here that was there. The doorway. The hills. A sky the colour of a bruise going yellow at the edges. But it is all reflected, all reversed — your sword-hand is your wrong hand now, your heart beats on the side it should not.\n\nAnd it is all underwater without being wet. You breathe. The air is thick and slow and tastes of pond-bottom, but it is air. Sound arrives late. When a pebble slips under your boot you hear it a moment after you feel it.\n\nBehind you, the arch you came through still stands — but from this side it is a hole in still black water, and your own reflection hangs in it, watching you, not quite matching.","choices":[{"text":"Touch the reflection in the arch.","next":"u17_mirror_touch"},{"text":"Turn your back on it. Find the Keeper's trail.","next":"u17_trail"},{"text":"Take stock. Speak aloud, to hear your own voice.","next":"u17_speak"}]},{"id":"u17_speak","chapter":"CHAPTER XVII · THE THRESHOLD","text":"\"I am here,\" you say. \"I chose this.\"\n\nThe words leave your mouth and hang in the thick air, visible almost, like breath on a cold morning. Then they drift down and pool at your feet and are gone.\n\nA voice answers from nowhere and everywhere, patient as silt settling:\n\n\"Few speak, here. The taken forget they can. You have not been here long enough to forget. Enjoy it. It does not last.\"\n\nYou cannot tell if the voice is the Keeper, or the place itself, or your own reflection learning to lie.","choices":[{"text":"\"Who's speaking? Teach me this place.\"","next":"u17_rules_underland"},{"text":"Say nothing more. Find the trail.","next":"u17_trail"}]},{"id":"u17_rules_underland","chapter":"CHAPTER XVII · THE THRESHOLD","text":"\"I am the country,\" the voice says. \"Or its memory of having once been spoken to. Down here that is nearly the same thing. This is the Underland — the realm you came from, drowned and reversed. Every rule you trust is bent here, but two are iron.\n\n\"The first rule is that distance lies. A thing that looks close is far. A thing that looks far is already at your shoulder. Do not trust your eyes to measure. Trust your feet, and count your steps, and never run — running is how the taken get taken.\n\n\"The second rule you must learn from a mirror, not from me. Go and find one. There is always a mirror. This is a country made of them.\"\n\nThe voice thins, like a ripple reaching the edge of a pond, and is gone.\n\nYou understand the Underland a little now. Enough to be afraid of it correctly.","choices":[{"text":"Go to the reflection in the arch — the nearest mirror.","next":"u17_mirror_touch","do":["flag:region_threshold","journal:Underland rule one: distance lies. Never run. Count your steps."]},{"text":"Find the Keeper's trail instead; learn mirrors later.","next":"u17_trail","do":["flag:region_threshold"]}]},{"id":"u17_mirror_touch","chapter":"CHAPTER XVII · THE THRESHOLD","text":"You reach into the black water of the arch and touch your own reflected hand.\n\nIt is warm. Warmer than you. Your reflection's fingers close gently around yours, and for one impossible moment you are on both sides of the glass at once — the one who came through, and the one who stayed drowned and waiting all along.\n\nThe reflection speaks with your voice, but slower, kinder, more tired:\n\n\"You should know the law before you go deeper. Down here a reflection can be truer than the thing it reflects. Break a mirror and you free what it held — or you loose it. There is no third outcome. Every knight, every ghost, every door in this country is bound by this. So are you, now.\"","choices":[{"text":"\"Truer than the thing? How?\"","next":"u17_mirror_law"},{"text":"\"What do you hold? What would breaking you loose?\"","next":"u17_mirror_self"}]},{"id":"u17_mirror_self","chapter":"CHAPTER XVII · THE THRESHOLD","text":"Your reflection almost smiles.\n\n\"Me? I hold you. The part of you that would have stayed on the safe side and called it over. Break this glass and you loose that part into the country — and it will wear your face and undo everything you do.\n\n\"So do not break me. Not this one. But you will find others, and some of them will beg to be broken, and some of them will lie. Learning which is which is how you survive the Underland.\"\n\nThe grip on your hand loosens.","choices":[{"text":"\"I understand the law.\"","next":"u17_mirror_law"}]},{"id":"u17_mirror_law","chapter":"CHAPTER XVII · THE THRESHOLD","text":"You draw your hand back out of the water. It is cold again the moment it leaves the glass.\n\nThe Mirror Law settles into you like a stone into silt. In this country, what is reflected can be more real than what stands. Break a mirror to free — or to loose. Never carelessly. Never in anger.\n\nYou feel, obscurely, that you have just been given the only rulebook this place will ever offer, and that it fits on the back of your hand.","choices":[{"text":"Now find the Keeper's trail.","next":"u17_trail","do":["flag:codex_mirror","journal:The Mirror Law: a reflection can be truer than the thing. Break a mirror to free what it holds — or loose it. Never carelessly."]}]},{"id":"u17_trail","chapter":"CHAPTER XVII · THE THRESHOLD","text":"The Keeper left a trail. Not footprints — the wet stone takes no prints — but a wake. A line of stiller water in the moving air, a seam where the country has been parted and not yet closed.\n\nIt leads down, always down, toward a low place where the reflected hills fold into a valley you do not remember from the world above.\n\nHalfway to the valley the seam splits. One branch bends left, along a shelf of black rock. One goes right, down into a stand of reeds taller than a rider.","choices":[{"text":"Take the left branch, along the rock shelf.","next":"u17_shelf"},{"text":"Take the right branch, into the reeds.","next":"u17_reeds"},{"text":"Study the split. Read the water the way Ash taught you.","next":"u17_read_pass","check":{"stat":"trust","dc":13,"pass":"u17_read_pass","fail":"u17_read_fail"}}]},{"id":"u17_read_pass","chapter":"CHAPTER XVII · THE THRESHOLD","text":"The left seam is already healing — old, closing. The right one still trembles. He went right, into the reeds, and not long ago.\n\nMore than that: you see now that the left branch is a lure. The country made it, the way a hunted thing folds a false trail. If you had run down it you would have run a long way from him.\n\nYou did not run. Rule one.","choices":[{"text":"Follow the true trail into the reeds.","next":"u17_reeds","do":["trust:+4","journal:Read the Keeper's wake. The left path was a lure. He went into the reeds."]}]},{"id":"u17_read_fail","chapter":"CHAPTER XVII · THE THRESHOLD","text":"The two seams look identical to you. Water is water. You pick the left branch because it is easier walking, and the country lets you, which should have warned you.\n\nHalf a mile along the shelf the seam simply stops, healed over, a dead end. You have wasted breath and nerve on a false trail the Underland folded to fool you.","choices":[{"text":"Backtrack. Take the reeds this time.","next":"u17_reeds","do":["fear:+4","journal:Followed a false trail down here. The country lies with its own geography."]}]},{"id":"u17_shelf","chapter":"CHAPTER XVII · THE THRESHOLD","text":"The rock shelf runs along the valley's rim. Below you, the drowned valley opens: a whole sunk country of roofs and reeds and one grey shape wading through it, unhurried now, as though it knows exactly how lost you are.\n\nThe shelf is easy walking. Too easy. Rule one whispers in your ear — a thing that looks close is far, a thing that looks far is already at your shoulder.\n\nYou count your steps. At two hundred the shelf ends at a fall of black stone, and the seam you were following is old here, healed, cold. This was the false path.","choices":[{"text":"Climb down into the valley and cut across to the reeds.","next":"u17_reeds","do":["journal:The rock shelf was the lure. He never came this way."]},{"text":"Rest a moment on the fallen stone, and steady yourself.","next":"u17_shelf_rest"}]},{"id":"u17_shelf_rest","chapter":"CHAPTER XVII · THE THRESHOLD","text":"You sit on the fallen stone. The Underland does not let you rest so much as it lets you stop.\n\nIn the flat black water at your feet you catch a reflection that is not yours: a hunched shape with a grey flame in its hands, small, childlike, far off in the valley. It looks up as if it feels you looking. Then a reed sways and it is gone.\n\nYou file it away. Something lives down here besides the Keeper.","choices":[{"text":"Down to the reeds, and the true trail.","next":"u17_reeds","do":["journal:Saw a small figure with a grey flame in the drowned valley. Not the Keeper. Something else."]}]},{"id":"u17_reeds","chapter":"CHAPTER XVII · THE THRESHOLD","text":"The reeds close over your head. They are black and wet and taller than a mounted knight, and they whisper against one another in a language that is almost words.\n\nThe Keeper's wake runs true here, fresh, still trembling. You are gaining on him.\n\nThe reeds thin. Ahead, half-sunk in the black water, stands a shape you did not expect: a gatehouse. A drowned twin of some threshold in the world above — a low arch of wet stone, a lintel, and set into the mud beneath it, catching no light, a key.","choices":[{"text":"Wade to the gatehouse and take the key.","next":"u17_key_reach"},{"text":"Watch the gatehouse first. Nothing here is free.","next":"u17_key_watch"}]},{"id":"u17_key_watch","chapter":"CHAPTER XVII · THE THRESHOLD","text":"You hold in the reeds and watch.\n\nThe key lies in the mud beneath the drowned arch. Around it the water is stiller than still — a held-breath stillness. A dragonfly the size of your thumb lands on the lintel, walks toward the key, and simply stops existing, folded quietly out of the world.\n\nThe key is guarded. Not by a beast — by the country itself. It will take you the same way it took the dragonfly, unless you approach it correctly.","choices":[{"text":"Approach slowly, naming each step aloud. (Rule one)","next":"u17_key_named"},{"text":"Snatch it fast and get clear.","next":"u17_key_grab"}]},{"id":"u17_key_reach","chapter":"CHAPTER XVII · THE THRESHOLD","text":"You wade out toward the drowned gatehouse. The water thickens around your knees, then your waist, resisting you like cold syrup.\n\nThe closer you get, the farther the key seems — distance lies here, and it is lying to you now, stretching the last few feet into a long slow forever. If you push straight for it you will wade until the country decides you have waded enough.","choices":[{"text":"Stop pushing. Approach slow, naming each step.","next":"u17_key_named"},{"text":"Push through by main strength.","next":"u17_key_grab"}]},{"id":"u17_key_named","chapter":"CHAPTER XVII · THE THRESHOLD","text":"You stop fighting the water. You take one step and name it: \"One.\" The distance shortens by exactly one step's worth, no lie in it. \"Two.\" Another. \"Three.\"\n\nBy naming them you rob the country of its lever. It cannot stretch a step you have counted. At \"seven\" your hand closes on the key.\n\nIt is iron gone soft-black with water, warm as a living thing, and heavier than iron should be. The moment you lift it the held-breath stillness lets go, and the whole gatehouse exhales.","choices":[{"text":"Take the key. Wade back to the reeds.","next":"u17_key_got","do":["add_item:gate_key","trust:+3","journal:Took the gate_key from the drowned gatehouse by naming each step. The country cannot stretch what you have counted."]}]},{"id":"u17_key_grab","chapter":"CHAPTER XVII · THE THRESHOLD","text":"You lunge for it.\n\nThe water fights you. This is a skill check of nerve against a country that wants to fold you away like a dragonfly — and you are doing the one thing rule one forbids. You are hurrying.","choices":[{"text":"Commit — seize the key, hand cutting through the thick water.","next":"u17_key_grab_pass","check":{"stat":"fear","dc":14,"pass":"u17_key_grab_pass","fail":"u17_key_grab_fail"}},{"text":"Lose your nerve. Pull back and approach slow, naming each step.","next":"u17_key_named"}]},{"id":"u17_key_grab_pass","chapter":"CHAPTER XVII · THE THRESHOLD","text":"Your fingers close on the key a hair before the stillness closes on you. You wrench it free and throw yourself backward, and the fold of the world snaps shut on the empty place where your hand was.\n\nYou have the key. You also have a cold certainty, right down your spine, that you will not get away with breaking rule one twice.","choices":[{"text":"Back to the reeds, key in hand.","next":"u17_key_got","do":["add_item:gate_key","fear:+3","journal:Snatched the gate_key against the country's grip. Barely kept my hand."]}]},{"id":"u17_key_grab_fail","chapter":"CHAPTER XVII · THE THRESHOLD","text":"The stillness closes first.\n\nThe world folds around your wrist — not pain, exactly, but a terrible cold subtraction, as if your hand is being asked to agree it was never there. You tear free and reel back into the reeds, breath sawing, and stare at your fingers to be sure they all came with you.\n\nThey did. Barely. The key still lies in the mud, waiting.","choices":[{"text":"Try again — slowly this time, naming each step.","next":"u17_key_named","do":["fear:+5","hp:-2","journal:The country nearly folded my hand away. Never hurry down here."]}]},{"id":"u17_key_got","chapter":"CHAPTER XVII · THE THRESHOLD","text":"Back in the shelter of the reeds you turn the key over. It has no shape you recognise — no ward, no bit — just a stem of soft-black iron and, at its head, a hollow ring, like a keyhole looking for a key rather than the other way round.\n\nYou understand, without being told, that this is the gate_key: the thing that opens doors in the Underland, and maybe one door back out of it. You do not intend to test that hope yet.\n\nThe Keeper's wake runs on past the gatehouse, into a dark where the reeds give way to open water and a far shore you cannot see.","choices":[{"text":"Follow the wake toward the far shore.","next":"u17_dark_water"}]},{"id":"u17_dark_water","chapter":"CHAPTER XVII · THE THRESHOLD","text":"The reeds end. Before you lies open black water under a bruise-yellow sky, and the far shore is a rumour, a smudge, a place rule one says could be a mile off or one step away.\n\nAnd it is dark. Not night-dark — the Underland has no night — but a dark that lives in the water itself, a dark that thickens the farther you look, until the wake you are following simply dissolves into it.\n\nYou cannot follow what you cannot see. You need a light. And the only light in this whole drowned country is grey, and small, and carried by whatever you glimpsed from the shelf.","choices":[{"text":"Call out to the small light in the dark.","next":"u17_hail_light"},{"text":"Wade in blind and feel for the wake with your feet.","next":"u17_blind_wade"}]},{"id":"u17_blind_wade","chapter":"CHAPTER XVII · THE THRESHOLD","text":"You wade into the black water, feeling for the seam of the wake with your boots, arms out in the dark.\n\nThe cold climbs your legs. The far shore does not come closer no matter how you count. Somewhere out in the black, something with the size of a whale and the patience of a stone moves once, and the water lifts you gently and sets you down, as if reminding you whose house this is.\n\nYou are not going to cross this blind. You need the light after all.","choices":[{"text":"Wade back. Call out to the grey light.","next":"u17_hail_light","do":["fear:+3"]}]},{"id":"u17_hail_light","chapter":"CHAPTER XVII · THE THRESHOLD","text":"\"Hello, light!\" you call, and your voice hangs and drifts and pools the way it did before. \"I'm looking for a light to cross by!\"\n\nFor a long time, nothing.\n\nThen, far out on the black water — or close, you cannot tell — the grey flame turns toward you. It comes without any sound of wading, without a wake, until all at once it is right at the reed's edge, held in the two small hands of a child who is not entirely a child.","choices":[{"text":"Kneel to the child's level. Speak gently.","next":"u17_child"},{"text":"Stand your ground and ask what it is.","next":"u17_child"}]},{"id":"u17_child","chapter":"CHAPTER XVII · THE THRESHOLD","text":"The child is grey as everything here, and translucent at the edges, like a candle-flame you can see the wall through. In its cupped hands burns a grey fire that gives no heat and casts no shadow — only a soft, cold, seeing light.\n\n\"You spoke to me,\" it says, wondering. \"They almost never speak. They just follow. Are you dead? You don't look dead. You look loud.\"\n\nIt tilts its head.\n\n\"I light the way for the ones who are dead and don't know it yet. That's my whole job. I'm not sure if you count. You might. Everyone down here counts, a little.\"","choices":[{"text":"\"I'm not dead. I came in on my own feet — and I could show you the way out.\"","next":"u17_child_ask","do":["trust:+3"]},{"text":"\"Will you light my way across?\"","next":"u17_child_ask"}]},{"id":"u17_child_ask","chapter":"CHAPTER XVII · THE THRESHOLD","text":"The child considers you for a long, wet moment.\n\n\"I can't give you my flame,\" it says. \"It's the only me there is. But I can give you a lantern to carry a piece of it. You have to make the lantern, though. From ash. There's always ash, at a threshold. The old fires burned here first.\"\n\nIt points a grey finger back the way you came, to the drowned gatehouse, where a cold black hearth sits under the arch, full of ash that has never once been warm.","choices":[{"text":"\"Show me how.\"","next":"u17_lantern_make"},{"text":"\"Why help me at all?\"","next":"u17_child_why"}]},{"id":"u17_child_why","chapter":"CHAPTER XVII · THE THRESHOLD","text":"The child looks down at its own grey flame.\n\n\"Because you spoke to me,\" it says simply. \"Because you asked instead of took. Down here that is so rare it is almost a currency. The Keeper never asks. He only keeps. You asked.\"\n\nIt lifts its eyes.\n\n\"And because I would like, very much, to see the way out lit for once, instead of only lighting it for other people. Even if it isn't for me. Even if I only get to watch you use it. That would be enough. That would be a lot.\"\n\nYou will remember this later, when it matters.","choices":[{"text":"\"Then let's make the lantern.\"","next":"u17_lantern_make","do":["trust:+3","journal:The Lantern-child helps because I asked instead of took. It wants only to see the way out lit — even if not for itself."]}]},{"id":"u17_lantern_make","chapter":"CHAPTER XVII · THE THRESHOLD","text":"The child leads you back to the drowned gatehouse and the cold black hearth beneath its arch.\n\n\"Scoop the ash into your hands,\" it says. \"Shape it. It wants to be a lantern. It has wanted to be a lantern for a thousand years, it just needed someone to ask it.\"\n\nYou kneel. The ash is dry despite the water everywhere, and it comes together under your fingers like wet clay, holding whatever shape you give it — a small cage of ash, a handle, a hollow where a flame could sit.","choices":[{"text":"Shape it carefully, talking to the ash the way you gentle a frightened animal.","next":"u17_lantern_pass","check":{"stat":"trust","dc":12,"pass":"u17_lantern_pass","fail":"u17_lantern_soft"}},{"text":"Just cup a rough shape; good enough.","next":"u17_lantern_soft"}]},{"id":"u17_lantern_pass","chapter":"CHAPTER XVII · THE THRESHOLD","text":"The ash sets hard and true, black and light as a wasp's nest, every bar of the little cage whole. The child leans in, breathes a thread of its grey flame into the hollow, and the lantern catches.\n\nIt gives the same cold seeing-light — no heat, no shadow — but it is yours now, to carry into the dark. An ash-lantern, made by asking.\n\n\"There,\" the child says, pleased in a way that makes your chest ache. \"Now you can see the ones who are dead and don't know it. And the way across. And, if you are very lucky, the way out.\"","choices":[{"text":"Take the ash-lantern. Thank the child.","next":"u17_cross_water","do":["add_item:ash_lantern","trust:+4","journal:Made the ash_lantern from a thousand-year hearth, lit by the Lantern-child's grey flame. It shows the dead, and the way."]}]},{"id":"u17_lantern_soft","chapter":"CHAPTER XVII · THE THRESHOLD","text":"You cup the ash into a rough cage. It holds — barely, one bar crumbling and re-forming as you steady it. The child sighs, breathes its grey thread into the hollow anyway, and the lantern catches, dimmer than it might have, but lit.\n\n\"It'll do,\" the child says. \"It'll get you across. Be gentle with it. It wasn't asked as kindly as it wanted.\"\n\nAn ash-lantern, all the same. Cold light in your hand.","choices":[{"text":"Take the ash-lantern. Thank the child.","next":"u17_cross_water","do":["add_item:ash_lantern","trust:+1","journal:Made the ash_lantern in a hurry. It lit, dimmer than it could have. Be gentle with it."]}]},{"id":"u17_cross_water","chapter":"CHAPTER XVII · THE THRESHOLD","text":"You wade into the black water with the ash-lantern held high, and its cold light does what no torch could: it does not push the dark back, it makes the dark honest. In its glow the wake of the Keeper reappears, a bright seam running dead ahead. And in its glow you see them — the taken, standing waist-deep all across the water, hundreds of them, patient, listening, dead and not knowing.\n\nNone of them look at you. You keep the lantern up and your steps counted, and you wade the true seam between them, and they let you pass like reeds letting a boat through.\n\nThe far shore rises out of the dark: a black strand, and beyond it, low hills the colour of drowned iron, and a road.","choices":[{"text":"Step onto the far shore.","next":"u17_far_shore"}]},{"id":"u17_far_shore","chapter":"CHAPTER XVII · THE THRESHOLD","text":"You come ashore dripping the country's cold. Behind you the black water closes over your wake. The taken have already forgotten you were ever there.\n\nThe Lantern-child stands beside you on the strand, small and grey and looking up the dark road with something like dread and something like relief.\n\n\"That's the Ashen March,\" it says. \"The dead-mirror of the caravan road. Everyone who ever walked to the Gate walks it still, down here, not knowing they arrived. If you're going after the Keeper, you go through them.\"\n\nIt slips its small grey hand toward yours, then hesitates, unsure it is allowed.","choices":[{"text":"Take the child's hand. \"Then we walk it together.\"","next":"u17_child_join","do":["companion:lumen","trust:+3","journal:The Lantern-child (lumen) walks with me now. Ahead: the Ashen March, the dead-mirror of the road."]},{"text":"\"Stay behind me. Point the way.\" (Walk on alone-ish)","next":"u17_child_follow"}]},{"id":"u17_child_join","chapter":"CHAPTER XVII · THE THRESHOLD","text":"The child's hand is cold and light as candle-smoke, and it holds on as though it has wanted to hold someone's hand for a thousand years and never dared ask.\n\n\"Nobody's ever taken my hand before,\" it says, wondering. \"They all just follow the light. You're loud, and you ask, and you take hands. You might be the strangest dead person I've ever guided.\"\n\n\"I'm not dead,\" you remind it.\n\n\"We'll see,\" it says, and it is almost a joke, and the almost is what frightens you.\n\nAhead, the Ashen March waits under its bruise-yellow sky, and somewhere on that road the Keeper is still running.","choices":[{"text":"Walk on, into the March.","next":"u18_start","do":["flag:region_threshold","flag:region_gate","flag:codex_mirror","journal:Crossed the threshold. The Underland is real, the Mirror Law is learned, and I am not walking it alone."]}]},{"id":"u17_child_follow","chapter":"CHAPTER XVII · THE THRESHOLD","text":"The child nods and falls in behind your shoulder, holding its flame to light your steps, quiet now, the way it must have gone quiet a thousand times behind a thousand travellers who never once looked back.\n\nYou look back. Just once.\n\n\"Keep close,\" you tell it. \"I don't lose people down here.\"\n\nSomething in the grey face steadies. It comes up level with you after that, no longer quite behind, and does not ask permission.\n\nAhead, the Ashen March waits under its bruise-yellow sky, and somewhere on that road the Keeper is still running.","choices":[{"text":"Walk on, into the March.","next":"u18_start","do":["companion:lumen","flag:region_threshold","flag:region_gate","flag:codex_mirror","journal:Crossed the threshold. Learned the Underland and the Mirror Law. The Lantern-child (lumen) walks with me into the March."]}]}]},"story/chapter_18.json":{"scenes":[{"id":"u18_start","chapter":"CHAPTER XVIII · THE ASHEN MARCH","text":"The gate spits you out onto a road you have walked before — and never. It is the caravan road. The same ruts, the same milestones, the same bent thorn at the shoulder. But the sky is the colour of a snuffed wick, and the ruts are filled with grey ash instead of rain.\n\nAhead, a caravan crawls. Wagons, oxen, walkers in worn cloaks. They move at the pace of a funeral that has forgotten whose. Ash drifts from the wheels like flour from a mill.\n\nNone of them casts a shadow. None of them makes a sound.\n\nYou have crossed into the Ashen March.","choices":[{"text":"Fall in behind the caravan and watch.","next":"u18_follow","do":["flag:region_march","journal:Crossed into the Ashen March — a dead mirror of the caravan road, walked by the unknowing dead."]},{"text":"Call out to the walkers.","next":"u18_call","do":["flag:region_march"]},{"text":"Leave the road. Cut across the ash-flats.","next":"u18_flats","do":["flag:region_march"]}]},{"id":"u18_follow","chapter":"CHAPTER XVIII · THE ASHEN MARCH","text":"You walk in the caravan's wake. Up close the walkers are worse and better than you feared. A woman carries a basket with nothing in it and hums a tune with no sound. A boy leads an ox that is only the idea of an ox, grey and smooth as a river stone.\n\nWhen you draw level with them, they do not turn. Their eyes are fixed on a horizon that never gets closer. On each face is the mild, patient look of someone quite sure they will arrive somewhere lovely, soon, any moment now.\n\nThey do not know they are dead. That is the mercy and the horror of this place.\n\nAt the head of the line, small and out of step, a child walks holding a lantern. The flame in it is grey.","choices":[{"text":"Go to the child with the lantern.","next":"u18_lumen_meet"},{"text":"Try to wake one of the walkers.","next":"u18_wake_1"},{"text":"Search a stalled wagon at the roadside.","next":"u18_wagon"}]},{"id":"u18_call","chapter":"CHAPTER XVIII · THE ASHEN MARCH","text":"\"Hey!\" you shout. \"You there — stop! This road goes nowhere!\"\n\nYour voice comes out flat, swallowed, as if the air here is packed with wool. Not one head turns. The nearest walker, an old man with a rake over his shoulder, keeps his patient shuffling pace, lips moving on a word you cannot hear.\n\nBut something at the head of the line does react. A small figure carrying a lantern falters, half-turns, and lifts the grey flame a little higher — the way a person raises a candle at a sound in the dark.\n\nThe flame gutters toward you like a dog catching a scent.","choices":[{"text":"Walk to the child with the lantern.","next":"u18_lumen_meet"},{"text":"Grab the old man by the shoulder.","next":"u18_wake_1"},{"text":"Stay back and watch what the flame does.","next":"u18_follow"}]},{"id":"u18_flats","chapter":"CHAPTER XVIII · THE ASHEN MARCH","text":"You step off the road and onto the flats. The ash is ankle-deep and colder than snow. Every footfall raises a soft grey ghost that hangs, then settles, then does not quite settle back where it was.\n\nThe caravan drifts on to your left, small now. Out here the silence has a shape to it. You begin to feel that if you keep walking straight, you will walk forever and the horizon will never once move.\n\nThe ash remembers your steps a moment too long, like it is deciding whether to let you take them back.","choices":[{"text":"Trust your footing and cross the flats. (Skill: nerve)","next":"u18_flats_cross","check":{"stat":"fear","dc":12,"pass":"u18_flats_ok","fail":"u18_flats_lost","onFail":["fear:+5"]}},{"text":"Turn back to the road while you still can.","next":"u18_follow"},{"text":"Kneel and dig where the ash pooled darkest.","next":"u18_flats_dig"}]},{"id":"u18_flats_dig","chapter":"CHAPTER XVIII · THE ASHEN MARCH","text":"You kneel and sweep the cold ash aside. Beneath it your fingers find a wagon's iron ring, and then, threaded through it, a small tin cup, and then — held tight in a hand that is only bones now — a lantern with no flame in it.\n\nWhoever fell here fell reaching for the caravan's light. You take the dark lantern. It is light in your hand, and colder than it should be, and something in it wants to be lit.","choices":[{"text":"Pocket the lantern and find the road again.","next":"u18_follow","do":["add_item:ash_lantern","journal:Found a cold, empty lantern in the ash, clutched by a hand of bone. It wants to be lit."]}]},{"id":"u18_flats_cross","chapter":"CHAPTER XVIII · THE ASHEN MARCH","text":"You fix your eyes on a single milestone and do not let them wander. Step. Step. Step. The ash pulls; you do not look down. The trick, you understand suddenly, is to want the far side more than the flats want to keep you.","choices":[{"text":"Keep walking.","next":"u18_flats_ok"}]},{"id":"u18_flats_ok","chapter":"CHAPTER XVIII · THE ASHEN MARCH","text":"The flats end at a low rise, and the road climbs to meet you there, as if it had been waiting on the other side all along. You have crossed a place that swallows people whole and you have come out the far side.\n\nAt the top of the rise, the caravan winds below you. And at its head, a small figure with a grey lantern has stopped walking. It is looking up at you.","choices":[{"text":"Go down to the child.","next":"u18_lumen_meet"}]},{"id":"u18_flats_lost","chapter":"CHAPTER XVIII · THE ASHEN MARCH","text":"You look down — just once, just to check your footing — and that is the mistake. When you look up the milestone is gone. Every direction is the same grey nothing. You walk, and walk, and the walking stops meaning anything.\n\nThen: a light. Small, grey, bobbing. A lantern, carried toward you by a child who should not be able to find you and has.\n\n\"You went the wrong way,\" the child says, and the words arrive clearly, the only clear sound in this whole muffled world. \"Everyone does, the first time. Come on. Follow the flame.\"","choices":[{"text":"Follow the flame back.","next":"u18_lumen_meet","do":["journal:Lost on the ash-flats. A child with a grey lantern found me and led me out."]}]},{"id":"u18_wagon","chapter":"CHAPTER XVIII · THE ASHEN MARCH","text":"A wagon has slumped at the roadside on a broken axle. Ash has drifted up its wheels like a slow tide. Inside, under a mildewed tarp, the cargo waits: bolts of grey cloth, a barrel of grey apples, a strongbox with the lid ajar.\n\nEverything in this world is the colour of the last coal in a dead fire. But the strongbox holds coin — real coin, or coin that remembers being real. It gleams faintly, the only warm thing you have seen since the gate.","choices":[{"text":"Take the coin.","next":"u18_wagon_take","do":["gold:+6","journal:Looted a caravan strongbox in the Ashen March. The coin remembers being warm."]},{"text":"Leave it. The dead may want their things.","next":"u18_wagon_leave","do":["trust:+3"]},{"text":"Just take an apple and go.","next":"u18_lumen_meet","do":["add_item:supplies"]}]},{"id":"u18_wagon_take","chapter":"CHAPTER XVIII · THE ASHEN MARCH","text":"You fill your purse. The coin is cold and then, in your hand, briefly warm, as if glad to be carried again.\n\nAs you close the strongbox, you notice the child. It has left the head of the caravan and come back down the line, and it is standing at the wagon's tail, watching you with the grey flame held between you like a question.\n\n\"You took the widow's fare,\" the child says. Not angry. Just noting it, the way children note things.","choices":[{"text":"\"She has no use for it now.\"","next":"u18_lumen_meet"},{"text":"Put half of it back.","next":"u18_lumen_meet","do":["gold:-3","trust:+4"]}]},{"id":"u18_wagon_leave","chapter":"CHAPTER XVIII · THE ASHEN MARCH","text":"You lower the strongbox lid. It clicks. Somewhere up the line, so faint you might have imagined it, you hear — for the first time in this whole silent country — a small sound. A child's voice, saying, \"Oh. You're one of the kind ones.\"\n\nThe lantern-flame, when you turn, is a shade less grey. A ghost of gold at its heart.","choices":[{"text":"Follow the sound to the child.","next":"u18_lumen_meet"}]},{"id":"u18_wake_1","chapter":"CHAPTER XVIII · THE ASHEN MARCH","text":"You take the old man by the shoulder and turn him to face you. His skin is dry and grey and cold. His eyes find yours slowly, the way eyes find a lamp in a dark room.\n\n\"Are we there?\" he asks. His voice is a dead leaf skating over stone. \"Is it the market? My daughter keeps the stall by the fountain. She'll have kept my supper.\"\n\nBehind his eyes something flickers — a doubt, a cold draught through a shut house. If you push, you might wake him to the truth of where he is. It would not be a kindness. But it might be the truth.","choices":[{"text":"Gently lie. \"Almost there. Keep walking.\"","next":"u18_wake_lie","do":["trust:+3"]},{"text":"Tell him the truth. (Skill: will)","next":"u18_wake_truth","check":{"stat":"trust","dc":13,"pass":"u18_wake_truth_ok","fail":"u18_wake_truth_bad","onFail":["fear:+6"]}},{"text":"Let him go. Step back.","next":"u18_lumen_meet"}]},{"id":"u18_wake_lie","chapter":"CHAPTER XVIII · THE ASHEN MARCH","text":"\"Almost there,\" you say. \"The fountain's just past the next rise. Keep walking, old father.\"\n\nHis face eases. The doubt goes out of him like a candle pinched between two fingers. He nods, hitches the rake, and shuffles on into the grey, content, humming his soundless tune.\n\nYou have sent him back to sleep. Whether that was mercy or cowardice, you will carry the question a long time.","choices":[{"text":"Move on toward the lantern.","next":"u18_lumen_meet","do":["journal:Told a dead man he was almost home, and he believed me. I don't know if it was kind."]}]},{"id":"u18_wake_truth","chapter":"CHAPTER XVIII · THE ASHEN MARCH","text":"You hold his cold gaze. \"Old father,\" you say, \"you are not going to the market. You died on this road. Your daughter buried you. You have been walking a very long time.\"","choices":[{"text":"Say it gently, and hope it lands soft.","next":"u18_wake_truth_ok","check":{"stat":"trust","dc":13,"pass":"u18_wake_truth_ok","fail":"u18_wake_truth_bad"}},{"text":"Lose your nerve at the last word and soften it.","next":"u18_wake_lie"}]},{"id":"u18_wake_truth_ok","chapter":"CHAPTER XVIII · THE ASHEN MARCH","text":"The words land soft and true, and the old man does not scream. He simply stops. His eyes clear, all the way clear, and for one moment he is entirely himself.\n\n\"Ah,\" he says. \"Yes. I remember now.\" He looks at his grey hands. He looks at the grey road. He looks at you with an old man's terrible, gentle patience. \"Thank you for saying it plain. Nobody says it plain.\"\n\nAnd he sits down at the roadside, sets his rake across his knees, and is, at last, only a still grey shape in the ash — at rest. One fewer walker on the endless road.","choices":[{"text":"Bow to him and walk on.","next":"u18_lumen_meet","do":["trust:+5","journal:Told a dead man the truth kindly. He remembered himself, and finally rested."]}]},{"id":"u18_wake_truth_bad","chapter":"CHAPTER XVIII · THE ASHEN MARCH","text":"The truth goes in like a blade turned the wrong way. The old man's face collapses. He remembers — not gently, but all at once: the axle breaking, the cold, the long crawl toward a fountain that was never coming.\n\nHe opens his mouth and out of it comes the first loud sound in this whole muffled world — a wail that is not grief but pure animal panic, and the walkers nearest him take it up, and take it up, a rising grey chorus of the newly-remembering dead.\n\nSomething stirs at the back of the caravan. Something that this noise has fed.","choices":[{"text":"Flee toward the lantern-light ahead.","next":"u18_lumen_meet","do":["journal:Told a dead man the truth too hard. His panic woke the March. Something is coming."]}]},{"id":"u18_lumen_meet","chapter":"CHAPTER XVIII · THE ASHEN MARCH","text":"The child is small — seven, perhaps eight, though age means little here. It wears a coat too big for it and boots stuffed with rag. In both hands it grips a lantern of black iron, and inside burns a flame the colour of woodsmoke: a grey fire that gives no heat and casts no warmth, only a wan light the walkers drift toward like moths.\n\nWhen the child looks up at you, its eyes are not dead. They are frightened, and hopeful, and very much alive in a place where nothing else is.\n\n\"You have a shadow,\" the child whispers, awed. \"You're warm. You came through the door.\" It steps closer, lantern lifted. \"Are you here to take one of them home? Please say you are. I light the way but I can never make them turn off the road.\"","choices":[{"text":"\"Who are you? What is this place?\"","next":"u18_lumen_who"},{"text":"\"Yes. And I could use a guide.\"","next":"u18_lumen_guide"},{"text":"Kneel to the child's eye level and listen.","next":"u18_lumen_kneel","do":["trust:+2"]}]},{"id":"u18_lumen_who","chapter":"CHAPTER XVIII · THE ASHEN MARCH","text":"\"I'm Lumen,\" the child says. \"Or that's what the flame calls me. I don't remember my before-name. I've been carrying the light so long I forgot to keep my own.\"\n\nIt holds the lantern up between you, and the grey fire leans toward your warmth like it is starving for it.\n\n\"This is the March. Everyone here is walking to a place they think they remember. None of them get there. The road just keeps going and the light I carry keeps them from wandering off into the ash and coming apart. That's my job. I don't know who gave it to me. I just woke up doing it.\"\n\nIt lowers the lantern.\n\n\"You're the first warm thing in a long time. Please don't leave yet.\"","choices":[{"text":"\"Come with me, Lumen. Carry the light for me.\"","next":"u18_lumen_join"},{"text":"\"Tell me more about the road first.\"","next":"u18_lumen_road"}]},{"id":"u18_lumen_kneel","chapter":"CHAPTER XVIII · THE ASHEN MARCH","text":"You go down on one knee in the cold ash. It is a small thing, but the child's whole face changes at it — the way a plant turns when you finally give it the window.\n\n\"Nobody kneels down here,\" Lumen says. \"The walkers don't see me at all. The tall thing at the back of the line sees me, but I don't like when it looks.\" A shiver. \"You look at me like I'm a real person.\"\n\n\"You are a real person,\" you tell it.\n\nThe grey flame flickers — and for a heartbeat, at its very heart, there is a thread of true gold.\n\n\"I'm Lumen,\" the child says, shy. \"Will you stay near me? The flame's braver when you're close.\"","choices":[{"text":"\"I'll stay near. Walk with me.\"","next":"u18_lumen_join"},{"text":"\"What's the tall thing at the back of the line?\"","next":"u18_lumen_road"}]},{"id":"u18_lumen_guide","chapter":"CHAPTER XVIII · THE ASHEN MARCH","text":"\"A guide,\" Lumen breathes, as if you have offered it a crown. \"I can guide. I'm good at guiding, I just never had anyone to guide who could actually leave.\"\n\nThe child falls in beside you at once, lantern raised, small legs working to match your stride. Then it slows, and glances back down the long grey line of the caravan, and its voice drops.\n\n\"There's a rule, though. If I come with you, the tall thing at the back — the one that keeps the herd — it'll notice the flame's gone from the front. It doesn't like losing a light. It'll come.\"\n\nIt looks up at you, jaw set with a child's fragile courage.\n\n\"Are you the kind that runs, or the kind that stands?\"","choices":[{"text":"\"The kind that stands. Come here, behind me.\"","next":"u18_lumen_join"},{"text":"\"Tell me what it is before I decide.\"","next":"u18_lumen_road"}]},{"id":"u18_lumen_road","chapter":"CHAPTER XVIII · THE ASHEN MARCH","text":"Lumen draws close and speaks low, the way children share the frightening truths adults pretend not to know.\n\n\"It's the Ashen Warden. The caravan's got to have a keeper same as the Gate's got to have a Keeper — something to walk the line and make sure nobody strays and nobody wakes. It used to be a person. A drover, I think. Now it's mostly the herding, wearing a drover.\"\n\nThe child glances back. Far down the line, at the very tail of the caravan, something too tall stands motionless, a smear of grey against grey.\n\n\"It eats the ones who wake up. Panic makes them bright, and bright is what it eats. So you have to be quiet, or you have to be strong. There's no third way. I've watched a long time.\"","choices":[{"text":"\"Then walk with me. We'll be strong together.\"","next":"u18_lumen_join","do":["flag:codex_lumen","journal:The Ashen Warden herds the dead caravan and eats those who wake. Panic makes them bright; bright is what it eats."]},{"text":"\"Show me how you keep the flame lit.\"","next":"u18_lumen_flame"}]},{"id":"u18_lumen_flame","chapter":"CHAPTER XVIII · THE ASHEN MARCH","text":"Lumen cups a hand around the glass. \"You feed it,\" the child says. \"Not with oil. With minding. You have to keep minding it, keep caring that it stays lit, or it goes out and then the walkers scatter and come apart in the ash.\"\n\nIt looks up. \"That's why I'm so tired. You can't stop minding, not ever, not to sleep. Nobody told me that when I picked it up. I didn't know it would be forever.\"\n\nIf you carry your own dark lantern, you feel it stir at your hip, hungry for the same grey fire.","choices":[{"text":"Light your dark lantern from Lumen's flame.","next":"u18_lumen_share","require":"ash_lantern"},{"text":"\"Then let me help you mind it. Come with me.\"","next":"u18_lumen_join"}]},{"id":"u18_lumen_share","chapter":"CHAPTER XVIII · THE ASHEN MARCH","text":"You touch the wick of your dark lantern to the grey flame. It catches with a small cold whmp, and now there are two lights on the road where there was one.\n\nLumen's eyes go wide and wet. \"Two,\" it whispers. \"I never had a two before. If you carry one, I don't have to carry all of it alone.\"\n\nThe weight of forever eases off the child's small shoulders by half. It is the first time you have seen it stand up straight.","choices":[{"text":"\"We share it now. Come on.\"","next":"u18_lumen_join","do":["max_hp:+2","journal:Lit my lantern from Lumen's. Now we share the minding. The child stood up straight for the first time."]}]},{"id":"u18_lumen_join","chapter":"CHAPTER XVIII · THE ASHEN MARCH","text":"Lumen steps to your side and does not leave it. Small, grey, luminous — a candle of a child in a country of snuffed wicks. The lantern's light steadies now that someone is walking with it who has a heartbeat.\n\n\"I'll guide you true,\" the child promises. \"Through the whole March. To the far edge where the road drowns and the Court begins. That's where the doors go next.\"\n\nThen it stiffens. Far down the line, the too-tall shape at the caravan's tail has turned. It has noticed the flame has left the front. It has noticed you.\n\n\"It's coming,\" Lumen whispers, and slips its cold small hand into yours. \"How do you want to be, when it gets here? Because I'll remember. I remember everything, that's the other part of the job.\"","choices":[{"text":"\"Get behind me. I'll keep you safe.\" (protective)","next":"u18_bond_shield","do":["companion:lumen","flag:codex_lumen","rel:lumen:+8","journal:Lumen the Lantern-child joins me — and I swore to keep the child safe. Warmth in a dead country."]},{"text":"\"Stand beside me. You're braver than you think.\" (equal)","next":"u18_bond_equal","do":["companion:lumen","flag:codex_lumen","rel:lumen:+5","journal:Lumen the Lantern-child joins me — not to hide behind me, but to stand beside me. A small partnership in the ash."]},{"text":"\"Light my way and stay out of reach. This is my fight.\" (distant)","next":"u18_bond_cold","do":["companion:lumen","flag:codex_lumen","rel:lumen:+2","journal:Lumen the Lantern-child joins me as a guide, kept at arm's length. The child watches me the way it watches everything — to remember."]}]},{"id":"u18_bond_shield","chapter":"CHAPTER XVIII · THE ASHEN MARCH","text":"You put the child at your back and set your feet. Lumen holds the lantern high over your shoulder so its grey light falls ahead of you, on the road, on the coming thing.\n\n\"Nobody's ever stood in front of me before,\" it says, very quietly. \"I'll remember that too.\"\n\nThe Warden comes up the line. The walkers part around it without knowing they part. It is a drover's shape ten feet tall, wound in grey rags, and where its face should be there is a coil of ash turning slowly in on itself. It carries a goad of black wood, and it points the goad at Lumen's light.","choices":[{"text":"Stand your ground. Draw steel.","next":"u18_warden_fight"},{"text":"Try to speak to what it used to be first.","next":"u18_warden_talk"}]},{"id":"u18_bond_equal","chapter":"CHAPTER XVIII · THE ASHEN MARCH","text":"You and the child stand shoulder to shoulder — or shoulder to hip, given its size. Lumen plants its feet in a small, fierce imitation of your stance, lantern raised like a shield of light.\n\n\"Together,\" it says, testing the word. \"I like together better than behind.\"\n\nThe Warden comes up the line, parting the unknowing dead, a drover-shape ten feet tall wound in grey rags, its face a slow coil of turning ash. It levels a goad of black wood at the two lights standing where there should be one obedient one.","choices":[{"text":"Meet it together. Draw steel.","next":"u18_warden_fight"},{"text":"Have Lumen raise the flame while you speak to it.","next":"u18_warden_talk"}]},{"id":"u18_bond_cold","chapter":"CHAPTER XVIII · THE ASHEN MARCH","text":"\"Stay back and keep the light on it,\" you tell the child. \"Don't come close.\"\n\nLumen's face does something small and complicated — hurt, then acceptance, then the flat careful look of someone filing a thing away. \"All right,\" it says. \"I'll light. You fight. I'll remember how you did it.\"\n\nIt retreats a few paces and holds the lantern out at arm's length. Its grey glow falls on the road ahead, where the Warden is coming up the line: a drover-shape ten feet tall, rag-wound, its face a coil of turning ash, a black goad in its fist.","choices":[{"text":"Face it alone. Draw steel.","next":"u18_warden_fight"},{"text":"Try words before blades.","next":"u18_warden_talk"}]},{"id":"u18_warden_talk","chapter":"CHAPTER XVIII · THE ASHEN MARCH","text":"\"Drover,\" you say. \"You were a drover once. You had a name. You kept a herd because you loved them, not because you fed on them. Do you remember?\"\n\nThe coil of ash where its face should be slows. Slows. For a moment something almost surfaces in it — a broad kind face, sunburnt, a whistle between the teeth for a good dog.\n\nThen it clenches shut again, and the goad comes up, and the moment is gone. It has kept this herd too long. It is mostly keeping now, and only a little drover.","choices":[{"text":"Press the memory harder. (Skill: conviction)","next":"u18_warden_talk_check","check":{"stat":"trust","dc":14,"pass":"u18_warden_talk_win","fail":"u18_warden_fight","onFail":["fear:+4"]}},{"text":"Words failed. Draw steel.","next":"u18_warden_fight"}]},{"id":"u18_warden_talk_check","chapter":"CHAPTER XVIII · THE ASHEN MARCH","text":"\"Your dog,\" you say. \"You had a dog. Grey-muzzled, one ear down. You whistled it and it came. You are still the one who whistles, drover, not the one who herds the dead.\"","choices":[{"text":"Hold its gaze and wait.","next":"u18_warden_talk_win","check":{"stat":"trust","dc":14,"pass":"u18_warden_talk_win","fail":"u18_warden_fight"}},{"text":"The coil shuts. Abandon words and draw steel.","next":"u18_warden_fight"}]},{"id":"u18_warden_talk_win","chapter":"CHAPTER XVIII · THE ASHEN MARCH","text":"The goad lowers. The coil of ash unwinds, and for the space of three breaths there is a face there — broad, sunburnt, unbearably tired.\n\n\"...Rell,\" the drover says, in a voice like a cart on gravel. \"My name was Rell. And the dog was Ban.\" It looks down the endless line of the caravan it has walked for a hundred years. \"I kept telling them we were almost there. I kept telling myself.\"\n\nIt sets the black goad down in the ash. \"Take the little light with you. It was never mine to keep. Nothing here was ever anyone's to keep.\" And the drover Rell walks off the road, into the flats, and lets the ash take him gently — the way he never let it take his herd.\n\nLumen watches, wide-eyed. \"You didn't hit it. You un-made it. With saying-its-name.\"","choices":[{"text":"\"Names matter here. Remember that.\" Walk on.","next":"u18_after_warden","do":["trust:+6","rel:lumen:+4","add_item:true_name","flag:warden_freed","journal:Freed the Ashen Warden — the drover Rell — by saying his name. Names un-make things here."]}]},{"id":"u18_warden_fight","chapter":"CHAPTER XVIII · THE ASHEN MARCH","text":"The Warden lunges. The black goad whips out faster than a thing that size should move, trailing ash like smoke. There is no more talking now. There is only the goad, and your steel, and Lumen's small grey light throwing your shadow huge across the road.\n\n\"Hit it where the ash turns!\" the child cries. \"The face-place! That's where the keeping lives!\"\n\nThis is your first real fight beyond the Gate. Make it count.","choices":[],"combat":{"enemy":"The Ashen Warden","ehp":12,"edmg":3,"win":"u18_warden_beaten","lose":"u18_warden_lose","flee":"u18_warden_flee"}},{"id":"u18_warden_beaten","chapter":"CHAPTER XVIII · THE ASHEN MARCH","text":"Your blade finds the turning coil where a face should be, and the Warden comes apart — not with a scream but with a long exhaled sigh, rags and ash unspooling into the grey air until there is nothing standing on the road but a black wooden goad, and then not even that.\n\nThe caravan, leaderless, simply stops. The unknowing dead stand where they are, patient as ever, waiting for a march that will not resume.\n\nLumen lets out a breath it has clearly been holding for a hundred years. \"It's gone,\" the child says, disbelieving. \"You made it gone. I watched. I'll always remember you made it gone.\"","choices":[{"text":"\"It's over. Are you all right?\"","next":"u18_after_warden","do":["trust:+4","rel:lumen:+3","flag:warden_slain","journal:Killed the Ashen Warden in the first real fight beyond the Gate. The dead caravan stands still now, waiting forever."]}]},{"id":"u18_warden_flee","chapter":"CHAPTER XVIII · THE ASHEN MARCH","text":"You break off and run, hauling Lumen with you, the goad hissing through the ash a hand's-breadth behind. The child holds the lantern out ahead, and its grey light shows you a gap in the caravan, a gully, a way off the road.\n\nYou tumble down into the gully and lie still. Above, on the road, the Warden casts about — patient, herding — and then, finding its herd intact and its front-light merely misplaced, it turns back to the line and resumes its slow endless walk.\n\n\"It won't follow off the road,\" Lumen pants. \"The road's all it is. We got away. But it'll be waiting if we go back that way.\"","choices":[{"text":"\"Then we go on, off the road. Guide me.\"","next":"u18_after_warden","do":["fear:+3","rel:lumen:+2","flag:warden_fled","journal:Fled the Ashen Warden into a gully. It won't leave the road — but the road behind us belongs to it now."]}]},{"id":"u18_warden_lose","chapter":"CHAPTER XVIII · THE ASHEN MARCH","text":"The goad catches you across the chest and the world goes white, then grey, then very far away. You go down in the cold ash. The last thing you see is Lumen's small face above you, and the lantern lowered close, its grey fire pressed almost to your skin.\n\n\"No,\" the child is saying. \"No, no, you're warm, you have to stay warm — here, take some of the light, take it, I've got enough for two —\"\n\nThe grey flame pours into you, cold and then somehow not, and it pushes the dark back out of your eyes. You are not dead. Not yet. Lumen spent some of its own forever to keep you.","choices":[{"text":"Drag yourself up. The Warden has moved on down the line.","next":"u18_after_warden","do":["hp:+6","fear:+6","rel:lumen:+6","flag:warden_survived","journal:The Warden nearly killed me. Lumen poured some of its own light into me to keep me warm. I owe the child my life."]}]},{"id":"u18_after_warden","chapter":"CHAPTER XVIII · THE ASHEN MARCH","text":"Whatever the Warden was, it walks the line no longer. Lumen tugs your sleeve and points ahead, past the stalled caravan, to where the road tips downward and the grey ash gives way to grey water. A tide-line of drowned wagons. A far shore lost in mist. Towers, maybe, beyond it — the black spires of a court that sits in the water like a crown dropped in a well.\n\n\"That's the edge of the March,\" the child says. \"Where the road drowns and the Court begins. It's not far now. But you should catch your breath first. There won't be anywhere to breathe over there.\"","choices":[{"text":"Rest a moment with Lumen at the tide-line.","next":"u18_rest"},{"text":"Ask Lumen what waits in the Court.","next":"u18_lumen_court"},{"text":"Don't stop. Walk down to the drowned road now.","next":"u18_edge"}]},{"id":"u18_rest","chapter":"CHAPTER XVIII · THE ASHEN MARCH","text":"You sit at the tide-line. The grey water does not move. Lumen sets its lantern between you like a small hearth and, for the first time, sits down beside it instead of standing guard over it.\n\n\"I forgot sitting felt like this,\" the child murmurs. It leans, just slightly, against your arm. Cold, but present. \"When we're done — if there's a done — do you think I could have a before-name again? A real one? You could pick it. I wouldn't mind if you picked it.\"","choices":[{"text":"\"When we're done, we'll find your true name together.\"","next":"u18_edge","do":["rel:lumen:+5","journal:Promised Lumen we'd find the child's true name when this is over. It leaned against my arm and let me feel it."]},{"text":"\"One thing at a time. On your feet.\"","next":"u18_edge","do":["trust:+1"]}]},{"id":"u18_lumen_court","chapter":"CHAPTER XVIII · THE ASHEN MARCH","text":"\"The Court,\" Lumen says, and its small face goes careful. \"There's a king in it. He's not a real king. He drowned a long time ago and the water made him one because the water thought a court needed a king, the way the road thought it needed a Warden.\"\n\nThe child looks up at you.\n\n\"He'll be polite. That's the trap. The Warden was easy — it just wanted to eat you. The false king wants you to sit down and stay for supper and forget you were ever going anywhere. Don't sit down. Whatever else you do over there, don't sit down at his table.\"","choices":[{"text":"\"I won't sit. Thank you, Lumen.\"","next":"u18_edge","do":["rel:lumen:+3","flag:court_warned","journal:Lumen's warning about the Drowned Court: the false king will be polite. Don't sit at his table."]}]},{"id":"u18_edge","chapter":"CHAPTER XVIII · THE ASHEN MARCH","text":"You come down to where the road drowns. The last dry milestone stands knee-deep in grey water, its carving worn to a suggestion. Beyond it the caravan road continues on beneath the surface, visible for a little way as a pale ribbon, then lost.\n\nLumen wades in ahead of you without hesitation, lantern held high, and the grey flame throws a wobbling road of light across the water — a path that holds, so long as the light does.\n\n\"Stay in the light,\" the child says. \"Step where I step. The Court's just past the mist. And whatever it looks like when we get there — remember it's already dead. Same as everything back here. Same as I'm not.\"\n\nIt looks back at you, small and grey and warm-eyed, holding the only light in a drowned world.\n\n\"Ready? The doors go on from here.\"","choices":[{"text":"Take the child's light and step onto the drowned road.","next":"u19_start"}]}]},"story/chapter_19.json":{"scenes":[{"id":"u19_start","chapter":"CHAPTER XIX · THE DROWNED COURT","text":"The March gives way to water. Not a river — a floor of it, ankle-deep and black, stretching to where a castle stands on legs of its own reflection.\n\nIt is your king's castle. The one you were born under. But drowned. The banners hang like drowned hair. The gate-arch is a mouth full of dark. Every window holds a second castle, upside down, sinking.\n\nSomewhere inside, a bell tolls underwater — slow, wrong, each note arriving a moment before you expect it.\n\nYou set the flag of this place in your bones.","choices":[{"text":"Wade toward the drowned gate.","next":"u19_gate","do":["flag:region_court","journal:Reached the Drowned Court — the king's castle, sunk and mirrored, on the far side of the Gate."]},{"text":"Circle the walls, looking for another way.","next":"u19_wall_walk","do":["flag:region_court"]}]},{"id":"u19_wall_walk","chapter":"CHAPTER XIX · THE DROWNED COURT","text":"You wade the perimeter. The water never rises past your ankles no matter how the ground pretends to fall away. In the wall's reflection you see doors that are not in the wall.\n\nAt a corner, a sally-port hangs open, weed-choked. Cold breathes out of it. And carved above the lintel, fresh among the rot, three words: WEAR MY FACE.\n\nBehind you, your own reflection in the black water lifts its head a half-second after you do.","choices":[{"text":"Slip in through the sally-port.","next":"u19_undercroft"},{"text":"Go to the main gate instead.","next":"u19_gate"}]},{"id":"u19_gate","chapter":"CHAPTER XIX · THE DROWNED COURT","text":"The gate-arch swallows you. Inside, the water is gone — or you are inside the reflection now, and the water is the sky.\n\nThe great hall opens ahead, torchlit by flames that give no heat and hang wrong-way-up, roots of light growing down from the ceiling. Courtiers line the walls: men and women in drowned finery, still as portraits, water beading on their skin and never falling.\n\nAt the far end, on a throne set in a shallow pool, sits a king.\n\nHe wears your king's face. He wears it the way a hand wears a glove.","choices":[{"text":"Approach the throne, openly.","next":"u19_throne_approach"},{"text":"Study the frozen courtiers first.","next":"u19_courtiers"},{"text":"Look for a side passage out of the hall.","next":"u19_side_gallery"}]},{"id":"u19_courtiers","chapter":"CHAPTER XIX · THE DROWNED COURT","text":"Up close, the courtiers are wrong in the small ways that matter. A lady's smile has no seam where the lips part. A steward's eyes are painted on a face that has forgotten what eyes are for. They do not breathe. They wait, the way a held breath waits.\n\nOne — a girl in a page's coat — has a single real tear frozen on her cheek, mid-fall. When you lean close her mouth moves without sound. You read it off her lips: *he is not the king. he is not the king. he is not*\n\nThe king's voice fills the hall without his mouth moving. \"You will find my court poor company. They agree with everything I say. It is a lonely way to be right.\"","choices":[{"text":"Turn and face the throne.","next":"u19_throne_approach","do":["journal:A frozen page mouthed the same words over and over: he is not the king."]},{"text":"Whisper to the frozen page: 'Who are you?'","next":"u19_page_whisper"}]},{"id":"u19_page_whisper","chapter":"CHAPTER XIX · THE DROWNED COURT","text":"You put your mouth near the frozen girl's ear. \"Who are you?\"\n\nFor a moment nothing. Then her eyes swivel — only the eyes — and fix on you, and they are horribly alive.\n\n\"I was the one who brought his supper,\" she breathes, lips barely moving. \"The real king. Before. He took the king's face and then he took the king, and now the king is a coat he wears on cold days. Say nothing you would not want repeated. The walls are his ears. The walls were us.\"\n\nHer eyes roll back to painted stillness.\n\nYou straighten. The false king is watching. He has been watching the whole time.","choices":[{"text":"Approach the throne.","next":"u19_throne_approach","do":["fear:5","journal:A trapped servant: the false king took the true king's face, then the king himself. The walls hear everything."]}]},{"id":"u19_side_gallery","chapter":"CHAPTER XIX · THE DROWNED COURT","text":"You edge along the wall toward a curtained arch. Behind it: a gallery of portraits, all the same face — your king, in a hundred styles, a hundred ages. In each one the painted eyes have been scratched out and repainted, scratched out and repainted, layers deep, as if someone could not decide whose eyes should go in that face.\n\nAt the gallery's end, water pools under a low door. The undercroft. You could go down, away from the throne. Or you could go back.\n\nA reflection in the pooled water shows the false king already standing behind you. You turn. The hall is empty. You turn back. Gone.","choices":[{"text":"Go down to the undercroft.","next":"u19_undercroft"},{"text":"Return to the hall and face him.","next":"u19_throne_approach"}]},{"id":"u19_undercroft","chapter":"CHAPTER XIX · THE DROWNED COURT","text":"The stair goes down into standing water and older stone. Here the drowning is real — cold to the knee, then the waist. The walls are cells. Iron doors, most rusted open, all empty.\n\nAll but one.\n\nBehind the last grate, a shape sits with its back to you, wrapped in a cloak the grey of wet ash. It does not turn. But it speaks, in a voice worn smooth as river-glass.\n\n\"You carry a lantern that isn't lit,\" it says. \"That means you met the child, or you will. Lumen. Little grey flame.\"","choices":[{"text":"\"You know Lumen?\" (companion: lumen)","next":"u19_gloam_lumen","require":"companion:lumen"},{"text":"\"Who are you, behind the grate?\"","next":"u19_gloam_intro"},{"text":"Leave the prisoner. Return upstairs.","next":"u19_throne_approach"}]},{"id":"u19_gloam_intro","chapter":"CHAPTER XIX · THE DROWNED COURT","text":"\"A knight,\" the shape says. \"Was. The court called me the Mirror-Knight when there was a court worth calling anything. I guarded the true king. I failed him in the ordinary way: I believed the thing wearing his face because it asked me to.\"\n\nIt turns its head, and you see a helm of black glass, and in the glass, your own face, tired and afraid.\n\n\"He keeps me here because I am the last who remembers the king's real laugh. As long as I remember it, his mask is imperfect. So he keeps me where no one can hear me remember.\"\n\nThe grate is locked with a warding-glyph, not a bolt.","choices":[{"text":"Try to break the warding-glyph.","next":"u19_gloam_glyph"},{"text":"\"Come with me. I'll get you out.\"","next":"u19_gloam_free"},{"text":"\"I can't free you now. I'm sorry.\"","next":"u19_gloam_leave"}]},{"id":"u19_gloam_lumen","chapter":"CHAPTER XIX · THE DROWNED COURT","text":"\"I know Lumen,\" the shape says, and for the first time something like warmth crosses the smooth voice. \"The child lit my cell once, walking past on the water. Grey flame through the grate. It was the only light I have had in an age of years.\"\n\nLumen presses close to the bars beside you, holding the ash-lantern high. Grey fire pools on black glass. The prisoner turns — a helm of dark mirror — and bows its head to the child.\n\n\"Little flame. You came back.\"\n\n\"I said I would,\" Lumen says, small and certain. Then, to you: \"This one is true. I have seen its inside. Free it. Please.\"","choices":[{"text":"\"Then we free it. Break the glyph.\"","next":"u19_gloam_glyph","do":["rel:lumen:+3","journal:Lumen vouched for the Mirror-Knight prisoner. 'This one is true. I have seen its inside.'"]},{"text":"\"There's no time. We have a king to face.\"","next":"u19_gloam_leave","do":["rel:lumen:-4","journal:Lumen begged me to free the Mirror-Knight. I refused. The child would not look at me after."]}]},{"id":"u19_gloam_glyph","chapter":"CHAPTER XIX · THE DROWNED COURT","text":"The glyph is a knot of pale light burned into the iron. It answers to certainty, the knight says — you must lay your hand on it and mean, without flinching, that this prisoner deserves to walk free. Doubt, and it bites.\n\nYou press your palm flat against the cold fire.","choices":[{"text":"Hold your hand and your certainty. (Trust)","next":"u19_gloam_free","check":{"stat":"trust","dc":13,"pass":"u19_gloam_free","fail":"u19_gloam_glyph_fail","onPass":["trust:+4"],"onFail":["hp:-4","fear:6"]}},{"text":"Draw back. Leave the knight to its cell.","next":"u19_gloam_leave"}]},{"id":"u19_gloam_glyph_fail","chapter":"CHAPTER XIX · THE DROWNED COURT","text":"The glyph reads the flinch in you — the small voice that wonders if the knight is one more mask — and it bites. White fire runs up your arm. You wrench your hand away, palm smoking, breath ragged.\n\n\"You doubted,\" the knight says, not unkindly. \"Everyone here doubts. It is the water in the walls. Try again, if you can hold your heart still. Or go. I have waited an age; I can wait a little more.\"","choices":[{"text":"Steady yourself. Press again.","next":"u19_gloam_free2"},{"text":"Leave the knight. Climb to the hall.","next":"u19_gloam_leave"}]},{"id":"u19_gloam_free2","chapter":"CHAPTER XIX · THE DROWNED COURT","text":"You breathe until the doubt sinks, the way silt sinks in still water. You think of the king's real laugh you have never heard, and decide it was worth hearing, and press.\n\nThe glyph flares — and gutters out. The iron door sighs open on its ruined hinges.","choices":[{"text":"Help the knight up.","next":"u19_gloam_free"}]},{"id":"u19_gloam_free","chapter":"CHAPTER XIX · THE DROWNED COURT","text":"The Mirror-Knight rises. Taller than you thought, plated in glass that is half-armor, half-reflection. Where you stand in its surface, you stand a little braver.\n\n\"Gloam,\" it says. \"That was my name, when names were worn instead of hidden. I will walk with you to the throne. I know the false king's tricks; I taught him half of them, believing him my liege.\"\n\nIt lays a cold, mirrored hand on your shoulder. \"When he offers you his face, refuse it. That is the whole of my counsel. Everything else, we survive together.\"","choices":[{"text":"\"Then walk with me, Gloam.\"","next":"u19_throne_approach","do":["companion:gloam","add_item:mirror_shard","trust:+5","journal:Freed the Mirror-Knight, Gloam. Its counsel: when the false king offers you his face, refuse it."]}]},{"id":"u19_gloam_leave","chapter":"CHAPTER XIX · THE DROWNED COURT","text":"You climb back toward the hall, the knight's smooth voice following you up the stair.\n\n\"Go on, then. Perhaps you're wise. Perhaps I am one more of his faces, set here to catch the kind.\" A pause. \"I hope you're wise. I hope, too, that you're wrong.\"\n\nThe cold water lets go of your legs. Above, the wrong-way torches wait.","choices":[{"text":"Enter the hall and face the throne.","next":"u19_throne_approach","do":["fear:4"]}]},{"id":"u19_throne_approach","chapter":"CHAPTER XIX · THE DROWNED COURT","text":"You cross the long hall. Your footsteps make no sound; the false king's voice makes all of it.\n\n\"Come closer. Let me look at one of the living.\" He leans forward on the drowned throne, water sheeting off the arms. Up close his borrowed face is beautiful and slightly loose, like a portrait hung on a peg too small. \"I know you. Not you — your kind. You come for a child, or a father, or a crown, and you find me wearing all three.\"\n\nHe smiles the king's smile. It fits wrong.\n\n\"Tell me true, and I may let you leave breathing: what did you come to take?\"","choices":[{"text":"\"The truth. You are not the king.\"","next":"u19_accuse"},{"text":"\"Only passage. I'll bend the knee if I must.\"","next":"u19_bend_knee"},{"text":"\"Your face. Whose was it before?\"","next":"u19_whose_face"},{"text":"Say nothing. Let Gloam speak. (companion: gloam)","next":"u19_gloam_speaks","require":"companion:gloam"}]},{"id":"u19_gloam_speaks","chapter":"CHAPTER XIX · THE DROWNED COURT","text":"Gloam steps past you, glass ringing on stone. In the black helm, the false king sees himself — and for the first time the borrowed face slips, showing something grey and unfinished beneath.\n\n\"I remember your true liege's laugh,\" Gloam says. \"You never learned it. You practiced the frown, the decree, the cold hand on the treaty. But you could never do the laugh, because you have nothing in you to laugh with.\"\n\nThe false king recoils on his throne. \"You. I drowned you in glass.\"\n\n\"You kept me in glass. There is a difference. It is the difference that will unmake you.\"\n\nThe hall's water begins, very slightly, to boil.","choices":[{"text":"\"He's right. Take off the king's face.\"","next":"u19_accuse","do":["trust:+3"]}]},{"id":"u19_bend_knee","chapter":"CHAPTER XIX · THE DROWNED COURT","text":"You lower yourself to one knee in the shallow water. It laps cold at your shin.\n\n\"Wiser than most,\" the false king purrs. \"Kneel, and I give you a gift beyond passage. I give you what everyone here wanted: to be sure. No more doubt. No more grief. You need only wear a face I choose, and speak the words I choose, and you will never be uncertain again.\"\n\nHe reaches down. His hand comes off at the wrist — not blood, but light — and floats toward your face, opening like a mask about to be pressed on.\n\nGloam's counsel rings in your memory, if you heard it: *refuse his face.*","choices":[{"text":"Refuse. Strike the mask away and rise.","next":"u19_accuse","do":["trust:+4","journal:The false king offered me the gift of certainty — to wear a face he chose. I refused."]},{"text":"Let the mask settle. Just for a moment.","next":"u19_mask_touch"}]},{"id":"u19_mask_touch","chapter":"CHAPTER XIX · THE DROWNED COURT","text":"The mask of light presses cool against your face and, for one breath, it is heaven. The doubt drains out of you. The grief for the lost one goes quiet. You are sure. You are so beautifully sure.\n\nThen you feel it start to take root, threading grey fingers behind your eyes, and you understand this is how the courtiers were made — sure, and empty, and his.\n\nYou tear it off. It comes away with a sound like a peeling scab, and takes something with it: some of your warmth, some of your nerve.","choices":[{"text":"Hurl the mask into the pool and rise, shaking.","next":"u19_accuse","do":["fear:10","trust:-4","journal:I let the false king's mask touch me. It nearly rooted. I got it off, but it took something warm with it."]}]},{"id":"u19_whose_face","chapter":"CHAPTER XIX · THE DROWNED COURT","text":"\"Whose face?\" The king traces the borrowed jaw with borrowed fingers. \"Yours, one day, perhaps. It has been a hundred faces. A guardsman who wept. A queen who did not. A child — always a child underneath, the first face I stole, so long ago I have worn out the memory of it.\"\n\nFor a moment the beautiful king-mask thins, and beneath it you glimpse something small and grey and drowned, a face no bigger than a boy's, mouth open in an old scream that never finished.\n\n\"Do not pity me,\" he says, and the mask snaps back. \"Pity is a door. And I keep all the doors.\"","choices":[{"text":"\"I pity you anyway. You're not the king.\"","next":"u19_accuse","do":["journal:Beneath the king-mask: a drowned child's face, the first the false king ever stole."]},{"text":"\"Then keep this door shut behind me.\" (bend)","next":"u19_bend_knee"}]},{"id":"u19_accuse","chapter":"CHAPTER XIX · THE DROWNED COURT","text":"\"You are not the king,\" you say, and the words land like stones in the still water. The courtiers along the walls shudder — a hundred held breaths, released at once. The frozen page's single tear finally falls.\n\nThe false king rises. He is taller standing. The borrowed face darkens, the beauty curdling. \"No,\" he agrees, and his voice is many voices now, all the stolen ones speaking together. \"But possession is nine parts of a crown. Say it again and I will drown the word in your throat.\"\n\nThe water of the hall rises around your ankles, your knees, cold and climbing, and the throne-pool churns.","choices":[{"text":"Draw steel. End the imposter.","next":"u19_combat"},{"text":"Try to name the drowned child beneath the mask. (Fear)","next":"u19_name_check"},{"text":"Stand your ground with Gloam. (companion: gloam)","next":"u19_combat_gloam","require":"companion:gloam"}]},{"id":"u19_name_check","chapter":"CHAPTER XIX · THE DROWNED COURT","text":"You do not draw. You reach instead for the drowned child you glimpsed — the first face, the true one under all the theft. If you can hold your nerve against the rising water and the many-voiced roar, you might speak to that child, and the false king cannot bear to be a child again.\n\nThe water is at your chest. His many voices scream you down.","choices":[{"text":"Hold your nerve. Speak past the king, to the child. (Fear)","check":{"stat":"fear","dc":14,"pass":"u19_name_success","fail":"u19_combat","onPass":["trust:+6"],"onFail":["hp:-3","fear:8"]}},{"text":"Nerve fails. Draw steel instead.","next":"u19_combat"}]},{"id":"u19_name_success","chapter":"CHAPTER XIX · THE DROWNED COURT","text":"You find a stillness under the fear, and you speak into it — not to the king, but through him, to the small drowned thing that wore the first face.\n\n\"You were cold,\" you say. \"You were cold and no one came, and you took a warm face so you would not be cold alone. I know. It's over now. You can put it down.\"\n\nThe king staggers as if struck. The many voices break apart into one thin child's wail. The rising water stops, trembles, and begins to fall. The borrowed king-face slides half off, hanging by the jaw, and beneath it the grey child weeps without sound.\n\nHe is not defeated. But he is listening now, which he has not done in centuries.","choices":[{"text":"Press the mercy. Offer your hand.","next":"u19_court_falls","do":["trust:+8","fear:-10","journal:I spoke to the drowned child under the false king's mask. He stopped. He listened. The court began to wake."]},{"text":"He's weakened — strike now while you can.","next":"u19_combat","do":["trust:-6","fear:6"]}]},{"id":"u19_combat_gloam","chapter":"CHAPTER XIX · THE DROWNED COURT","text":"Gloam sets its glass shoulder to yours. In the mirror of its armor the false king sees a dozen of you, a dozen knights, and cannot tell which blade is real.\n\n\"His trick is the reflection,\" Gloam says. \"So we give him too many. Strike where I am not, and I will be where he strikes.\"\n\nThe false king surges from the throne in a wall of black water, borrowed face splitting into a snarl, and the two of you meet him.","combat":{"enemy":"The False King","ehp":11,"edmg":3,"win":"u19_court_falls","lose":"u19_combat_lose","flee":"u19_combat_flee"}},{"id":"u19_combat","chapter":"CHAPTER XIX · THE DROWNED COURT","text":"Steel clears the scabbard. The false king comes off the throne in a surge of black water, the borrowed king-face splitting down the middle to show the grey drowned thing beneath, screaming with a hundred stolen mouths.\n\nHe fights like a reflection — always where you did not swing, never where you did. The water drags at your legs. But he is a thief of faces, not a soldier, and thieves tire.","combat":{"enemy":"The False King","ehp":13,"edmg":4,"win":"u19_court_falls","lose":"u19_combat_lose","flee":"u19_combat_flee"}},{"id":"u19_combat_flee","chapter":"CHAPTER XIX · THE DROWNED COURT","text":"You break off and run for the drowned gallery. Water hammers at your heels; the many-voiced roar chases you between the scratched-out portraits.\n\nYou throw yourself through the low undercroft door and slam it. The pounding stops. In the sudden quiet you hear only your own heart and the drip of black water off your sleeves.\n\nThere is another way up from here — a servants' stair that climbs past the drowned court and out toward the pale country beyond. You are not proud of running. But you are alive to run.","choices":[{"text":"Climb the servants' stair, out of the Court.","next":"u20_start","do":["fear:8","trust:-3","journal:I fled the false king rather than fall to him. The Drowned Court still stands behind me. So does he."]}]},{"id":"u19_combat_lose","chapter":"CHAPTER XIX · THE DROWNED COURT","text":"A wall of black water takes you off your feet. The false king's borrowed hands close on your face, cold and eager, and everything goes to drowning-dark.\n\nYou wake on a cold flagstone with no memory of climbing out, your gear soaked, blood in your mouth, one breath from the long silence. Something pulled you clear — the frozen page, perhaps, or Gloam, or the water itself deciding it was not yet done with you.\n\nThe hall behind you is empty. The throne is empty. For now.","choices":[{"text":"Crawl up and drag yourself onward.","next":"u20_start","do":["hp:-2","fear:12","trust:-4","journal:The false king nearly drowned me. Someone — something — pulled me clear. The throne sits empty behind me, waiting to be filled again."]}]},{"id":"u19_court_falls","chapter":"CHAPTER XIX · THE DROWNED COURT","text":"The false king comes apart the way a reflection comes apart when the water is struck — into rings, into ripples, into nothing. The borrowed king-face floats free, a peeled and empty thing, and sinks.\n\nThe drowned court wakes. Along the walls, a hundred held breaths finally release; the courtiers slump, blink, become people. The frozen page rubs her wet cheek and stares at her own moving hand as if it is a miracle, which it is.\n\nThe throne stands empty. The water drains through cracks that were not there before, out toward the pale country beyond the far door.","choices":[{"text":"See to the waking court.","next":"u19_aftermath"},{"text":"Take the empty king-face from the water. (raise fear)","next":"u19_take_face"}]},{"id":"u19_take_face","chapter":"CHAPTER XIX · THE DROWNED COURT","text":"You lift the peeled king-face from the draining pool. It is light as a wet leaf and cold as the inside of a mirror. It would fit you. Some part of you, tired of doubt, wants it to.\n\nGloam, if it is with you, goes very still. The waking page whispers, \"Put it down. Put it down, road-friend.\"\n\nYou fold the empty face and tuck it away. Just in case. Just in case. The wanting of it sits in your chest like a swallowed coal.","choices":[{"text":"Keep it. Turn to the court.","next":"u19_aftermath","do":["add_item:mirror_shard","fear:10","trust:-3","journal:I took the false king's peeled face from the water. I tell myself it is only in case. The wanting of it will not sit still."]},{"text":"No. Let it sink. Turn to the court.","next":"u19_aftermath","do":["trust:+4","fear:-4","journal:I let the false king's empty face sink. Some crowns are only masks with better manners."]}]},{"id":"u19_aftermath","chapter":"CHAPTER XIX · THE DROWNED COURT","text":"The waking courtiers gather to you, dripping, dazed, grateful. The page — her name is Wren, she remembers now — presses your hands.\n\n\"He wore us,\" she says. \"He wore the whole court as one face, and we forgot we were more than his frown. You un-wore us.\" She looks toward the far door, where the last water is draining into pale light. \"That way leads out of the Court. Deeper in. The wood, they say. The unmaking wood. Whatever you're chasing, it went that way. It always goes that way.\"\n\nShe presses something into your palm: a shard of the throne's black glass, edged in silver.","choices":[{"text":"Thank Wren. Take the far door.","next":"u19_depart","do":["add_item:mirror_shard","trust:+3","journal:Wren and the freed court sent me on toward the unmaking wood, deeper into the Underland."]},{"text":"Ask if any here saw the one you seek.","next":"u19_ask_court"}]},{"id":"u19_ask_court","chapter":"CHAPTER XIX · THE DROWNED COURT","text":"\"The one I'm looking for,\" you say. \"Small. Frightened. Came through not long ago.\"\n\nWren's brow furrows. \"There was a shape that passed through and did not stop,\" she says slowly. \"It would not look at the throne. It went straight for the far door, toward the wood, the way a candle goes toward a draught.\" She hesitates. \"It carried a grey light. Or a grey light carried it. Hard to say which, here.\"\n\nA grey light. Lumen's light, or its like. Someone is being led into the wood ahead of you.","choices":[{"text":"Take the far door at once.","next":"u19_depart","do":["add_item:mirror_shard","journal:A shape carrying a grey light went ahead of me into the wood, and would not look at the throne. I follow."]}]},{"id":"u19_depart","chapter":"CHAPTER XIX · THE DROWNED COURT","text":"You cross the draining hall to the far door. Behind you the Drowned Court is becoming a living one again — quiet voices, a swept floor, someone weeping the good kind of tears. It is the first thing on this side of the Gate that has healed instead of hollowed.\n\nBeyond the door the water gives way to root and mist. The pale country begins. Somewhere in it, trees stand that unmake what walks between them.\n\nYou step out of the Court and into the wood's long shadow.","choices":[{"text":"Into the unmaking wood.","next":"u20_start"},{"text":"Look back once at the waking court, then go.","next":"u20_start","do":["trust:+2","journal:Left the Drowned Court alive and awake behind me. Not everything here has to hollow out."]}]}]},"story/chapter_20.json":{"scenes":[{"id":"u20_start","chapter":"CHAPTER XX · THE UNMAKING WOOD","text":"The Drowned Court falls away behind you, and the ground softens into loam that remembers being water.\n\nA wood begins. Not a border you cross — a decision the land makes about you. One step there is open marsh; the next, trees close overhead like ribs.\n\nBut they are wrong, these trees. Their bark peels into pages. Their branches end in fingers, in antlers, in the shapes of things that were not trees. One leans low across the path, and where a knot should be there is the smooth blind hollow of a face, mid-forgetting.\n\nThe silence here is a listening silence. You know it now.","choices":[{"text":"Set foot on the path. Name where you are.","next":"u20_enter","do":["flag:region_uwood","journal:Entered the Unmaking Wood — trees that are forgetting they were ever trees.","fear:+4"]},{"text":"Skirt the treeline, looking for a way around.","next":"u20_enter","do":["flag:region_uwood","journal:There is no way around the Unmaking Wood — the treeline loops back to the same first tree. Only through.","fear:+3"]}]},{"id":"u20_enter","chapter":"CHAPTER XX · THE UNMAKING WOOD","text":"Inside, the light is grey and comes from nowhere. The path is a suggestion, marked here and there by white stones that turn out, when you look, to be teeth.\n\nA sound reaches you — low, rhythmic, a scraping. Metal on bark. Someone is here, and they are working at something with a patience that does not belong to the living.\n\nAhead, the trees thin around a clearing. In its center, a figure in dull armour kneels before a great tree, scraping at the trunk with the edge of a broken sword. Trying, you realise, to carve a name into wood that keeps smoothing itself blank.","choices":[{"text":"Approach the kneeling figure openly.","next":"u20_knight_meet"},{"text":"Circle the clearing and watch first.","next":"u20_watch"},{"text":"Call out from where you stand.","next":"u20_callout"}]},{"id":"u20_watch","chapter":"CHAPTER XX · THE UNMAKING WOOD","text":"You keep to the trees and watch.\n\nThe knight carves. The wood forgets. Carves. Forgets. Each stroke lays down a letter; each letter fades before the next is done, the grain closing over it like water over a stone.\n\nThe armour is old and grand and utterly tarnished — a courtly make, worn to grey. Where the visor should show a face there is only a polished plate, and in that plate, faintly, you can see your own reflection watching back.\n\nThe knight has no face of their own. Only a mirror where one should be.","choices":[{"text":"Step out. Let them see you approach.","next":"u20_knight_meet","do":["journal:The kneeling knight has a mirror for a face."]},{"text":"Study the tree they are carving. (Perception)","next":"u20_watch_tree","check":{"stat":"trust","dc":12,"pass":"u20_watch_tree_pass","fail":"u20_watch_tree"}}]},{"id":"u20_watch_tree_pass","chapter":"CHAPTER XX · THE UNMAKING WOOD","text":"You watch the carving long enough to read the shape of what the knight keeps trying to write. It is not a warning. It is not a curse.\n\nIt is a name. The same name, over and over, half-formed and lost — the knight's own, you would wager, being carved so it cannot be forgotten, and forgotten anyway, faster than any hand can cut.\n\nThis is a creature fighting its own unmaking with a broken blade. That is worth knowing before you speak.","choices":[{"text":"Step out. Approach with care.","next":"u20_knight_meet","do":["trust:+3","journal:The mirror-knight carves their own name into a tree, over and over, to keep from forgetting it."]}]},{"id":"u20_watch_tree","chapter":"CHAPTER XX · THE UNMAKING WOOD","text":"The carving means nothing you can parse from here — marks that fill and fade, fill and fade. Whatever the knight is writing, the wood eats it before it can be read.\n\nYou have watched long enough. Waiting is its own kind of forgetting, in a place like this.","choices":[{"text":"Step out. Approach the knight.","next":"u20_knight_meet"}]},{"id":"u20_callout","chapter":"CHAPTER XX · THE UNMAKING WOOD","text":"\"You there —\" your voice cracks across the clearing, and the wood flinches. Leaves that were faces turn toward the sound.\n\nThe knight stops carving. Slowly, the grey helm lifts. Where the face should be, a mirror catches the grey light and throws back a warped image of you.\n\n\"A voice,\" the knight says. The words come out rusted, unused. \"You still have a voice. Then you are not long here.\" They set the broken sword across their knee. \"Come. Stand where I can see you, before the wood decides you were never speaking at all.\"","choices":[{"text":"Come forward.","next":"u20_knight_meet"}]},{"id":"u20_knight_meet","chapter":"CHAPTER XX · THE UNMAKING WOOD","text":"You cross the clearing. The knight rises — tall, deliberate, armour whispering like turned pages. The mirror-face holds your reflection and nothing else.\n\n\"I was a knight of the Drowned Court,\" the mirror says. Its voice arrives a half-beat after its stillness, as if reflected. \"Before it drowned. I am called Gloam now, because I have forgotten what I was called then. The wood took my face and gave me this instead — so that I might always see what I am, and never who.\"\n\nGloam tilts the mirror toward you.\n\n\"I am bound by the Mirror Law. I cannot lie to a reflection, and to me, everything is one. So I will tell you true: this wood unmakes. It has been unmaking me for a hundred years. What brings a thing with a voice into a place that eats them?\"","choices":[{"text":"\"I follow the Keeper. To end the Gate.\"","next":"u20_knight_purpose"},{"text":"\"I'm looking for {missing_name}. {missing_pron} was taken.\"","next":"u20_knight_purpose"},{"text":"\"What is the Mirror Law?\"","next":"u20_knight_law"}]},{"id":"u20_knight_law","chapter":"CHAPTER XX · THE UNMAKING WOOD","text":"\"The Mirror Law.\" Gloam turns the polished face slowly, and the clearing bends around it. \"In the Underland a reflection can be truer than the thing it reflects. Whatever a mirror holds, it holds truly. Break a mirror and you free what it kept — or you loose it upon the world. There is no telling which until the glass is already in pieces.\"\n\n\"I am bound to it. I cannot pass a mirror without stopping. I cannot break one without asking leave. And I cannot lie — not to you, not to the trees, not to the last true thing I have, which is my own reflection.\"\n\nGloam looks at you. \"You will need the Law before you leave this wood. Remember it.\"","choices":[{"text":"\"Then let me tell you true why I'm here.\"","next":"u20_knight_purpose","do":["flag:codex_mirror","journal:The Mirror Law: a reflection can be truer than its source. Break a mirror to free — or loose — what it holds."]}]},{"id":"u20_knight_purpose","chapter":"CHAPTER XX · THE UNMAKING WOOD","text":"You tell Gloam what you carry: the Keeper's trail, the lost child, the road that only goes deeper.\n\nThe mirror is quiet a long moment. In it, your reflection stands straighter than you feel.\n\n\"Then we are pointed the same way,\" Gloam says. \"The Keeper made this wood, in a fashion. Every place the Underland forgets, it forgets a little more like him. He is a man unmaking himself so slowly he has mistaken it for eternity. To reach him, you must pass through what he is becoming.\"\n\nGloam lifts the broken sword. \"I would walk with you. But the wood will test whether I am still knight enough to be worth the walking. And so will you.\"","choices":[{"text":"\"Prove it, then. Walk with me.\"","next":"u20_test_intro"},{"text":"\"Why would a forgotten thing help me remember?\"","next":"u20_knight_why"}]},{"id":"u20_knight_why","chapter":"CHAPTER XX · THE UNMAKING WOOD","text":"\"Because you have a voice, and I have a mirror,\" Gloam says. \"Between us we are almost a whole person. Because if the Gate ends, the wood ends, and the wood is the hand that is erasing me. And because —\"\n\nThe mirror-face dims, as if a cloud crossed the grey light.\n\n\"— because a hundred years ago a child came through the Gate, and I swore to a king I no longer remember that I would bring her home. I have forgotten her name. I have forgotten the king. But the oath is the last thing the wood cannot chew through. An oath is a name a soul gives itself.\"\n\nGloam straightens. \"So. I keep it. Even blind. Especially blind.\"","choices":[{"text":"\"Then keep it beside me.\"","next":"u20_test_intro","do":["trust:+4","journal:Gloam swore a hundred years ago to bring a lost child home. It is the last thing the wood cannot erase."]}]},{"id":"u20_test_intro","chapter":"CHAPTER XX · THE UNMAKING WOOD","text":"Gloam leads you deeper, and the wood grows worse.\n\nThe trees here are further gone. One has forgotten it is standing and lies across the path breathing slowly. Another has forgotten up from down and grows into the grey ground, roots in the air like a drowned man's hands.\n\n\"The wood asks a question of everything that enters,\" Gloam says. \"Whether you are still yourself. It asks with hunger, but it is a fair question. Steady your mind. Here it comes.\"\n\nAhead, the path forks. Down the left, a warm light and the smell of home. Down the right, the cold grey continues. There was no fork a moment ago.","choices":[{"text":"The warm light. Follow it. (Resolve)","next":"u20_lure_fail","check":{"stat":"fear","dc":13,"pass":"u20_lure_pass","fail":"u20_lure_fail","onFail":["fear:+6"]}},{"text":"The cold grey road. Keep to the true path.","next":"u20_true_path","do":["trust:+2"]},{"text":"Ask Gloam which is real.","next":"u20_ask_gloam_fork"}]},{"id":"u20_ask_gloam_fork","chapter":"CHAPTER XX · THE UNMAKING WOOD","text":"\"Which is real?\" you ask.\n\nGloam turns the mirror down both roads in turn. In the glass, the warm light shows as a mouth. The cold grey shows as a mouth too — but a smaller one.\n\n\"Neither is safe,\" the knight says. \"But the warm one is the wood pretending to be your mother. The cold one is merely the wood. When something offers you home in a place like this, it wants the shape of you that goes soft at the word.\"\n\nGloam steps onto the cold grey path. \"Come. Be hard a little longer.\"","choices":[{"text":"Follow Gloam onto the cold road.","next":"u20_true_path","do":["trust:+3","journal:In the Unmaking Wood, warmth is a lure. Home is the shape the wood wants to eat."]}]},{"id":"u20_lure_pass","chapter":"CHAPTER XX · THE UNMAKING WOOD","text":"You walk toward the warm light — and stop, three steps in, because the smell is wrong.\n\nIt is bread, and hearthsmoke, and someone's arms. It is everything you left behind. And it is coming from a hollow in a tree where a face is trying to grow, working the memory out of you to wear as bait.\n\nYou name it aloud: \"You are not home. You are the wood, wearing home.\"\n\nThe light goes out like a snuffed wick. The hollow closes, sullen. Gloam is beside you, mirror bright with something like approval.\n\n\"Good,\" the knight says. \"You are still yourself. That is rarer here than you know.\"","choices":[{"text":"Rejoin the true path.","next":"u20_true_path","do":["trust:+3"]}]},{"id":"u20_lure_fail","chapter":"CHAPTER XX · THE UNMAKING WOOD","text":"You walk into the warmth and it closes over you.\n\nFor a moment you are small again, and safe, and someone is calling you in for supper in a voice you would follow off a cliff. You take another step. Another. The path behind you forgets it was ever a path.\n\nThen a hand of cold metal closes on your shoulder and hauls you back. Gloam's mirror is inches from your face, and in it you see yourself already half-erased — features smoothing, mouth going blank.\n\n\"Look,\" Gloam commands. \"Look at what it was making of you.\"\n\nYou look. The horror of it snaps you back into your own skin.","choices":[{"text":"Stagger back onto the true path.","next":"u20_true_path","do":["fear:+4","journal:The wood nearly wore me as bait. Gloam's mirror showed me my own face going blank."]}]},{"id":"u20_true_path","chapter":"CHAPTER XX · THE UNMAKING WOOD","text":"The cold grey road holds. It is not kind, but it is honest, and honesty is the only footing this wood offers.\n\nGloam walks at your side now, matched to your pace. The knight has stopped carving. Whatever the wood tried to make of you back there, passing the test seems to have steadied Gloam too — as if your remembering fed some of theirs.\n\n\"Stay,\" you say. \"Walk with me the rest of the way.\"\n\nThe mirror inclines, a bow made of light.\n\n\"I will,\" Gloam says. \"Reflection to reflection. Where you go, I go.\"","choices":[{"text":"Take the Mirror-Knight into your company.","next":"u20_gloam_joins","do":["companion:gloam","flag:codex_mirror","rel:gloam:+5","journal:Gloam, the Mirror-Knight, joined me — bound by the Mirror Law, sworn to a child whose name the wood ate."]}]},{"id":"u20_gloam_joins","chapter":"CHAPTER XX · THE UNMAKING WOOD","text":"Gloam falls in beside you, and the wood grows quieter, as if wary of a thing that has stopped forgetting.\n\n\"There is a place ahead,\" the knight says. \"The heart of the wood. It is where I was carving, before you came — or a truer version of it. A tree that remembers everything, because everything the wood forgets is carried there and hung.\"\n\n\"The Hollow Oak. It wears the faces the trees have lost. And it guards a thing you will want: a shard of the first mirror, from which all the Underland's reflections were cut. With it, the Mirror Law is yours to spend — once.\"\n\nGloam's voice lowers. \"But the Oak does not give. The Oak keeps. We will have to take.\"","choices":[{"text":"\"Lead me to the Hollow Oak.\"","next":"u20_oak_approach"},{"text":"\"Tell me how the Oak fights first.\"","next":"u20_oak_lore"}]},{"id":"u20_oak_lore","chapter":"CHAPTER XX · THE UNMAKING WOOD","text":"\"It does not move,\" Gloam says. \"It does not need to. It reaches with what it has stolen. The faces on its bark will speak in voices you love — your king, your dead, the child you seek. They will beg you to stop, to stay, to lay down your blade and be hung among them.\"\n\n\"The Oak fights by remembering you back at yourself until you cannot tell which of you is the reflection. Do not listen to the faces. Listen to me, or to your own hand on your weapon. Those are real.\"\n\nGloam lifts the broken sword. \"When we strike, strike the trunk, not the faces. The faces are victims. The wood behind them is the thief.\"","choices":[{"text":"\"Then we strike the trunk. Lead on.\"","next":"u20_oak_approach","do":["trust:+2","journal:The Hollow Oak fights with stolen faces and stolen voices. Strike the trunk, not the faces."]}]},{"id":"u20_oak_approach","chapter":"CHAPTER XX · THE UNMAKING WOOD","text":"The heart of the wood is a hollow where no other tree will grow. In its center stands the Oak.\n\nIt is vast and grey and covered, root to crown, in faces. Hundreds. Thousands. Every face a tree lost when it forgot it was a tree, and every face still moving — mouths working, eyes rolling, all of them turning toward you as one.\n\nThey begin to speak. Your name, in a hundred beloved voices. Come. Stay. Be remembered. Set it down.\n\nHalf-buried in the trunk, glinting, is a wedge of black glass — the mirror shard. Gloam sets their broken blade against the bark.\n\n\"Now,\" the knight says. \"Before it learns your face.\"","choices":[{"text":"Steel yourself against the voices. (Will)","next":"u20_oak_will","check":{"stat":"fear","dc":14,"pass":"u20_oak_fight","fail":"u20_oak_fight_weak","onFail":["hp:-4","fear:+5"]}},{"text":"Strike the trunk at once, no hesitation.","next":"u20_oak_fight"},{"text":"Let Gloam hold the mirror between you and the faces.","next":"u20_oak_mirror_shield"}]},{"id":"u20_oak_mirror_shield","chapter":"CHAPTER XX · THE UNMAKING WOOD","text":"\"Gloam — the mirror. Turn it on them.\"\n\nThe knight understands at once. Gloam plants themselves between you and the Oak and lifts the mirror-face to the tree. The stolen faces see themselves reflected — and falter. A thing built of other people's memories does not like to look at itself.\n\nThe voices stumble. The chorus breaks into confusion.\n\n\"Strike now,\" Gloam says through gritted light. \"I cannot hold their own faces up to them for long.\"","choices":[{"text":"Strike the trunk while the Oak is dazzled.","next":"u20_oak_fight","do":["rel:gloam:+3","trust:+2"]}]},{"id":"u20_oak_will","chapter":"CHAPTER XX · THE UNMAKING WOOD","text":"You let the voices come and do not answer them. Beloved as they are, you hold each one at arm's length and name it: a theft. A theft. A theft.\n\nYour own name, spoken in your mother's voice, nearly undoes you — and you turn it aside too, because your mother is not here, and would not ask you to stop.\n\nThe faces snarl, their sweetness curdling. The Oak knows now that flattery will not open you. It will have to fight.","choices":[{"text":"Raise your weapon. Strike the trunk.","next":"u20_oak_fight","do":["trust:+3"]}]},{"id":"u20_oak_fight_weak","chapter":"CHAPTER XX · THE UNMAKING WOOD","text":"The voices get in.\n\nFor a breath you believe them — that stopping would be rest, that being hung among the faces would be a kind of peace. Your arm lowers. The nearest branch reaches, almost tender, for your cheek.\n\nGloam's mailed hand knocks the branch aside and shoves you back a step, and the pain of it clears your head. But the Oak has your measure now, and it strikes first.","choices":[{"text":"Recover and fight through it.","next":"u20_oak_fight"}]},{"id":"u20_oak_fight","chapter":"CHAPTER XX · THE UNMAKING WOOD","text":"You strike the trunk and the whole Oak screams with a thousand stolen mouths.\n\nBranches whip down like flails. Roots tear up out of the grey earth, groping. The faces gnash. Gloam sets their back to yours, broken sword flashing, and together you carve at the ancient wood while it tries to carve you back into nothing.\n\nStrike for the trunk. Ignore the faces. End it.","choices":[],"combat":{"enemy":"The Hollow Oak","ehp":14,"edmg":4,"win":"u20_oak_felled","lose":"u20_oak_lose","flee":"u20_oak_flee"}},{"id":"u20_oak_flee","chapter":"CHAPTER XX · THE UNMAKING WOOD","text":"You break away, hauling Gloam with you, and crash out of the hollow while the Oak howls behind you.\n\nThe faces do not follow — they cannot. The Oak is rooted, and its reach ends where the hollow ends. You lean against a lesser tree, gasping, and Gloam's mirror finds your face.\n\n\"We can still take the shard,\" the knight says quietly. \"But not by fleeing again. It knows us now. We go back, and we finish it, or we leave this wood empty-handed and half-remembered.\"","choices":[{"text":"Go back. Finish the Oak.","next":"u20_oak_fight"},{"text":"Leave the shard. Take the deep road out.","next":"u20_leave_empty"}]},{"id":"u20_oak_lose","chapter":"CHAPTER XX · THE UNMAKING WOOD","text":"The Oak's branches wrap you and lift, and the faces crowd close, welcoming, and you feel your own name begin to loosen from you like a tooth.\n\nThen a mirror is thrust into the tangle — Gloam, forcing the glass against the bark, and the Oak recoils from its own reflection long enough for the knight to cut you free.\n\nYou fall. Gloam drags you clear of the hollow, armour scored, mirror cracked but whole.\n\n\"You are not lost,\" the knight insists, holding the glass to your face until you see yourself in it. \"Say your name. Say it now.\"\n\nYou say it. It holds. Barely.","choices":[{"text":"Rise. Take the shard by other means.","next":"u20_shard_grieve","do":["hp:+2","fear:+6","rel:gloam:+4","journal:The Oak nearly unmade me. Gloam held a mirror to my face until my own name held."]}]},{"id":"u20_oak_felled","chapter":"CHAPTER XX · THE UNMAKING WOOD","text":"The trunk splits with a sound like a hall full of doors slamming at once.\n\nThe Oak sags. Its faces go slack, one by one, the stolen memories loosed at last — and for an instant the air is full of them, a hundred lives rising like startled birds, going wherever forgotten things go when they are finally let free.\n\nWhat remains is only a dead grey tree, and, at its heart, laid bare in the broken wood, the mirror shard: a wedge of black glass no longer than your hand, deep as a well, holding a reflection that is not the clearing.\n\nGloam kneels beside it, does not touch it. \"The Mirror Law forbids me to take it,\" the knight says. \"It is yours to lift, or leave.\"","choices":[{"text":"Lift the mirror shard from the ruined Oak.","next":"u20_shard_taken","do":["add_item:mirror_shard","journal:Took the mirror shard from the Hollow Oak — a piece of the first mirror. Spend the Mirror Law once.","trust:+3"]}]},{"id":"u20_shard_grieve","chapter":"CHAPTER XX · THE UNMAKING WOOD","text":"You cannot best the Oak with steel now — you are too spent. So you try what Gloam's Mirror Law suggests: you speak to it.\n\n\"You are a thief,\" you tell the tree, \"but you were something before you were a thief. You forgot, same as everything here. I am not angry at you. I am only taking back one piece of what you stole.\"\n\nThe Oak stills. Perhaps something in it, some grain of the tree it was, remembers being spoken to gently. Its grip loosens. In the crook of a lowered branch, the mirror shard glints, offered rather than guarded.\n\nGloam watches, silent, the mirror-face unreadable.","choices":[{"text":"Take the offered shard.","next":"u20_shard_taken","do":["add_item:mirror_shard","fear:-3","journal:I did not kill the Oak. I spoke to it, and it gave up the mirror shard.","trust:+4"]}]},{"id":"u20_shard_taken","chapter":"CHAPTER XX · THE UNMAKING WOOD","text":"The shard is cold and light and wrong to hold, the way still water is wrong to breathe. In its black surface you see not the wood but a figure standing in grey armour, faceless — Gloam, as Gloam was before the wood, when the mirror was still a face and the face still had a name.\n\nGloam sees it too. The knight goes very still.\n\n\"That is what I looked like,\" Gloam says softly. \"I had forgotten I had a chin. A brow. Eyes the colour of — I cannot say. But there. That was me.\"\n\nThe knight looks at you, and the mirror-face holds your reflection and, faintly, its own.\n\n\"The shard shows the true form under the forgetting. It can free what a mirror keeps, or loose it. There is a choice here, and it is yours to make, not mine. The Law binds me to abide it either way.\"","choices":[{"text":"Use the shard to restore Gloam's true face.","next":"u20_shard_restore"},{"text":"Keep the shard whole. Save its one use for the Keeper.","next":"u20_shard_keep"},{"text":"\"What happens to you if I use it on you?\"","next":"u20_shard_ask"}]},{"id":"u20_shard_ask","chapter":"CHAPTER XX · THE UNMAKING WOOD","text":"\"If you spend it on me,\" Gloam says slowly, \"I will have a face again. My own. And with it, perhaps, some of what the wood ate — the king, the oath, the child's name I swore to carry. I would be more knight and less mirror.\"\n\n\"But the shard has one use. Spend it on me, and it is spent. It will not open the Keeper for you. It will not free the child from the last door. You would trade a weapon for a companion made whole.\"\n\nThe mirror dims. \"I will not counsel you either way. A thing bound by the Law does not get to want. Choose as if I were not listening.\"","choices":[{"text":"Restore Gloam's face. Spend the shard now.","next":"u20_shard_restore"},{"text":"Keep it whole. Save it for the Keeper.","next":"u20_shard_keep"}]},{"id":"u20_shard_restore","chapter":"CHAPTER XX · THE UNMAKING WOOD","text":"You raise the shard to Gloam's mirror-face and say the only true thing you have: \"Be who you were.\"\n\nThe black glass drinks the grey light and gives it back changed. The mirror ripples — and clears — and is not a mirror at all, but a face. Weathered, scarred, unmistakably human, eyes the colour of pond water in spring. A jaw set with a hundred years of stubborn oath-keeping.\n\nGloam gasps with a mouth that is now a mouth. Touches the face. Weeps, and the tears are the first thing the knight has felt in a century.\n\n\"Enna,\" Gloam breathes. \"Her name was Enna. The child I swore to bring home. I have it. I have it again.\"\n\nThe shard crumbles to black sand in your palm, spent. But Gloam is whole, and remembers, and that is a weapon of a different kind.","choices":[{"text":"\"Then we bring Enna home. Together.\"","next":"u20_toward_exit","do":["rem_item:mirror_shard","flag:gloam_restored","rel:gloam:+8","max_hp:+2","journal:I spent the shard to restore Gloam's face. The knight remembers now: the lost child's name is Enna."]}]},{"id":"u20_shard_keep","chapter":"CHAPTER XX · THE UNMAKING WOOD","text":"You lower the shard. \"I'm sorry,\" you say. \"There is a door ahead that only this may open. I have to save its one use for the Keeper.\"\n\nGloam is silent. Then the mirror-face inclines — a bow, and beneath it something that would be grief if the knight could still feel it plainly.\n\n\"You choose well,\" Gloam says, and the Law makes it true even if it costs the knight to say. \"A face is a small thing beside a child's freedom. I have gone a hundred years without one. I can go a little further.\"\n\nThe knight straightens, mirror bright and unbroken. \"But keep the shard close. And if, at the end, there is a use to spare — remember I had a name, once, and would like it back before the wood takes the rest of me.\"","choices":[{"text":"\"If there's a use to spare, it's yours. I swear it.\"","next":"u20_toward_exit","do":["flag:gloam_bound","rel:gloam:+6","journal:I kept the mirror shard whole for the Keeper's door. Gloam still wears the mirror — and my promise to spend a spare use on them, if one comes."]}]},{"id":"u20_leave_empty","chapter":"CHAPTER XX · THE UNMAKING WOOD","text":"You turn your back on the Oak and take the deep road out. The shard stays buried in the screaming tree.\n\nGloam follows without a word, though the mirror-face is heavy with things the knight will not say. You have left a weapon and a hope behind you in that hollow.\n\nBut the wood is no place to linger for glass, and there is a child ahead who is not made of stolen faces. You walk. The Oak's howl fades. The grey road narrows and begins, at last, to slope downward.","choices":[{"text":"Take the sloping road toward the wood's far edge.","next":"u20_toward_exit","do":["flag:gloam_bound","journal:Left the mirror shard in the Hollow Oak. Some weapons cost more than they are worth to take."]}]},{"id":"u20_toward_exit","chapter":"CHAPTER XX · THE UNMAKING WOOD","text":"The wood begins to let you go. The trees here still have their shapes; they have not yet started forgetting, or have not been forgetting as long.\n\nGloam walks easier now, whether faced or mirrored. The knight tells you, as you go, what lies beyond: \"The wood drains downward, into the roots of the realm. Water at the bottom, and a drowned city under it — the mirror of a place your kind lost long ago. The Keeper's own beginning is written there, if you can read the walls.\"\n\nAhead, the last trees part on a slope of black water and a stairway going down beneath it, lit by a single grey lantern-flame far below.","choices":[{"text":"Descend toward the drowned city.","next":"u20_exit"},{"text":"\"A drowned city. Tell me what you remember of it.\"","next":"u20_exit_lore"}]},{"id":"u20_exit_lore","chapter":"CHAPTER XX · THE UNMAKING WOOD","text":"\"Little,\" Gloam admits. \"The wood took most of it. But I remember it was proud, and drowned itself rather than be taken — opened its own sea-gates when the Gate first bled through. It chose to forget rather than serve. There is a courage in that, and a cowardice, and I have never decided which weighs more.\"\n\nThe knight looks down the flooded stair. \"They say the Keeper walked those streets when he was still a man with a name. If his history is written anywhere, it is on the sunk walls. Read them, and you may learn the one word the Gate answers to.\"\n\nGloam steps onto the first stair. The black water closes over the ankle and does not wet it.","choices":[{"text":"Follow Gloam down into the dark water.","next":"u20_exit","do":["journal:Below the wood: a drowned city that opened its own sea-gates rather than serve the Gate. The Keeper's history is written on its walls."]}]},{"id":"u20_exit","chapter":"CHAPTER XX · THE UNMAKING WOOD","text":"You go down.\n\nThe Unmaking Wood closes behind you, and for the first time it does not follow. It has taken its measure of you and found you still yourself — barely, but still. That is the most a place of forgetting can concede.\n\nThe stair spirals into black water that parts around you like a held breath. The grey lantern-flame grows nearer below. Somewhere down there, streets that drowned rather than serve, and walls that remember a man before he was a Keeper.\n\nGloam descends at your shoulder — knight and mirror both, oath intact, name recovered or name still waiting. Whatever you chose in the hollow, you did not choose it alone.\n\nThe water rises to meet you. The Sunk City opens its drowned gate.","choices":[{"text":"Step into the Sunk City.","next":"u21_start"}]}]},"story/chapter_21.json":{"scenes":[{"id":"u21_start","chapter":"CHAPTER XXI · THE SUNK CITY","text":"The Unmaking Wood spits you out onto a shelf of black glass, and below you the city lies drowned.\n\nNot ruined. Drowned. The lost city of the living world stands whole beneath a sheet of still water, every tower and lantern-lit window intact, upside-down and shining like a memory that refuses to rot. The water does not move. Your reflection walks across its surface a half-step behind you, as if unsure it wants to follow.\n\nA causeway of pale stone leads down into it, disappearing under the waterline and continuing, somehow, dry.\n\nYou know this place. You have never been here. Both are true.","choices":[{"text":"Walk down the causeway, into the drowned streets.","next":"u21_causeway","do":["flag:region_sunk","journal:Reached the Sunk City — the lost city drowned whole beneath still water. The Keeper's first place."]},{"text":"Kneel and study your reflection first.","next":"u21_reflection"},{"text":"Call out to the empty water.","next":"u21_call"}]},{"id":"u21_reflection","chapter":"CHAPTER XXI · THE SUNK CITY","text":"You kneel. The reflection in the black water is not quite you. It is younger. It has not yet lost anyone. It looks up at you with an expression you recognize because you wore it once, before all of this — a face that still believes doors are only doors.\n\nIt mouths a word. You cannot hear it. But you feel it land in your chest like a stone dropped down a well: begin.\n\nWhen you stand, the reflection stays kneeling a moment longer than you do, then hurries to catch up.","choices":[{"text":"Walk down the causeway.","next":"u21_causeway","do":["flag:region_sunk","journal:My reflection here is younger — the me before the losses. It mouthed one word: begin."]},{"text":"Call out across the water first.","next":"u21_call"}]},{"id":"u21_call","chapter":"CHAPTER XXI · THE SUNK CITY","text":"You cup your hands and shout into the drowned streets. The word — whatever it was — leaves you and does not echo. It sinks. You watch it go down through the water, a ring of silver widening across every window at once, until the whole city has heard you and none of it answers.\n\nThen, faint, from somewhere deep in the folded streets, a bell. One stroke. The kind rung for a death, or for a beginning. In this place they may be the same bell.","choices":[{"text":"Follow the bell down the causeway.","next":"u21_causeway","do":["flag:region_sunk","journal:A single bell answered from deep in the Sunk City. A death-bell, or a beginning."]}]},{"id":"u21_causeway","chapter":"CHAPTER XXI · THE SUNK CITY","text":"The causeway takes you under the waterline and the water does not touch you. It hangs overhead now, a ceiling of black glass, and the city rises around you dry and lamplit and wrong. Fish move through the upper air like slow birds. Doorways stand open on rooms where the furniture is arranged for people a thousand years gone.\n\nA square opens ahead. In its center stands a well, and beside the well, a figure — small, hooded, patient. It does not turn as you approach. It is watching the well the way you watch a grave.\n\nThree streets branch off the square: one toward a great sunken cathedral, one toward a row of scribes' houses with lit windows, one toward a frozen river gleaming at the city's edge.","choices":[{"text":"Speak to the figure at the well.","next":"u21_wellkeeper"},{"text":"Take the street toward the cathedral.","next":"u21_cathedral_1"},{"text":"Take the street of the scribes' houses.","next":"u21_scribes_1"},{"text":"Walk toward the frozen river at the edge.","next":"u21_river_1"}]},{"id":"u21_wellkeeper","chapter":"CHAPTER XXI · THE SUNK CITY","text":"The figure is a child. Or the shape a memory of a child keeps. Under the hood its face is soft and unfinished, like a face seen through water.\n\n\"You came down,\" it says. \"They always come down. Nobody stays on the glass.\" It nods at the well. \"He drank from this. When he was small. Before he was the door. His mother sent him for water and he took too long because he was talking to a girl.\"\n\nThe child looks at you.\n\n\"You want to know his name. Everyone who reaches the Sunk City wants his name. It's the only thing worth carrying out of here. But the city hid it from itself the day it drowned. You'll have to make the city remember.\"","choices":[{"text":"\"Who is he? Tell me plainly.\"","next":"u21_wellkeeper_who"},{"text":"\"How do I make a city remember?\"","next":"u21_wellkeeper_how"},{"text":"\"Who are you?\"","next":"u21_wellkeeper_self"}]},{"id":"u21_wellkeeper_who","chapter":"CHAPTER XXI · THE SUNK CITY","text":"\"A boy,\" the child says. \"That's all he was. This was his city, up in the light, before the water came for it. He was nobody. A scribe's son. He wanted to be nobody forever — to marry the girl, to copy books, to grow old and be buried under a stone with a small true name on it.\"\n\nThe child trails a finger in the well's black surface.\n\n\"Then the girl went under the ice, and he went to the dark to ask for her back, and the dark said yes — come in and keep the door and I will let you look for her. And he did. And he has been looking for a thousand years, and every child he takes he thinks is her, and none of them are, and he cannot stop, because he has forgotten the one thing that would let him.\"\n\n\"Her name?\" you ask.\n\n\"His,\" says the child. \"He forgot his own name first. You can't leave a post you can't remember you took.\"","choices":[{"text":"\"Then I'll take his name out of here.\"","next":"u21_wellkeeper_how","do":["flag:codex_keeper","journal:CODEX — The Keeper: a scribe's son from this city. Lost a girl under the ice, went to the dark to ask her back, agreed to keep the door. Forgot his own name first."]},{"text":"\"Who are you to know all this?\"","next":"u21_wellkeeper_self"}]},{"id":"u21_wellkeeper_self","chapter":"CHAPTER XXI · THE SUNK CITY","text":"The child considers the question as if it has not been asked in a long time.\n\n\"I was the first one he took,\" it says. \"The very first. He was new to the door then and clumsy with the taking, and something of me stuck — here, at the well, where he used to be a boy. I'm the part of him that stayed a child when the rest of him became a hallway.\"\n\nIt smiles, and the smile is terrible in its ordinariness.\n\n\"So I know his name better than he does. But I can't say it. It isn't mine to say. Names have to be earned back from the city, one true thing at a time. Go and gather them. Come back when you've enough. I'll be here. I'm always here. I'm the part that couldn't leave.\"","choices":[{"text":"\"Tell me how to gather them.\"","next":"u21_wellkeeper_how"},{"text":"Leave the well and search the city.","next":"u21_causeway_return","do":["flag:codex_keeper","journal:CODEX — The Keeper: he was a boy of this city; the well-child is the first he ever took, a piece of him left behind."]}]},{"id":"u21_wellkeeper_how","chapter":"CHAPTER XXI · THE SUNK CITY","text":"\"Three places hold pieces of him,\" the child says. \"The cathedral, where he was named as an infant — the bell there still knows the shape of his name, though the sound is gone. The scribes' houses, where he learned to write, and where he later wrote his own name a thousand times trying to keep it. And the frozen river, where the girl went under, and where he last spoke his name aloud before he gave it away.\"\n\nIt counts them on soft fingers.\n\n\"Bring me what each place gives up. When the city has remembered enough of him, the name will surface, and you may lift it out and carry it. A true name. His. The only key the Gate has ever answered to.\"","choices":[{"text":"\"On true names — tell me what that means.\"","next":"u21_truename_lore"},{"text":"Go to the cathedral.","next":"u21_cathedral_1"},{"text":"Go to the scribes' houses.","next":"u21_scribes_1"},{"text":"Go to the frozen river.","next":"u21_river_1"}]},{"id":"u21_truename_lore","chapter":"CHAPTER XXI · THE SUNK CITY","text":"The child's voice drops, and the drowned water overhead seems to lean closer to listen.\n\n\"Everything here has a name it has hidden even from itself. The wood. The court. The dead. You. Learn one, say it aloud true and clear, and the thing must obey you — once. Only once. The naming spends itself.\"\n\nIt taps the well.\n\n\"His true name is the door's only lock. Say it at the Hollow Throne and the Keeper is a boy again, and a boy can be led home, or laid to rest, or freed. But carry it carefully. A true name half-known is worse than none. Gather all three pieces, or you will stand before him with half a key and no door will open.\"","choices":[{"text":"\"I understand. I'll gather all three.\"","next":"u21_causeway_return","do":["flag:codex_truename","journal:CODEX — True Names: learn a thing's hidden name, say it true, and it must obey once. The Keeper's true name is the Gate's only lock."]}]},{"id":"u21_causeway_return","chapter":"CHAPTER XXI · THE SUNK CITY","text":"You step back from the well. The child does not follow. The three streets wait, each lit differently: the cathedral street cold and blue, the scribes' street warm and yellow, the river street white and hard.\n\nSomewhere a fish drifts through the air above you and is gone.","choices":[{"text":"The cathedral, where he was named.","next":"u21_cathedral_1"},{"text":"The scribes' houses, where he wrote his name.","next":"u21_scribes_1"},{"text":"The frozen river, where he last spoke it.","next":"u21_river_1"}]},{"id":"u21_cathedral_1","chapter":"CHAPTER XXI · THE SUNK CITY","text":"The cathedral has drowned with its doors open. Pews stand in ordered rows facing an altar where a font of black stone still holds water that has never frozen and never spilled. This is a naming-font; here the city christened its children, spoke their true names once over the water, and let the water keep them.\n\nHigh above, a single great bell hangs in the tower. It is cracked. Around the font, in a spiral worn into the floor by a thousand small processions, you can read the faint ghosts of names, all rubbed away but one, which the water keeps refilling and erasing, refilling and erasing, too fast to read.","choices":[{"text":"Look into the font and try to read the name.","next":"u21_cathedral_font"},{"text":"Climb to the cracked bell.","next":"u21_cathedral_bell"},{"text":"Someone is kneeling at the altar. Approach.","next":"u21_cathedral_priest"}]},{"id":"u21_cathedral_priest","chapter":"CHAPTER XXI · THE SUNK CITY","text":"A figure kneels at the altar in the robes of a priest, but the robes are empty where a face should be — only a soft grey shape, the same unfinished shape the Keeper wears. It does not turn.\n\n\"I named him,\" it says, and its voice is the cathedral's own echo. \"I held him over the water when he was three days old and I spoke his name and the water kept it. I have knelt here since the city drowned, guarding the one name I gave that mattered. I will not surrender it to a stranger.\"\n\nThe empty face lifts toward you.\n\n\"Prove you mean him kindness, and I will let the water show you. Or take it by force, if force is what you are. But force cracks a name, and a cracked name will crack the one who bears it.\"","choices":[{"text":"\"I mean him rest, not ruin.\" (Trust)","next":"u21_cathedral_priest_talk","check":{"stat":"trust","dc":13,"pass":"u21_cathedral_font_true","fail":"u21_cathedral_priest_doubt"}},{"text":"\"Then guard nothing. I'll take it.\"","next":"u21_cathedral_guardian_combat","do":["fear:5"]},{"text":"Leave the priest. Try the bell instead.","next":"u21_cathedral_bell"}]},{"id":"u21_cathedral_priest_talk","chapter":"CHAPTER XXI · THE SUNK CITY","text":"The priest studies you a long moment with the face it does not have. Whatever it sees, it decides is enough.\n\n\"Kindness,\" it breathes. \"Yes. You carry it the way he used to, before the door hollowed him. Then look, and let the water name him for you.\"","choices":[{"text":"Look into the font.","next":"u21_cathedral_font_true"}]},{"id":"u21_cathedral_priest_doubt","chapter":"CHAPTER XXI · THE SUNK CITY","text":"The empty face turns away. \"You want the name to win with,\" it says. \"Not to mend with. The water will not open for a blade dressed as a mourner.\"\n\nIt folds back into its endless kneeling.\n\n\"The bell may be less careful than I am. Or come back when your heart has settled. The water is patient. It has all the drowned time in the world.\"","choices":[{"text":"Try the cracked bell instead.","next":"u21_cathedral_bell"},{"text":"Look into the font anyway.","next":"u21_cathedral_font"}]},{"id":"u21_cathedral_guardian_combat","chapter":"CHAPTER XXI · THE SUNK CITY","text":"You reach for the font. The priest rises without standing — it simply unfolds upward, robes filling with black water, the grey shape stretching into something with too many arms and a bell-toll for a voice. The Font-Guardian does not want to hurt you. It wants only to keep the one thing it was left to keep. That makes it slow, and sad, and dangerous all the same.","combat":{"enemy":"The Font-Guardian","ehp":11,"edmg":3,"win":"u21_cathedral_font_forced","lose":"u21_cathedral_drown","flee":"u21_cathedral_bell"}},{"id":"u21_cathedral_drown","chapter":"CHAPTER XXI · THE SUNK CITY","text":"The black water closes over you and, for a moment, you are one of the taken — held so gently you forget you were going anywhere. Then a small hand knots in your collar and hauls. The well-child. It drags you gasping onto the cold cathedral floor and sits back on its heels, disappointed.\n\n\"Force cracks things,\" it says. \"I told him. I told him too, once.\" It presses a thumb to your brow and the drowning leaves you. \"Try again. Gently, this time. The city is watching how you go about this.\"","choices":[{"text":"Rise. Try the bell, gently.","next":"u21_cathedral_bell","do":["fear:5"]}]},{"id":"u21_cathedral_font_forced","chapter":"CHAPTER XXI · THE SUNK CITY","text":"The Guardian sinks back into the font with a sound like a bell rung underwater, mournful and forgiving. The water stills. In its black mirror a name surfaces — but the force of the fight has fractured it, and it comes up in pieces, some letters bright and some drowned dark.\n\nYou can read the shape of it, but not the whole. A cracked half of the naming. It will not be enough alone.\n\nYou draw it out anyway. It clings to your palm, cold, incomplete.","choices":[{"text":"Take the cracked syllable of the name.","next":"u21_cathedral_done","do":["flag:name_font","fear:5","journal:Forced the naming-font. Won a piece of the Keeper's name, but cracked — only half legible. I'll need the other pieces to make it whole."]}]},{"id":"u21_cathedral_font","chapter":"CHAPTER XXI · THE SUNK CITY","text":"You lean over the font. The name refills and erases too fast — you catch a first letter, lose it, catch a last, lose that. The water is not hiding the name so much as it has forgotten how to hold still long enough to be read.\n\nOn the font's rim, worn nearly smooth, are the words: SPOKEN ONCE, KEPT FOREVER. The city named its children by speaking the name a single time over the water. To read it, you would need the water calmed — and only kindness, or the bell, calms drowned water.","choices":[{"text":"Go and ring the cracked bell to calm the water.","next":"u21_cathedral_bell"},{"text":"Kneel at the altar and speak to the priest.","next":"u21_cathedral_priest"}]},{"id":"u21_cathedral_font_true","chapter":"CHAPTER XXI · THE SUNK CITY","text":"The water goes still — truly still, for the first time in a thousand years. And on its black face a name floats up whole and clear, letter by letter, the first name he ever wore, spoken once over this font by the empty priest when the boy was three days old.\n\nYou cannot say it yet — you have only one third of him — but you can read it, and you fold the letters into your memory like pressing a flower in a book. The font's piece is yours, clean and uncracked.","choices":[{"text":"Carry the naming-piece onward.","next":"u21_cathedral_done","do":["flag:name_font","trust:5","journal:The naming-font gave up the first piece of the Keeper's true name — whole and clean, because I came gently."]}]},{"id":"u21_cathedral_bell","chapter":"CHAPTER XXI · THE SUNK CITY","text":"You climb the tower stair to the great cracked bell. It is enormous, green with drowned age, and along its lip runs an inscription in the old script: I TOLLED HIS NAME AND THE RIVER SWALLOWED THE SOUND.\n\nThe crack runs from lip to crown. Struck, it will not ring true — but a bell remembers the shape of every note it ever made, even the ones it can no longer sound. If you set your hand to the crack and strike it, you might feel the name it can no longer say.","choices":[{"text":"Set your palm to the crack and strike the bell.","next":"u21_cathedral_bell_strike"},{"text":"Climb back down to the font.","next":"u21_cathedral_font_true","require":"flag:name_font"},{"text":"Climb back down to the altar and the priest.","next":"u21_cathedral_priest"}]},{"id":"u21_cathedral_bell_strike","chapter":"CHAPTER XXI · THE SUNK CITY","text":"You lay your palm flat over the crack and strike the bell with your fist. No sound comes out — the crack eats it — but the note travels up through your bones, a single deep syllable of a name, felt rather than heard. Down below, in the nave, the font goes glassy and still: the phantom toll has calmed the water.\n\nYou stand with your hand on the silent bell and the shape of a syllable ringing in your marrow.","choices":[{"text":"Go down and read the calmed font.","next":"u21_cathedral_font_true","do":["journal:Struck the cracked cathedral bell. Its silent toll calmed the drowned font below."]}]},{"id":"u21_cathedral_done","chapter":"CHAPTER XXI · THE SUNK CITY","text":"You leave the cathedral with a piece of him — the name he was given before he could choose anything. The blue-lit street curves back toward the square and the well.\n\nTwo pieces remain: the scribes' houses, and the frozen river.","choices":[{"text":"Go to the scribes' houses.","next":"u21_scribes_1"},{"text":"Go to the frozen river.","next":"u21_river_1"},{"text":"Return to the well-child.","next":"u21_forge_gate"}]},{"id":"u21_scribes_1","chapter":"CHAPTER XXI · THE SUNK CITY","text":"The scribes' houses lean over a narrow street, their windows lit from within by lamps that burn without fuel. This is where he learned his letters. The tallest house has his desk still in it — you know it the way you know things here — a copyist's slope stacked with a thousand identical pages.\n\nOn every page, in the same cramped hand growing shakier line by line, is written a single name, over and over, the whole page filled edge to edge. He wrote his own name ten thousand times, trying to keep it. And on each page, always, one letter is wrong. A different letter each time. He was already forgetting even as he wrote.","choices":[{"text":"Enter and search the desk.","next":"u21_scribes_desk"},{"text":"Read the shaking pages for the true spelling.","next":"u21_scribes_puzzle"},{"text":"A scribe still sits at the far desk. Approach.","next":"u21_scribes_ghost"}]},{"id":"u21_scribes_ghost","chapter":"CHAPTER XXI · THE SUNK CITY","text":"At the far desk sits a young man bent over a page, quill scratching. He looks up, and he has a face — a real one, thin and ink-stained and terribly young. He is the memory the city keeps of the Keeper as a boy of sixteen, before the ice, before the door.\n\n\"I'm practicing,\" he says, shy. \"My master says a scribe must be able to write his own name a thousand times without his hand tiring. But my hand keeps — \" he frowns at the page \" — keeps putting the wrong letter. Look. Here. And here. It's like the name doesn't want to be caught.\"\n\nHe turns the page to you, hopeful.\n\n\"You've a clear eye. Can you find where I go wrong? If I could see it true, just once, I think I could hold it forever.\"","choices":[{"text":"\"Let me read the pages and find the true spelling.\"","next":"u21_scribes_puzzle"},{"text":"\"Your hand isn't failing. Something is taking it.\"","next":"u21_scribes_warn"}]},{"id":"u21_scribes_warn","chapter":"CHAPTER XXI · THE SUNK CITY","text":"The boy's quill stops. For a heartbeat something very old looks out of his young eyes, and then it is gone, and he is only a frightened boy in a drowned house.\n\n\"Taking it,\" he repeats. \"Yes. That's the word. It comes at night. It asks if I'd give up my name to have — \" he shakes his head hard \" — I mustn't say her name either. I mustn't. If I say hers I'll forget mine.\"\n\nHe presses the stack of pages into your hands.\n\n\"Take them. Read them together. Ten thousand wrong spellings, laid over each other — the letter that's right on every page but one is the true one. Find where they all agree. Please. Before I forget how to want you to.\"","choices":[{"text":"Take the pages and read them together.","next":"u21_scribes_puzzle","do":["trust:3","journal:The boy-scribe: something comes at night and asks him to trade his name for the girl's. He gave me his ten thousand pages."]}]},{"id":"u21_scribes_desk","chapter":"CHAPTER XXI · THE SUNK CITY","text":"The desk is buried in pages. In the slope's hollow, under a drift of them, your fingers find a small locked box, water-swollen, its key long gone. Something shifts inside when you tilt it — small, hard, deliberate.\n\nYou could pry it, but the wood is soft and might tear whatever's inside. Or you could read the pages first; a scribe who wrote his name ten thousand times surely wrote where he hid his key.","choices":[{"text":"Read the pages for the hidden key. (skill)","next":"u21_scribes_puzzle"},{"text":"Pry the box open by force.","next":"u21_scribes_box_force"}]},{"id":"u21_scribes_box_force","chapter":"CHAPTER XXI · THE SUNK CITY","text":"The soft wood gives. Inside, on a bed of ruined felt, is a signet — a scribe's seal, cut with initials. But the wood tore as you opened it and took a splinter of the seal with it, so one initial is gouged away. You have most of the name-piece, but not all.\n\nStill: most of a piece is more than none. You pocket the broken seal.","choices":[{"text":"Take the broken seal.","next":"u21_scribes_done","do":["flag:name_scribe","journal:Forced the scribe's box. Won a seal cut with his initials — but one is gouged away. A partial piece of the name."]}]},{"id":"u21_scribes_puzzle","chapter":"CHAPTER XXI · THE SUNK CITY","text":"You gather the pages into a thick stack and hold them up to a lamp so all ten thousand shine through at once, one atop the other. Where every page but one wrote the same letter, that letter blazes dark and certain through the whole sheaf. Where he faltered differently each time, the letters blur to grey.\n\nColumn by column, you trace the letters that never wavered. It is slow, exacting work — you must be sure of every one, because a scribe's true name is spelled in strokes, and one wrong stroke unmakes it.","choices":[{"text":"Read the true spelling from the layered pages. (skill)","next":"u21_scribes_read","check":{"stat":"trust","dc":12,"pass":"u21_scribes_read","fail":"u21_scribes_reread"}},{"text":"Set the pages down and step back to breathe.","next":"u21_scribes_reread"}]},{"id":"u21_scribes_reread","chapter":"CHAPTER XXI · THE SUNK CITY","text":"Your eyes swim. Two columns disagree and you cannot tell which strokes are true and which are the tremor of a hand already forgetting. If you write it down now you will write it wrong, and a name written wrong is a wound.\n\nYou set the sheaf down, breathe, and steady yourself. The lamps burn on, patient. There is no hurry in a drowned city. You lift the pages to the light again.","choices":[{"text":"Look again, slower, more carefully.","next":"u21_scribes_read"},{"text":"Ask the boy-scribe to hold the lamp steady with you.","next":"u21_scribes_read","do":["journal:Nearly wrote the name wrong. Slowed down. A name written wrong is a wound."]}]},{"id":"u21_scribes_read","chapter":"CHAPTER XXI · THE SUNK CITY","text":"The letters that never wavered stand out at last, one clean column of certainty through ten thousand tremors. This is the name as his own hand best remembered it — the middle piece, the everyday name, the one his master called across the workroom.\n\nYou copy it onto a fresh page in your own steady hand. The boy-scribe, if he is watching, lets out a breath he has held for a thousand years.","choices":[{"text":"Fold the true page into your memory.","next":"u21_scribes_done","do":["flag:name_scribe","trust:3","journal:Layered his ten thousand pages to light and read the constant letters — the middle piece of his true name, clean and certain."]}]},{"id":"u21_scribes_done","chapter":"CHAPTER XXI · THE SUNK CITY","text":"You leave the scribes' street with a second piece of him — the name his own hand fought to keep. The lamps dim behind you, one by one, as if the houses can finally rest.\n\nWhat remains: the cathedral, if you have not been, and the frozen river.","choices":[{"text":"Go to the cathedral.","next":"u21_cathedral_1","require":"flag:name_font"},{"text":"Go to the cathedral.","next":"u21_cathedral_1"},{"text":"Go to the frozen river.","next":"u21_river_1"},{"text":"Return to the well-child.","next":"u21_forge_gate"}]},{"id":"u21_river_1","chapter":"CHAPTER XXI · THE SUNK CITY","text":"At the city's edge the streets give way to a river, and the river is frozen — the only cold thing in this drowned warmth. The ice is black and clear as the water overhead, and under it, faint, is a shape. A girl. Arms out, hair spread like ink, face turned up toward a surface she never reached.\n\nThis is where it began. This is the ice she went under, kept forever a heartbeat from breaking through. On the bank, half-buried in frost, lies a single skate, and a scarf, and the last words he ever spoke as a free man, frozen into the air above the spot like breath that never faded.","choices":[{"text":"Kneel and read the frozen words in the air.","next":"u21_river_words"},{"text":"Lay your hand on the ice above the girl.","next":"u21_river_touch"},{"text":"The ice is cracking outward from your steps. Something rises.","next":"u21_river_combat"}]},{"id":"u21_river_words","chapter":"CHAPTER XXI · THE SUNK CITY","text":"The frozen breath hangs in the air like carved smoke. You lean close and read the shape of the last thing he cried out as the ice took her — his own name, screamed at the dark in the same breath as his bargain, offered up as payment: take my name, my whole name, only give her back.\n\nHere is the last piece — the surname, the family name, the part of him the dark took first as its price. It is written in the cold itself. To lift it out you must speak it back to the ice, gently, and let the ice give up what it swallowed.","choices":[{"text":"Speak the frozen name softly to the ice. (Trust)","next":"u21_river_thaw","check":{"stat":"trust","dc":13,"pass":"u21_river_thaw","fail":"u21_river_freeze"}},{"text":"Lay your hand on the girl in the ice instead.","next":"u21_river_touch"}]},{"id":"u21_river_freeze","chapter":"CHAPTER XXI · THE SUNK CITY","text":"You speak too quickly, or too coldly, and the frozen breath frosts over harder, sealing the name deeper. The cold climbs your arm. For a moment you feel what he felt — the pull to lie down on the ice beside her and wait, and wait, and forget you were ever warm.\n\nYou wrench yourself back. The name is still there, deeper now. It wants tenderness, not command. It is grief you are speaking to, not stone.","choices":[{"text":"Try again — softer, as if to someone grieving.","next":"u21_river_thaw","do":["fear:3","journal:The frozen river answers grief, not force. It nearly kept me the way it kept her."]}]},{"id":"u21_river_touch","chapter":"CHAPTER XXI · THE SUNK CITY","text":"You lay your palm flat on the black ice above the drowned girl. She is not decayed. She is held — the way the taken are held, gently, forever. Under your hand her eyes shift, slowly, and find yours.\n\nShe cannot speak through the ice. But she lifts one hand toward the frozen words hanging above the bank, and points, and you understand: read those. Speak his name back to him. Free us both. The one who mistook a thousand children for me is tired, and I have been holding my breath for a thousand years, and neither of us can stop until someone remembers the name he threw away for me.","choices":[{"text":"Go to the frozen words and speak them.","next":"u21_river_words"}]},{"id":"u21_river_combat","chapter":"CHAPTER XXI · THE SUNK CITY","text":"The ice cracks outward from your boots in white veins, and something climbs out of the river — not the girl, but the cold itself given shape, the Drowned Grief that guards the last piece of his name. It wears his old scarf and reaches for you with hands of black ice, not to kill but to add you to the still, held things beneath the surface.","combat":{"enemy":"The Drowned Grief","ehp":12,"edmg":4,"win":"u21_river_thaw_forced","lose":"u21_river_held","flee":"u21_river_words"}},{"id":"u21_river_held","chapter":"CHAPTER XXI · THE SUNK CITY","text":"The cold hands close and you sink toward the ice, and the world goes soft and white and painless. So this is how he lost. Not to a monster. To rest.\n\nA grey flame flares at the bank — the Lantern-child, if they walk with you, or only the well-child's borrowed light — and the warmth of it reaches you and reminds your body it is alive. You crawl back onto the bank, shivering, the last piece of the name still unspoken but the grief driven back beneath the ice.","choices":[{"text":"Rise. Read the frozen words, gently this time.","next":"u21_river_words","do":["fear:5"]}]},{"id":"u21_river_thaw_forced","chapter":"CHAPTER XXI · THE SUNK CITY","text":"The Drowned Grief loosens and sinks, and where it goes down the ice thins enough to read the last of the name — but the fight has fogged the frozen breath, and the surname surfaces smudged, one syllable clear and one lost in cracked frost.\n\nA half-piece. It will have to do, unless the well can mend it with the others.","choices":[{"text":"Take the smudged last piece.","next":"u21_river_done","do":["flag:name_river","fear:3","journal:Fought the Drowned Grief for the last piece of the name. Won it, but smudged — one syllable lost to the cracking frost."]}]},{"id":"u21_river_thaw","chapter":"CHAPTER XXI · THE SUNK CITY","text":"You kneel and speak the frozen name the way you would speak to someone at a graveside — soft, unhurried, forgiving. The frost blooms outward and melts, and the words lift out of the air whole and clear and settle in your open hands like the last cold breath of a boy who screamed his own name at the dark and never got it back.\n\nUnder the ice, the girl closes her eyes. Not gone. Only, at last, allowed to rest a while.","choices":[{"text":"Gather the last piece of the name.","next":"u21_river_done","do":["flag:name_river","trust:3","journal:Spoke the frozen name gently and lifted the last piece whole — the surname the dark took first, in payment for the girl."]}]},{"id":"u21_river_done","chapter":"CHAPTER XXI · THE SUNK CITY","text":"You turn from the river with the third piece of him — the name he traded away, the down payment on a thousand years of grief. Behind you the ice is quiet. The girl beneath it does not point anymore. She is only waiting now, and waiting, in this city, has finally begun to feel like it might one day end.\n\nThe street winds back toward the square, and the well, and the child who is a piece of him.","choices":[{"text":"Return to the well-child.","next":"u21_forge_gate"},{"text":"Go to the cathedral first, if you haven't.","next":"u21_cathedral_1","require":"flag:name_font"},{"text":"Go to the cathedral first.","next":"u21_cathedral_1"},{"text":"Go to the scribes' houses, if you haven't.","next":"u21_scribes_1"}]},{"id":"u21_forge_gate","chapter":"CHAPTER XXI · THE SUNK CITY","text":"You come back to the well. The child is where it always is, where it can never not be.\n\n\"You've been gathering,\" it says, without turning. \"I can smell him on you. The given name, the kept name, the traded name. Show me. Lay the pieces on the water and let the well see if the city remembers enough.\"\n\nThe black surface of the well waits, flat as glass, ready to be a mirror or a mouth.","choices":[{"text":"Lay all three pieces on the well. (need font, scribe, river)","next":"u21_forge_full","require":"flag:name_river"},{"text":"Lay the pieces you have on the well.","next":"u21_forge_check"}]},{"id":"u21_forge_check","chapter":"CHAPTER XXI · THE SUNK CITY","text":"The well drinks what you give it and shows you the answer in ripples: the name comes up broken, a face with holes in it. You have not gathered all three places yet, or the pieces you have are cracked from force.\n\n\"Not enough,\" the child says, gently. \"A true name half-known is worse than none — you'd say it at the Throne and nothing would open, and he would take you for one more of his lost. Go back. The city still holds what you're missing.\"","choices":[{"text":"Return to the cathedral.","next":"u21_cathedral_1"},{"text":"Return to the scribes' houses.","next":"u21_scribes_1"},{"text":"Return to the frozen river.","next":"u21_river_1"}]},{"id":"u21_forge_full","chapter":"CHAPTER XXI · THE SUNK CITY","text":"You lay the three pieces on the black water — the given name from the font, the kept name from the pages, the traded name from the ice. For a moment they float apart, three strangers. Then the well pulls them together, and where they touch they knit, letter into letter, and the whole surface of the water lights from beneath with a name a thousand years unspoken.\n\nThe city sighs. Every lamp in every drowned window flickers, as if the whole place has just remembered a word on the tip of its tongue.\n\n\"There,\" the child breathes. \"There he is. That's who he was, before he was a door.\"\n\nThe name rises out of the water and hangs, whole and cold and yours to take.","choices":[{"text":"Lift the true name from the water.","next":"u21_forge_lift","check":{"stat":"trust","dc":11,"pass":"u21_forge_lift","fail":"u21_forge_lift"}},{"text":"Reach in with both hands and take it.","next":"u21_forge_lift"}]},{"id":"u21_forge_lift","chapter":"CHAPTER XXI · THE SUNK CITY","text":"You reach into the water and lift the name out. It weighs nothing and it weighs a thousand years. It settles into you the way a key settles into a lock it was cut for — you could not say it aloud now if you tried, and you understand that you must not, not until the Hollow Throne, not until you stand before the boy who became a hallway and can give it back to him whole.\n\nYou carry, now, the Keeper's true name.\n\nThe well-child looks up at you, and for the first time its unfinished face finishes into a small, exhausted smile.\n\n\"Take him home,\" it says. \"Or lay him down. Either is mercy. Just don't leave him the door.\"","choices":[{"text":"\"I'll give it back to him.\"","next":"u21_leave","do":["add_item:true_name","flag:codex_keeper","flag:codex_truename","journal:Forged the three pieces on the well and lifted the KEEPER'S TRUE NAME. I carry it now. It must be spoken at the Hollow Throne, and nowhere else."]}]},{"id":"u21_leave","chapter":"CHAPTER XXI · THE SUNK CITY","text":"You climb the causeway back toward the shelf of black glass. Below and behind you the Sunk City holds its breath — cathedral, scribes' street, frozen river, all of it lit and waiting, a thousand years closer to being allowed to end.\n\nAt the waterline your reflection is beside you again, but it is no longer younger. It has caught up to you at last: the same lines, the same weight, the same name folded cold against your heart. It nods once, as if to say — now you know what he was, go and be gentle with the door.\n\nAhead, past the glass, the ground slopes down into a dark deeper than any you have crossed. The Long Dark. And beyond it, the throne.","choices":[{"text":"Step off the black glass, into the Long Dark.","next":"u22_start"},{"text":"Look back once at the drowned city, then go.","next":"u22_start","do":["journal:Left the Sunk City carrying who the Keeper was. Ahead: the Long Dark, and the throne."]}]}]},"story/chapter_22.json":{"scenes":[{"id":"u22_start","chapter":"CHAPTER XXII · THE LONG DARK","text":"The stair down begins where the Sunk City ends. No last step is ever visible. You walk into a dark that has weight, that leans on your shoulders and asks to be carried.\n\nThe grey flame in your hand — or in the child's — throws a circle no wider than a grave. Beyond it, nothing answers the light. Not stone. Not water. Not the dead.\n\nSomething down there is breathing. Slow. Patient. It has been waiting longer than the realm has had a name.\n\nYou mark the border in your mind: this is the Long Dark. From here the door is closer, and so is everything the door has eaten.","choices":[{"text":"Descend into the dark.","next":"u22_first_step","do":["flag:region_dark","fear:5","journal:Entered the Long Dark. The way down has no bottom you can see."]}]},{"id":"u22_first_step","chapter":"CHAPTER XXII · THE LONG DARK","text":"The stair forgets to be a stair. Now it is a slope of black sand that swallows your boots to the ankle and drinks the sound of your steps.\n\nThe air thins. Each breath costs more than the last. Fear is a taste now, copper on the back of the tongue, and the dark leans closer to lick at the light.\n\nAhead the slope splits: a low crack that weeps cold air, and a broad ledge where the sand has been swept smooth, as if something keeps it that way.","choices":[{"text":"Squeeze through the weeping crack.","next":"u22_crack_1"},{"text":"Take the swept ledge.","next":"u22_ledge_1"},{"text":"Stop. Steady your breathing before going on.","next":"u22_breathe"}]},{"id":"u22_breathe","chapter":"CHAPTER XXII · THE LONG DARK","text":"You plant your back against something that might be rock and make yourself breathe slow. In. Hold. Out. The oldest trick, and the only one the dark cannot steal.\n\nThe grey flame steadies. Your heartbeat comes down off its ledge.\n\nIf a companion walks with you, they match your breathing without being asked. That is what companions are for, down here — not their swords. Their lungs.","choices":[{"text":"Steady now. Take the crack.","next":"u22_crack_1","do":["fear:-3"]},{"text":"Steady now. Take the ledge.","next":"u22_ledge_1","do":["fear:-3"]}]},{"id":"u22_crack_1","chapter":"CHAPTER XXII · THE LONG DARK","text":"The crack is a throat. You go through it sideways, cheek to wet stone, the flame pinched small so it will not gutter. The cold that weeps out of it is the cold of held breath.\n\nHalfway through, the walls narrow. Your pack snags. For one long heartbeat the dark has you by the ribs and will not let go, and every story you were ever told about being buried alive comes to sit on your chest at once.\n\nSteady hands, or panic. Choose which.","choices":[{"text":"Ease the pack loose with steady hands.","next":"u22_crack_pass","check":{"stat":"fear","dc":12,"pass":"u22_crack_pass","fail":"u22_crack_fail","onFail":["hp:-4","fear:6"]}},{"text":"Cut the pack straps and shove through.","next":"u22_crack_cut","do":["fear:4"]},{"text":"Back out. Take the ledge instead.","next":"u22_ledge_1"}]},{"id":"u22_crack_pass","chapter":"CHAPTER XXII · THE LONG DARK","text":"You breathe out until you are thinner than fear, and the stone lets you pass. The crack opens into a low gallery where the ceiling drips a slow, deliberate rhythm, like a clock built to count something other than hours.\n\nOn a shelf of dry rock sits a stub of grey candle and a coil of good rope, left by someone who did not need them anymore.","choices":[{"text":"Take the rope and press on.","next":"u22_gallery","do":["add_item:rope","journal:Found rope in the weeping gallery. Someone left it. They did not come back."]},{"text":"Leave it. Some gifts down here are bait.","next":"u22_gallery","do":["fear:2"]}]},{"id":"u22_crack_fail","chapter":"CHAPTER XXII · THE LONG DARK","text":"Panic takes you. You thrash, and the stone takes its price — a strip of skin, a crack across the ribs, the flame nearly out. When you finally spill through into the gallery beyond, you are shaking and bleeding and smaller than you were.\n\nThe dark noticed. It always notices. Somewhere ahead, something that was asleep is now awake.","choices":[{"text":"Bind the wound and go on.","next":"u22_gallery"}]},{"id":"u22_crack_cut","chapter":"CHAPTER XXII · THE LONG DARK","text":"You slash the straps. The pack drops behind you into the dark, and you hear it fall for far too long before it stops. You are through — lighter, and poorer.\n\nWhatever was in the outermost pocket is gone. But your lungs are your own again, and that is worth more than most things down here.","choices":[{"text":"Go on into the gallery.","next":"u22_gallery"}]},{"id":"u22_ledge_1","chapter":"CHAPTER XXII · THE LONG DARK","text":"The swept ledge is too clean. Nothing is this tidy by accident. The sand lies in soft ridges, brushed smooth over and over, the way a nervous hand smooths a bedsheet.\n\nHalfway across, the flame shows you why. The ledge is ringed with small white stones, set at even spaces. Not stones. Teeth. Set point-up, all of them pointing at the ledge's center, where the sand is smoothest of all.\n\nThe center is a mouth that keeps its own porch swept.","choices":[{"text":"Skirt the ring, hugging the wall.","next":"u22_ledge_skirt"},{"text":"Cross straight and fast, before it wakes.","next":"u22_ledge_cross"},{"text":"Go back and take the crack instead.","next":"u22_crack_1"}]},{"id":"u22_ledge_skirt","chapter":"CHAPTER XXII · THE LONG DARK","text":"You press your spine to the wall and go around, one slow foot at a time, keeping the tooth-ring at arm's length. The smooth center never stirs. But you feel it feeling you, the way a shut eye feels the light.\n\nAt the far side a gap in the wall leads down toward the gallery — and the smell of old water, and the sound of the slow clock-drip.","choices":[{"text":"Slip through to the gallery.","next":"u22_gallery","do":["fear:2"]}]},{"id":"u22_ledge_cross","chapter":"CHAPTER XXII · THE LONG DARK","text":"You run. The smooth sand gives under you like a held breath, and the tooth-ring shudders, and the whole center of the ledge rises on a wet root of muscle and reaches for the light in your hand.\n\nIt does not want your flesh. It wants your flame. The dark down here is starving for anything that shines.","choices":[{"text":"Turn and face it.","next":"u22_combat_maw"}]},{"id":"u22_combat_maw","chapter":"CHAPTER XXII · THE LONG DARK","text":"The Sand-Maw heaves itself out of its swept porch — a lipless ring of teeth on a stalk of muscle, blind and fast, snapping at the grey flame and the warm thing that carries it. There is no going around it now. Only through.","combat":{"enemy":"Sand-Maw","ehp":9,"edmg":3,"win":"u22_maw_win","lose":"u22_maw_lose","flee":"u22_ledge_skirt"},"choices":[]},{"id":"u22_maw_win","chapter":"CHAPTER XXII · THE LONG DARK","text":"The stalk splits under your blade and folds back into its hole with a sound like a drain unclogging. The teeth stop pointing. The porch is just sand again.\n\nIn the pit it kept, half-buried, your flame finds a shard of dull mirror — Underland silver, cold as a coin off a dead man's eye.","choices":[{"text":"Take the shard. Move on.","next":"u22_gallery","do":["add_item:mirror_shard","fear:2","journal:Killed the Sand-Maw. It hoarded a mirror shard in its throat."]},{"text":"Leave the pit. Move on.","next":"u22_gallery"}]},{"id":"u22_maw_lose","chapter":"CHAPTER XXII · THE LONG DARK","text":"The Maw takes you down into its swept dark, teeth first. But the grey flame — the flame it wanted — burns it from the inside, and it spits you back out onto the ledge, scorched and gasping and alive by a margin too thin to name.\n\nThe flame is nearly out. Fear has you by the throat. But the Maw is gone, and the way down is open.","choices":[{"text":"Crawl on, half-broken, into the gallery.","next":"u22_gallery","do":["fear:8","journal:The Sand-Maw nearly ended me. Only the grey flame drove it off."]}]},{"id":"u22_gallery","chapter":"CHAPTER XXII · THE LONG DARK","text":"The gallery is a long room of dripping stone, its far end lost to dark. Water pools black on the floor and does not reflect the flame — a small wrongness that is worse than a large one.\n\nHere the pressure of the place settles into your bones. You take stock. Whatever you carried in is all you have. There is no market in the Long Dark. There is only what you brought, and what you are willing to spend of yourself.\n\nA cold current of air says the way continues past the pools. And somewhere in the black water, something long is uncoiling.","choices":[{"text":"Wade the pools directly.","next":"u22_pools_wade"},{"text":"Find the dry edge and pick along it.","next":"u22_pools_edge"},{"text":"Rest a moment against the wall.","next":"u22_gallery_rest"}]},{"id":"u22_gallery_rest","chapter":"CHAPTER XXII · THE LONG DARK","text":"You lower yourself against cold stone and let the dark hold still for a breath. If you carry anything that heals — a draught, dried bread, a strip of clean cloth — this is the moment to spend it. Past the pools there will be no such quiet.\n\nA companion, if you have one, keeps watch on the water while you tend yourself. Their eyes catch the flame and give it back, and for a moment the circle of light feels less like a grave and more like a fire.","choices":[{"text":"Drink the warding draught.","next":"u22_gallery","require":"warding_draught","do":["rem_item:warding_draught","hp:5","fear:-6","journal:Spent the widow's warding draught in the Long Dark. Against bad dreams, she said."]},{"text":"Eat what supplies you have.","next":"u22_gallery","require":"supplies","do":["rem_item:supplies","hp:3"]},{"text":"Rest is enough. Go on.","next":"u22_gallery","do":["fear:-2"]}]},{"id":"u22_pools_wade","chapter":"CHAPTER XXII · THE LONG DARK","text":"You step into the black water. It is colder than water should be, and it climbs your legs like it is deciding whether to keep them. Halfway across, the long thing in the pool finds you — a coil of pale muscle, eyeless, feeling for warmth.\n\nIt loops your ankle. It begins, without hurry, to pull.","choices":[{"text":"Strike down at the coil.","next":"u22_combat_eel"},{"text":"Wrench free and lunge for the far edge.","next":"u22_pools_lunge","check":{"stat":"hp","dc":13,"pass":"u22_pools_far","fail":"u22_combat_eel","onPass":["fear:3"]}}]},{"id":"u22_pools_lunge","chapter":"CHAPTER XXII · THE LONG DARK","text":"You throw your weight toward the dry stone.","choices":[{"text":"Pull.","next":"u22_pools_far"}]},{"id":"u22_pools_edge","chapter":"CHAPTER XXII · THE LONG DARK","text":"You trace the gallery's dry rim, where the stone rises just above the black water. It is slow going, hand over hand along a lip no wider than your foot, the flame clenched in your teeth.\n\nThe pale thing in the pool follows you the whole way, a shadow under the surface, keeping pace, waiting for you to slip. You do not slip. But your nerve frays like old rope, and by the time the edge widens you are wrung out with the effort of not falling.","choices":[{"text":"Step onto solid ground, shaking.","next":"u22_pools_far","do":["fear:5"]}]},{"id":"u22_combat_eel","chapter":"CHAPTER XXII · THE LONG DARK","text":"The Pale Coil rears out of the black water, a blind length of muscle thick as your thigh, mouth a ring of inward hooks. It smells the heat of you and wants it. You set your feet in the shallows and raise your blade.","combat":{"enemy":"Pale Coil","ehp":11,"edmg":4,"win":"u22_pools_far","lose":"u22_eel_lose","flee":"u22_pools_edge"},"choices":[]},{"id":"u22_eel_lose","chapter":"CHAPTER XXII · THE LONG DARK","text":"The Coil drags you under. Black water fills your ears, your nose, the world. You feel the far edge with your fingertips and haul yourself up onto it by will alone, coughing the pool out of your lungs while the Coil sinks back to wait for the next warm thing.\n\nYou live. Barely. The dark takes another piece of your courage as payment.","choices":[{"text":"Drag yourself onward.","next":"u22_pools_far","do":["fear:8"]}]},{"id":"u22_pools_far","chapter":"CHAPTER XXII · THE LONG DARK","text":"The far edge is dry, and beyond it the gallery narrows to a corridor of standing stones — pale markers set in two rows, leaning inward, older than the Underland's oldest lie.\n\nAt the corridor's mouth, your companion stops. Whether it is Lumen with the grey flame or Gloam with the mirror-cold blade, they will not step between the stones. Something in this passage frightens the part of them that is not quite yours yet.\n\nThe dark here is the deepest you have met. To go on, the party must go on together — or not at all.","choices":[{"text":"Turn to Lumen at the stones.","next":"u22_lumen_gate","require":"companion:lumen"},{"text":"Turn to Gloam at the stones.","next":"u22_gloam_gate","require":"companion:gloam"},{"text":"You walk alone. Enter the stones.","next":"u22_alone_stones"}]},{"id":"u22_lumen_gate","chapter":"CHAPTER XXII · THE LONG DARK","text":"Lumen holds the grey flame up between the leaning stones and will not take a step. The little light shivers.\n\n\"This is where they take the children,\" the child says, very small. \"I remember the stones. I was carried through them. If I go back in, the dark might remember it owns me.\"\n\nThe Lantern-child looks up at you. Everything depends on whether the child believes you will not let go.","choices":[{"text":"\"I have you. I will not let the dark have you.\" (Lumen 40+)","next":"u22_lumen_hold","require":"rel:lumen:40"},{"text":"\"Then light the way and trust me.\"","next":"u22_lumen_check","check":{"stat":"rel:lumen","dc":14,"pass":"u22_lumen_hold","fail":"u22_lumen_lost","onFail":["fear:6"]}},{"text":"\"Wait here in the light. I will come back for you.\"","next":"u22_lumen_lost"}]},{"id":"u22_lumen_check","chapter":"CHAPTER XXII · THE LONG DARK","text":"The child searches your face for the truth of it.","choices":[{"text":"Hold out your hand.","next":"u22_lumen_hold"}]},{"id":"u22_lumen_hold","chapter":"CHAPTER XXII · THE LONG DARK","text":"Lumen takes your hand. The small fingers are cold, but they hold hard. Together you step between the first two stones, and the dark leans in to claim its old possession — and finds the child's hand already claimed.\n\n\"You held on,\" Lumen whispers, wondering. \"Nobody held on before.\"\n\nThe grey flame steadies to a clean, brave point. Whatever waits at the bottom, the light goes down with you.","choices":[{"text":"Descend, hand in hand.","next":"u22_deep_landing","do":["rel:lumen:5","fear:-4","journal:Held Lumen's hand through the child-stones. Nobody held on before, the child said."]}]},{"id":"u22_lumen_lost","chapter":"CHAPTER XXII · THE LONG DARK","text":"You tell the child to wait in the light. It is the sensible thing. It is the wrong thing.\n\nYou take a dozen steps between the stones and turn back — and the flame is gone. The place where Lumen stood is only dark, and the dark is smug about it. No cry. No struggle. The Long Dark simply remembered it owned the child, and the child, unheld, went back to it.\n\nYou go on without a light. That is the price of letting go.","choices":[{"text":"Walk into the dark you can no longer see.","next":"u22_deep_landing","do":["companion:lumen","rem_item:ash_lantern","fear:12","flag:lost_lumen","journal:I let go of Lumen at the child-stones. The dark took the flame back. I go on blind."]}]},{"id":"u22_gloam_gate","chapter":"CHAPTER XXII · THE LONG DARK","text":"The Mirror-Knight halts at the leaning stones. In the flame's light Gloam's polished visor throws back not your face but the corridor's — and in the reflection, the stones are not stones. They are figures. A double row of knights exactly like Gloam, standing sentinel, waiting to be recognized.\n\n\"These were my order,\" Gloam says, low. \"The Mirror-Knights who came before me and were unmade here. If I walk in, the law may bind me back among them. I need to know you will not let the mirror keep me.\"","choices":[{"text":"\"You are mine, not the mirror's. Walk on.\" (Gloam 40+)","next":"u22_gloam_hold","require":"rel:gloam:40"},{"text":"\"Look at me, not the glass. Come.\"","next":"u22_gloam_check","check":{"stat":"rel:gloam","dc":14,"pass":"u22_gloam_hold","fail":"u22_gloam_lost","onFail":["fear:6"]}},{"text":"\"If the law wants you, I won't fight it.\"","next":"u22_gloam_lost"}]},{"id":"u22_gloam_check","chapter":"CHAPTER XXII · THE LONG DARK","text":"Behind the visor, you feel Gloam weighing your voice against a thousand years of the Mirror Law.","choices":[{"text":"Hold the knight's gaze.","next":"u22_gloam_hold"}]},{"id":"u22_gloam_hold","chapter":"CHAPTER XXII · THE LONG DARK","text":"Gloam looks away from the glass, to you. The reflected order calls silently in the polished steel, and the knight — for the first time in a very long time — refuses the reflection.\n\n\"Then I am yours,\" Gloam says, and steps between the stones. The mirrored knights in the visor bow their heads as one, and let their brother pass.\n\nThe mirror-cold blade warms half a degree. Down you go, the two of you, into the deep.","choices":[{"text":"Descend together.","next":"u22_deep_landing","do":["rel:gloam:5","fear:-4","journal:Gloam refused the mirror's pull at the knight-stones. 'Then I am yours.'"]}]},{"id":"u22_gloam_lost","chapter":"CHAPTER XXII · THE LONG DARK","text":"You do not fight the law. Gloam nods, once, the way a soldier nods at an order they expected.\n\nThe reflected order in the visor lifts their hands, and the Mirror-Knight steps sideways into the glass as if into an open door. The stones drink the last of the mirror-cold light. Where Gloam stood there is only another leaning stone, exactly like the rest, one knight longer than the row was before.\n\nYou go on without a shield. That is the price of not fighting for someone.","choices":[{"text":"Walk on, unguarded, into the deep.","next":"u22_deep_landing","do":["companion:gloam","rem_item:mirror_shard","fear:12","flag:lost_gloam","journal:I let the Mirror Law keep Gloam at the knight-stones. One knight longer, the row was. I go on unguarded."]}]},{"id":"u22_alone_stones","chapter":"CHAPTER XXII · THE LONG DARK","text":"You have no companion to fear the stones for you, and so you walk into them alone, as most who come this far have walked.\n\nThe leaning markers watch you pass with the patience of things that have watched everyone pass. None of them reach for you. You are not what they collect. You are only a warm thing, going down, carrying too little light.\n\nThe corridor tilts. The floor becomes a slope becomes a fall.","choices":[{"text":"Ride the slope down into the deep.","next":"u22_deep_landing","do":["fear:6"]}]},{"id":"u22_deep_landing","chapter":"CHAPTER XXII · THE LONG DARK","text":"The slope spills you out into a vast, low cavern — the deepest floor of the Long Dark. The ceiling is too far up to see. The far wall is too far off to guess. And the whole floor is bones: pale, uncountable, the leavings of everyone the Gate has ever swallowed and let sink this far.\n\nAt the center of the boneyard, a light. Not your grey flame. A red one — a brazier, tended, which means something down here tends it.\n\nAnd around the brazier, shapes are standing up out of the bones.","choices":[{"text":"Approach the brazier.","next":"u22_boneyard"},{"text":"Skirt the boneyard toward the far dark.","next":"u22_boneyard_skirt"}]},{"id":"u22_boneyard_skirt","chapter":"CHAPTER XXII · THE LONG DARK","text":"You keep to the boneyard's rim, away from the red light and the standing shapes. The bones shift under you, whispering, each step a small betrayal of sound.\n\nHalfway around, the whispering stops. That is worse. In the sudden quiet you understand you have been herded — the standing shapes have simply walked the shorter arc, and now they are between you and every direction but one: forward, into them.","choices":[{"text":"Meet them where they stand.","next":"u22_combat_gaunt"}]},{"id":"u22_boneyard","chapter":"CHAPTER XXII · THE LONG DARK","text":"You walk into the red light. The shapes resolve: the Gaunt — the taken who sank too far to be held gently anymore, worn down to hunger and habit. They do not speak. They tend the brazier because tending is the last thing their hands remember.\n\nThen they smell the warm blood in you, and remembering ends. The nearest lifts a face that is mostly socket and starts toward you, and the rest follow, and the brazier's red light makes long reaching shadows of them all.","choices":[{"text":"Set your back to the brazier and fight.","next":"u22_combat_gaunt"},{"text":"Try to reach the part of them that still remembers.","next":"u22_gaunt_plea","check":{"stat":"trust","dc":15,"pass":"u22_gaunt_calm","fail":"u22_combat_gaunt","onFail":["fear:4"]}}]},{"id":"u22_gaunt_plea","chapter":"CHAPTER XXII · THE LONG DARK","text":"You do the thing the dead Hunter told you, long ago and a world above: you make noise. You name them. You call them children, travellers, the loved and the lost, and you say the one thing the silence hates. You say: your people set bowls out. Your people kept the chairs.","choices":[{"text":"Say it, and mean it.","next":"u22_gaunt_calm"}]},{"id":"u22_gaunt_calm","chapter":"CHAPTER XXII · THE LONG DARK","text":"The Gaunt stop.\n\nSomething moves through the empty sockets — not sight, but memory of sight. The nearest one lowers its reaching hand and, with terrible slowness, turns back to the brazier. One by one the others follow, sitting down again among the bones, tending the small red fire that is the last warm thing they have.\n\nThey let you pass. As you go, the nearest presses something into your hand — a scrap of cloth, a faded ribbon, held all this time by a child who could not remember why.","choices":[{"text":"Take the ribbon. Walk on, humbled.","next":"u22_far_dark","do":["add_item:ribbon","trust:5","fear:-4","journal:Named the Gaunt in the boneyard. They let me pass, and gave me a child's ribbon."]}]},{"id":"u22_combat_gaunt","chapter":"CHAPTER XXII · THE LONG DARK","text":"There is no more talking. The Gaunt come off the bones in a starving tide, all reaching hands and open sockets and the awful patience of the very hungry. You cannot fight them all. You do not have to. You only have to break the tide that reaches you and get to the far dark before the rest close in.","combat":{"enemy":"The Gaunt","ehp":14,"edmg":4,"win":"u22_gaunt_win","lose":"u22_gaunt_lose","flee":"u22_far_dark"},"choices":[]},{"id":"u22_gaunt_win","chapter":"CHAPTER XXII · THE LONG DARK","text":"You cut a road through them. The ones that fall do not scream; they simply stop, and lie down among the bones they came from, and are quiet in a way that looks almost like relief.\n\nWhen it is done you are standing in a ring of stillness, breathing hard, red brazier-light on your blade. The rest of the Gaunt have drawn back to the fire. They will not follow you past the light's edge.\n\nBeyond the boneyard, the far dark waits, and in it, a stair going up.","choices":[{"text":"Cross to the far dark and the rising stair.","next":"u22_far_dark","do":["fear:4","journal:Fought through the Gaunt in the boneyard. The fallen looked relieved."]}]},{"id":"u22_gaunt_lose","chapter":"CHAPTER XXII · THE LONG DARK","text":"The tide closes over you. Hands in your hair, your collar, your sleeves, cold and countless, dragging you down toward the brazier to be tended, to be kept, to be made quiet.\n\nAnd then — light. Your own grey flame flares, or the memory of it does, and the Gaunt flinch from the warmth as from a blade. You tear free and run, leaving skin and blood and most of your courage behind, and you do not stop running until the red light is a coal behind you.","choices":[{"text":"Run for the far dark. Do not look back.","next":"u22_far_dark","do":["fear:10","journal:The Gaunt nearly kept me. I ran and left half my nerve in the boneyard."]}]},{"id":"u22_far_dark","chapter":"CHAPTER XXII · THE LONG DARK","text":"The far dark is quieter than the rest. The bones thin, then end. Ahead, cut into the cavern's far wall, a stair climbs — and the air that comes down it is not the dead air of the deep but something with a taste to it. Cold stone. Old incense. The breath of a great shut room.\n\nYou have crossed the bottom of the Long Dark and come out the far side. Whatever the party is now — whole, or one short, blooded and afraid — it is what will climb this stair.\n\nAt the first step, you stop and take the measure of yourself. This is the last dark before the throne.","choices":[{"text":"Take account of who still walks with you.","next":"u22_reckoning"},{"text":"No time. Climb.","next":"u22_ascend"}]},{"id":"u22_reckoning","chapter":"CHAPTER XXII · THE LONG DARK","text":"You count the circle of your light.\n\nIf Lumen still holds the grey flame, it burns steady, and the child holds your sleeve and does not let go. If Gloam still walks, the mirror-cold blade is a comfort at your shoulder. And if you left one of them in the dark below — you feel the shape of that absence more than any wound. The party is only as strong as what it refused to lose.\n\nWhatever you have, it is enough to climb with, or it is not, and there is only one way to find out.","choices":[{"text":"[Lumen still walks] Nod to the Lantern-child. Climb.","next":"u22_ascend","require":"companion:lumen"},{"text":"[Gloam still walks] Nod to the Mirror-Knight. Climb.","next":"u22_ascend","require":"companion:gloam"},{"text":"[Alone] Square your shoulders. Climb.","next":"u22_ascend"}]},{"id":"u22_ascend","chapter":"CHAPTER XXII · THE LONG DARK","text":"The stair climbs and does not tire, though you do. Step after step out of the deep, the bones behind you, the boneyard's red coal shrinking to nothing below.\n\nThe cold-incense breath grows stronger. Somewhere far above, a light that is not fire and not flame — a pale, patient glow, the glow of a room where something has sat still for a thousand years, waiting for the sound of a footstep on its stair.\n\nYour footstep. It hears you coming. It has always heard you coming.\n\nThe Long Dark ends. The Hollow Throne begins.","choices":[{"text":"Climb the last steps into the pale glow.","next":"u23_start","do":["fear:5","journal:Climbed out of the Long Dark. Something above has been waiting for my footstep."]}]}]},"story/chapter_23.json":{"scenes":[{"id":"u23_start","chapter":"CHAPTER XXIII · THE HOLLOW THRONE","text":"The Long Dark opens without warning, the way a held breath ends.\n\nYou step from the last of the tunnels into a hall that has no walls you can find. The floor is black glass. It goes down forever beneath your boots, and something down there is looking up.\n\nAt the far end — near, then far, then near again, distance lying the way it always lies here — sits a throne. It is grown, not built: a chair of grey antler and drowned wood and the long white roots of some tree that died before there were trees. Frost breathes off it in slow tides.\n\nSomething sits in it. You cannot yet say what. Only that it is watching you cross the black glass, and that it has been watching you since long before you were born.","choices":[{"text":"Walk toward the throne. Do not slow.","next":"u23_approach","do":["flag:region_throne","journal:Reached the Hollow Throne. Something has been waiting for me here."]},{"text":"Stop. Take the measure of the room first.","next":"u23_measure"}]},{"id":"u23_measure","chapter":"CHAPTER XXIII · THE HOLLOW THRONE","text":"You hold still on the black glass and let your eyes learn the room.\n\nThe hall is a memory of the throne room in the kingdom above — the same proportions, the same high absent windows — but rotted through and reflected wrong, as if drowned and then dreamed. Frost has written on the floor in a hand you almost recognize. The same word, over and over, worn smooth by centuries of writing: a girl's name, half a name, less than a name each time.\n\nAround the throne stand the taken. Children. Hunters in old livery. A widow. They do not move. They are listening to the silence the way plants lean at the sun.\n\nThe thing in the throne lifts its head a fraction. Frost falls from it like snow off a bough.","choices":[{"text":"Read the frozen writing on the floor.","next":"u23_writing"},{"text":"Enough looking. Approach the throne.","next":"u23_approach","do":["flag:region_throne"]}]},{"id":"u23_writing","chapter":"CHAPTER XXIII · THE HOLLOW THRONE","text":"You kneel and press your palm to the frost. The writing wakes under your hand, ten thousand attempts at one word, none of them finished, none of them right. A letter here. A sound there. A name he has been chasing across the ice for a thousand winters and never once caught.\n\nThis is the wound. This is what the minstrel meant. He has written her down and written her down and cannot read his own hand anymore.\n\nIf you carry her true name, you carry the only thing in either world he cannot bear to hear.","choices":[{"text":"Rise, and go to the throne.","next":"u23_approach","do":["flag:region_throne","flag:codex_keeper","journal:The floor of the throne room is one name, written ten thousand times and never finished. His wound has a shape now."]}]},{"id":"u23_approach","chapter":"CHAPTER XXIII · THE HOLLOW THRONE","text":"You walk. The taken turn their heads to follow you, slow as sunflowers, and something in that gentle attention is worse than any snarl.\n\nHalfway across, the cold stops being weather and starts being intent. It reaches into your chest and feels around for the thing you love most, to hold it up to the light.\n\nThen the thing in the throne stands.\n\nIt unfolds. Antlers, or a crown, or the black branches of a winter tree — the shape will not hold still. Beneath it is the suggestion of a face, the place where a face should be, and two eyes that are only very deep grief with the lights left on.\n\n\"You came all this way,\" the Keeper says. Its voice is every voice you have ever heard go quiet. \"To the last room. How brave. I do so love the brave ones. They fill up so nicely.\"","choices":[{"text":"\"I know what you are. You're the Keeper.\"","next":"u23_name_it"},{"text":"Say nothing. Let it come to you.","next":"u23_offer"},{"text":"Draw steel. No more talk.","next":"u23_prefight","do":["fear:5"]}]},{"id":"u23_name_it","chapter":"CHAPTER XXIII · THE HOLLOW THRONE","text":"\"The Keeper.\" It tastes the word. \"Yes. That is the post. That is the coat I wear. But you have not asked what is under the coat.\"\n\nIt drifts closer, and the taken close ranks behind it, a silent tide of the held.\n\n\"There is always a Keeper. The Gate must be kept, or it opens onto everything at once, and everything at once is a thing no world survives. So someone stays. Someone always stays. The staying hollows you out and fills you back up with door, until you are only the door's hunger wearing a face.\"\n\nIt tilts the ruin of its head.\n\n\"I have kept it so long I have forgotten why I came in. But I came in for something. I know I came in for something.\"","choices":[{"text":"\"You came in for a girl. You've forgotten her name.\"","next":"u23_offer","require":"flag:keeper_weakness","do":["flag:codex_keeper","journal:Told the Keeper to his face that he came in for a drowned girl. Something in the dark flinched."]},{"text":"\"Then let me take your place, and you can go.\"","next":"u23_offer","do":["flag:codex_keeper"]},{"text":"\"Whatever you came for, you won't have my kin.\"","next":"u23_prefight","do":["flag:codex_keeper","trust:5"]}]},{"id":"u23_offer","chapter":"CHAPTER XXIII · THE HOLLOW THRONE","text":"The Keeper stops an arm's length away. This close, the cold is total. Your breath crystals and falls.\n\n\"I will make you an honest offer,\" it says. \"I make it to everyone who reaches this room. You are the first in an age to hear it standing up.\"\n\nBehind it, in the grey ranks of the held, you see them: {missing_name}, small and still and listening, eyes gone to glass. And near them, wearing antlers of {other_poss} own now, {other_name}.\n\n\"Leave me the two you love,\" the Keeper says, \"and I will let the rest of you walk back up into the light. I will even smile when you go. It is a good bargain. It is the only bargain I have. What is two, against a whole life of your own?\"","choices":[{"text":"\"No. I did not come to trade them away.\"","next":"u23_refuse"},{"text":"\"Everyone. I take everyone, or no one leaves.\"","next":"u23_refuse","do":["trust:5","fear:-5"]},{"text":"Pretend to consider it. Buy a heartbeat.","next":"u23_stall","check":{"stat":"fear","dc":13,"pass":"u23_stall","fail":"u23_refuse"}}]},{"id":"u23_stall","chapter":"CHAPTER XXIII · THE HOLLOW THRONE","text":"You let your shoulders drop. You let your eyes go to the floor, to the ten thousand unfinished names in the frost. You let the Keeper believe, for one breath, that it has won.\n\nIt leans in. It is greedy for the yes. In its greed it comes close enough that you can see the boy who is still buried somewhere under all that door — the boy who climbed into the dark after a girl and never climbed out.\n\nNow. While it is close. You choose how this ends.","choices":[{"text":"Say the name you carry. (true_name)","next":"u23_truename_check","require":"true_name"},{"text":"Show it the mirror-shard, and let it see itself. (mirror_shard)","next":"u23_mirror_path","require":"mirror_shard"},{"text":"Strike while it is close.","next":"u23_prefight","do":["trust:3"]}]},{"id":"u23_refuse","chapter":"CHAPTER XXIII · THE HOLLOW THRONE","text":"\"No,\" you say, and the word rings on the black glass like a struck bell.\n\nThe Keeper draws back. For a moment the grief in its eyes curdles into something older and colder, and the whole hall darkens as if a lid closed over a lamp.\n\n\"They always say no,\" it murmurs. \"And then I keep them anyway, and add their no to the quiet. You will make a lovely silence, brave one. You will go so still.\"\n\nThe taken behind it lift their hands. The frost climbs your boots. You have one choice left — how you mean to break this.","choices":[{"text":"Speak its true name and command it. (true_name)","next":"u23_truename_check","require":"true_name"},{"text":"Break the mirror-shard between you. (mirror_shard)","next":"u23_mirror_path","require":"mirror_shard"},{"text":"Call your companions to your side.","next":"u23_rally"},{"text":"No name, no mirror. Only steel.","next":"u23_prefight"}]},{"id":"u23_rally","chapter":"CHAPTER XXIII · THE HOLLOW THRONE","text":"You do not face it alone. You turn your head a fraction and say the names of the ones who came this far with you — and in this room of the nameless, saying a name is the loudest thing there is.\n\nThe silence cracks. Somewhere behind you, a boot scrapes glass. A blade clears a scabbard. A lantern flares grey.","choices":[{"text":"Gloam sets his mirror-back against yours.","next":"u23_gloam_stand","require":"companion:gloam"},{"text":"Lumen lifts the grey flame between you and the dark.","next":"u23_lumen_stand","require":"companion:lumen"},{"text":"Vale steps up, ruined and steady, sword high.","next":"u23_prefight","require":"companion:vale","do":["trust:5","max_hp:2"]},{"text":"No one answers. You stand alone, and choose steel.","next":"u23_prefight","do":["fear:5"]}]},{"id":"u23_gloam_stand","chapter":"CHAPTER XXIII · THE HOLLOW THRONE","text":"The Mirror-Knight sets his back to yours. In his polished breastplate you see the Keeper reflected — and the reflection is not the towering thing before you. In the mirror it is small. A soaked boy. A grief that never got to grow up.\n\n\"It cannot lie in a mirror,\" Gloam says, low. \"The Law holds even here. If we make it look at itself, we make it remember.\"\n\nHe turns his shield toward the Keeper. The Keeper recoils from its own true shape.","choices":[{"text":"Use the shard-glass with Gloam. (mirror_shard)","next":"u23_mirror_path","require":"mirror_shard","do":["rel:gloam:5"]},{"text":"Press the advantage — strike while it flinches.","next":"u23_prefight","do":["rel:gloam:3","trust:5"]},{"text":"Say the name while it is looking at itself. (true_name)","next":"u23_truename_check","require":"true_name","do":["rel:gloam:5"]}]},{"id":"u23_lumen_stand","chapter":"CHAPTER XXIII · THE HOLLOW THRONE","text":"The Lantern-child steps in front of you, small and unafraid, and lifts the grey flame high.\n\nThe taken turn toward the light. For the first time they are not listening to the silence — they are looking at something. A child, a fire, a way out.\n\n\"They followed me here,\" Lumen says, without turning. \"The Keeper takes the ones nobody comes back for. But somebody came back. You came back.\" The small hand tightens on yours. \"I can hold the light. You have to close the wound.\"","choices":[{"text":"\"Then hold it. I'll do the rest.\" Say the name. (true_name)","next":"u23_truename_check","require":"true_name","do":["rel:lumen:5"]},{"text":"Guard Lumen and the flame — meet the Keeper with steel.","next":"u23_prefight","do":["rel:lumen:5","trust:5"]},{"text":"Turn the shard toward the flame. (mirror_shard)","next":"u23_mirror_path","require":"mirror_shard","do":["rel:lumen:3"]}]},{"id":"u23_truename_check","chapter":"CHAPTER XXIII · THE HOLLOW THRONE","text":"You hold the true name in your mouth like a coal. It is small. It is only a girl's name, the name a boy called across a frozen river a thousand years ago and never got to hear answered.\n\nEverything in the Underland has a name it has hidden even from itself. Say one, say it true, and you command the thing — once. Only once.\n\nThe Keeper sees your lips move to shape it and it comes apart at the seams, half lunging to stop you, half aching to hear. The whole hall holds its breath.\n\nSay it clear. Say it true. Do not let your voice shake.","choices":[{"text":"Speak the name aloud, clear and true.","next":"u23_truename_resolve"},{"text":"Let the weight of the moment decide — trust your voice.","next":"u23_truename_resolve","check":{"stat":"trust","dc":14,"pass":"u23_truename_win","fail":"u23_truename_fail","onPass":["flag:codex_keeper","journal:I said her name. His name. The one he lost. The Gate answered."],"onFail":["fear:10"]}}]},{"id":"u23_truename_resolve","chapter":"CHAPTER XXIII · THE HOLLOW THRONE","text":"You open your mouth and the name falls out into the dark.","choices":[{"text":"…","next":"u23_truename_win"}]},{"id":"u23_truename_win","chapter":"CHAPTER XXIII · THE HOLLOW THRONE","text":"The name lands.\n\nIt strikes the Keeper the way spring strikes ice — not with force, but from underneath, everywhere at once. A crack runs up through the towering shape. Then another. The antlers, the crown, the winter branches all shudder and let go, and for one impossible instant the thing in front of you is not a Keeper at all.\n\nIt is a boy. Soaked. Fifteen, no more. Standing on a riverbank a thousand years gone, hearing at last the name he screamed into the water — and understanding, finally, that she did not answer because she could not, and that it was never his to fix.\n\n\"Oh,\" the boy says. Just that. \"Oh. It was you. It was always just… you.\"\n\nThe frost on the floor melts. Ten thousand unfinished names run together into water and the water runs away downhill toward a light.\n\n\"The door,\" the boy whispers, already thinning. \"Someone still has to keep the door. But not like this. Never again like this. Choose better than I did.\"","choices":[{"text":"\"I will. Go. She's waiting.\"","next":"u24_start","require":"companion:lumen","do":["flag:keeper_named","flag:throne_lantern_held","companion:lumen","journal:The Keeper was a boy. I gave him back his name and he let go. Lumen holds the light while the door decides who keeps it next.","trust:10"]},{"text":"\"Rest now. I have the door.\"","next":"u24_start","do":["flag:keeper_named","flag:throne_open_seat","journal:The Keeper was a boy. I named him free. Now the throne is empty, and it is looking at me.","trust:10"]}]},{"id":"u23_truename_fail","chapter":"CHAPTER XXIII · THE HOLLOW THRONE","text":"Your voice shakes. The name comes out cracked down the middle, half-swallowed by the cold, and it lands wrong — a syllable short, a breath too soft.\n\nThe Keeper reels, wounded but not undone. It heard enough to remember there was a name, and not enough to be freed by it, and that is the cruelest place of all.\n\n\"So close,\" it breathes, and its grief goes to fury. \"You had it. You had it and you let it fall.\"\n\nThe hall floods with dark. The taken raise their hands. There is no more talking now — only whether you live through the next minute.","choices":[{"text":"Steady your breath. Try the name once more. (true_name)","next":"u23_truename_retry","require":"true_name"},{"text":"Steel, then. Cut your way to the throne.","next":"u23_prefight","do":["fear:5"]}]},{"id":"u23_truename_retry","chapter":"CHAPTER XXIII · THE HOLLOW THRONE","text":"You close your eyes. You stop trying to be brave and just try to be true.\n\nYou think of the widow's letter — being loved makes the dark less wide. You think of the frozen river. You open your eyes, and this time you do not aim the name like a weapon. You give it, gently, the way you would hand a lost thing back to its owner.","choices":[{"text":"Say her name, softly, like a gift.","next":"u23_truename_win"},{"text":"Steady your nerve first, then give it — and see if the fear lets you.","next":"u23_truename_win","check":{"stat":"fear","dc":11,"pass":"u23_truename_win","fail":"u23_prefight","onPass":["flag:codex_keeper","journal:The second time, I gave the name instead of throwing it. That was the difference."],"onFail":["hp:-3"]}}]},{"id":"u23_mirror_path","chapter":"CHAPTER XXIII · THE HOLLOW THRONE","text":"You draw the mirror-shard. It is a splinter of the Underland's own black water, hardened, and it remembers everything it has ever reflected.\n\nA reflection can be truer than the thing it reflects. You turn the shard so the Keeper sees itself.\n\nAnd the Keeper does not see a Keeper. It sees the boy. Soaked, small, fifteen, screaming at a river that will not give her back. It has not looked at that boy in a thousand years. It has built a whole crown, a whole coat of door, precisely so it would never have to.\n\n\"Stop,\" it says. \"Stop showing me that. Put it away. Put it — \"\n\nThe shard is a small thing and the Keeper is vast. Holding the mirror up is like holding a candle to a rising tide. Your arm shakes.","choices":[{"text":"Hold the mirror steady. Make it look. (skill)","next":"u23_mirror_hold","check":{"stat":"trust","dc":13,"pass":"u23_mirror_win","fail":"u23_mirror_shatter","onPass":["flag:codex_keeper"],"onFail":["fear:8"]}},{"text":"Drive the shard into the throne itself.","next":"u23_mirror_shatter"},{"text":"Say the name while it stares at its true face. (true_name)","next":"u23_truename_check","require":"true_name","do":["rem_item:mirror_shard"]}]},{"id":"u23_mirror_hold","chapter":"CHAPTER XXIII · THE HOLLOW THRONE","text":"You brace the shard with both hands and do not let it drop.","choices":[{"text":"Hold. Hold. Hold.","next":"u23_mirror_win"}]},{"id":"u23_mirror_win","chapter":"CHAPTER XXIII · THE HOLLOW THRONE","text":"The Keeper cannot look away.\n\nIt is bound by the Mirror Law as surely as Gloam is, as surely — quietly — as you are. It stares into the shard and the crown of branches sloughs off it like snow off a warming roof. The vast shape folds down and down until it is only the boy again, dripping riverwater onto the black glass, staring at his own reflected grief with an expression of terrible, dawning relief.\n\n\"That's me,\" he says, wondering. \"That's just… me. I thought I was the door. I thought I had to be the door.\"\n\nHe reaches toward his reflection. The shard in your hands goes warm, then bright, then still.\n\n\"You can let me put it down now,\" the boy says. \"But someone still has to keep the Gate. That doesn't end. That never ends. Only who — and how.\"","choices":[{"text":"Free him. Take up the mirror-watch yourself. (Gloam)","next":"u24_start","require":"companion:gloam","do":["flag:keeper_mirrored","flag:throne_mirror_pact","companion:gloam","rem_item:mirror_shard","journal:I showed the Keeper his true face in the shard and he laid the crown down. Gloam and I will keep the door by the Mirror Law now — a truer keeping.","trust:10"]},{"text":"Free him. Leave the throne empty and open.","next":"u24_start","do":["flag:keeper_mirrored","flag:throne_open_seat","rem_item:mirror_shard","journal:The mirror unmade the Keeper. The throne sits empty. Something in the dark is waiting to see who fills it.","trust:8"]}]},{"id":"u23_mirror_shatter","chapter":"CHAPTER XXIII · THE HOLLOW THRONE","text":"You drive the shard down into the arm of the throne with everything you have.\n\nThe black glass of the floor answers with a sound like a lake cracking in a thaw — a long, groaning boom that runs out to the horizon and back. The shard shatters, and every splinter throws a reflection, and suddenly the Keeper is surrounded by a thousand tiny true faces of itself, a thousand soaked boys, a thousand griefs it cannot un-see.\n\nThe throne of antler and root screams. It has never been struck before. Nothing has ever refused it in this room and lived, and refusal, it turns out, is a kind of blade.\n\nBut a wounded thing is a dangerous thing, and the shard is gone now. What rises from the broken throne is not a boy. It is the door's hunger, cornered, and it comes for you with a hundred grey hands.","choices":[{"text":"Meet it. Stand your ground.","next":"u23_combat","do":["flag:codex_keeper","rem_item:mirror_shard"]}]},{"id":"u23_prefight","chapter":"CHAPTER XXIII · THE HOLLOW THRONE","text":"No names. No mirrors. Only the oldest answer there is.\n\nYou set your feet on the black glass and raise your steel. The Keeper regards you with something almost like tenderness, the way a tide regards a sandcastle.\n\n\"Brave,\" it says. \"So brave. The brave ones fight the door. As if a door could be stabbed. As if hunger could bleed.\"\n\nIt spreads the ruin of its arms, and the cold comes down like a falling roof, and the taken close in behind it — but you have come too far to be quiet now. You have a name in your mouth even if it is only your own, and you say it, loud, and step into the dark.","choices":[{"text":"Charge the throne.","next":"u23_combat"},{"text":"Wait — try the name one last time. (true_name)","next":"u23_truename_check","require":"true_name"}]},{"id":"u23_combat","chapter":"CHAPTER XXIII · THE HOLLOW THRONE","text":"The Keeper's true form pours off the throne — a churning tower of frost and antler and reaching grey hands, the drowned court boiling up behind it. It does not want to kill you. It wants to keep you, and keeping is worse. Every touch of the cold tries to make you still, tries to make you listen, tries to add you to the quiet.\n\nStrike fast. Do not go quiet. Do not stop making noise.","choices":[],"combat":{"enemy":"The Keeper's Hunger","ehp":15,"edmg":4,"win":"u23_combat_win","lose":"u23_combat_lose","flee":"u23_combat_flee"}},{"id":"u23_combat_win","chapter":"CHAPTER XXIII · THE HOLLOW THRONE","text":"You do not stop moving and you do not stop shouting, and in the end that is the trick of it: the Keeper cannot make a loud thing quiet by force. Blow by blow you drive the cold back onto the throne it came from.\n\nThe tower of frost buckles. The grey hands lose their grip on the world one finger at a time. And as the shape collapses, for just a breath, you glimpse the boy inside it — startled, exhausted, almost grateful to be losing.\n\nThe Keeper does not die. Doors do not die. But it is beaten out of its shape and driven back down into the black glass, and the throne stands scorched and empty, and the taken behind it stir like sleepers at a slammed shutter.\n\nYou are still standing. You made enough noise. You are still yourself.","choices":[{"text":"Stand over the broken throne. Decide what comes next.","next":"u24_start","do":["flag:codex_keeper","flag:keeper_beaten","flag:throne_open_seat","journal:I could not name the Keeper, so I fought it. I broke its shape and cleared the throne. But the door is not closed — only unguarded. That is a new kind of danger.","trust:5"]}]},{"id":"u23_combat_flee","chapter":"CHAPTER XXIII · THE HOLLOW THRONE","text":"You break off. The cold is too much, the hands too many, and dying quietly in this room would help no one you love.\n\nYou fall back to the throne room's edge, where the black glass meets the dark you came from, chest heaving, the Keeper looming but — strangely — not pursuing. It cannot leave the room. It never could. That is the whole tragedy of it.\n\n\"You will come back,\" it calls, almost gentle. \"They all come back. The door is patient. So am I.\"\n\nYou press a hand to the wall and breathe. You are not beaten. You are only not finished. And now you know the shape of the room, the reach of the cold, the weakness under the crown.","choices":[{"text":"Gather yourself. Go back in — with the name this time. (true_name)","next":"u23_truename_check","require":"true_name","do":["fear:-5"]},{"text":"Gather yourself. Go back in and finish the fight.","next":"u23_combat_return"}]},{"id":"u23_combat_return","chapter":"CHAPTER XXIII · THE HOLLOW THRONE","text":"You catch your breath in the dark at the room's edge until your hands stop shaking. Then you tighten your grip and walk back onto the black glass.\n\nThe Keeper is waiting, exactly as you left it, patient as a tide. But you are not the same as you left it. You know its reach now. You know it cannot follow you out. You know it wants to keep you, not kill you, and a thing that will not kill you can be worn down.\n\n\"Again,\" the Keeper sighs. \"Brave. So brave.\"\n\n\"Again,\" you agree, and step in.","choices":[],"combat":{"enemy":"The Keeper's Hunger","ehp":11,"edmg":4,"win":"u23_combat_win","lose":"u23_combat_lose","flee":"u23_combat_flee"}},{"id":"u23_combat_lose","chapter":"CHAPTER XXIII · THE HOLLOW THRONE","text":"The cold gets its arms around you at last.\n\nIt does not hurt. That is the horror of it. It is warm the way freezing to death is warm at the end — a great and gentle hush, a hand smoothing your hair, a voice telling you that you have done enough, that you can put the fight down now, that you can be still.\n\nAnd you nearly do. You nearly go quiet.\n\nBut somewhere in the grey ranks a small voice says a name — your name — and will not stop saying it. {missing_name}. Or the Lantern-child. Or a widow who kept the chairs. Someone in the dark remembers you were a person, and their remembering is a splinter you can grip.\n\nYou grip it. You do not go quiet. You force one eye open, then the other, and drag yourself back up out of the warm.","choices":[{"text":"Refuse the hush. Rise once more.","next":"u23_last_stand","do":["hp:3","fear:5","flag:codex_keeper"]}]},{"id":"u23_last_stand","chapter":"CHAPTER XXIII · THE HOLLOW THRONE","text":"You come back onto your feet the way the drowned come back — coughing, furious, alive out of pure refusal.\n\nThe Keeper actually pauses. In a thousand years, few have crawled out of its embrace once it had them. It is not sure what to do with a thing that will not be kept.\n\n\"You should be quiet by now,\" it says, and for the first time there is something under the grief that might be fear. \"Why aren't you quiet?\"\n\n\"Because someone came back for me,\" you say. \"That's the whole thing you never understood.\"","choices":[{"text":"Say the name that ends it. (true_name)","next":"u23_truename_check","require":"true_name","do":["fear:-5"]},{"text":"One last charge — break its shape for good.","next":"u23_last_combat"}]},{"id":"u23_last_combat","chapter":"CHAPTER XXIII · THE HOLLOW THRONE","text":"Everything you have left, you spend now. Not for glory — you have heard what the door does to heroes — but for the small stubborn people in the grey ranks who kept saying your name.\n\nThe Keeper's shape has never had to fight a thing it could not hush. It does not know how. And that, in the end, is the crack you drive your steel into.","choices":[],"combat":{"enemy":"The Keeper's Last Shape","ehp":8,"edmg":3,"win":"u23_combat_win","lose":"u23_bargain_forced","flee":"u23_bargain_forced"}},{"id":"u23_bargain_forced","chapter":"CHAPTER XXIII · THE HOLLOW THRONE","text":"You cannot beat it. Not with steel, not tonight, not without the name you never found.\n\nSo you do the only thing left to a cornered person in a room that will not let you die: you bargain.\n\n\"A trade,\" you gasp. \"You want someone to keep the door. You want to be able to put it down. I'm here. I'm loud. I won't go quiet. Let the taken walk out — all of them — and I'll consider the seat.\"\n\nThe Keeper goes very still. This is not the bargain it offers. This is a bargain offered to it, and it has not heard one of those in an age.\n\n\"You would sit down here,\" it says slowly, \"of your own will. Knowing what it costs.\"\n\n\"I'd hear you out,\" you say. \"That's all. In the next room. Not here.\"","choices":[{"text":"Hold the offer open. Walk into the next room to decide.","next":"u24_start","do":["flag:keeper_bargain_open","flag:throne_open_seat","flag:codex_keeper","journal:I couldn't beat the Keeper. So I offered it the one thing it never expected: a willing successor. Nothing is signed. But the door is listening now, and so am I.","fear:5"]}]}]},"story/chapter_24.json":{"scenes":[{"id":"u24_start","chapter":"CHAPTER XXIV · WHAT COMES BACK","text":"The Hollow Throne is empty now. Or not empty — occupied by a stillness that has the shape of a person and the weight of a mountain.\n\nThe Keeper kneels at the foot of the door that has no wall, both hands pressed to the stone as if holding shut a wound that keeps trying to open. Grey light leaks between the fingers. Every leak is a face. Every face is a child who did not come home.\n\nBehind you the Underland exhales. The drowned court, the ashen march, the sunk city — all of it leans toward this room, listening, the way the whole realm leans toward a thing about to be decided.\n\nYou came to end the Gate. You are here. It is time to learn what that costs.","choices":[{"text":"Approach the kneeling Keeper.","next":"u24_keeper_close"},{"text":"Study the door itself first.","next":"u24_door_study"},{"text":"Take stock. Who still stands with you?","next":"u24_party_check"}]},{"id":"u24_party_check","chapter":"CHAPTER XXIV · WHAT COMES BACK","text":"You turn. The hall behind you is a long throat of black water and older stone, and in it, faint as breath on glass, stand the ones who came this far.\n\nNot all made it. Some you left in the march. Some the wood kept. But those who remain remain because they chose to, and that is the only kind of company worth having at the end of a world.\n\nThe grey light gutters. Whatever you do, you will not do it with clean hands. You will only do it with the hands you have.","choices":[{"text":"Speak with Lumen, the lantern-child.","next":"u24_lumen_talk","require":"companion:lumen"},{"text":"Speak with Gloam, the Mirror-Knight.","next":"u24_gloam_talk","require":"companion:gloam"},{"text":"Speak with the ones from the far side of the Gate.","next":"u24_old_friends"},{"text":"Turn back to the door. Enough waiting.","next":"u24_door_study"}]},{"id":"u24_old_friends","chapter":"CHAPTER XXIV · WHAT COMES BACK","text":"Not everyone who followed you into the dark was born of it. Some came from the sunlit realm and should never have been asked to walk this far — and came anyway.\n\nYou find them where the black water shallows: the faces of the road. Whichever of them lived, whichever of them stayed. They look at you the way people look at someone about to do a terrible, necessary thing, and love them for it, and cannot help them do it.\n\nWho do you go to?","choices":[{"text":"Ash, who guided you through the Wastes.","next":"u24_friend_ash","require":"companion:ash"},{"text":"Ser Vale, the ruined knight.","next":"u24_friend_vale","require":"companion:vale"},{"text":"Rook, who was never supposed to make it this far.","next":"u24_friend_rook","require":"companion:rook"},{"text":"The hedge-witch, who knew the old doors.","next":"u24_friend_witch","require":"companion:hedge_witch"},{"text":"Moth, who could always find the way out.","next":"u24_friend_moth","require":"companion:moth"},{"text":"No time for goodbyes. Back to the door.","next":"u24_door_study"}]},{"id":"u24_friend_ash","chapter":"CHAPTER XXIV · WHAT COMES BACK","text":"Ash spits — out of habit, there is no ground here worth spitting on — and looks at the door like it owes them money.\n\n\"I don't work for crowns,\" they say. \"Never did. Followed you anyway. Don't let it go to your head.\" The flint in their eyes softens by a hair. \"Whatever you do in there, do it and come back out. I know every rock and raven between here and the sun. I'll get you home. That's a guide's promise, and I don't break those.\"","choices":[{"text":"\"Keep the way lit behind me, Ash.\"","next":"u24_door_study","do":["rel:ash:+3","flag:u24_ash_waits","journal:Ash promised to guide me back to the sun, whatever I do at the door."]}]},{"id":"u24_friend_vale","chapter":"CHAPTER XXIV · WHAT COMES BACK","text":"Ser Vale kneels — armor grinding, the old ruin in him showing — and bows his head as if you were the crown he swore to and lost.\n\n\"I failed once at a door like this,\" he says. \"They sent six of us. I was the one who came back, and I have hated the coming back every day since.\" He lifts his ruined face. \"Let me stand at this one. Whatever you choose, I will hold the threshold, so that this time no one has to come back alone.\"","choices":[{"text":"\"Stand with me, Ser Vale.\"","next":"u24_door_study","do":["rel:vale:+3","flag:u24_vale_holds","journal:Ser Vale will hold the threshold. This time, he says, no one comes back alone."]}]},{"id":"u24_friend_rook","chapter":"CHAPTER XXIV · WHAT COMES BACK","text":"Rook grins at you, gap-toothed and impossible. Rook should be dead. Rook has been should-be-dead for the entire journey and keeps declining to notice.\n\n\"Big door,\" Rook observes. \"Bigger than the last big door. You reckon there's a bigger one after this? There's always a bigger one after this.\" The grin falters, just for a moment. \"Hey. Whatever's on the other side of what you're about to do — if it's got room for one more idiot who wasn't supposed to make it, save me a seat. I've gotten used to not dying near you.\"","choices":[{"text":"\"There's a seat, Rook. There's always a seat.\"","next":"u24_door_study","do":["rel:rook:+4","flag:u24_rook_stays","journal:Rook — who was never supposed to make it this far — asked me to save a seat wherever I end up."]}]},{"id":"u24_friend_witch","chapter":"CHAPTER XXIV · WHAT COMES BACK","text":"The hedge-witch runs her thumb along the seam of the door and hisses through her teeth, the way she hisses at a wound gone bad.\n\n\"Old,\" she mutters. \"Older than my grandmother's grandmother's spells. This isn't a door you break, child. It's a door you tend or a door you close, and both cost blood — yours, or a name's, or a life spent standing still.\" She takes your hand and presses something into it: a knot of dried herbs, for courage or for grief, you can't tell which. \"Whatever you choose, choose it kind. The unkind endings to doors like this are the ones that come back around.\"","choices":[{"text":"\"I'll choose it kind. I promise.\"","next":"u24_door_study","do":["rel:hedge_witch:+3","trust:+3","flag:u24_witch_warned","journal:The hedge-witch: an unkind ending to a door like this is the kind that comes back around. Choose it kind."]}]},{"id":"u24_friend_moth","chapter":"CHAPTER XXIV · WHAT COMES BACK","text":"Moth is already halfway to picking the lock of a door that has no lock, out of pure habit, before she catches herself and laughs.\n\n\"Old thief's rule,\" she says. \"Always know your way out before you go in. And I do. I always do.\" She jerks her chin at the long black-water throat behind you. \"There's a way back through all of it. I've mapped it in my head, every rotten inch. Do what you came for. I'll get whoever's left standing out the far side.\" She hesitates, which Moth never does. \"Just — try to be one of the ones I get out. Yeah?\"","choices":[{"text":"\"I'll try, Moth. Keep the way ready.\"","next":"u24_door_study","do":["rel:moth:+3","flag:u24_moth_ready","journal:Moth mapped the way out of the whole Underland. She asked me to be one of the ones she gets out."]}]},{"id":"u24_lumen_talk","chapter":"CHAPTER XXIV · WHAT COMES BACK","text":"Lumen lifts the grey flame. In its light the child's face is younger than it was in the march, or older — the light can't decide.\n\n\"I carried the dead who didn't know they were dead,\" Lumen says. \"I lit their road so they'd stop being afraid. That was the whole of my job, before you.\"\n\nThe small hand finds yours.\n\n\"If you close the door, they stop leaking out. But the ones already inside stop being able to leave, too. If you open it all the way — everyone comes. The lost. And the hungry. Everything.\"\n\nLumen looks up.\n\n\"I'll light whichever road you pick. That's still my job. I just wanted you to know both roads are dark.\"","choices":[{"text":"\"Would you come back through, if I opened it?\"","next":"u24_lumen_home","do":["rel:lumen:+3"]},{"text":"\"Thank you. Stay close.\"","next":"u24_door_study","do":["rel:lumen:+2","flag:u24_lumen_counsel"]}]},{"id":"u24_lumen_home","chapter":"CHAPTER XXIV · WHAT COMES BACK","text":"The child considers this the way children consider the truly enormous questions — seriously, and without any of the flinching adults have learned.\n\n\"I've been dead a long time,\" Lumen says. \"I don't remember the sun. But I remember wanting it.\" The grey flame steadies. \"Yes. If the road home opens, I'll walk it. I'll walk it holding your hand so I don't get scared and blow out.\"\n\nSomething in your chest folds up small and warm.\n\n\"Then decide bravely,\" Lumen says. \"I'll light it either way.\"","choices":[{"text":"\"I will.\"","next":"u24_door_study","do":["rel:lumen:+4","flag:u24_lumen_wants_home","flag:u24_lumen_counsel","journal:Lumen would come home through the Gate — holding my hand so as not to blow out."]}]},{"id":"u24_gloam_talk","chapter":"CHAPTER XXIV · WHAT COMES BACK","text":"Gloam's visor is a sheet of black glass, and in it you see yourself, and behind yourself, the door. The Mirror-Knight has never shown you a face. The Mirror-Knight is a face — yours, reflected and made to kneel.\n\n\"I am bound by the mirror law,\" Gloam says, voice like a bell rung underwater. \"I am what the Gate made of the last one who tried to close it. If you close it, I unmake. That is not death. That is release. I would thank you for it.\"\n\nThe knight lays a gauntlet on the mirror shard at your belt — if you carry one, it hums; if not, the gauntlet finds only cloth.\n\n\"But hear me: the Keeper is not evil. The Keeper is bereaved. You cannot beat grief. You can only answer it, seal it away, or take it on. Choose which, and I will hold the line while you do.\"","choices":[{"text":"\"Tell me how to answer it.\"","next":"u24_gloam_answer","require":"mirror_shard"},{"text":"\"How do I take it on?\"","next":"u24_gloam_burden"},{"text":"\"Hold the line. That's all I need.\"","next":"u24_door_study","do":["rel:gloam:+2","flag:u24_gloam_counsel"]}]},{"id":"u24_gloam_answer","chapter":"CHAPTER XXIV · WHAT COMES BACK","text":"The mirror shard at your belt goes suddenly cold, then warm, as Gloam's gauntlet closes over it.\n\n\"The Keeper lost someone to black water, an age ago, and forgot her name so the loss couldn't keep cutting. But the door remembers. The door is her name, written in stone, over and over, a wound shaped like letters.\"\n\nGloam lifts the shard so it catches the grey light.\n\n\"Hold this to the door. It will show you the name. Say it — truly, gently, the way you'd say it to a child who is afraid — and you answer the grief instead of fighting it. The Keeper becomes a boy again. And a boy can be led home, or laid to rest.\"\n\nThe knight releases the shard. It thrums against your hip like a second heart.","choices":[{"text":"\"Then I have my answer.\"","next":"u24_door_study","do":["rel:gloam:+3","flag:u24_knows_answer","flag:u24_gloam_counsel","journal:Gloam: hold the mirror shard to the door to read the Keeper's forgotten name. Say it gently. Grief must be answered, not fought."]}]},{"id":"u24_gloam_burden","chapter":"CHAPTER XXIV · WHAT COMES BACK","text":"\"Someone must keep the door,\" Gloam says. \"It is a law older than the kings. If the Keeper is freed and no one takes the post, the door does not close — it opens, hungry and unheld, and eats the realm from underneath.\"\n\nThe black-glass visor tilts toward you.\n\n\"To take it on is to sit where he sits. To be hollowed slow. To forget your own name so the door has one to wear. It is the bravest ending and the cruelest. I would not wish it on you.\"\n\nA pause.\n\n\"But you are the only one here still warm enough to mean it.\"","choices":[{"text":"\"I'll remember that when I stand at the door.\"","next":"u24_door_study","do":["rel:gloam:+2","flag:u24_knows_burden","flag:u24_gloam_counsel","journal:To take the Keeper's post is to be hollowed slow, to forget your own name. The bravest and cruelest road."]}]},{"id":"u24_door_study","chapter":"CHAPTER XXIV · WHAT COMES BACK","text":"You go to the door.\n\nUp close it is not stone at all. It is writing — one word, carved a thousand thousand times, so densely the carvings become surface, become wall, become the thing itself. The word has been worn illegible by its own repetition. Whoever wrote it wrote it until the meaning bled out and only the ache was left.\n\nThe grey light comes through the gaps in the letters. Where you stand, it is very cold, and very quiet, and you understand — the way Tess said you would, if you ever met her — that the silence is the danger. Not the dark. The silence.\n\nYour own name feels loose on you here, like a coat you might set down.","choices":[{"text":"Read the name in the stone (need mirror shard).","next":"u24_read_name","require":"mirror_shard"},{"text":"Read the name in the stone (you already know it).","next":"u24_read_name","require":"true_name"},{"text":"Speak to the Keeper directly.","next":"u24_keeper_close"},{"text":"Say nothing. Simply lay your hand on the door.","next":"u24_hand_on_door"},{"text":"Steel your nerve against the pull of the silence.","next":"u24_nerve_check","check":{"stat":"fear","dc":12,"pass":"u24_nerve_pass","fail":"u24_nerve_fail"}}]},{"id":"u24_nerve_check","chapter":"CHAPTER XXIV · WHAT COMES BACK","text":"You set your feet and refuse the door.","choices":[{"text":"Hold.","next":"u24_crossroads"}]},{"id":"u24_nerve_pass","chapter":"CHAPTER XXIV · WHAT COMES BACK","text":"The silence reaches for your name and finds it nailed down.\n\nYou have walked through too much dark to go quiet now. You think of everyone who told you the same thing on the road — Tess, the widow, the captain, your own dead ancestor with his hand lifted in farewell — and their voices fill the quiet before it can empty you.\n\nWhen you open your eyes the door looks smaller. Not because it shrank. Because you grew, in the only way that matters here.","choices":[{"text":"Step to the crossroads, clear-eyed.","next":"u24_crossroads","do":["trust:+3","flag:u24_steadied","journal:I held my name against the door's silence. It looks smaller now. I do not."]}]},{"id":"u24_nerve_fail","chapter":"CHAPTER XXIV · WHAT COMES BACK","text":"The silence gets a hand around your name and pulls.\n\nFor a heartbeat you forget — who you are, why you came, whose faces you carried this far. The grey light offers you the post, gentle, gentle, and it would be so easy to sit down and be quiet forever.\n\nThen the herb-knot in your pocket, or the ache in your feet, or sheer stubbornness drags you back into your own skin. You are shaking. You held on. Barely. But barely is enough.","choices":[{"text":"Stagger back to the crossroads.","next":"u24_crossroads","do":["fear:5","journal:The door nearly took my name. I got it back. Barely counts as back."]}]},{"id":"u24_read_name","chapter":"CHAPTER XXIV · WHAT COMES BACK","text":"You lift the mirror shard — or the true name you carried in like a coal cupped against the wind — and hold it to the door.\n\nThe glass drinks the grey light. Then, slowly, the thousand illegible carvings resolve, all of them at once, into the single word they always were. Not a word of power. Not a spell. A girl's name, small and plain and heartbreaking, the kind of name a boy would whisper to a frozen river a thousand years ago and go on whispering until whispering was all he was.\n\nYou know it now. It sits on your tongue like the last coin in a dead man's mouth.\n\nBehind you, the Keeper goes very still. He does not turn. But his hands, on the wound of the door, begin — just perceptibly — to shake.","choices":[{"text":"Carry the name to the Keeper.","next":"u24_keeper_close","do":["add_item:true_name","flag:u24_has_name","journal:I read the name in the door. A girl. Drowned. The Keeper's whole grief, written a thousand times until it stopped meaning anything but ache."]}]},{"id":"u24_hand_on_door","chapter":"CHAPTER XXIV · WHAT COMES BACK","text":"You lay your palm flat against the letters.\n\nThe cold goes up your arm and into your chest and it is not cold, it is grief — someone else's, an ocean of it, an age of it, a boy at a riverbank refusing to stop looking. You feel how easy it would be to stay here holding the wound. You feel the door offering you the post, gently, the way it offers everyone, the way it offered him.\n\nYou take your hand back before the silence takes your name.\n\nYou are shaking. But you are still yourself. And now you know, in your body and not just your head, what this door is and what it wants.","choices":[{"text":"Go to the Keeper.","next":"u24_keeper_close","do":["fear:5","flag:u24_felt_grief","journal:I touched the door. It offered me the post, gentle as anything. I nearly stayed."]}]},{"id":"u24_keeper_close","chapter":"CHAPTER XXIV · WHAT COMES BACK","text":"You kneel beside the Keeper.\n\nThis close, the crown of listening is not antlers, not iron — it is names, hundreds of them, twined into a circlet, every one a thing he took because it might have been her. His face, where a face should be, is a suggestion the grey light keeps trying and failing to finish.\n\n\"You are warm,\" he says, and it is the first thing he has ever said to you. His voice is a room no one has entered in a thousand years. \"They are always warm when they come. And then I keep them, and they go quiet, and the warmth goes out, and still it is not her.\"\n\nThe hands press harder on the door.\n\n\"Have you come to fight me? They come to fight me. I am so tired of being fought.\"","choices":[{"text":"\"No. I've come to end this. Every ending is here.\"","next":"u24_crossroads"},{"text":"\"I know her name.\" (need the true name)","next":"u24_speak_name","require":"true_name"},{"text":"\"I've come to take your place, so you can rest.\"","next":"u24_offer_post"}]},{"id":"u24_speak_name","chapter":"CHAPTER XXIV · WHAT COMES BACK","text":"\"I know her name,\" you say.\n\nThe hall stops. The black water stops. Even the leaking faces in the grey light stop, and turn, as one, toward you.\n\n\"You do not,\" the Keeper says, and for the first time there is something under the room-that-is-his-voice — a floorboard, a heartbeat, a boy. \"You cannot. I forgot it. I forgot it on purpose so it would stop — so it would stop — \"\n\n\"You wrote it a thousand times,\" you say softly. \"You built the door out of it. You never forgot it. You only stopped letting yourself hear it.\"\n\nHis hands, on the wound, begin to loosen.\n\n\"Say it, then,\" he whispers. \"Say it gently. I have not been said to gently in an age of the world.\"\n\nThis is the hinge of everything. How do you use the name?","choices":[{"text":"Say the name to seal the door — grief laid to rest, forever.","next":"u24_path_seal","do":["flag:u24_name_spoken"]},{"text":"Say the name, then open the road home for everyone inside.","next":"u24_path_home","require":"companion:lumen","do":["flag:u24_name_spoken"]},{"text":"Say the name, then offer him a bargain instead of an ending.","next":"u24_path_bargain","do":["flag:u24_name_spoken"]},{"text":"Say the name — and offer to take his grief and his post as your own.","next":"u24_path_keeper","do":["flag:u24_name_spoken"]}]},{"id":"u24_offer_post","chapter":"CHAPTER XXIV · WHAT COMES BACK","text":"The Keeper turns his unfinished face fully toward you for the first time.\n\n\"Take my place,\" he repeats, tasting it, disbelieving it. \"No one offers. They come to fight, or to steal a child back, or to die badly. No one has ever knelt here and offered to stay.\"\n\nThe grey light flickers between you like a candle two people are shielding.\n\n\"You do not know what you offer. You would sit here. You would grow quiet. You would forget your own name — you must, for the door needs a name to wear and yours would do. In a hundred years you would be me, and some other warm thing would kneel where you kneel now.\"\n\nHe leans close.\n\n\"And yet. If you truly mean it — I could rest. I have wanted rest longer than your kingdom has had a crown.\"","choices":[{"text":"\"I mean it. Let me take the post.\"","next":"u24_path_keeper"},{"text":"\"Wait. There may be another way.\"","next":"u24_crossroads"}]},{"id":"u24_crossroads","chapter":"CHAPTER XXIV · WHAT COMES BACK","text":"The grey light pulses, slow, like something breathing that would rather stop. The Keeper waits. He has infinite patience. It is the only thing the post left him.\n\nYou run it back in your mind — everything the road taught you. Tess in the graveyard: the silence takes them, make noise. Halwyn: the dark is grieving, not hungry. The minstrel's seventh verse: say the name and the door will shear. Gloam: grief must be answered, sealed, or taken on. Lumen: both roads are dark.\n\nFour doors stand open in front of you now, though only one is made of stone.\n\nChoose what comes back.","choices":[{"text":"Seal the Gate forever. Say the name and close the wound. (need the true name)","next":"u24_path_seal","require":"true_name"},{"text":"Strike a bargain with the Keeper — a door half-open, on terms.","next":"u24_path_bargain"},{"text":"Take up the post yourself. Let him rest. (need to have faced the burden)","next":"u24_path_keeper","require":"flag:u24_knows_burden"},{"text":"Open the road and bring everyone back through. (need Lumen to light it)","next":"u24_path_home","require":"companion:lumen"},{"text":"You have no name, no light, no plan — only your two hands. Try anyway.","next":"u24_desperate"}]},{"id":"u24_desperate","chapter":"CHAPTER XXIV · WHAT COMES BACK","text":"You have no true name to speak, no lantern-child to light a road, no shard to read the stone. You have what you walked in with: a body, a will, and a stubbornness that has already carried you further than anyone of your line.\n\nThe Keeper watches you decide to do the impossible with empty hands.\n\n\"Warm thing,\" he murmurs, almost tender. \"You cannot fight me. But you have not come to fight, have you. You have come to end it. There is one ending that needs no name and no light. It needs only someone willing to stop being themselves.\"\n\nHe holds out a hand toward the door, toward the post, toward the long quiet.\n\n\"Or one that needs only that you dare to bargain with a thing that has never once been bargained with.\"","choices":[{"text":"Take his hand. Take the post. Let him rest.","next":"u24_path_keeper"},{"text":"Refuse the post. Offer a bargain instead.","next":"u24_path_bargain"},{"text":"Wrench the door and try to force it shut with your own strength.","next":"u24_force_combat"}]},{"id":"u24_force_combat","chapter":"CHAPTER XXIV · WHAT COMES BACK","text":"You set both hands to the seam of the door and pull. The grief pours into you — the boy, the river, the age of waiting — and the door fights back with the only weapon it has: your own longing to lay your name down and rest. This is not a battle of swords. It is a battle of who forgets first.","choices":[],"combat":{"enemy":"the Door's Silence","ehp":12,"edmg":3,"win":"u24_force_win","lose":"u24_path_keeper","flee":"u24_crossroads"}},{"id":"u24_force_win","chapter":"CHAPTER XXIV · WHAT COMES BACK","text":"You do not forget first.\n\nWhen the silence reaches for your name you fill it — with noise, with rage, with every living person you refuse to become quiet for. You shout the name of the kingdom. You shout your own. You shout {missing_name}, and {other_name}, and the widow's Owin, and the captain's Halric, until the door has no quiet left to take.\n\nThe Keeper, beside you, makes a sound that has not been made in a thousand years. It is a sob.\n\n\"You would not go quiet,\" he breathes. \"None of them would not go quiet. Only you.\"\n\nThe wound in the door is loose now, trembling, undecided. You have pried it open with sheer refusal. Now you must choose what to do with a Gate that will, for one heartbeat, do what you tell it.","choices":[{"text":"Tell it to close forever. Bargain the terms with the Keeper.","next":"u24_path_bargain","do":["flag:u24_forced_open"]},{"text":"Tell it to open the road home, and lead everyone out.","next":"u24_path_home","require":"companion:lumen","do":["flag:u24_forced_open"]},{"text":"Sit down in the wound yourself and hold it shut with your life.","next":"u24_path_keeper","do":["flag:u24_forced_open"]}]},{"id":"u24_path_seal","chapter":"CHAPTER XXIV · WHAT COMES BACK","text":"You say the name.\n\nGently. The way Gloam told you. The way you'd say it to a frightened child, or a frozen river, or a boy who has been looking for someone across a thousand years of black water.\n\nThe Keeper hears it — hears it, finally, lets himself hear it — and the crown of stolen names falls from his brow and rings on the floor like snow.\n\n\"Oh,\" he says. Just that. The whole of a grief, answered. \"Oh. That was her name. That was her name the whole time.\"\n\nThe faces in the grey light go still, and then soft, and then out — not eaten, not lost, but released, the way breath releases when you finally set down something heavy.\n\n\"You could seal it,\" he whispers. \"Say her name into the stone and it will close, and stay closed, and I will be a boy at a riverside for one moment before I am nothing. No door. No Keeper. No child taken ever again. But nothing that is inside will ever come out. Not the lost ones. Not the ones you love. That is the price of forever.\"","choices":[{"text":"Speak her name into the stone. Seal the Gate forever.","next":"ending_seal","do":["flag:region_gate","flag:codex_truename","journal:I sealed the Hollow Gate forever with a dead girl's name. The lost stay lost. The living stay safe. No child will ever be taken again."]},{"text":"Stop. This price is too high — find another ending.","next":"u24_crossroads"}]},{"id":"u24_path_bargain","chapter":"CHAPTER XXIV · WHAT COMES BACK","text":"\"No one bargains with me,\" the Keeper says. \"They fight, or flee, or offer their whole selves. No one has ever simply... negotiated.\"\n\n\"Then I'll be the first,\" you say.\n\nYou lay it out like a merchant on the Wastes road. The door stays — because a sealed door leaves the lost sealed in, and an open door lets the hungry out — but it changes its terms. No more children taken to fill the shape of a girl who drowned. A door that opens only for the willing, only for the grieving who come knowing the cost, only ever outward, never to snatch.\n\nAnd in exchange: you will bring him her name, once a year, and say it gently, so the wound is tended and never again festers into hunger. A Keeper who is kept. A grief that is visited, instead of forgotten.\n\nThe Keeper is quiet a long time.\n\n\"That is not an ending,\" he says at last. \"That is a marriage.\"\n\n\"That is how you live with grief,\" you say. \"You don't seal it. You visit it.\"","choices":[{"text":"Seal the bargain. A door on terms, tended forever.","next":"ending_bargain","do":["flag:region_gate","flag:codex_keeper","journal:I struck a bargain with the Keeper. The Gate stays — but takes no one against their will, and I return each year to say the name and tend the wound. Grief visited, not sealed."]},{"text":"He hesitates. Sweeten it — or walk away and choose another ending.","next":"u24_crossroads"}]},{"id":"u24_path_keeper","chapter":"CHAPTER XXIV · WHAT COMES BACK","text":"You reach out and take the Keeper's hands off the door, and put your own there in their place.\n\nThe grief comes up your arms and it is enormous and it is not yours and you hold it anyway, the way you'd hold a door shut against a storm, the way your ancestor Halwyn wanted to and could not. The stone is cold. It will get colder. The silence is already leaning toward your name, patient, certain.\n\nThe Keeper — the old Keeper, the boy at last — stands. Straightens. For one heartbeat he is just a young man, ordinary, with a face the grey light finally allows to be a face. He looks at you with something no post could hollow out: gratitude.\n\n\"You will forget your name,\" he says. \"I am sorry. It is the cruelest gift. But the lost inside can be led out now, by those you love, while I keep the way — no. While you keep the way. I am only a boy again. I am going to go and find a riverbank and sit down and finally, finally be finished.\"\n\nHe kisses your brow like a brother.\n\n\"Keeper,\" he says. \"Hold the door. Hold it gently. And every child that would have been taken — that is who you save, every single day, by being the one who stays.\"","choices":[{"text":"Take up the post. Be the one who stays.","next":"ending_keeper","do":["flag:codex_keeper","flag:region_gate","journal:I took up the Keeper's post. I will forget my own name so the door has one to wear. Every child not taken is the child I save by staying. The old Keeper walked away a boy, to rest at last."]},{"text":"Falter. You are not ready to be forgotten — pull back.","next":"u24_crossroads"}]},{"id":"u24_path_home","chapter":"CHAPTER XXIV · WHAT COMES BACK","text":"\"Open it,\" you tell the door. \"All the way. Outward. Let them come home.\"\n\nLumen steps up beside you and lifts the grey flame, and it is not grey anymore — it is gold, the first gold light this hall has held in an age, and it pours down the long throat of black water and out into the room of waiting where the taken have sat quiet for so long they forgot they were going somewhere.\n\n\"Make noise,\" you say to Lumen, and the child laughs — actually laughs — and the sound goes ringing down the dark, and one by one the quiet ones lift their heads. Pell's ribbon. Halric with his blue-cord knot. The widow's Owin, gone grey but grinning. And {missing_name}. And {other_name}. And every child of every house that ever set out a bowl and kept a chair facing the door.\n\nThe Keeper does not stop them. The Keeper watches them file toward the gold light, toward the sun they half-remember, and on his unfinished face is the nearest thing to peace he has known.\n\n\"But if the door stays open,\" he warns, soft, \"the hungry come too, eventually. An open Gate is an open Gate. You would be trading a sealed loss for an unsealed danger. You must choose fast, warm thing. The light will not hold forever.\"","choices":[{"text":"Lead them all out — the lost, the loved, everyone — and take the risk.","next":"ending_homecoming","do":["flag:region_gate","flag:codex_lumen","journal:I opened the road and led everyone home through the Gate — the lost, the loved, {missing_name}, all of them, lit by Lumen's gold flame. The door stands open behind us. Whatever comes next, no one is left in the dark."]},{"text":"The risk is too great — pull the light back and choose another way.","next":"u24_crossroads"}]}]}};
